{{also|Plum|plüm}}
==English==
{{slim-wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|plŭm}}, {{IPA|en|/plʌm/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-plum.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌm|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|plumb}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|plomme}}, {{m|enm|ploume}}, from {{inh|en|ang|plūme}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*plūmā}}, borrowed from {{der|en|la|prūnum}}. {{doublet|en|prune}}.

====Noun====
[[Image:Plum on tree.jpg|thumb|A plum growing on a plum tree.]]
{{en-noun}}

# ''The fruit and its tree.''
## An edible, fleshy [[stone fruit]] of {{taxfmt|Prunus domestica|species}} ({{vern|European plum}}), often of a dark red or purple colour. {{defdate|from 8th c.}}
## A [[stone fruit|stone-fruit]] tree which bears this fruit, {{taxfmt|Prunus domestica|species}}. {{defdate|from 8th c.}}
## The edible, fleshy stone fruit of several species resembling the plum, or the tree from which they grow. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
### {{taxlink|Prunus sect. Prunus|section}}
#### {{taxfmt|Prunus cerasifera|species}} ([[cherry plum]] or [[myrobalan]])
#### {{taxlink|Prunus salicina|species}} ([[Chinese plum]] or [[Japanese plum]])
#### {{taxfmt|Prunus spinosa|species}} ([[sloe]])
#### {{taxlink|Prunus ursina|species}} ({{vern|bear's plum}})
### {{taxlink|Prunus sect. Prunocerasus|section}} North American plums
#### {{taxlink|Prunus americana|species}} ({{vern|American plum}})
#### {{taxfmt|Prunus angustifolia|species}} ([[Chickasaw plum]] or [[sand plum]])
#### {{taxlink|Prunus hortulana|species}} ({{vern|hortulan plum}})
#### {{taxlink|Prunus nigra|species}} ({{vern|Canadian plum}} or [[black plum]])
#### {{taxlink|Prunus rivularis|species}} ([[creek plum]] or [[hog plum]])
#### {{taxfmt|Prunus subcordata|species}} ([[Klamath plum]] or [[Oregon plum]])
### {{taxlink|Prunus sect. Armeniaca|section}} (better known as [[apricot]]s)
#### {{taxfmt|Prunus mume|species}}, an Asian fruit more closely related to the [[apricot]] than the plum, usually consumed pickled, dried, or as a juice or wine; [[ume]].
## {{lb|en|now|rare}} A dried grape or [[raisin]], as used in a pudding or cake. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1870|title=An Old-Fashioned Girl|author=Louisa May Alcott|text=“Bright boy! here’s a '''plum''' for you,” and Polly threw a plump raisin into his mouth.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1877|title=Mother Gooose’s Nursery Rhymes|text=Little Jack Horner sat in a corner, eating a Christmas pie; he put in his thumb, and he took out a '''plum''', and said, “What a good boy am I!”}}
# ''Extended senses.''
## {{lb|en|now|rare|archaic}} One hundred thousand [[pound]]s; {{i|generally}} a [[fortune]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Smollett Peregrine Pickle|volume=I|1|text=He {{...}} acquired a plentiful fortune, tho', to his infinite regret, he died before it amounted to a '''Plum''' {{...}}.}}
## A [[desirable]] or choice thing of its kind; a prize selection; a choice appointment, assignment etc. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The mayor rewarded his cronies with cushy '''plums''', requiring little work for handsome pay.}}
##* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1880|title= The Enemies of Books|author=w:William Blades| page=52| text=Such chances as this do not fall to a man's lot twice; but Edmond Werdet relates a story very similar indeed, and where also the "'''plums'''" fell into the lap of a London dealer.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=13 Dec 80|volume=8|number=21|journal=Gay Community News|page=5|author=Ronnie Allen|title=Anita's Latest|text=As to Rev. Jerry [Falwell], he, of course, is "not anti-homosexual" but "anti-homosexuality." I guess that's his same bit of down home sophistry that explains that God listens to the prayers of Jews, if they are consciously praying to a Christian God, one of the Rev.'s latest dialectic '''plums'''.}}
## A dark [[bluish]]-[[red]] [[color]]/[[colour]], the colour of some plums. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##: {{color panel|5F4951}}
##: {{color panel|web plum|CC99CC}}
## {{lb|en|vulgar|slang|usually|in the plural}} A [[testicle]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
## {{lb|en|pejorative|chiefly|UK}} A [[fool]], an [[idiot]].

