==English==
{{phrasebook|en|Greetings}}
{{was wotd|2007|November|19}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|hallo}} {{qualifier|UK old style}}
* {{l|en|hilloa}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}
* {{l|en|hullo}} {{qualifier|UK}}

===Etymology===
''Hello'' (first attested in 1826), from {{m|en|holla}}, {{m|en|hollo}} (attested 1588). This variant of {{m|en|hallo}} is often credited to [[w:Thomas Edison|Thomas Edison]] as a coinage for telephone use, but its appearance in print predates the invention of the telephone by several decades.

Ultimately from a variant of Old English {{m|ang|ēalā}}, such as {{m|ang|hēlā}}, which was used colloquially at the time similarly to how ''hey'' and (in some dialects) ''hi'' are used nowadays. Thus, equivalent to a compound of {{m|en|hey}} and {{m|en|lo}}. Used when drawing attention to yourself.

Possibly influenced by {{ncog|osx|halo|halo!}}, imperative of {{m|osx|halōn|t=to call, fetch}}, used in hailing a ferryman, akin to {{ncog|goh|hala}}, {{m|goh|hola|hola!}}, imperative forms of {{m|goh|halōn}}, {{m|goh|holōn|t=to fetch}}. More at {{l|en|hallo}}.

OED and Merriam-Webster also suggested that it is a variant of {{m|en|holla}}, a variant of {{m|en|holloo}}. Further beyond, the origin remains uncertain. OED and Merriam-Webster suggested that it has a connection between {{m|en|hallow|t= to shout, to cry out loud}}, which came from Old French {{m|fro|holloer}}.

According to ''Altfranzösisches Wörterbuch'', Old French {{m|en|holloer}} is from Old Saxon {{m|osx|halon}}.

