==English==
[[Image:Potato and cross section.jpg|thumb|right|250 px|An intact '''potato''' (vegetable) and a cross-section of a second.]]
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|potatoe||obsolete}}
* {{alter|en|p'tater|tater||dialectal or informal}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|en|es|patata}}, itself borrowed from {{der|en|tnq|batata||[[sweet potato]]}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pəˈteɪ.təʊ/|[pə̥ˈtʰeɪtʰəʊ]|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Nattes à chat-potato.wav}}
** {{audio|en|En-uk-potato.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{enPR|pə-tāʹtō|a=GA}}, {{IPA|en|/pəˈteɪ.toʊ/|[pə̥ˈtʰeɪɾoʊ]|[pə̥ˈtʰeɪɾə]}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-potato.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪtəʊ|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|es}}

# The [[tuber]] of a plant, {{taxfmt|Solanum tuberosum|species}}, eaten as a [[starchy]] [[vegetable]], particularly in the [[Americas]] and [[Europe]]; this plant.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Condensed Replies to Various Letters|editor=Leroy M[ilton] Yale| journal=Babyhood: The Mother’s Nursery Guide, Devoted to the Care of Children| volume=VII| location=New York, N.Y.; London| publisher=Babyhood Publishing Company| year=1891| page=230| column=2| passage=Do not let your child have '''potato''' for another year; he will probably take milk well enough after he is weaned.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=C[harles] F[ord] Langworthy; Caroline L[ouisa] Hunt|title=Cheese and Its Economical Uses in the Diet|series=[[w:Farmers' Bulletin|Farmers’ Bulletin]] 487|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|Government Printing Office]]| year=1912| page=24| pageurl=https://archive.org/details/CAT87202469/page/24/mode/1up|passage=The protein value of this dish is equal to that of 1⅛ pounds of '''potato''' and beef, the fuel value, however, being much in excess of these.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Annual Report of the Local Government Board for Scotland| year=1915| page=17| passage=A number of the children said that they had '''potato''' for breakfast, '''potato''' and herring for dinner, '''potato''' and milk for supper.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Climate Change and the Health of Nations|author=Anthony J. McMichael; Alistair Woodward; Cameron Muir|year=2017|ISBN=9780190262952|page=213| passage='''Potatoes''' were introduced to Ireland in 1590 after being brought to Europe by Spanish conquistadores from their place of origin in the South American Andes.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 December 1|author=Nigel Harris|title=St Pancras and King's Cross: 1947| journal=RAIL| issue=945| page=42| text=The rocketing popularity of '''potatoes''' in London's diet led to the conversion of the temporary GNR passenger station north of the canal [...] as a dedicated '''potato''' terminal, whose significant traffic was stolen from coastal shipping.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|UK}} A [[conspicuous]] [[hole]] in a [[sock]] or [[stocking]].
# {{n-g|Metaphor for a person or thing of little value.}}
#* {{RQ:Smollett Reprisal|scene=ii|page=8|passage=I don't value Monſieur de Champignon a rotten '''potatoe'''; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|slang|offensive}} A [[mentally]] [[handicapped]] [[person]].
## {{lb|en|humorous}} A [[camera]] that takes [[poor]]-[[quality]] [[picture]]s.
## {{lb|en|humorous|slang|computing}} An [[underpowered]] [[computer]] or other [[device]], especially when [[small]] in [[size]].<!--may relate to the "potato-powered Web server" hoax-->
##* {{quote-journal|en|url=https://www.pcgamer.com/overwatch-community-donates-pc-parts-to-fan-who-could-barely-run-it-at-800x600/| date=2017-03-17| author=Steven Messner| title=Overwatch community donates PC parts to fan who could barely run it at 800x600| journal=PC Gamer| passage=When most people refer to their computers as a '''potato''' they're being hyperbolic, but not Ethan. He plays Overwatch at 800x600 resolution with all settings on low and is excited when he gets 30 fps.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|url=https://www.eurogamer.net/articles/2019-09-22-meet-the-people-making-music-with-mega-drives-and-guitars-from-game-boys| date=2019-09-22| author=Benjamin Burns| title=Meet the people making music with Mega Drives, Game Boys and gAtaris| journal=Eurogamer| passage=If you want to have a go right now, then Famitracker will let you compose songs for the NES and it'll run on a Windows-operated '''potato'''.}}

