From {{inh|sh|sla-pro|*i}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|sh|/i/}}{{attention|sh|tone?}}
* {{audio|sh|Sr-i.ogg}}

====Conjunction====
{{sh-conjunction}}

# [[and]]
#: {{ux|inline=1|sh|Ivica i Marica se vole|Ivica and Marica love each other.}}
#: {{ux|inline=1|sh|i tako dalje|and so on}}
# (i… i…) [[both]]… [[and]]…
#: {{ux|inline=1|sh|ne možeš istovremeno i tužiti i suditi.|you can't simultaneously both sue and judge}}
# [[also]], [[too]], [[as well]]
#: {{ux|inline=1|sh|i meni se sviđa vaš odabir|I like your choice too}}
# [[even]] (usually preceded by {{m|sh|čak|čȁk}})
#: {{ux|inline=1|sh|(čak) i ja sam pozvan na zabavu!|even I have been invited to the party}}
# ({{m|sh|ne}} {{m|sh|samo|sȁmo}}… {{m|sh|nego|nȅgo}}/{{m|sh|već|vȅć}} {{m|sh||i}}…) [[also]], [[too]]
#: {{ux|inline=1|sh|on je ne samo darovit, nego i jako marljiv|he is not only talented, but also very industrious}}
# [[so]], so [[that]] (= {{m|sh|te}}, {{m|sh|pa}})
#: {{ux|inline=1|sh|umorio sam se i nisam mogao više igrati košarku|I grew tired, so I couldn't play basketball anymore}}

==Sicilian==

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|scn|la|i|ī|pos=''the name of the letter'' {{m|la|I}}}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|scn|/i/}}

====Noun====
{{head|scn|nouns|g=f}}

# {{Latn-def|scn|name|I|i}}; [[#English|i]]

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|scn|i greca}}
* {{l|scn|i longa}}

===Etymology 2===
From the lenition of {{m|scn|li}}, from the conflation of the apheresis of {{der|scn|la|illi|illī}} and {{m|la|illae}}, both nominative plurals of {{m|la|ille}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|scn|/i/}} {{qual|stressed}}
* {{IPA|scn|/ɪ/}} {{qual|unstressed}}

====Article====
{{head|scn|articles|g=m-p|g2=f-p}}

# {{lb|scn|masculine and feminine plural definite article}} [[the]]
#: {{syn|scn|li}}

=====Usage notes=====
* As for other Romance languages, such as Neapolitan or Portuguese, Sicilian definite articles have undergone a consonant lenition that has led to the phonetic fall of the initial ''l''. The use of this illiquid variant has not yet made the use of liquid variants disappear, but today it is still the prevalent use in speech and writing.
* In the case of the production of literary texts, such as singing or poetry, or of formal and institutional texts, resorting to "liquid articles" and "liquid articulated prepositions" confers greater euphony to the text, although it may sound a form of courtly recovery.
* Illiquid definite articles can be phonetically absorbed by the following noun. I.e: ''l'arancini'' (liquid) and ''ârancini'' (illiquid).

