{{also|That|thật|þat}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|'at||colloquial, reduced form}}
* {{alter|en|'t||eye dialect, contraction}}
* {{alter|en|dat|thet||dialectal, nonstandard, pronunciation spellings}}
* {{alter|en|yt|[[yt|y{{sup|t}}]]|yͭ|yt.||obsolete abbreviations}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*só}} From {{inh|en|enm|that}}, from {{inh|en|ang|þæt||the, that|pos=neuter definite article and relative pronoun}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*þat}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*þat}}. Cognate to {{cog|stq|dät}}, {{cog|fy|dat}}, {{cog|nl|dat}}, {{cog|nds|dat}}, {{cog|de|dass}} and {{m|de|das}}, {{cog|da|det}}, {{cog|sv|det}}, {{cog|is|það}}, {{cog|got|𐌸𐌰𐍄𐌰}}. Further from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*tód}}; compare {{cog|grc|τό}}, {{cog|sa|तद्|tr=tád}}, {{cog|wbk|ta}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{qualifier|stressed}} {{enPR|''th''ăt}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈðæt/|a=UK,US,CA,AusE}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Back ache-that.wav|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-that.ogg|a=US}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈðɛt/|a=NZ}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈd̪æt/|/ˈd̪ɛt/|/ˈd̪æʈ/|/ˈd̪ʱæʈ/|a=India}},
* {{rhymes|en|æt|s=1}}
* {{qualifier|unstressed}} {{enPR|''th''ət}}, {{IPA|en|/ðət/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-that.wav|a=Southern England}}
: The demonstrative pronoun and determiner ''that'' is usually stressed; the conjunction and relative pronoun ''that'' is usually unstressed.
<!-- 1 syllable words !-->

===Conjunction===
{{en-con}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Introducing a clause that is the object of a verb, especially a reporting verb or verb expressing belief, knowledge, perception, etc.}}
#: {{ux|en|He told me '''that''' the book is a good read.}}
#: {{ux|en|I believe '''that''' it is true.''}}
#: {{ux|en|I can see '''that''' the ladder won't reach.''}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Introducing a clause that is the subject of a verb, especially the 'be' verb or a verb expressing judgement, opinion, etc.}}
## {{non-gloss definition|As delayed subject.}}
##: {{ux|en|It is almost certain '''that''' she will come.}}
##: {{ux|en|It amazes me '''that''' people still believe this nonsense.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|_|literary}} {{non-gloss definition|As direct subject.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''That''' she will come is almost certain.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''That''' people still believe this nonsense amazes me.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Introducing a clause that complements an adjective or passive participle.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm sure '''that''' you are right.}}
#: {{ux|en|She is convinced '''that''' he is British.}}
## {{non-gloss definition|Expressing a reason or cause:}} [[because]], [[in that]].
##: {{ux|en|Be glad '''that''' you have enough to eat.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Introducing a subordinate clause modifying an adverb.}}
#: {{ux|en|Was John there? — Not '''that''' I saw.}}
#: {{ux|en|How often did she visit him? — Twice '''that''' I saw.}}
#* {{RQ:Trollope The Claverings|passage="{{...|Tell her from me," Lady Ongar had said, "that}} I will go anywhere '''that''' she may wish if she will go with me,"}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Introducing a clause that describes the information content of a preceding reporting noun.}}
#: {{ux|en|I heard a rumour '''that''' they got married.}}
#: {{ux|en|Reports '''that''' he left the country are circulating.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Introducing — especially, but not exclusively, with an antecedent like ''[[so]]'' or ''[[such]]'' — a subordinate clause expressing a result, consequence{{,}} or effect.}}
#: {{ux|en|The noise was so loud '''that''' she woke up.}}
#: {{ux|en|The problem was sufficiently important '''that''' it had to be addressed.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=w:Zoe Williams|title=I swore I wouldn't be an embarrassing mother. But the key to being a success in life is changing your mind, right?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140814182542/https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2008/may/23/familyandrelationships.women|date=23 May 2008
|passage=My dad apparently always said that no child of his would ever be harassed for its poor eating habits, and then I arrived, and I was so disgusting '''that''' he revised his opinion.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} {{non-gloss definition|Introducing a subordinate clause that expresses an aim, purpose{{,}} or goal ("final"), and usually contains the auxiliaries ''[[may]]'', ''[[might]]''{{,}} or ''[[should]]'':}} [[so]], [[so that]], [[in order that]].
#: {{ux|en|He fought '''that''' others might have peace.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice Q1|act=I|scene=iii|sig=B2|verso=1|page=32|text=''Baſſ''[''anio'']. Be aſſured you may. / ''Shy''[''lock'']. I will be aſſured I may: and '''that''' I may be aſſured, I will bethinke me, may I ſpeake with ''Anthonio''?}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Rape of the Lock|360|text=When hungry Judges ſoon the Sentence ſign, / And Wretches hang '''that''' Jury-men may Dine; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1833|title=Parley's Magazine|volume=1|page=23
|passage=Ellen's mamma was going out to pay a visit, but she left the children a large piece of rich plumcake to divide between them, '''that''' they might play at making feasts.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1837|title=The Edinburgh New Philosophical Journal|volume=23|page=222
|passage='''That''' he might ascertain whether any of the cloths of ancient Egypt were made of hemp, M. Dutrochet has examined with the microscope the weavable filaments of this last vegetable.}}
#* {{RQ:Browning Poems|edition=new|volume=II|poem=Sonnets from the Portuguese|sonnet=XIV|page=451|passage={{...}}Since one might well forget to weep who bore / Thy comfort long, and lose thy love thereby. / But love me for love's sake, '''that''' evermore / Thou may'st love on through love's eternity.}}
#* {{RQ:Asbjornsen Braekstad Tales|chapter=Legends of the Mill|page=156|passage=In the olden days people had a stronger belief in all kinds of witchery; now they pretend not to believe in it, '''that''' they may be looked upon as sensible and educated people, as you say.}}
#* {{RQ:R. F. Burton Arabian Nights|supp=yes|volume=III|chapter=Alaeddin; or, The Wonderful Lamp|night=547|page=108|passage=Now one day of the days,{{...}}the Sultan cast his eyes upon her as she stood before him, and said to his Grand Wazir, "This be the very woman whereof I spake to thee yesterday, so do thou straightway bring her before me, '''that''' I may see what be her suit and fulfil her need."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Dallas R. Burdette|title=Biblical Preaching and Teaching|isbn=1615790853|page=340|passage=Jesus died '''that''' we might live "through" Him.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|poetic}} {{non-gloss definition|Introducing a premise or supposition for consideration:}} seeing as; inasmuch as; given that; as would appear from the fact that.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Comedy of Errors|act=III|scene=ii|page=91|column=2|text=What are you mad, '''that''' you doe reaſon ſo?}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Tale of Two Cities|chapter=The Period|page=1|text=[I]n short, the period was so far like the present period, '''that''' some of its noisiest authorities insisted on its being received, for good or for evil, in the superlative degree of comparison only.}}
#* {{circa|1911}}, {{w|D.H. Lawrence}}, third draft of what became ''{{w|Sons and Lovers}}'', in Helen Baron (editor), ''Paul Morel'', Cambridge University Press (2003), {{ISBN|978-0-521-56009-2}}, [http://books.google.com/books?id=fpHGg5qeawEC&pg=PA234&dq=that page 234]:
#*: “She must be wonderfully fascinating,” said Mrs Morel, with scathing satire. “She must be very wonderful, '''that''' you should trail eight miles, backward and forward, after eight o’clock at night.”
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|poetic}} {{non-gloss definition|Introducing an exclamation expressing a desire or wish.}}
#: {{ux|en|Oh '''that''' spring would come!}}
#* {{RQ:Douay Bible|tome=OT1|book=Deuteronomy|chapter=32|verse=29|page=463|passage=O '''that''' they were wiſe, and vnderſtoode, {{...}}}}
#* '''1864''', T. S. Norgate's translation of the ''Iliad'', book 10, page 613:
#*: 'Would '''that''' my rage and wrath would somehow stir me, / Here as I am, to cut off thy raw flesh / And eat it, {{...}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|poetic}} {{non-gloss definition|Introducing an exclamation expressing a strong emotion such as sadness or surprise.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''That''' men should behave in such a way!}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|I|ii|page=2|column=2|text=I pray thee marke me, '''that''' a brother ſhould / Be ſo perfidious: {{...}}}}

====Usage notes====
* ''That'' can be used to introduce subordinate clauses, but in most cases can just as easily be omitted: one can say either “he told me '''that''' it’s a good read” (in which case the second clause is a “''that'' clause”) or “he told me it’s a good read” (in which case the second clause is a “bare clause”). Generally speaking, the omission of ''that'' imparts an informal or conversational feel. In a few such patterns, ''that'' is mandatory; for example, in the archaic sense of "in order that", we must say "He fought that others might have peace", not "He fought others might have peace".
* Historically, ''that'' was usually preceded by a comma (“he told me, that it’s a good read”) — such usage was, for example, recommended by the grammarian [[w:Joseph Robertson (priest)|Joseph Robertson]] in his 1785 ''An Essay on Punctuation'' — but this is now considered nonstandard.
* Historically, ''that'' was sometimes used after a preposition to introduce a clause that was the object of the preposition, as in “after that things are set in order here, we’ll follow them” (Shakespeare, ''1 Henry VI''), which simply means “after things are set in order...” and would be worded thus in modern English.<ref>''The Century Dictionary and Cyclopedia'' (1903)</ref><!--see talk page for Century’s entry-->
* See also the usage notes for {{m|en|which}}.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|that clause}}
* {{l|en|that'd}}

