==English==

===Etymology 1===
The letter name is from {{inh|en|enm|a|ā}}, from {{der|en|fro|-}}, ultimately from {{der|en|la|ā}}. Use of the Latin letter in (Old) English displaced the [[futhorc]] letter {{m|ang|ᚪ}} beginning in the 7th century, and partially also {{m|ang|ᚫ}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{q|letter name}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈeɪ/|a=UK,US}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-a.ogg|a=US}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈæɪ/|a=AusE}}
** {{rhymes|en|eɪ|s=1}}
*: The current pronunciation resulted from the [[w:Great Vowel Shift|Great Vowel Shift]]. Before the early part of the 17th century, the pronunciation was similar to that in other languages.
* {{q|phoneme}} {{IPA|en|/æ/|/ɑː/|/eɪ/|/ə/}}, etc.

====Letter====
{{head|en|letter|lowercase||uppercase|A|plural|as|or|a's}}

# {{Latn-def|en|letter|1}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:George Kelly|title=w:Craig's Wife|year=1926|section=Act I|page=5|passage=Passmore. Capital P-'''a'''-double s-m-o-r-e.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In English, the letter ''a'' usually denotes the [[w:near-open front unrounded vowel|near-open front unrounded vowel]] ({{IPA|en|/æ/}}), as in ''[[pad]]'', the [[w:open back unrounded vowel|open back unrounded vowel]] ({{IPA|en|/ɑː/}}) as in ''[[father]]'', the [[w:mid-central vowel|mid-central vowel]] ({{IPA|en|/ə/}}) when unstressed as in ''[[about]]'', or, when followed by another vowel, the diphthong {{IPA|en|/eɪ/}}, as in ''[[ace]]''.
* ''A'' is the third most common letter in English.

=====Derived terms=====
* Digraph sequences: [[ae]], [[ah]], [[ai]], [[au]], [[aw]], [[ay]], [[ea]], [[eau]], [[ia]], [[oa]], [[ua]]

=====See also=====
* (''Latin script letters'') '''[[letter]]'''; [[A]][[a]], [[B]][[b]], [[C]][[c]], [[D]][[d]], [[E]][[e]], [[F]][[f]], [[G]][[g]], [[H]][[h]], [[I]][[i]], [[J]][[j]], [[K]][[k]], [[L]][[l]], [[M]][[m]], [[N]][[n]], [[O]][[o]], [[P]][[p]], [[Q]][[q]], [[R]][[r]], [[S]][[s]], [[T]][[t]], [[U]][[u]], [[V]][[v]], [[W]][[w]], [[X]][[x]], [[Y]][[y]], [[Z]][[z]]

====Numeral====
{{head|en|numeral symbols|lower case||upper case|A}}

# {{n-g|The [[ordinal]] number '''[[first]]''', derived from this [[letter]] of the English [[alphabet]], called a and written in the [[Appendix:Latin script|Latin script]].}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|a's|aes|pl2qual=rare}}

# {{n-g|The name of the [[Appendix:Latin script|Latin script]] letter '''''[[A]]''''' / '''''a'''''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William Young Ottley|title=An Inquiry Into the Origin and Early History of Engraving{{nb...|Upon Copper and in Wood, With an Account of Engravers and Their Works, From the Invention of Chalcography by Maso Finiguerra, to the Time of Marc' Antonio Raimondi}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} John and Arthur Arch,{{nb...|61, Cornhill,}} by J. M'Creery|year=1816|volume=II|page=621|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/inquiryintoorigi02ottl/page/621/mode/1up|passage=This piece somewhat resembles an '''''a'''''. On the left is a man seated on the ground, with a dog between his legs, and a large bird of prey in his hands, which appears to be biting his head.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=II|poem=The Epic|page=2|passage=But with some prelude of disparagement, / Read, mouthing out his hollow oes and '''aes''', / Deep-chested music, and to this result.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Leigh H[adley] Irvine|chapter=Abbreviations in General|title=The Magazine Style Code: A Manual For The Guidance Of Authors, Reporters And All Who Write|location=San Francisco, C.A.|publisher=Crown Publishing Company|year=1906|page=15–16|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/stylecodmagazine00irvirich/page/15/mode/1up|passage=Letters should be spelled as follows; '''aes''', bees, cees, dees, ees, efs, gees, aitches, ies, jays, kays, els, ems, ens, oes, pees, ques, ars, esses, tees, ues, vees, ws or dubleyuz, exes, wyes, zees.|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Wesley Young|author2=Frank Millett Morgan|title=Elementary Mathematical Analysis|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:The Macmillan Company|year=1917|page=487|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/elementarymathem00younrich/page/487/mode/1up|passage=This expression is zero, for we have replaced the column of '''''a''{{'}}s''' by the column of ''b''{{'}}s and hence the determinant has two columns identical.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ervin A. Dennis|author2=John D. Jenkins|chapter=A Font of Type|title=Comprehensive Graphic Arts|location=Indianapolis, I.N.|publisher=w:Howard W. Sams & Co., Inc.|year=1974|page=26|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegra00denn/page/26/mode/1up|column=2|isbn=0672208865|passage=Note that with 18-point type, fifteen capital ''A''{{'}}s, twenty-five lowercase '''a'''{{'}}s, and twelve ''1''{{'}}s are obtained with one font. With this information, it is possible to refer to Table 10-1 which gives the number of characters for each letter, punctuation mark, or figure.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Frank Pagden|chapter=Teaching|title=The Gospel According to St. Lynas|location=Tunbridge Wells|publisher=Mitre|year=1993|page=unnumbered|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/gospelaccordingt0000pagd/page/n144/mode/1up|isbn=1854242423|passage=St Lynas was chatting with a group of rebellious young college students one day, who decried the moral standards of the past. ¶ So St Lynas drew an {{'}}'''a'''{{'}} on some paper, and asked them what it was. ¶ 'A' they said.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Margaret McPhee|title=Mistress to the Marquis|location=Toronto, Ont.|publisher=w:Harlequin Historical|year=2013|page=249|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/mistresstomarqui0000mcph/page/249/mode/1up|isbn=9781460315699|passage=Across every sheet of paper were lines and lines of letters of the alphabet. A row of '''a's''' followed by a row of b's and so on, pages of them, like pages from a copy book, crudely formed as if from the hand of a young child.}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|author=w:Rivka Galchen|title=What's Become of the So-Called Literary Bad Boy?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220616230025/https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/23/books/review/whats-become-of-the-so-called-literary-bad-boy.html|date=23 February 2014|passage=In the seventh grade I admired a charismatic, witty girl who had a particular way of writing her lowercase '''a's'''. After some practice, I took to writing my lowercase '''a's''' in the same fashion.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Amanda Stevens|title=Secret of Shutter Lake|location=Toronto, Ont.|publisher=w:Harlequin Intrigue|year=2023|page=237|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/secretofshutterl0000aman/page/237/mode/1up|isbn=9781335591340|passage=Compare Lydia's signature on some of the work orders with the warning notes. The loop through the lowercase '''''a''''' is similar. See what I mean?}}

