{{head|en|adverbs|cat2=uncomparable adverbs|not [[Appendix:Glossary#comparable|comparable]]}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|Scotland}} [[all#adverb|All]]. {{defdate|from ca. 1350—1470}}

====Adjective====
{{head|en|adjectives|cat2=uncomparable adjectives|not [[Appendix:Glossary#comparable|comparable]]}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|Scotland}} [[all#adjective|All]]. {{defdate|from ca. 1350—1470}}

===Etymology 8===

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|'a}}

====Particle====
{{head|en|particles}}

# {{senseid|en|to}} {{pronunciation spelling of|en|to}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Sunshine of Childhood (Contributed)|editor=Benedict Brown|journal=The Grail|volume=4|issue=9|location=St. Meinrad, Ind.|publisher=[[w:Saint Meinrad Archabbey#Abbey_Press|The Abbey Press]]|month=January|year=1923|page=284|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_marriage-family_1923-01_4_9/page/284/mode/1up|column=2|passage=James was going with his mother to attend the ceremonies at which his oldest sister in the convent would make perpetual vows. Being asked where he was going, he answered, “I’m goin’ '''a''' see my sister make percapital vowels.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:BK Loren|chapter=Got Tape?|editor=w:Barry Lopez|title=The Future of Nature: Writing on a Human Ecology from Orion Magazine|location=Minneapolis, Minn.|publisher=w:Milkweed Editions|year=2007|page=43|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/futureofnaturewr0000unse/page/43/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-57131-306-5|passage=The man walks toward me. “I met that asshole. He’s tryin’ '''a''' sell us a bag a bullshit.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Tom Wolfe|title=[[w:Back to Blood|Back to Blood: A Novel]]|edition=Large Print|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Little, Brown and Company|date=23 October 2012|page=66|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/backtobloodnovel0000wolf_z4s4/page/66/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-316-22424-6|passage=Don’tcha try deny it, / ’Cause Hose knows you dyin’ '''a''' try it— {{...}} Knows you out tryin’ '''a''' buy it, / But Hose only gives it free}}

=====Related terms=====
{{col3|en|gonna|gotta|oughta|tryna|wanna}}

===Etymology 9===
{{contraction|en|gonna}}, itself a reduction of {{m|en|going to}}; see Etymology 8 above (“to”).

====Contraction====
{{en-cont}}

# {{lb|en|AAVE}} {{n-g|Used to express a future action}}; [[going to#Phrase|going to]].
#: {{ux|en|I'm '''a''' go see what's going on out there.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Todd Bridges|title=Killing Willis: From ''Diff'rent Strokes'' to the Mean Streets to the Life I Always Wanted|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Touchstone Books|year=2010|page=146|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/killingwillisfro0000brid/page/146/mode/1up|isbn=9781439148983|passage="Sure, Billy, I'm '''a''' run downstairs to the machine and get me a pack of [[bigarette#Noun|bigarettes]]," he said, taking off with his Melody.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Bertrand E. Brown|title=Sylvia's Dilemma: A Novel||year=2012|page=95|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sylviasdilemmano0000brow/page/95/mode/1up|isbn=9781479319091|passage=Ain't nothin' in the house to eat and now that we has Mr. Alex staying with us a few days I'm '''a''' need to buy some groceries so yous two can have the house to yo'self 'til I get back.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Monica Jeremie|title=Married to a Dade County Bully 2|publisher=Urban Chapters Publications|year=2018|page=85|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/marriedtodadecou0000jere/page/85/mode/1up|isbn=9781721659944|passage=I'm '''a''' head out there now and take a look.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ioan Grillo|title=Blood Gun Money: How America Arms Gangs and Cartels|location=New York, N.Y.{{nb...}}|publisher=w:Bloomsbury Publishing|year=2021|page=141|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bloodgunmoneyhow0000gril/page/n154/mode/1up|isbn=9781635572780|passage="The Glock 26 and the motherfucking, uh, the Hi-Point. I'm '''a''' try to get the both of them," another said.}}

====Usage notes====
* Mainly <!--always?--> used in the phrase {{m|en|I'm a}}, which is usually spelled {{m|en|Imma}}.

====Related terms====
{{col-auto|en|Imma|I'm 'onna}}

===Etymology 10===
{{contraction|en|and}}.<ref name="OED conj">{{R:OED Online|id=1916096307|pos=conj}}</ref>

====Conjunction====
{{en-con}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|dialectal|rare}} {{contraction of|en|and}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William Barton|title=Man's Monitor, or, the Free-school of Virtue; Holding Forth the Duties Required and Sins Forbidden in the Two Tables of the Law.|location=London|publisher=W.D. for T. Underhill|year=1655|page=unnumbered|pageurl=https://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/eebo2/A76065.0001.001/1:4?rgn=div1;view=fulltext|newversion=republished in|title2=Early English Books Online|location2=Ann Arbor, Mich.|publisher2=w:Text Creation Partnership|year2=p. 2011|passage=[[by cock and pie#Phrase|By cock '''a''' pie]] and Mous-foot ''Dent'' bring's in, / Examples to express forbidden Sin:}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Exmoor Scolding: ''Or, a'' Devonſhire ''Dialogue:''|magazine=w:The Gentleman's Magazine, and Historical Chronicle|location=London|publisher=[[w:Edward Cave|Edw. Cave]]|year=1746|volume=XVI|page=353|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/s2492id1329998/page/353/mode/1up|column=2|issn=2043-2992|oclc=7898058|passage=Thee lace ma? Chem a laced well-'''a'''-fine aready.—Zey wone word more, and chell breſh tha, chell make thy boddize pilmee.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Edward Moor|title=Suffolk Words and Phrases: Or, An Attempt to Collect the Lingual Localisms of that County|location=London|publisher=J. Loder|year=1823|page=2|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/suffolkwordsand01moorgoog/page/n28/mode/1up|passage=4. as ''if''. "I'll gi ye a dunt i' the hid '''{{'}}a''' ye dew so no more." This is equivalent to the "''an if''" of some of our old writers.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The ''{{w|Oxford English Dictionary}}'' notes: "The form is not common in any period, and some of the earlier examples could instead show a transmission error for {{m|en|an}} in its abbreviated form (i.e. ā, with mark of suspension)."<ref name="OED conj"/>

