==Welsh==

===Etymology 1===

====Alternative forms====
* {{q|with [[grave accent]] to indicate otherwise unpredictable short vowel}} {{l|cy|à}}
* {{q|with [[acute accent]] to indicate unusually stressed short vowel}} {{l|cy|á}}
* {{q|with [[circumflex]] to indicate otherwise unpredictable or unusually stressed long vowel}} {{l|cy|â}}
* {{q|with [[diaeresis]] to indicate disyllabicity}} {{l|cy|ä}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|cy|/aː/}}
* {{rhymes|cy|aː|s=1}}

====Letter====
{{head|cy|letter|lower case||upper case|A}}

# {{Latn-def|cy|letter|1|a}} ''It is followed by {{l|cy|b}}.''

=====Mutation=====
* a cannot be mutated but, being a vowel, does take {{l|en|h-prothesis}}, for example with the word {{m|cy|afal||apple}}:
{{cy-mut|afal}}

=====Derived terms=====
* Digraph sequences: {{l|cy|ae}}, {{l|cy|ai}}, {{l|cy|au}}, {{l|cy|aw}}, {{l|cy|ay}}

=====See also=====
* [[Wiktionary:About Welsh#Alphabetization|see other Welsh letters and their names]]

====Noun====
{{cy-noun|f|âu}}

# {{Latn-def|cy|name|A|a}}

=====Mutation=====
{{cy-mut}}

===Etymology 2===

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|cy|/aː/}}
* {{rhymes|cy|aː|s=1}}

====Verb====
{{head|cy|verb form}}

# {{lb|cy|colloquial}} {{inflection of|cy|mynd||1|s|futr}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{l|cy|af}} {{q|literary}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|cy|owl|a|a(c)}}, from {{inh|cy|cel-bry-pro|*(h)a}}, from {{inh|cy|ine-pro|*ad-gʰe}} (compare {{cog|cy|ag}} and {{cog|kw|ha}}).

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|cy|/a/}}
* {{rhymes|cy|a|s=1}}

====Conjunction====
{{head|cy|conjunction}} {{q|triggers aspirate mutation (but not always in colloquial language)}}

# [[and]]

=====Synonyms=====
* {{l|cy|ac}} {{q|used before a vowel}}

===Etymology 4===

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|cy|/a/}}
* {{rhymes|cy|a|s=1}}

====Pronoun====
{{head|cy|pronoun}} {{q|triggers soft mutation}}

# {{lb|cy|relative}} that, which, who ''(used in 'direct' relative clauses, i.e. where the pronoun refers to the subject or the direct object of an inflected verb (as opposed to a periphrastic construction with ''[[bod#Welsh|bod]]'', to be)).''
#: {{ux|cy|Y dyn '''a w'''elais i|inline=1|The man '''whom''' I saw}}

=====Usage notes=====
# ''a'' is not used with the third person singular present of the verb bod, where the relative verb form ''[[sydd#Welsh|sydd]]'' is used instead
#: {{uxi|cy|Mae'r dyn yn ifanc|The man is young}}
#: {{uxi|cy|Y dyn '''sydd''' yn ifanc|The man who is young"}}
#: not ''*Y dyn '''a''' yw'n ifanc'' 
# ''a'' is not used in indirect relative clauses, where the pronoun is part of a genitive or periphrastic construction. Instead the second relative pronoun ''[[y#Welsh|y]]'' is used
#: {{uxi|cy|Roedd chwaer y dyn yma|The man's sister was here}}
#: {{uxi|cy|Y dyn '''yr''' oedd ei chwaer yma|The man whose sister was here}}
#: not ''*Y dyn '''a''' oedd ei chwaer yma''

