From {{inh|yol|enm|þe}}, from {{inh|yol|ang|þe}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|yol|ee}}, {{l|yol|eee}}, {{l|yol|the}}, {{l|yol|tha}}, {{l|yol|thay}}, {{l|yol|th'}}, {{l|yol|ye}}, {{l|yol|y'}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|yol|/iː/|/ðiː/|/ð/|/jiː/|/j/}}
* {{homophones|yol|thaaye}}

====Article====
{{head|yol|articles}}
{{attention|yol|what does this definition even mean, aside from just "the"?}}

# [[the]], in later times '''''the'''''.
#* {{quote-book|yol|year=1867|chapter=THE WEDDEEN O BALLYMORE|number=3|page=94|title=SONGS, ETC. IN THE DIALECT OF FORTH AND BARGY|passage=Maade '''a''' nicest coolecannan that e'er ye did zee.|translation=Made '''the''' nicest coolecannan that ever you did see.}}
#* {{quote-book|yol|year=1867|chapter=SONG|number=4|page=108|title=SONGS, ETC. IN THE DIALECT OF FORTH AND BARGY|passage='''A''' vursth stroke hea strooke|translation='''The''' first stroke he struck}}
#* {{quote-book|yol|year=1867|page=110|chapter=JAMEEN QOUGEELY EE-PEALTHE|line=5-6|title=SONGS, ETC. IN THE DIALECT OF FORTH AND BARGY|passage=If ich hadh Peeougheen '''a''' Buch, Meyleare '''a''' Slut, Peedher Ghiel-laaune, an Jackeen Bugaaune,|translation=If I had Hugh '''the''' Buck, Meyler '''the''' Sloven, Peter the Smart Man, and John Boggan,}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|yol|enm|a}}, {{m|enm|an}}, from {{inh|yol|ang|an|ān|one; a; lone; sole}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|yol|e}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|yol|/ə/}}

====Article====
{{head|yol|articles}}

# [[one]]
#* {{quote-book|yol|year=1867|title=GLOSSARY OF THE DIALECT OF FORTH AND BARGY|passage='''A''' chi of barach.|translation='''A''' little barley.|page=30}}

===Etymology 3===
Unstressed form of {{m|yol|an}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|yol|/ə/}} 

====Preposition====
{{head|yol|prepositions}}

# [[on]]
#* {{quote-book|yol|year=1867|title=GLOSSARY OF THE DIALECT OF FORTH AND BARGY|passage=Aar's dhurth '''a''' heighe.|translation=There's dirt '''on''' high.|page=35}}
#* {{quote-book|yol|year=1867|line=4-6|page=116|title=CONGRATULATORY ADDRESS IN THE DIALECT OF FORTH AND BARGY|passage=Yer name var zetch avancet avare ye, e'en '''a''' dicke var hye, arent whilke ye brine o'zea an ye craggès o'noghanes cazed nae balke.|translation=Your fame for such came before you even '''into''' this retired spot, to which neither the waters of the sea below nor the mountains above caused any impediment.}}

===Etymology 4===

====Pronoun====
