Page 19 Wiktionary:About Arabic: -------- begin text --------
{{policy-TT|Language considerations (Arabic)}}{{shortcut|WT:AAR}}

:''See also'' '''[[:Category:Arabic language]]'''
----

==Orthography==

===We use all hamazāt and tāʔ marbūṭa (required reading)===
{{lang|ar|أ}} and {{lang|ar|إ}} at the beginning of a word should or else never be written with plain {{lang|ar|ا}}, although words spelled this way can be entered as lemmas with {{temp|alternative spelling of}} used to redirect to the proper spelling. (This is a {{l|en|bot}} job, though.)

When to use initial {{lang|ar|أ}}/{{lang|ar|إ}} and when to use initial {{lang|ar|ا}}? A good test that appeals to native Arabic speakers' intuition here is to attempt to prefix {{m|ar|وَ|t=and}} to the word in question. Does the sound of the hamza remain, as with e.g. {{m|ar||وَأَكْتُبُ|t=and I write}}? Then the word must be spelled with either {{lang|ar|أ}} or {{lang|ar|إ}}. Does the sound of the hamza disappear, as in {{m|ar||وَٱمْتِحَان|t=and an exam}}? Then the word must be spelled with a hamza-less {{lang|ar|ا}}.

However, intuition can't be universally relied upon, so the actual codified rules are laid out below. Most words require {{lang|ar|أ}}/{{lang|ar|إ}}, except the following:
# The definite article {{lang|ar|ال}}.
# The past tense, imperative, and verbal noun of all verbs whose past tense begins with a ''kasra'' in speech. Namely, this refers to all verbs in forms VII and higher, but excludes form IV verbal nouns (such as {{m|ar|إِضْرَاب}} and {{m|ar|إِضَافَة}}, as the past tense of Form IV begins with the sound of a ''fatḥa'') and borrowed words such as {{m|ar|إِنْجِلِيز|tr=ʔingilīz}}, {{m|ar|إِسْبَان}}, and {{m|ar|إِفْرِيقِيَا}}.
# Imperative verbs in Form I. Verbs of hamza-initial roots are tricky here, because the {{lang|ar|وَ}}-test above appears to show that they begin with a hamza; for example, {{m|ar|أتى|وَٱئْتِ|t=and come}}. However, this is only because the prefix causes elision of the initial hamza-less {{lang|ar|ا}}, allowing the root's hamza to show itself. The unprefixed word is {{m|ar|أتى|اِيتِ|t=come}} and pronounced without the root's hamza due to Arabic's historical haplology of adjacent phonetic glottal stops.
# All other words that underlyingly start with a two-consonant cluster. This includes but is not limited to the nouns {{m|ar|اِبْن}}, {{m|ar|اِسْم}} and {{m|ar|اِسْت}}.

Regarding tāʔ marbūṭa: if a word can have {{m|ar|ة|tr=-}}, the entry has to use it, not {{m|ar|ه|tr=-}}.

===ʔiʕrāb – final short vowels and nunation (required reading) ===
{{shortcut|WT:IRAB}}
We use the following system for deciding whether to include ʔiʕrāb (final, normally unpronounced short vowels and [[nunation]], e.g. the third-person masculine singular past-tense ending ''-a'' or the indefinite nominative singular ending ''-un'') in headwords, which generally follows Hans Wehr:
# Verbs are shown with full ʔiʕrāb, e.g. {{m|ar|كَتَبَ||he wrote}} and {{m|ar|يَكْتُبُ||he writes}} rather than #{{m|ar|كَتَب}} and #{{m|ar|يَكْتُب}}.
# Triptote and diptote nouns, adjectives, and participles (those ending in ''-un'' and ''-u'' in the indefinite nominative singular, respectively) are normally shown without the ʔiʕrāb, e.g. {{m|ar|قَلْب||heart}}, {{m|ar|مَكَاتِب||desks}} rather than #{{m|ar|قَلْبٌ}}, #{{m|ar|مَكَاتِبُ}}.
# Duals and sound masculine and feminine plurals omit the ʔiʕrāb, e.g. {{m|ar|بَيْتَان||two houses}}, {{m|ar|مُسْلِمُون||Muslims}}, {{m|ar|أُمَّهَات||mothers}}, {{m|ar|مُعَمَّوْن||disguised (masc. pl.)}} rather than #{{m|ar|بَيْتَانِ}}, #{{m|ar|مُسْلِمُونَ}}, #{{m|ar|أُمَّهَاتٌ}}, #{{m|ar|مُعَمَّوْنَ}}.
# Other declension types include full ʔiʕrāb, e.g. {{m|ar|قَاضٍ||judge}}, {{m|ar|مُسْتَشْفًى||hospital}}, {{m|ar|دُنْيَا||world}}. Note that pages for words ending in ''-in'' such as {{m|ar|قَاضٍ||judge}} and {{m|ar|وَادٍ||valley}} are found under e.g. {{m|ar|قاض|tr=-}} and {{m|ar|واد|tr=-}} rather than #{{m|ar|قاضي}} or #{{m|ar|وادي}}, although the latter may be created as non-lemma (construct state) forms; see the example for {{m|ar|وَادِي}}.

The same forms are copied into the declension and conjugation tables which automatically display full ʔiʕrāb. For participles and verbal nouns listed in conjugation tables the above rules about ommission of nunation apply. Usage examples may or may not include ʔiʕrāb, depending on how formal they are.

===When is manual transliteration needed?===
Primarily in the following situations:
# Whenever a word ending in {{lang|ar|ة}} occurs as other than the final word. In that case, the automatic transliteration will render the ending as ''(t)''; the manual transliteration should render it as either ''t'' or nothing, depending on whether it's in the construct state. Note that manual transliteration is ''not'' necessary when the {{lang|ar|ة}} is followed by ʔiʕrāb such as {{m|ar||ً|tr=-an}}; in such a case the {{lang|ar|ة}} will automatically be rendered as ''t''. (Analogous considerations apply to the ending {{lang|ar|اة}}, which should be rendered either ''āt'' or ''āh'' in manual translit.)
# When a word is preceded by a [[clitic]] prefix such as {{m|ar|وَـ}}, {{m|ar|بِـ}} or {{m|ar|لِـ}}, so that the hyphen can be written. The automatic transliteration will also have problems with some prefixes followed by the definite article, e.g. {{m|ar|كتاب|وَالْكِتَاب|tr=wa-l-kitāb}}, rendered as {{m|ar|كتاب|وَالْكِتَاب}} without manual transliteration, and {{m|ar|شيء|وَالشَّيْء|tr=wa-š-šayʔ}}, which without manual transliteration is rendered {{m|ar|شيء|وَالشَّيْء}}, without any transliteration. Note that manual translit is not necessary in words beginning with the definite article, e.g. {{m|ar|كتاب|الكِتَاب}}. Note also that as a special case, words with ''bi-'' + definite article are automatically handled correctly, e.g. {{m|ar|كِتَاب|بِالكِتَاب}} or {{m|ar|تَأْكِيد|بِالتَأْكِيد}}.
# In borrowed words where written long vowels are pronounced short, e.g. {{m|ar|فِيلْم|tr=film||film}}; where the vowels ''e'', ''ē'', ''o'' or ''ō'' occur, e.g. {{m|ar|بِيَانُو|tr=biyānō||piano}}; or where a letter has an unexpected pronunciation such as ''g'', e.g. {{m|ar|إِنْجْلِيزِيّ|tr=ʔinglīziyy||English}}.
# In the small number of native words not pronounced as spelled, mostly archaic spellings like {{m|ar|أُولٰئِكَ|tr=ʔulāʔika||those}}, {{m|ar|مِائَة|tr=miʔa||hundred}}, {{m|ar|عَمْرو|tr=ʿamr||Amr}}, {{m|ar|صَلٰوة|tr=ṣalāh||prayer}}.

=== How the romanization works and some guidelines to it ===
{{shortcut|WT:AR TR}}
Arabic transliterations (that is, romanizations) are not words. Arabic entries should only be written in the Arabic script. Normally the transcriptions are automatically and correctly provided by the module [[Module:ar-translit|ar-translit]] if you use the correct templates and enter the words with their Arabic vowel signs, but for details how this works:

The Wiktionary romanization system as well as the orthography for Arabic is based on the system found in Hans Wehr ''A Dictionary of Modern Written Arabic'', 4th edition, with the following modifications:
# Hamza (ʔ) is always written at the beginning of a word, except when the word begins with an elidable hamza (hamzat al-qaṭʿ).
# ''-iyy-'', ''-uww-'' are used in place of ''-īy-'', ''-ūw-''. This is because automatic transcriptions can be more easily done this way.
# ''-ay-'', ''-aw-'' are used in place of ''-ai-'', ''-au-''.
# Words with the nisba ending {{lang|ar|ـِيّ}} are transcribed with ''-iyy'' instead of ''-ī''.
# The third-person masculine singular object pronoun is always written ''-hu/-hi'' with a short vowel (Hans Wehr writes ''-hū/-hī'' following a short vowel, ''-hu/-hi'' following a long vowel). Again, even though the vowels are technically long as in {{m|ar|هذه}}, there is no way for programs to know this.

Other important points:
# {{lang|ar|ة}} gives ''a'' normally, but ''at'' in an ʔiḍāfa construction.
# {{lang|ar|اة}} gives ''āh'' normally, but ''āt'' in an ʔiḍāfa construction.
# Similarly, {{lang|ar|آة}}, as in e.g. {{l|ar|مِرْآة|gloss=[[mirror]]}}, gives ''ʔāh'' normally, but ''ʔāt'' in an ʔiḍāfa construction.
# Hamzas are always written ʔ regardless of which letter they sit on.
# Orthographic, silent و and ا occurring at the end of certain words are not transliterated. For example, the third-plural ending {{lang|ar|ـُوا}} is transliterated ''-ū'', and the name عَمْرو is transliterated ''ʿamr'' in accordance with its pronunciation. (Note that the silence of these letters appears in the Arabic spelling, indicated by the lack of any diacritic over the previous letter when fully vocalized.)
# Assimilation and elision of the definite article ''is'' shown; hence ''al-'' may appear as ''ad-'', ''aṭ-'', etc. before a sun letter or as elided ''l-'', ''d-'', ''ṭ-'' etc. after a vowel and before a sun letter. (Assimilation and elision is also reflected in fully vocalized Arabic spelling.)
# The character ''-'' is used to separate articles and other clitics (e.g. ''bi-'', ''wa-'', ''al-'', etc.). However, ''allāh'' and related forms of this word are written without the hyphen.
# Stress is ''not'' shown, since different dialects have widely varying stress systems and it conveys no distinction whatsoever in Standard Arabic.
# Capitalisation of proper nouns or beginnings of sentences is dispreferred, e.g. {{m|ar|اَلْقَاهِرَة|tr=-}} is transliterated as “al-qāhira”, not “al-Qāhira”.

