# {{lb|ar|Egypt|Sudan}} The ''yāʾ'' {{l|ar|ي}} in the final position, as spelled by the Egyptians and the Sudanese. This was the original form of the letter in final position for both of ''-ī'' and ''-ā'', before the creation of {{l|ar|ي}} in the late nineteenth century in the [[Levant]]. The continued use of this form long after the creation of {{l|ar|ي}} may be due to Ottoman influence in Egypt where the newer Arabic form was rare and this form was the prevalent. Compare that in Egypt, the Arabic alphabet letters are (still) named based on the Ottoman convention, a practice now almost exclusive to Egypt and Sudan. Electronically, the similar shaped [[ى]] is used.

