{{also|Hamza}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|en|ar|هَمْزَة}}. {{root|en|ar|ه م ز}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[sign]] ({{l|ar|ء}}{{LR}} - a stand-alone hamza) used in the [[written]] [[Arabic]] [[language]] representing a [[glottal stop]]. Hamza may appear as a stand-alone [[letter]] or most commonly [[diacritically]] over or under other letters, e.g. {{l|ar|أ}}{{LR}} (over an [[alif]] - {{l|ar|ا}}{{LR}}), {{l|ar|إ}}{{LR}} (under an alif), {{l|ar|ؤ}}{{LR}} (over a [[waw|wāw]] - {{l|ar|و}}{{LR}}) or {{l|ar|ئ}}{{LR}} (over a dotless [[yaa|yāʾ]] - {{l|ar|ى}}{{LR}}). The exact seat of hamza is governed by an orthographic rule - "seat of hamza rule".
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Claire Cock-Starkey|title=Hyphens & Hashtags|publisher=Bodleian Library|page=33|passage=Written words such as Qurʼan (which in written Arabic uses a symbol know as '''hamza''' to indicate the glottal stop) are rendered with an apostrophe.}}

====Usage notes====
* Sometimes transliterated as an apostrophe.

====Derived terms====
{{col-auto|en|hamzated|unhamzated}}

====Translations====
