Page 52 Appendix:Arabic verbs: Skipped, no changes
Page 52 Appendix:Arabic verbs: -------- begin text --------
==Derived stems==
[[File:Arabic Verb Chart.png|right|thumbnail|Arabic Verbs]]
[[File:Arabic Noun Chart.png|right|thumbnail|Arabic Nouns]]
Arabic verbs are noted for an unusual system of [[derivation]]. From any particular [[root]] various verb [[stem]]s may be formed. Western scholars usually refer to these derivations as “form I”, “form II”, ... up through “form XV,” though these designations are not used indigenously, where they are referred to by derivations from the root {{ar-root|ف|ع|ل|nocat=1}}. Accordingly, form I would be {{m|ar||فَعَلَ}}, form II would be {{m|ar||فَعَّلَ}}, etc. These forms refer to triliteral roots (those made of three consonants). There are also quadriliteral roots, made up of four consonants, which come in four forms, “form Iq”, “form IIq”, “form IIIq” and “form IVq”. Triliteral forms XI through XV and quadriliteral forms IIIq and IVq are rare and tend to be intransitive, often stative, verbs (having the meaning “to be or become Y” where Y is an adjective).

These forms and their associated participles and verbal nouns are the primary means of forming vocabulary in Arabic. All of the examples shown here are the [[citation]] forms, which in Arabic means the 3rd-person masculine singular perfect (e.g., “he did”, “he wrote”).

===Form I===
Perfective {{m|ar||فَعَلَ}}, {{m|ar||فَعِلَ}}, {{m|ar||فَعُلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَفْعَلُ}}, {{m|ar||يَفْعِلُ}}, {{m|ar||يَفْعُلُ}}, active participle {{m|ar||فَاعِل}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مَفْعُول}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِفْعَلْ}}, {{m|ar||اِفْعِلْ}}, {{m|ar||اُفْعُلْ}}

This is the simplest basic form of a verb; it gives the general idea of its root. Most verbs are [[triliteral]], but there are a few [[quadriliteral]] ones.

* {{l|ar|قَتَلَ|t=to [[kill]]|lit=he killed}}
* {{l|ar|سَلِمَ|t=to be [[safe]]|lit=he was safe}}
* {{l|ar|ضَحِكَ|t=to [[laugh]]|lit=he laughed}}
* {{l|ar|حَبَّ|t=to [[love]]|lit=he loved}}

When the middle vowel of the perfective is ''a'', the middle vowel of the imperfective may be ''a'', ''i'', or ''u''. When the perfective vowel is ''i'', the imperfective vowel is usually ''a''; when the perfective vowel is ''u'', the imperfective vowel is also ''u''.
{| class="wikitable"
! perfective<br>vowel !! imperfective<br>vowel !! colspan="3" | example
|-
| rowspan="3" | a || u || {{l|ar|كَتَبَ}} || {{l|ar|يَكْتُبُ}} || to write
|-
| i || {{l|ar|غَسَلَ}} || {{l|ar|يَغْسِلُ}} || to wash
|-
| a || {{l|ar|ذَهَبَ}} || {{l|ar|يَذْهَبُ}} || to go
|-
| rowspan="2" | i || a || {{l|ar|شَرِبَ}} || {{l|ar|يَشْرَبُ}} || to drink
|-
| i || {{l|ar|وَثِقَ}} || {{l|ar|يَثِقُ}} || to trust
|-
| u || u || {{l|ar|كَرُمَ}} || {{l|ar|يَكْرُمُ}} || to be generous
|}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-I verbs}}

===Form II===
Perfective {{m|ar||فَعَّلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يُفَعِّلُ}}, verbal noun generally {{m|ar||تَفْعِيل}}, in [[#Weak verbs|weak verbs]] necessarily and final-hamzated verbs facultatively {{m|ar||تَفْعِلَة}}, with [[#geminate verbs|geminate verbs]] sometimes {{m|ar||تَفْعَال}}, in rare historical cases a {{m|ar||فِعَّال}} verbal noun exists, active participle {{m|ar||مُفَعِّل}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مُفَعَّل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||فَعِّلْ}}

This stem is formed by doubling the second [[radical]]. The meaning this form imparts is [[intensive]], [[causative]], or [[declarative]].
* {{l|ar|كَتَّبَ|t=to cause to [[write]]|pos=causative}}
* {{l|ar|قَتَّلَ|t=to [[massacre]]|pos=intensive}}
* {{l|ar|كَبَّرَ|t=to make [[big]]|pos=causative}}
* {{l|ar|مَرَّضَ|t=to nurse the sick|pos=negative}}

It is frequently used as a [[denominative]] formation to convert nouns or adjectives into verbs.
* {{l|ar|جَنَّسَ|t=to classify}}, from {{l|ar|جِنْس|t=kind}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-II verbs}}

===Form III===
Perfective {{m|ar||فَاعَلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يُفَاعِلُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||مُفَاعَلَة}} or: {{m|ar||فِعَال}} active participle {{m|ar||مُفَاعِل}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مُفَاعَل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||فَاعِلْ}}

This stem is formed by lengthening the vowel after the first [[radical]]. The meaning of this form is associative, meaning the action of the root is either done with somebody or something else, or to somebody or something else, and it is usually transitive. The [[indirect object]] of form I is the [[direct object]] of form III. Thus, the object of the preposition {{m|ar|إِلَى|t=to}} in {{m|ar||كَتَبَ إِلَى أَحْمَدَ|t=he wrote to Ahmad}} becomes the direct object of the verb in {{m|ar||كَاتَبَ أَحْمَدَ|t=he corresponded with Ahmad}}.
* {{l|ar|كَاتَبَ|t=to [[correspond]] with}}
* {{l|ar|قَاتَلَ|t=to try to [[kill]]}}
* {{l|ar|سَالَمَ|t=to make [[peace]] with}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-III verbs}}

===Form IV===
Perfective {{m|ar||أَفْعَلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يُفْعِلُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||إِفْعَال}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفْعِل}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مُفْعَل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||أَفْعِلْ}}

This stem is formed by prefixing {{m|ar||أَـ}} and dropping the vowel of the first [[radical]]. In the imperfect, the {{m|ar||أَ}} disappears and the regular imperfect prefix takes the vowel '''u''', and the characteristic is '''i''': {{m|ar|يُكْتِبُ}}. The meaning this form imparts is usually [[causative]].
* {{l|ar|أَكْتَبَ|t=to [[dictate]]; to make someone write}}
* {{l|ar|أَخْلَفَ|t=to [[leave]], to [[compensate]]}}
* {{l|ar|أَسْلَمَ|t=to [[submit]]}}

Sometimes it has a [[declarative]] meaning: to say that someone has a certain quality.
* {{l|ar|أَحْمَدَ|t=to consider praiseworthy}} — from {{l|ar|حَمِدَ|t=to praise}}

Occasionally Form IV is derived from a noun and has an intransitive meaning:
* {{l|ar|أَذْنَبَ|t=to [[sin]]}} — from {{m|ar|ذَنْب|t=a sin}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-IV verbs}}

===Form V===
Perfective {{m|ar||تَفَعَّلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَتَفَعَّلُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||تَفَعُّل}} or {{m|ar||تِفِعَّال}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُتَفَعِّل}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مُتَفَعَّل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||تَفَعَّلْ}}

This stem is formed by prefixing {{m|ar|تَـ}} to form II. The meaning this form imparts is the [[reflexive]] or [[passive]] of form II. Out of the [[reflexive]] also arises the [[effective]]. This differs from the [[passive]] in that the latter indicates that the person is the object of, or experiences the effect of, the action of a another; whereas the [[effective]] implies that an act is done to a person, or a state produced in them, whether it is caused by another or themselves.

* {{l|ar|تَفَرَّقَ|t=to [[scatter]]|pos=intransitive}}
* {{l|ar|تَخَلَّفَ|t=to [[hold back]] from}}
* {{l|ar|تَسَلَّمَ|t=to receive the [[surrender]]}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-V verbs}}

In pre-classical language the formant can be {{m|ar|تْـ}} instead of {{m|ar|تَـ}} and assimilates then to alveolars. Example: {{m|ar|اِصَّدَعَ}} instead of {{m|ar|تَصَدَّعَ}}, present {{m|ar|يَصَّدَّعُ}} instead of {{m|ar|يَتَصَدَّعُ}}.<ref name="Fischer47">{{R:ar:Fischer|section=§ 47|page=26}}</ref>

===Form VI===
Perfective {{m|ar||تَفَاعَلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَتَفَاعَلُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||تَفَاعُل}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُتَفَاعِل}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مُتَفَاعَل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||تَفَاعَلْ}}

This stem is formed by prefixing {{m|ar|تَـ}} to form III. The imperfect has the vowel '''a''' throughout, except for the last: {{m|ar|يَتَكَاتَبُ}}, ''yatakātabu''. The meaning this form imparts is [[reciprocal]] or one of [[pretence]].
* {{l|ar|تَقَاتَلَ|t=to [[fight]] with one another}}
* {{l|ar|تَكَاتَبَ|t=to [[write]] to each other}}
* {{l|ar|تَسَالَمَ|t=to make [[peace]] together}}
* {{l|ar|تَنَاوَمَ|t=to pretend to [[sleep]]}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-VI verbs}}

In pre-classical language the formant can be {{m|ar|تْـ}} instead of {{m|ar|تَـ}} and assimilates then to alveolars (sun letters).<ref name="Fischer47"/>

===Form VII===
Perfective {{m|ar||اِنْفَعَلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَنْفَعِلُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِنْفِعَال}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُنْفَعِل}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مُنْفَعَل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِنْفَعِلْ}}

This stem is formed by prefixing {{m|ar|نـ}} with a [[prothetic]] vowel ({{m|ar|اِ}}) where necessary (''in-''). The meaning this form imparts is [[reflexive]] or [[passive]]. Note: this form should not be made from roots whose first radical is &#x202D;{{m|ar|ر}}, {{m|ar|ل}}, {{m|ar|ي}}, {{m|ar|و}}, {{m|ar|أ}}, or {{m|ar|ن}}, although some people do it.
* {{l|ar|اِنْكَتَبَ|t=to [[subscribe]]}}
* {{l|ar|اِنْكَسَرَ|t=to be [[broken]]}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-VII verbs}}

===Form VIII===
Perfective {{m|ar||اِفْتَعَلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَفْتَعِلُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِفْتِعَال}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفْتَعِل}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مُفْتَعَل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِفْتَعِلْ}}

This stem is formed by infixing {{m|ar|ـتَـ}} after the first [[radical]], and with a [[prothetic]] vowel ({{m|ar|اِ}}) where necessary. The meaning this form imparts is the [[reflexive]] or sometimes [[passive]], of the first form.
* {{l|ar|اِقْتَتَلَ|t=to [[fight]] with one another}}
* {{l|ar|اِكْتَتَبَ|t=to [[write]] to each other}}
* {{l|ar|اِسْتَلَمَ|t=to [[receive]]}}

When the first radical of the root is {{m|ar|ط}} , {{m|ar|ض}} , {{m|ar|ص}} , {{m|ar|ز}} , {{m|ar|ذ}} , {{m|ar|د}} , {{m|ar|ث}} , {{m|ar|ت}} , or {{m|ar|ظ}}, the infixed {{m|ar|ـتـ}} is completely assimilated, or assimilated in voicing or emphasis:
* {{m|ar|اِدَّرَكَ}} = {{m|ar|ت}} + {{m|ar|دَرَكَ}} &ndash; complete assimilation
* {{m|ar|اِزْدَحَمَ}} = {{m|ar|ت}} + {{m|ar|زَحَمَ}} &ndash; voicing assimilation
* {{m|ar|اِضْطَرَبَ}} = {{m|ar|ت}} + {{m|ar|ضَرَبَ}} &ndash; emphasis assimilation; formerly assimilation of both voicing and emphasis when {{m|ar|ط}} was a voiced consonant

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-VIII verbs}}

===Form IX===
Perfective {{m|ar||اِفْعَلَّ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَفْعَلُّ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِفْعِلَال}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفْعَلّ}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِفْعَلَّ}}

This stem is formed by dropping the vowel of the first radical, adding a [[prothetic]] vowel ({{m|ar|اِ}}) where necessary, and doubling the final radical. This form is used by only a small number of verbs denoting color or bodily defect. This form has no passive participle. It is frequently connected to an adjective with the form {{m|ar||أَفْعَل}}; see {{section link|Appendix:Arabic_nominals#Color_or_defect_adjectives}}.
* {{l|ar|اِحْمَرَّ|t=to turn [[red]], to [[blush]]}} &ndash; {{l|ar|أَحْمَر|t=red}}
* {{l|ar|اِصْفَرَّ|t=to turn [[yellow]], to [[pale]]}} &ndash; {{l|ar|أَصْفَر|t=pale}}
* {{l|ar|اِبْيَضَّ|t=to turn [[white]]}} &ndash; {{l|ar|أَبْيَض|t=white}}
* {{l|ar|اِزْرَقَّ|t=to turn [[blue]]}} &ndash; {{l|ar|أَزْرَق|t=blue}}
* {{l|ar|اِعْوَجَّ|t=to be [[twisted]]}}<!-- is this correct? &ndash; {{subst:chars|l|ar|eaEwaj|t=twisted}} -->

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-IX verbs}}

===Form X===
Perfective {{m|ar||اِسْتَفْعَلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَسْتَفْعِلُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِسْتِفْعَال}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُسْتَفْعِل}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مُسْتَفْعَل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِسْتَفْعِلْ}}

This stem is formed by prefixing {{l|ar||ـسْتَـ}}, with a [[prothetic]] vowel ({{l|ar|اِ}}) where necessary, and dropping the vowel of the first [[radical]]. The meaning this form imparts is to ask or think that the sense of form I should be done.
* {{l|ar|اِسْتَقْتَلَ|t=to [[risk]] oneʼs life}}
* {{l|ar|اِسْتَكْتَبَ|t=to ask someone to [[write]]}}
* {{l|ar|اِسْتَسْلَمَ|t=to [[submit]], to keep to the [[middle of the road]]}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-X verbs}}

===Other forms (XI-XV)===
These forms were already rare in Classical Arabic, and are even more so in Modern Standard Arabic.

====Form XI====
Perfective {{m|ar||اِفْعَالَّ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَفْعَالُّ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِفْعِيلَال}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفْعَالّ}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِفْعَالَّ}}

This stem is formed from form IX by lengthening the vowel after the second [[radical]]. This form is very rare and it is usually used only for metrical purposes in poetry as an alternative to form IX. Some scholars <ref>{{cite-book|last=Haywood|first=J.A.|last2=Nahmad|first2=H.M.|title=A New Arabic Grammar of the Written Language by Haywood|publisher=Harvard University Press|year=1962|page=185}}</ref> suggest the XI form may have a more volatile meaning than form IX, as well as one slightly more intensive.
: Example: {{l|ar|اِحْمَارَّ|t=to turn [[red]], to [[blush]]}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-XI verbs}}

====Form XII====
Perfective {{m|ar||اِفْعَوْعَلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَفْعَوْعِلُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِفْعِيعَال}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفْعَوْعِل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِفْعَوْعِلْ}}

: Example: {{l|ar|اِحْدَوْدَبَ|t=to be or become humpbacked}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-XII verbs}}

====Form XIII====
Perfective {{m|ar||اِفْعَوَّلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَفْعَوِّلُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِفْعِوَّال}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفْعَوِّل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِفْعَوِّلْ}}

: Example: {{l|ar|اِعْلَوَّطَ|t=to ride or mount a camel without a saddle}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-XIII verbs}}

====Form XIV====
Perfective {{m|ar||اِفْعَنْلَلَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَفْعَنْلِلُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِفْعِنْلَال}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفْعَنْلِل}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِفْعَنْلِلْ}}

: Example: {{l|ar|اِسْحَنْكَكَ|t=to be or become caliginous}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-XIV verbs}}

====Form XV====
Perfective {{m|ar||اِفْعَنْلَى}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَفْعَنْلَى}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِفْعِنْلَاء}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفْعَنْلٍ}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِفْعَنْلَ}}

: Example: {{l|ar|اِسْرَنْدَى|t=to vanquish}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-XV verbs}}

===Form Iq===
Perfective {{m|ar||فَعْلَقَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يُفَعْلِقُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||فَعْلَقَة}} or: {{m|ar||فَعْلَاق}}, {{m|ar||فِعْلَاق}}, {{m|ar||فُعْلَاق}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفَعْلِق}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مُفَعْلَق}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||فَعْلِقْ}}

This is the simplest basic form of a quadriliteral verb. Most verbs are transitive, although a subset with reduplicated roots often are not. This form is similar to form II of triliteral roots.

* {{l|ar|طَمْأَنَ|t=to [[pacify]]}}
* {{l|ar|تَرْجَمَ|t=to [[translate]]}}
* {{l|ar|هَنْدَسَ|t=to [[engineer]]}}
* {{l|ar|قَهْقَهَ|t=to [[laugh]] loudly}}

The formation is sometimes used to convert nouns into verbs.

* {{l|ar|فَيْلَسُوف|t=philosopher}} &rarr; {{l|ar|فَلْسَفَ|t=to philosophize}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-Iq verbs}}

===Form IIq===
Perfective {{m|ar||تَفَعْلَقَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَتَفَعْلَقُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||تَفَعْلُق}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُتَفَعْلِق}}, passive participle {{m|ar||مُتَفَعْلَق}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||تَفَعْلَقْ}}

This is a quadriliteral root stem, formed by prefixing {{m|ar||تَ}} to form Iq. The meaning this form imparts is the [[reflexive]] or [[passive]] of form Iq. This form is similar to form V of triliteral roots.

* {{l|ar|تَبَلْوَرَ|t=to be [[crystalize]]d}}
* {{l|ar|تَزَحْلَقَ|t=to [[ski]], to [[skate]]}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-IIq verbs}}

====Form IIIq====
Perfective {{m|ar||اِفْعَنْلَقَ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَفْعَنْلِقُ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِفْعِنْلَاق}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفْعَنْلِق}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِفْعَنْلِقْ}}

This stem is formed by prefixing {{m|ar||اِـ}}, dropping the vowel of the first radical, and adding a {{m|ar||ـَ}} to and infixing {{m|ar||نـ}} after the second [[radical]].

: Example: {{l|ar|اِحْرَنْجَمَ|t=to crowd}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-IIIq verbs}}

====Form IVq====
Perfective {{m|ar||اِفْعَلَقَّ}}, imperfective {{m|ar||يَفْعَلِقُّ}}, verbal noun {{m|ar||اِفْعِلْقَاق}}, active participle {{m|ar||مُفْعَلِقّ}}, imperative (2nd person, m, sg) {{m|ar||اِفْعَلِقَّ}}

This stem is formed by prefixing {{m|ar||اِـ}}, dropping the vowel of the first radical, and adding a {{m|ar||ـَ}} to the second radical, and [[gemination|geminating]] the final radical.

* {{l|ar|اِطْمَأَنَّ|}} (ṭ-m-’-n) — to be [[calm]].
* {{l|ar|اِقْشَعَرَّ}}
* {{l|ar|اِشْمَأَزَّ}}

:  {{also|:Category:Arabic form-IVq verbs}}

==Root types==

===Sound verbs===
Verbs are considered sound if none of the radicals is {{lang|ar|و}} or {{lang|ar|ي}} or {{lang|ar|ء}}, nor are the second and third radicals identical.
:  {{also|:Category:Arabic sound verbs}}

===Hamzated verbs===
Verbs are called hamzated if {{lang|ar|ء}} (hamza) is one of the root consonants (radicals). The phonetical pattern of the conjugation of these verbs doesn't differ from other verbs in all forms but because of the rules of positioning of [[w:hamza|hamza]], the spelling is affected in some forms, e.g. the verb {{l|ar|أَكَلَ}} (ʾ-k-l) has the form {{l|ar|يَأْكُلُ}} in the imperfect masculine singular and the verb {{l|ar|قَرَأَ}} (q-r-ʾ) has the form {{l|ar|تَقْرَئِينَ}} in the imperfect feminine singular.
:  {{also|:Category:Arabic hamzated verbs}}

===Weak verbs===
''TO DO''
Weak verbs are verbs that have one of the radicals {{lang|ar|و}} or {{lang|ar|ي}}.
#  Assimilated verbs (R1 = {{lang|ar|و}} or {{lang|ar|ي}})
#:  Form I: {{l|ar|وَجَدَ}} - "to find", imperfective {{l|ar|يَجِدُ}}.
#:  {{also|:Category:Arabic assimilated verbs}}
#  Hollow verbs (R2 = {{lang|ar|و}} or {{lang|ar|ي}})
#:  Form I:  {{l|ar|قَالَ}} - "to say" {{l|ar|قَالَتْ}} "she said" but {{l|ar||قُلْتُ|t=I said}}, {{l|ar||قُلْتَ|t=you {{g|m}} said}}, {{l|ar||قُلْتِ|t=you {{g|f}} said}}; imperfective {{l|ar|يَقُولُ}} "he said", {{l|ar|يَقُلْنَ}} "they (f.) say".
#:  {{also|:Category:Arabic hollow verbs}}
#  Final-weak verbs (R3 = {{lang|ar|و}} or {{lang|ar|ي}}), e.g. {{l|ar|مَشَى}} - "to walk", {{l|ar|رَمَى}} - "to throw", {{l|ar|حَظِيَ}} - "to be in the good graces of, to enjoy", {{l|ar|بَقِيَ}} - "to stay, to remain", {{l|ar|دَعَا}} - "to call sb., to summon sb."., {{l|ar|لَقِيَ}} - "to meet sb.".
#:  {{also|:Category:Arabic final-weak verbs}}

Other examples of weak verbs:
*  {{l|ar|عَلَا}} - "to rise"
*  {{l|ar|عَلَى}} - "to raise"
*  {{l|ar|وَلِيَ}} - "to follow" (assimilated and final-weak)
*  {{l|ar|سَرُوَ}} - "to become honourable"
*  {{l|ar|غَنَّى}} - "to sing" (form II)
*  {{l|ar|غَطَّى}} - "to cover, to wrap" (form II)
*  {{l|ar|آمَنَ}} - "to have faith" (form IV, assimilated)
*  {{l|ar|آوَى}} - "to shelter" (form IV, hollow and final-weak (and initially-hamzated))
*  {{l|ar|تَغَيَّرَ}} - "to change" (form V)
*  {{l|ar|تَطَوَّعَ}} - "to volunteer" (form V)
*  {{l|ar|اِتَّهَمَ}} - "to accuse" (form VIII, root w-h-m)
*  {{l|ar|اِحْتَاجَ}} - "to need" (form VIII)
*  {{l|ar|اِزْدَوَجَ}} - "to be doubled" (form VIII)
*  {{l|ar|اِسْتَوْرَدَ}} - "to import" (form X)

===Quadriliteral verbs===
''TO <!--The Arabic verb forms

Most Arabic words are derived from a three-letter (trilateral) root. And each trilateral Arabic root can theoretically be transformed into one of fifteen possible verb forms ({{m|ar|الْأَوْزَان}}). (Forms 11 through 15 are very rare, so people usually just focus on forms 1 through 10, although 9 is also pretty rare). Each form has a basic meaning associated with the general meaning of the root being used. Here's a more detailed breakdown, using فعل (fa3ala, to do) as an example. (This is all taken from old handouts I got at the AUC, so it's not my original work.)
Form 1 - فعل (fa3ala)
Expresses the general verbal meaning of the root in question

Root	Form 1 verb
خ ر ج (x-r-j) - leaving, departing	خرج (xaraja) - to leave, go out
ج م ع (j-m-3) - joining, uniting	جمع (jama3a) - to gather, collect
ع م ل (3-m-l) - doing, making	عمل (3amala) - to work, to do, to make
ق ط ع (q-T-3) - cutting	قطع (qaTa3a) - to cut, cut off
ب ع د (b-3-d) - separating, distance     	بعد (ba3ada) - to be far from

Form 2 - فعّل (fa33ala)
Built on form 1 by doubling the middle radical of the form 1 verb (adding a shadda to it)
Often is a causative version of the form 1 verb
خرج (xaraja) means "to go out"; خرّج (xarraja) means "to make (s.o.) go out; to graduate (s.o.)"

Often an intensive version of the form 1 verb (especially if the form 1 verb is transitive)
جمع (jama3a) means "to collect, gather"; جمّع (jamma3a) means "to amass, to accummulate"

Form 3 - فاعل (faa3ala)
Built on form 1 by adding an alif between the first and second radicals of the form 1 verb
Usually gives an associative meaning to the form 1 verb; describes someone doing the act in question to or with someone else
عمل (3amala) means "to work"; عامل (3aamala) means "to treat or deal with (s.o.)"

Form 4 - أفعل (af3ala)
Built on form 1 by prefixing an alif to the form 1 verb and putting a sukuun over the first radical
Similar to form 2 in that it is usually a causative version of the form 1 verb
خرج (xaraja) means "to go out"; خرّج (xarraja) means "to graduate (s.o.)"; أخرج (axraja) means "to expel, to evict; to produce"

Form 5 - تفعّل (tafa33ala)
Built on form 2 by adding the prefix تـ to the form 2 verb
Often a reflexive version of the form 2 verb
خرّج (xarraja) means "to graduate (s.o.)"; تخرج (taxarraja) means "to graduate" (Note: form 5 is usually intransitive)

Sometimes an intensive version of a form 1 verb
جمع (jama3a) means "to collect, gather"; تجمّع (tajamma3a) means "to congregate, to flock together"

Form 6 - تفاعل (tafaa3ala)
Built on form 3 by adding the prefix تـ to the form 3 verb
Usually a reflexive version of the form 3 verb
عامل (3aamala) means "to treat or deal with (s.o.)"; تعامل (ta3aamala) means "to deal with each other" (Form 6 is usually intransitive)

Form 7 - انفعل (infa3ala)
Built on form 1 by adding the prefix انـ to the form 1 verb
Usually a reflexive and/or passive version of the form 1 verb
قطع (qaTa3a) means "to cut, to cut off"; انقطع (inqaTa3a) means "to be cut off (from); to abstain (from)"

Form 8 - افتعل (ifta3ala)
Built on form 1 by adding the prefix ا to the form 1 verb and placing a sukuun must be placed over its first radical
Often a reflexive version of the form 1 verb
جمع (jama3a) means "to collect, gather"; اجتمع (ijtama3a) means "to meet; to agree (on)"

Sometimes has a specially derived meaning relative to a form 1 verb
بعد (ba3ada) means "to be far away"; ابتعد (ibta3ada) means "to avoid"

Form 9 - افعلّ (if3alla)
Built on form 1 by adding the prefix ا to the form 1 verb, placing a sukuun over its first radical, and adding a shadda to the last radical
Relates to colors
ح م ر (H-m-r) relates to "redness"; احمرّ (iHmarra) means "to become or turn red"

Form 10 - استفعل (istaf3ala)
Built on form 1 by adding the prefix استـ to the form 1 verb and inserting a ت between the first and second radicals; a sukuun must be placed over the first radical
Often a considerative version of the form 1 verb; means "to -->DO'' Quadriliteral verbs are made from roots having four radicals, e.g. {{m|ar|تَرْجَمَ}} - "to translate"; {{m|ar|هَنْدَسَ}} - "to engineer"; {{m|ar|قَهْقَهَ}} - "to laugh loudly"; {{m|ar|تَبَلْوَرَ}} - "to be crystalized" (form IIq); {{m|ar|اِحْرَنْجَمَ}} - "to press one another" (form IIIq); {{m|ar|اِطْمَأَنَّ}} - "to be calm" (form IVq).
:  {{also|:Category:Arabic verbs with quadriliteral roots}}

===Geminate verbs===
''TO DO'' Geminate verbs are verbs that have the second and the third radicals the same, e.g. {{m|ar|مَدَّ}} - "to stretch"; {{m|ar|دَلَّ}} - "to indicate"; {{m|ar|ظَنَّ}} - "to think"; {{m|ar|حَاقَّ}} - "to sue, to litigate" (form III); {{m|ar|أَحَبَّ}} - "to love" (form IV); {{m|ar|تَضَادَّ}} - "to be opposed to one another" (form VI); {{m|ar|اِنْشَقَّ}} - "to split" (form VII); {{m|ar|اِحْتَلَّ}} - "to occupy" (form VIII); {{m|ar|اِسْتَرَدَّ}} - "to demand back" (form X).
:  {{also|:Category:Arabic geminate verbs}}

