{{character info|ﺀ}}
==Arabic==
{{wikipedia|hamza}}

===Etymology===
Most Arabic dialects early on lost the {{w|Proto-Semitic}} glottal stop, which caused compensatory lengthening of a preceding short vowel. At the beginning of the Islamic period only some of the most conservative lects preserved the consonant. Therefore it was not reflected in the spelling: One generally wrote and pronounced {{m|ar|ذِيب|t=wolf}}, for example. The grammarians, however, favoured the conservative pronunciation and indicated the glottal stop with various diacritic marks, which ultimately led to the contemporary {{m|ar|ذِئْب}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|ء}}

===Letter===
{{head|ar|letter|head2=[[هَمْزَة]]}}

# {{ngd|{{l|en|hamza|Hamza}} ({{m-self|ar|ء|tr=-}}) is a sign in the Arabic script that represents the glottal stop {{IPAchar|/ʔ/}}. It can be considered a [[diacritic]] or a [[letter]], but it is not counted among the 28 letters of the alphabet. The exact spelling of the glottal stop depends on the combination of vowels that follow and precede it. It may be written with {{m|ar|آ|tr=-}} {{IPAchar|/ʔaː/}}, {{m|ar|أ|tr=-}} {{IPAchar|/ʔa/|/aʔ/|/ʔu/}}, {{m|ar|إ|tr=-}} {{IPAchar|/ʔi/}}, {{m|ar|ئ|tr=-}} {{IPAchar|/ʔi/|/iʔ/}}, {{m|ar|ؤ|tr=-}} {{IPAchar|/ʔu/|/uʔ/}}, or, usually after a long vowel, {{m|ar|ء|tr=-}}.}}

====Usage notes====
* The curious vowel-dependent spelling of {{m|ar||ء|tr=-}} means that the [[triptotic]] noun declension, which is normally not reflected in the spelling, may become visible before a pronominal suffix. Compare: {{m|ar||أَصْدِقاؤُنَا|t=our friends|pos=nominative}}, {{m|ar||أَصْدِقائِنَا|pos=genitive}}, {{m|ar||أَصْدِقاءَنَا|pos=accusative}}.

====See also====
* {{l|ar|آ}}, {{l|ar|أ}}, {{l|ar|إ}}, {{l|ar|ؤ}}, {{l|ar|ئ}}, {{l|ar|ٱ|tr=-}}
* {{pedia|Arabic alphabet}}

