==Arabic==

===Etymology 1===

====Pronunciation====
* {{lbl|ar|informal}} {{ar-IPA|تَا مَرْبُوطَة}}

====Letter====
{{head|ar|letter|tr=-}}{{LR}} ({{l|ar|تَاء مَرْبُوطَة}})

# {{n-g|{{l|ar|ة|tr=tāʔ marbūṭa}} is a variant of the letter {{l|ar|ت|tr=tāʔ}} used at the end of words. It is formed from the letter {{l|ar|ه|tr=hāʔ}} with the addition of the two overdots of {{l|ar|ت|tr=tāʔ}}.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* It mostly exists in grammatically feminine words. It always follows a [[fatḥa]] <big>{{m|ar|ـَ|tr=-}}</big> ("a"), and it is replaced with a normal {{m|ar|ت|tr=tāʔ}} when a suffix is added, e.g. {{m|ar|غُرْفَة|gloss=a room}} -> {{m|ar||غُرْفَتِي|gloss=my room}} ({{m|ar|غُرْفَة}}{{LR}} ({{lang|ar|ة}}{{LR}} -> {{m|ar|ت|tr=-}}{{LR}}) + {{m|ar|ـِي}}{{LR}}). It may only be in the middle or end of a word.
* When [[desinential inflection]] is used in Classical Arabic or in formal Modern Standard Arabic, non-pausal {{m|ar|ة|tr=-}} is pronounced as {{IPAchar|/t/}} (preceded by usually unwritten fatḥa "a") and has -u/un, -i/-in, -a/an case endings, e.g. {{m|ar|لُغَةٌ|tr=-}} - "luḡah" (pausal), "luḡa''tun''" formal, non-pausal pronunciation ([[nominative case]], indefinite).
* Adverbials from ''tāʾ marbūṭa'' do not add alif ({{m|ar|ا}}) with fatḥatān. Instead, fatḥatān is attached directly to tāʾ marbūṭa and the "hidden t" is pronounced with the ending "-an" e .g. {{m|ar|عَادَةً|gloss=usually}}.
* In a more relaxed orthography {{m|ar|ة|tr=-}} is replaced with {{m|ar|ه|tr=hāʔ}} with the same pronunciation, especially in Egypt and Sudan.
* Loanwords in other languages (e.g. {{cog|fa|-}} or {{cog|tr|-}}) from words with ''tāʾ marbūṭa'' use the endings ''at'', ''et'', ''ah'', ''a'' or ''e''.

===Etymology 2===
From {{der|ar|sem-pro|*-at-}}, from {{der|ar|afa-pro|*-t|*-t, *-Vt|pos=feminine suffix}}. See also {{cog|egy|-t}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{lbl|ar|informal|pausal}} {{IPA|ar|/-a/|/-ah/}}
* {{IPA|ar|/-e/|a=Levantine Arabic}} {{q
|following a buccal, non-{{w|emphatic consonant|emphatic}} consonant other than {{IPAchar|/r/}}; following {{IPAchar|/iːr/}}}}
* {{IPA|afb|/-ə/|a=Gulf}} {{q|except when preceded by {{l|afb|ـا|tr=ā}}}}

====Suffix====
{{head|ar|suffix|head=ـَة}}

# {{n-g|Suffix used to form feminine adjectives from masculine adjectives.}}
# {{n-g|Suffix used to form feminine nouns from masculine nouns referring to people.}}
# {{n-g|Suffix used to form [[singulative]] nouns (referring to one item) from [[collective]] nouns (referring to a collection of items): for instance, {{m|ar|بَقَرَة|t=[[cow]]}} from {{m|ar|بَقَر|t=[[cattle]]}} or {{m|ar|شَجَرَة|t=[[tree]]}} from {{m|ar|شَجَر|t=[[trees]]}}.}}
# {{senseid|ar|instance noun}} {{n-g|Suffix used to form [[instance noun]]s (''nomina vicis'', referring to a single instance of an action) from [[verbal noun]]s: for instance, {{m|ar|اِبْتِسَامَة|t=a [[smile]]}} from {{m|ar|اِبْتِسَام|t=[[smiling]]}} or {{m|ar|اِنْتِفَاضَة|t=an uprising, an [[intifada]]}} from {{m|ar|اِنْتِفَاض|t=[[rising]] [[up]], [[shaking]] [[off]]}}.}}
# {{n-g|Suffix used to form [[abstract noun]]s from [[nisba]] adjectives in {{m|ar|ـِيّ}}: for instance, {{m|ar|اِشْتِرَاكِيَّة|t=[[socialism]]}} from {{m|ar|اِشْتِرَاكِيّ|t=[[socialist]]}}. Alternatively, the combination of the two suffixes can be viewed as a single suffix {{m|ar|ـِيَّة}}.}}
# {{n-g|Suffix used to form nouns referring to devices from occupational/characteristic nouns and adjectives (e.g. {{m|ar|دَبَّابَة|t=[[tank]]}} from {{m|ar|دَبَّاب|t=[[crawling]], [[crawler]]}}) and from active participles (e.g. {{m|ar|طَابِعَة|t=[[printer]] {{qualifier|computing device}}}} from {{m|ar|طَابِع|t=[[printer]] {{qualifier|person}}}}).}}
# {{n-g|Suffix used to form nouns of various sorts from adjectives: for instance, {{m|ar|قَرِينَة|t=[[connection]]}} from {{m|ar|قَرِين|t=[[connected]]}} or {{m|ar|مُعَلَّقَة|t=[[poster]], [[placard]]}} from {{m|ar|مُعَلَّق|t=[[suspended]]}}.}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|ar}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|afb|ـة|tr=-a}}
* {{desc|mt|-a}}

{{cln|ar|inflectional suffixes|noun-forming suffixes|feminine suffixes}}

==South Levantine Arabic==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ajp|ar|ـَة}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ajp-IPA|e<p:e><p:a><p:ɑ><q:Lemma Form>|a<p:a><p:ɑ><q:Lemma Form>|t<p:(ɪ)t><q:Construct Form>}}

===Suffix===
{{ajp-suffix|head=ـة|tr=-e/-a/-t|g=f}}

# {{n-g|Ending of most feminine nouns in the singular}}
# {{n-g|Feminine suffix for nouns referring to people}}
# {{n-g|Feminine suffix for adjectives}}
# {{n-g|[[singulative|Singulative]] suffix from [[collective]] nouns (they become grammatically feminine)}}

====Usage notes====
* Nouns ending in {{m-self|ajp|ـة}} are not always feminine, it can also occur in broken plurals and rarely in masculine singular nouns.
* According to the dialect, the underlying phoneme may be either {{IPAchar|/e/}} or {{IPAchar|/a/}}. Among those with {{IPAchar|/e/}}, the realization varies according to the previous consonant as follows:
** “-a” ({{ajp-IPA|e<p:ɑ>}}) after “emphatic” consonants (ḍ, q, r, ṣ, ṭ, ẓ), e.g. {{m|ajp|قصّة|tr=ʔuṣṣa}}, {{m|ajp|فراطة|tr=frāṭa}}, {{m|ajp|فكرة|tr=fikra}}
** “-a” ({{ajp-IPA|e<p:a>}}) after “throat” consonants (ʔ, ʕ, ḡ, h, ḥ, ḵ), e.g. {{m|ajp|لغة|tr=luḡa}}, {{m|ajp|جهة|tr=jiha}}, {{m|ajp|شقّة|tr=šaʔʔa}}
** “-e” ({{ajp-IPA|e<p:e>}}) after the remaining consonants and “r” in the ending “-īr”, e.g. {{m|ajp|غرفة|tr=ġurfe}}, {{m|ajp|كاسة|tr=kāse}}, {{m|ajp|كبير|كبيرة|tr=kbīre}}
** silent after a vowel “a”, which is then pronounced long and stressed, e.g. {{m|ajp|حياة|tr=ḥayā}}, {{m|ajp|صلاة|tr=ṣalā}}
* In the construct form (i.e. when followed by an enclitic determiner or as the first noun of an [[idafa]]), the pronunciation is {{IPAchar|/t/}}.
** In the case of [[idafa]], the spelling remains unchanged:
*: {{uxi|ajp|[[لهجة]] [[بلد|البلد]]|tr=lahj'''it''' il-balad|t=the language of the country}}
** When a suffix is added, the spelling changes to {{m|ajp|ت|tr=t}}:
*: {{uxi|ajp|[[لهجة|لهجت]][[ها]]|tr=lahj'''it'''-ha|t=her language}}

====See also====
* {{n-g|Feminine plural suffix for nouns}}: {{l|ajp|ـات|tr=-āt}}
