Wiktionary uses the following system to transcribe Moroccan Arabic (Darija), based on the urban koine standard spoken in Casablanca.

{|class = "wikitable"
|-
!Letter
!Romanization
!IPA
!Notes
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ا]]}}
|
|
|Used as a silent carrier for vowels in initial position, and a elsewhere. The sound a is {{IPAchar|[æː]}} in most positions, but {{IPAchar|[ɑː]}} when subject to emphasis spreading. Used for initial {{IPAchar|[ʔ]}} when it carries a hamza.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[أ]]}}
|ʾa ʾo ʾ
|{{IPAfont|ʔa ʔʊ ʔ}}
|It is used to spell a combination of glottal stop and a vowel ({{IPAfont|ʔa, ʔu}}), elsewhere just a glottal stop ({{IPAfont|ʔ}}). Always transliterate the glottal stop, including in initial position (using the ʾ character, a half-ring; do not use an apostrophe).
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[إ]]}}
|ʾi
|{{IPAfont|ʔiː}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[آ]]}}
|ʾa
|{{IPAfont|ʔaː}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ب]]}}
|b
|{{IPAfont|b}}
| Romanized as ḅ when used to write the emphatic form {{IPAchar|[bˤ]}}, although some systems (e.g. Harrell's) do not mark it when there is another emphatic consonant in a word, because only the emphatic form can be found in that context. The marginal phoneme {{IPAchar|[bˤʷ]}} is romanized as ḅ̂, and unlike normal emphatics, is pronounced with strong pharyngealization and only affects the quality of neighboring short vowels.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[پ]]}}
|p
|{{IPAfont|p}}
| A marginal phoneme only found in borrowings, often pronounced as {{IPAchar|[b]}} and written as ب.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ت]]}}
|t
|{{IPAfont|tˢ}}
| Romanized as t́ when used to write the plain form {{IPAchar|[t]}}, a marginal phoneme only found in borrowings.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ث]]}}
|t
|{{IPAfont|tˢ}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ج]]}}
|ž
|{{IPAfont|ʒ}}
| The sound represented by this letter in MSA can sometimes correspond to {{IPAchar|[ɡ]}} or occasionally {{IPAchar|[d]}} in stems where a sibilant, lateral, or rhotic consonant also occurs. Unlike in MSA, ج is a sun letter.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ح]]}}
|ḥ
|{{IPAfont|ħ}}
| Emphatic consonant.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[خ]]}}
|ḵ
|{{IPAfont|χ}}
| Emphatic consonant.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[د]]}}
|d
|{{IPAfont|d}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ذ]]}}
|d
|{{IPAfont|d}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ر]]}}
|r
|{{IPAfont|ɾ}}
| Romanized as ṛ when used to write the emphatic form {{IPAchar|[ɾˤ]}}. When geminated, it is pronounced as {{IPAchar|[r]}}.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ز]]}}
|z
|{{IPAfont|z}}
| In stems with a following ž, etymological z may harmonize to ž, in which case it may be written as ج.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[س]]}}
|s
|{{IPAfont|s}}
| In stems with a following š, etymological s may harmonize to š, in which case it may be written as ش.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ش]]}}
|š
|{{IPAfont|ʃ}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ڜ]]}}
|č
|{{IPAfont|t͡ʃ}}
| A marginal phoneme only found in borrowings, which may be written with a digraph, as تش.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ص]]}}
|ṣ
|{{IPAfont|sˤ}}
| Emphatic consonant. In stems with a following š, etymological ṣ may harmonize to š, in which case it may be written as ش.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ض]]}}
|ḍ
|{{IPAfont|dˤ}}
| Emphatic consonant.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ط]]}}
|ṭ
|{{IPAfont|tˤ}}
| Emphatic consonant.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ظ]]}}
|ḍ
|{{IPAfont|dˤ}}
| Emphatic consonant.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ع]]}}
|ʿ
|{{IPAfont|ʕ}}
| Emphatic consonant.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[غ]]}}
|ḡ
|{{IPAfont|ɣ}}
| Emphatic consonant.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ف]]}}
|f
|{{IPAfont|f}}
| The marginal phoneme {{IPAchar|[fˤʷ]}} is romanized as f̣̂, and unlike normal emphatics, is pronounced with strong pharyngealization and only affects the quality of neighboring short vowels.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ڥ]]}}
|v
|{{IPAfont|v}}
| A marginal phoneme only found in borrowings, often pronounced as {{IPAchar|[f]}} and written as ف.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ق]]}}
|q or g
|{{IPAfont|q}} or {{IPAfont|ɡ}}
| Unpredictable; must be specified. The Casablanca standard tends toward exclusive use of {{IPAchar|[q]}}, but {{IPAchar|[ɡ]}} has been introduced through the influence of Hilalian dialects. When pronounced as {{IPAchar|[q]}}, it is an emphatic consonant.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ك]]}}
|k
|{{IPAfont|k}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ݣ]]}}/{{lang|ar|[[گ]]}}
|g
|{{IPAfont|ɡ}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ل]]}}
|l
|{{IPAfont|l}}
| Romanized as ḷ when used to write the emphatic form {{IPAchar|[lˤ]}}.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[م]]}}
|m
|{{IPAfont|m}}
| Romanized as ṃ when used to write the emphatic form {{IPAchar|[mˤ]}}, although some systems (e.g. Harrell's) do not mark it when there is another emphatic consonant in a word, because only the emphatic form can be found in that context. The marginal phoneme {{IPAchar|[mˤʷ]}} is romanized as ṃ̂, and unlike normal emphatics, is pronounced with strong pharyngealization and only affects the quality of neighboring short vowels.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ن]]}}
|n
|{{IPAfont|n}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ه]]}}
|h
|{{IPAfont|h}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[و]]}}
|w
|{{IPAfont|w}}
| For vowel uses, see below.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ي]]}}
|y
|{{IPAfont|j}}
| For vowel uses, see below.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ى]]}}
|a
|{{IPAfont|æː}}
| For allophones, see below.
|-
|{{lang|ar|[[ء]]}}
|ʾ
|{{IPAfont|ʔ}}
| Etymological use is often silent.
|-
!colspan=5|Diacritics
|-
|{{lang|ar|ـَ}}
|e or ă
|{{IPAfont|ə}} or {{IPAfont|æ}}
| e surfaces as {{IPAchar|[ɐ]}} near emphatic consonants and as {{IPAchar|[u]}} after w. The sound ă is used by many speakers, but only when adjacent to ḥ or ʿ, and may be replaced by e entirely for other speakers.
|-
|{{lang|ar|ـُ}}
|o
|{{IPAfont|ʊ}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|ـِ}}
|e or ĭ
|{{IPAfont|ə}} or {{IPAfont|i}}
| For the allophones of e, see above. ĭ is a marginal phoneme only found in borrowings from MSA.
|-
|{{lang|ar|ـَو}}
|ăw or u
|{{IPAfont|aw}} or {{IPAfont|uː}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|ـُو}}
|u
|{{IPAfont|uː}}
| u is {{IPAchar|[oː]}} when subject to emphasis spreading.
|-
|{{lang|ar|ـَی}}
|ăy or i
|{{IPAfont|aj}} or {{IPAfont|iː}}
|
|-
|{{lang|ar|ـِی}}
|i
|{{IPAfont|iː}}
| i is {{IPAchar|[eː ~ ɨː]}} when subject to emphasis spreading.
|}

==References==
*Dominique Caubet (2007), "Moroccan Arabic" in Kees Verstegh (ed.), ''Encyclopedia of Arabic Language and Linguistics'', Vol. 3. Brill, {{ISBN|9004144730}}.
*Richard Harrell (2004), ''A Dictionary of Moroccan Arabic: Moroccan-English''. Georgetown University Press, {{ISBN|1589011031}}.
*Jan Hoogland (2013), "Towards a Standardized Orthography of Moroccan Arabic Based on Best Practices and Common Ground Among a Selection of Authors" in Paula Santillán, Luis Miguel Pérez, and Francisco Moscoso (eds.), ''Árabe marroquí: de la oralidad a la enseñanza''. Ediciones de la Universidad de Castilla-La Mancha, Cuenca.
*Janet Watson (2002), ''The Phonology and Morphology of Arabic''. Oxford University Press, {{ISBN|978-0-19-925759-1}}.

==Other Resources==
* Richard S. Harrell, Mohammed Abu-Talib, William S. Carroll (2006), ''A Basic Course in Moroccan Arabic with MP3 Files''.
