* Dental, labial and velar stops are always aspirated, with few exceptions.

* {{IPAchar|/t/}} and {{IPAchar|/d/}} are phonetically dental in nearly all varieties. Though they are also phonemically dental in Hazaragi and some other varieties of Dari.

* Alif is, with few exceptions, only a glottal stop in word initial positions

* Geminated consonants are shown with the consonant diacritic [[tashdid]] ({{lang|fa|ـّ}}). Geminated consonants are transliterated with doubled letters, both in IPA and in transliterations.

* hē ({{lang|fa|ه}}) in word final positions may act as a placeholder for any short vowel instead of a consonant. Most commonly the short vowel {{IPAchar|/a/}}.

====About retroflex forms====
Some regional dialects in Afghanistan, such as Hazaragi, have the retroflex consonants {{IPAchar|/ʈ/}} and {{IPAchar|/ɖ/}} as distinct phonemes. However, Hazaragi is treated by its speakers as a spoken form of Dari so it does not have a standardized written form. As these phonemes are not present in standard Dari, their standardized forms are always written with tē ({{lang|fa|ت}}) and dāl ({{lang|fa|د}}) respectively. 

Occasionally the nonstandard characters {{lang|fa|ٹ}}, {{lang|fa|ڈ}} or {{lang|fa|ټ}}, {{lang|fa|ډ}} are used by Hazaragi speakers. But there is no consensus on how these forms should be treated.

===Vowels===
The vocalization used by Classical Persian and Dari differs slightly from the vocalization used by Iranian Persian. The table below shows the vocalization used by Classical Persian and Dari.
