==Catalan==

===Etymology 1===
From the final syllable of {{der|ca|la|domine||Mister}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ca-IPA}}

====Article====
{{head|ca|articles|elided|n'|g=m-s|feminine|na}}

# {{lb|ca|Eastern Catalan}} {{n-g|Personal article used before masculine given names instead of the definite article {{m|ca|el}}.}}
#: {{ux|ca|'''En''' Pau i na Maria arribaran demà.|Pau and Maria will be arriving tomorrow.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* While this article (and its feminine counterpart {{m|ca|na}}) is standard in Balearic Catalan, in other Eastern Catalan dialects its use is waning, and the elided of the definite article, {{m|ca|l'}}, is used before names beginning with vowels. There is no plural personal article, so the plural definite article {{m|ca|els}} is used in all dialects.

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|ca|can}} {{qualifier|contraction of ''ca'' and ''ne''}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|ca|la|in||in, inside}}, from {{inh|ca|itc-pro|*en}}, from {{inh|ca|ine-pro|*h₁én||in}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ca-IPA}}

====Preposition====
{{head|ca|prepositions}}

# [[in]]

===Etymology 3===
{{inh+|ca|la|inde||thence}}. Compare {{cog|fr|en}}, {{cog|it|ne}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ca-IPA}}

====Pronoun====
{{head|ca|pronouns|cat2=personal pronouns|proclitic||contracted|n'|enclitic|ne|contracted enclitic|'n}}

# {{n-g|Represents an indeterminate number or quantity of a given noun.}}
# {{n-g|Represents a place (associated with the action described by the verb) that would be introduced by the preposition {{l|ca|de}}.}}
# {{n-g|Replaces a phrase introduced by the preposition {{l|ca|de}}.}}
# {{n-g|Replaces the object of a [[causative]] verb.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* ''en'' cannot be used more than once as the object of a given verb.
* While ''en'' is usually used to replace phrases beginning with the preposition {{m|ca|de}}, adverbial phrases (e.g., de {{m|ca|pressa}}) are replaced with {{m|ca|hi}}.
* ''en'' is sometimes used instead of {{m|ca|ho}} to replace an adjective or indefinite noun as the predicate of a verb.
* ''en'' is sometimes used popularly to add emphasis to a sentence: in this sense, it has no translation in English.
* {{U:ca:pronouns|form=reinforced}}

=====Declension=====
{{ca-decl-ppron}}

=====See also=====
* {{l|ca|hi}}
* {{l|ca|ho}}

