==Swedish==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|sv|gmq-osw|en|ēn}}, {{m|gmq-osw|æn}}, from {{inh|sv|non|einn}}, from {{inh|sv|gem-pro|*ainaz||one, some}}, from {{inh|sv|ine-pro|*h₁óynos|t=one}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|sv|/ɛnː/|;|/ɛŋː/<qq:numeral and article when used before ''g'' in close syntactic connection>}}
* {{homophones|sv|än|N|n}}

====Pronoun====
{{head|sv|pronouns|genitive|ens}}

# [[one]]; ''object form of'' [[man#Swedish|man]] (=''[[one]]'')
#: {{ux|sv|Det man inte vet skadar '''en''' inte|What one doesn’t know doesn’t hurt '''one'''.}}
# [[one]] (see usage notes)

=====Usage notes=====
''En'' has in recent years been used as a more gender-conscious alternative to the impersonal pronoun {{m|sv|man}}. The development is in some ways parallel to the gender-neutral pronoun {{m|sv|hen}}. Usage is common among certain speaker groups, but not universally acknowledged in the standard language.[http://www.dn.se/kultur-noje/ordet-man-blir-en-i-kampen-for-jamlikhet/] Previously it has also been used in some dialects.

