==English==
{{wikipedia}}
{{wikipedia|human feces}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|faeces||British}}
* {{alter|en|fæces||archaic}}

===Etymology===
{{dercat|en|qfa-sub}}
From {{bor|en|la|faecēs}}, nominative plural of {{m|la|faex||residue, dregs}}, further origin unknown; possibly borrowed from a {{der|en|qfa-sub}} language.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfiːsiːz/}}
{{rfap|en}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːsiːz|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|p}} {{tlb|en|North American}}

# [[digested|Digested]] [[waste]] material (typically solid or semi-solid) [[discharge]]d from a [[human]] or other [[mammal]]'s [[stomach]] to the [[intestine]]s; [[excrement]].

====Usage notes====
* This word can be used with plural verbs ("feces have a strong smell") or singular ones ("feces has a strong smell"). Use with plural verbs is more common, especially in Britain, and is the only use recognized by some dictionaries,<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref> while others recognize both plural and singular use.<ref>{{R:TheFreeDictionary}}</ref>
* A singular form {{m|en|faex}} is rarely used, in some academic contexts, particularly when the spelling {{m|en|faeces}} is used for the plural.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|discharged animal waste}} {{l|en|dung}}, {{l|en|excrement}},  {{l|en|droppings}}, {{l|en|faecal matter}}, {{l|en|caca}}, {{l|en|spoor}}
* {{sense|discharged domestic animal waste}} {{l|en|manure}}
* {{sense|discharged bird or bat waste}} {{l|en|guano}}
* {{l|en|night soil}}, {{l|en|number two}}  {{qualifier|euphemistic}}
* {{l|en|doo}}, {{l|en|poo}}, {{l|en|poop}}, {{l|en|boo-boo}}, {{l|en|doo-doo}}, {{l|en|dookie}} and {{l|en|doody}} {{qualifier|euphemistic or hypocoristic}}
* {{l|en|crap}}, {{l|en|shit}}, {{l|en|turd}}, {{l|en|log}} {{qualifier|vulgar}}
* See also [[Thesaurus:feces]]

