{{also|-ś|-'s|Appendix:Variations of "s"}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/s/}} {{q|following a [[voiceless]] consonant {{ic|/p, t, k, f, θ/}}}}
* {{IPA|en|/z/}} {{q|[[postvocalic]] or following a [[voiced]] consonant {{ic|/m, n, ŋ, b, d, ɡ, v, ð, l, ɹ/}}}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɪz/}} {{q|following a [[sibilant]] consonant {{ic|/s, z, ʃ, ʒ, tʃ, dʒ/}}—usually written [[-es#English|-es]]}}
** {{IPA|en|/əz/|a=weak vowel}}
* {{homophones|en|-'s|'s}}

===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|en|enm|-s}}, {{m|enm|-es}}, from {{inh|en|ang|-as}}, nominative-accusative plural ending of masculine a-stem (i.e. strong) declension nouns, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*-ōs}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*-ōs}}, {{m|gem-pro|*-ōz|*&#8209;ōz}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*-es}}, {{m|ine-pro|*-oes|pos=plural endings}}. The spread of this ending in later Middle English was once argued to have been the result of {{cog|xno|-}} influence; however, ''-as'' was already the most common Old English plural marker (used in approximately 40% of Old English nouns), and was initially more common in the North of England where French influence was weakest, only later gradually spreading south. Cognate with {{cog|sco|-s|pos=plural ending}}, {{cog|stq|-s|pos=plural ending}}, {{cog|fy|-s|pos=plural ending}}, {{cog|nl|-s|pos=plural ending}}, {{cog|nds|-s|pos=plural ending}}, {{cog|da|-er|pos=plural ending}}, {{cog|sv|-r}}, {{m|sv|-ar}}, {{m|sv|-or|pos=plural ending}}, {{cog|is|-ar|pos=plural ending}}, {{cog|got|-𐍉𐍃|pos=nominative plural ending of a-stem masculine nouns}}. Not directly related to {{cog|de|-er|pos=plural ending}} which has a different origin.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix|cat2=inflectional suffixes}}

# {{senseid|en|regular plural}} {{non-gloss definition|Used to form [[regular]] [[plural]]s of [[noun]]s.}}
#: {{ux|en|one computer → five computer'''s'''}}
# {{senseid|en|pluralia tantum}} {{non-gloss definition|Used to form many [[plurale tantum|pluralia tantum]] (nouns that are almost or entirely without singular forms).}}
#: {{ux|en|short'''s'''}}
#: {{ux|en|sunglass'''es'''}}
# {{senseid|en|numerical range}} {{non-gloss definition|When appended to a number ending in at least one 0, expresses a range of numbers which share the digits before some or (usually) all of the 0s; frequently used for decades, centuries and temperatures.}}
#: {{ux|en|We often look back on the 1960'''s''' ''[the years from 1960 to 1969]'' with rose-tinted glasses.}}
#: {{ux|en|The Industrial Revolution went into full-swing during the 1800'''s''' ''[the years from 1800 to 1899 – but see usage notes]''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The temperature today will be in the low 30'''s'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|He placed in the 200'''s''' in the end.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{sense-lite|regular plurals}} In semi-formal or formal contexts, where the plurality of a noun depends on some unknown aspect of the sentence, the ''s'' may be parenthesised: "The winner(s) will be invited to a prize ceremony."
* {{sense-lite|number}} Decades formed with ''-s'' are usually pronounced as if they were written as two separate numbers. For example, ''1970s'' is read as ''nineteen-seventies'', as if it were written as ''19 70s'', not as ''*nineteen-hundred seventies'' or ''*one thousand nine hundred and seventies''.
* {{sense-lite|number}} When the number before the ''-s'' ends in more than one zero, there is ambiguity about the range of numbers that is meant. For example, ''1800s'' could mean "the years from 1800 to 1899; the entire 19th century" or "the years from 1800 to 1809; the first decade of the 19th century". The intended meaning can usually be derived from context.

