|-en
|-es
|-i
|-ir
|-a
|[[-z]] {{q|nonstandard}}
}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|-es|-(e)s|pos=third-person singular ending}}, from Northumbrian {{inh|en|ang|[[-es]], [[-as]]|pos=third-person singular endings}}. Gradually replaced the older {{m|en|-eth}}, from {{cog|ang|-eþ|-(e)þ}}, {{m|ang|-aþ}}, during the Middle English and Early Modern English periods.

It is predominantly believed that {{m|enm|-es|-(e)s}} is identical to the Old English second-person singular ending {{m|ang|-es}}, {{m|ang|-est}} (cf. archaic Modern English {{m|en|-est}}, as in {{m|en||thou sing'''est'''}}). The use for the third-person singular would have been caused by speakers of {{der|en|non|-}} who switched to speaking English and confounded the endings due to analogy with their native tongue. In Old Norse, the second and third person singular indicative forms were identical (e.g. {{m|non||þú [[masa|mas'''ar''']], hann mas'''ar'''}}; {{m|non||þú [[þekkja|þekk'''ir''']], hann þekk'''ir'''}}; etc.).

An alternative theory sees the shift from {{IPAchar|/θ/}} in {{m|enm|-eth}} to {{IPAchar|/s/}} (later {{IPAchar|/z/}}) in {{m|enm||-(e)s}} as a mere phonetic simplification due to the frequency of the ending, but the objection to this is that no such development can be observed anywhere else in English. Nevertheless the relative similarity in sound between both forms may have facilitated the spread of {{m|enm||-es}}.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix|cat2=inflectional suffixes}}

# Used to form the [[third person|third-person]] [[singular]] [[indicative]] [[present tense]] of [[verb]]s.
#: ''to eat'' → ''he eat'''s'''''

=====Usage notes=====
* In Standard English, the ''-s'' suffix is only used to mark the third person singular present of verbs; however, in some varieties of English, particularly northern English, Scottish, US Southern and [[AAVE]], the ''-s'' can be extended to other persons/numbers as well, as in: ''I eat'''s''' me spinach''; ''I hate'''s''' the Yankees''; ''they like'''s''' it here''; etc.
* As in modern nonstandard varieties, the ''-s'' suffix can be used to mark the third-person plural in {{w|Early Modern English}}. However, it is less common than the modern standard {{w|Null morpheme|zero ending}}, and is frequently only found in restricted contexts; for instance, {{w|Shakespeare}} only uses it where a plural subject has "singular" semantics.<ref>{{cite-book|chapter=The third person present plural in Shakespeare’s First Folio: A case of interaction of morphology and syntax?|author=Herbert Schendl|title=Words: Structure, meaning, function. A festschrift for Dieter Kastovsky|location={{w|Berlin}}|publisher=Mouton de Gruyte|year=2000|pages=263-276}}</ref>

====See also====
{{col4|en|title=other verb endings
|-ed
|-en
|-ing
|-t
}}

====References====
<references/>

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|-es}}, from {{inh|en|ang|-es}}, the masculine and neuter genitive singular ending of  [[Appendix:Old English strong declension|strong nouns]]. More at {{l|en|-'s}}.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix|cat2=adverb-forming suffixes}}

# {{senseid|en|adverbial}} {{non-gloss definition|Used in the formation of certain English adverbs.}}
#: {{sufex|en|forward|forwards}}
#: {{sufex|en|downward|downwards}}
#: {{sufex|en|alway|always}}
#: {{sufex|en|sometime|sometimes}}
#: {{sufex|en|betime|betimes}}
#: {{sufex|en|while|whiles}}
#: {{sufex|en|betide|betides}}
#: {{sufex|en|toward|towards}}
#: {{sufex|en|beside|besides}}
#: {{sufex|en|evening|evenings}}
#: {{sufex|en|unaware|unawares}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|-st}}

===Etymology 4===
A variant spelling of {{m|en|-'s}}, partly an archaism, partly by dropping the apostrophe.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|-'s||on pronouns; now nonstandard}}
* {{l|en|-es}} {{q|obsolete}}

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}}

# {{lb|en|on pronouns}} {{n-g|Possessive marker, indicating that an object belongs to the word bearing the marker.}}
#: {{m|en|hers|her'''s'''}}; {{m|en|its|it'''s'''}}; {{m|en|ones|one'''s'''}} {{q|now {{m|en|one's|one’s}}}}; {{m|en|theirs|their'''s'''}}
#: {{q|[[neopronoun]]s}}: {{m|en|eirs|eir'''s'''}}; {{m|en|hirs|hir'''s'''}}; {{m|en|pers|per'''s'''}}; {{m|en|thons|thon'''s'''}}; {{m|en|xyrs|xyr'''s'''}}; {{m|en|zirs|zir'''s'''}}
# {{lb|en|on nouns|now|nonstandard}} {{alternative form of|en|-'s}}
#: {{m|en|devils doorbell|devil'''s''' doorbell}}; {{m|en|dogs-bane|dog'''s'''-bane}}; {{m|en|St. Marys|St. Mary'''s'''}}; {{q|obsolete}} {{m|en|kings|king'''s'''}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In most cases where {{m|en|-s}} is found nowadays as a possessive case marker, it is a simple misspelling of {{m|en|-'s}}. However, possessive determiners derived from personal pronouns use ''-s'' (e.g., {{m|en|its}}, not {{m|en|it's}}). The same is true of pronouns derived from possessive determiners (e.g., {{m|en|theirs}}, not {{m|en|their's}}). The possessive form of {{m|en|who}} takes ''-se'' ({{m|en|whose}}, not {{m|en|who's}}).
* Bare ''-s'' is used in some business names that derive from possessive family names, e.g., {{w|Barclays}} and {{w|Harrods}}, but compare [[w:Sainsbury's|Sainsbury’s]]; compare [[w:Apostrophe#Possessives_in_business_names|Wikipedia's article on possessives in business names]]. In speech, /z/ (or /s/) is sometimes added to business names which have neither ''-s'' nor ''-'s'' in writing, resulting in {{w|s-form}}s, which see.
* Sometimes used in place names; e.g., ''[[w:Harpers Ferry, West Virginia|Harpers Ferry]]'' (formerly spelled “Harper’s Ferry”), {{m|en|Queens County}} (note that the former name of {{w|County Laois}} was officially “Queen’s County”, however, the apostrophe-less spelling is well-attested).

===Etymology 5===
Modern sense in slang {{datedef|circa 1936}}. According to {{w|Oxford English Dictionary|OED}}, a colloquial [[clipping]] of the [[hypocoristic]] diminutive suffix {{m|en|-sy}}. As {{w|American Heritage Dictionary|AHD}} writes, ''-sy'' itself usually being informal, ironic and/or jocular, and possibly a combination of {{m|en|-s||plural marker}} and conflation of {{m|en|-y}} as adjectival with its sense as a [[diminutive]] suffix (e.g., {{m|en|puppy}}, {{m|en|kitty}}), the latter notion probably from {{der|en|sco|-}}.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}}

# {{senseid|en|hypocoristic}} {{lb|en|informal|colloquial|sometimes|_|humorous}} {{non-gloss definition|Hypocoristic suffix}}
#: {{m|en|Babs|Bab'''s'''}}; {{m|en|moms|mom'''s'''}}; {{m|en|pops|pop'''s'''}}; {{m|en|homes|home'''s'''}}; {{m|en|toots|toot'''s'''}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|en|id=hypocoristic}}
Unsorted:
{{suffixsee|en}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|-sies}}

