==Dutch==

===Etymology 1===
{{unk|nl}}. Not present in {{noncog|odt|-}}, which used {{m|odt|-a}} from {{cog|gem-pro|*-ōz}} as the plural ending. Possibly spread from {{der|nl|gml|-s}}, {{m|gml|-es}}, from {{der|nl|osx|-os}}, {{m|osx|-as}}, from {{der|nl|gem-pro|*-ōs}}. Further etymology is unknown, but cognate with {{cog|ang|-as}} ({{cog|en|-s}}).

====Suffix====
{{head|nl|suffix|cat2=inflectional suffixes|g=p}}

# {{n-g|Used to form regular plurals of nouns that end in certain suffixes or syllables, such as ''-el'', ''-er'', ''-en'', ''-em'', ''-eur'', ''-aar'', ''-aard'', diminutive {{m|nl|-je}}, etc.}}
#: {{sufex|nl|bodem|bodems}}
# {{n-g|Used to form irregular plurals of many other nouns, chiefly of foreign origin.}}
#: {{sufex|nl|telefoon|telefoons}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Nouns ending in unstressed ''-e'' generally have a plural in ''-s'' and one in {{m|de|-n}} ({{m|nl|ziekte}} > {{m|nl|ziektes}}, {{m|nl|ziekten}}). Individual words, however, allow just one of the two ways.
* Most words of Latin origin ending in {{m|nl|-um}} are pluralized with the suffix ''-s'' ({{m|nl|museum}} > {{m|nl|museums}}) or by replacing ''-um'' with {{m|nl|-a}} (> {{m|nl|musea}}). The latter tends to be preferred in formal style.

===Etymology 2===
From the genitive case of masculine and neuter nouns and adjectives, {{inh|nl|dum|-s}}, {{m|dum|-es}}, from {{inh|nl|odt|-es}}, {{m|odt|-is}}, from {{inh|nl|gem-pro|*-as}}, {{m|gem-pro|*-is}}.

====Suffix====
{{head|nl|suffix|cat2=inflectional suffixes|cat3=adverb-forming suffixes}}

# {{lb|nl|archaic|except in fixed expressions}} {{n-g|Used to form the genitive case of (strong) masculine and neuter nouns.}}
#: {{l|nl|tijd}} → de tand des tijd'''s'''
# {{n-g|Used to form the genitive case of proper nouns and some pronouns.}}
#: {{l|nl|Pieter}} → Pieter'''s''' jas
#: {{l|nl|iemand}} → iemand'''s''' jas
# {{n-g|Used to form the partitive form of the adjective}}
#: {{l|nl|lief}} → iets lief'''s'''
# {{n-g|Used to form adverbs}}
#: stad → {{l|nl|steeds}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|nl}}

The adverbial/adjectival -s combines with other suffixes like:
* {{l|nl|-lijk}} &rarr; {{l|nl|-lijks}}
* {{l|nl|-je}} &rarr; {{l|nl|-jes}}
* {{l|nl|-ling}} &rarr; {{l|nl|-lings}}
* {{l|nl|-waarts}}

===Etymology 3===
From earlier {{m|nl|-sch}}, from {{inh|nl|dum|-sch}}, from {{inh|nl|odt|-isc}}, from {{inh|nl|gmw-pro|*-isk}}, from {{inh|nl|gem-pro|*-iskaz}} (from which also {{m|nl|-isch}} via German), from {{der|nl|ine-pro|*-iskos}}.

====Suffix====
{{head|nl|suffix|cat2=adjective-forming suffixes}}

# {{senseid|nl|adjective}} {{non-gloss definition|Used to form adjectives of characteristic from nouns.}}
#: {{sufex|nl|winter|t1=winter|winters|t2=wintery}}
#: {{sufex|nl|spel|t1=game|speels|t2=playful}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Used to form adjectives or language names from names of nations or countries.}}
#: {{sufex|nl|Engeland|t1=England|Engels|t2=English}}
#: {{sufex|nl|Finland|t1=Finland|Fins|t2=Finnish}}

===Etymology 4===
From earlier forms such as {{m|nl|-sz}}, {{m|nl|-se}}, {{m|nl|-sen}}; all ultimately from the patronymic use of {{m|nl|zoon}} with a connecting genitival interfix {{m|nl|-s-}}. Bare ''-s'' as a patronymic may also represent an underlying patronymic use of {{m|nl|dochter}}, as in the name ''Neeltje Jansdochter'', which could reduce to ''Neeltje Jans'' by simple elision of ''-dochter''. {{etystub|nl}}

====Suffix====
{{head|nl|suffix|cat2=noun-forming suffixes}}

# {{senseid|nl|patronymic}} {{n-g|Used to form patronymic surnames.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alter|nl|-sz|-se|-sse|-sen|-ssen}} 

=====Derived terms=====
''More at the pages of the various alternative forms listed above, such as {{m|nl|-sen}}.''
{{suffixsee|nl|id=patronymic}}

