===Etymology 5===
From apocope of the final vowel of {{m|fi|-ssa}}, {{m|fi|-ssä}}.

====Suffix====
{{fi-suffix|inflectional}}

# {{lb|fi|case suffix|colloquial|or|dialectal}} {{alternative form of|fi|-ssa|pos=inessive}}

[[Category:Finnish lemmas|s]]

==French==

===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|fr|frm|-s}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|-s}}, from {{inh|fr|la|[[-os]], [[-es]]|pos=accusative plural}}.

====Pronunciation====
* Silent, except in liaison environments, when it may be pronounced {{IPAchar|/z‿/}}. This liaison is usual in adjectives, but fairly rare in nouns.
* Irregularly pronounced {{IPAchar|/s/}} in {{m|fr|tous|t=all}} when used as a pronoun.
* {{audio|fr|LL-Q150 (fra)-Lyokoï--s.wav|a=liaison}}

====Suffix====
{{fr-suffix}}

# {{n-g|Used to form the regular plurals of most nouns and adjectives.}}
#: {{sufex|fr|homme|hommes}}
#: {{sufex|fr|bon|bons}}
# {{n-g|Used to form the irregular plurals of a few nouns and adjectives in -au, -eu (which regularly add {{m|fr|-x}}) and in -al (which regularly make -aux).}}
#: {{sufex|fr|landau|landaus}}
#: {{sufex|fr|bleu|bleus}}
#: {{sufex|fr|carnaval|carnavals}}

===Etymology 2===
{{inh+|fr|frm|-s}}, from {{inh|fr|fro|-s}}, from {{inh|fr|la|[[-es]], [[-is]]|pos=second-person singular}}.

The spread of this suffix to the first-person singular started out from Old French verbs whose stems happened to end in a sibilant, most notably the regular conjugation in {{m|fro|-ir}}, e.g., {{m|fro||je [[fenir|fenis]], tu fenis}} (from {{m+|VL.||fin-'''is'''c-o, fin-'''is'''c-is}}). In later Old French, final {{IPAchar|/s/}} was only pronounced in [[liaison]] and in [[pausa]]. This weakened the distinction in those verbs which had originally had ''-s'' in the second-person only, but by analogy now began to take it in the first-person as well. The grammarians later on standardised the usage, prescribing ''-s'' in some cases and rejecting it in others.

====Pronunciation====
* Silent, except in liaison environments, when it may be pronounced {{IPAchar|/z‿/}}. This liaison is optional after full vowels (as in {{m|fr||tu [[devoir|dois]]}}). After silent ''-e-'' (as in {{m|fr||tu [[aimer|aimes]]}}) it is rare and often discouraged.

====Suffix====
{{fr-suffix}}

# {{n-g|Used to form the second-person singular of most verb conjugations.}}
# {{n-g|Used to form the first-person singular of some verb conjugations.}}

===See also===
* {{l|fr|-x}}

==German==

===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|de|gmh|-es}}, from {{inh|de|goh}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|de|-es}}
* {{alt|de|-ens||proper nouns ending with a sibilant consonant; dated}}
* {{alt|de|-'||proper nouns ending with a sibilant consonant}}
* {{alt|de|-'s||common nouns; now proscribed}}
* {{alt|de|-'s||proper nouns; correct in certain cases, but often seen as a misspelling}}

====Suffix====
{{head|de|inflectional suffixes}}

# {{n-g|used to form the genitive singular of most masculine nouns, neuter nouns, and proper nouns of all genders}}

=====Usage notes=====
The formation of the strong genitive singular (in ''-s, -es'', or no ending) may be of some difficulty both for learners and native speakers.

Only one form is possible in some nouns:
* Nouns in unstressed ''-as, -es,- is, -os, -us'' remain unchanged (except those in {{m|de|-nis}}, which make ''-nisses'').
* Other nouns in ''-s, -ß, -x, -z'' take ''-es''.
* Nouns ending in a vowel or in unstressed ''-el, -em, -en, -er, -or, -um'' take ''-s''. (Only those in a diphthong or in ''-h'' allow ''-es'', alternatively.)
* Nouns forming their plural in ''-s'' take the same ending also in the genitive singular. (Apparent exceptions will generally have an alternative plural in ''-e''.)

Otherwise, both forms are usually correct, but certain tendencies can be observed:
* The ''es''-form is strongly preferred in a number of frequently used monosyllables, to the degree that the ''s''-form may even sound odd. No hard rule can be given to identify these nouns; they include e.g. {{m|de|Land}}, {{m|de|Mann}}, {{m|de|Weg}}, etc.
* The ''es''-form is also preferred, for euphonic reasons, in words ending in certain clusters like ''-pf, -sch, -st''.
* Most other monosyllables have no clear preference.
* The ''s''-form is usually preferred in polysyllables, regardless of their being simple or compound and regardless also of stress patterns.

Note, finally, that there is a fairly strong tendency for proper nouns (used with the article) and for newer or less common loanwords to remain unchanged in the genitive singular.

===Etymology 2===
Probably derived from the genitive ''-s'' (etymology 1), but developed into a noun-forming suffix in {{der|de|nds-de|-}} and [[Central German]] dialects.

====Suffix====
{{head|de|suffix|g=m}}

# {{n-g|used to form nouns from verb stems}}
#: {{sufex|de|klacken|Klacks|alt2=Klack'''s'''}}
#: {{sufex|de|schnappen|Schnaps|alt2=Schnap'''s'''}}

===Etymology 3===
{{bor+|de|gml}}. Reinforced by the fact that {{der|de|fr|-}} and {{der|de|en|-}} also use ''-s'' as a plural suffix.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|de|-'s||now proscribed}}
* {{alt|de|-ens||family names ending with a sibilant consonant}}

====Suffix====
{{head|de|inflectional suffixes}}

# {{n-g|Used to form the plurals of some nouns.}}
# {{n-g|Used to form the plurals of personal names, particularly family names.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The plural ending ''-s'' is most typical of loanwords (as in {{m|de|Schals}}, {{m|de|Parfüms}}, {{m|de|Videos}}), though there is a tendency for naturalised loanwords to switch to {{m|de|-e}} or {{m|de|-en}} (compare {{m|de|Generäle}}, {{m|de|Lifte}}, {{m|de|Pizzen}} with older {{m|de|Generals}}, {{m|de|Lifts}}, {{m|de|Pizzas}}). Conversely, ''-s'' is also used in a certain number of native words (as in {{m|de|Fräuleins}}, {{m|de|Mädels}}, {{m|de|Uhus}}). Moreover, it is the most productive plural marker in contemporary German, typically used to pluralise initialisms ({{m|de|LKWs}}), neologisms ({{m|de|Honks}}), and words that do not otherwise have a common plural form ({{m|de|Streits}}).

===See also===
* {{l|de|-s-}}

