==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{inh+|la|ine-pro|*-s}}. Cognate with {{cog|grc|-ς}}.

===Suffix===
{{head|la|suffix}}

# {{ng|suffix marking the nominative singular form of non-neuter nouns in declensions other than the [[Appendix:Latin first declension|first]].}}

====Usage notes====
[[Appendix:Latin second declension|Second declension]] non-neuter nominative singular forms originally ended in this suffix preceded by the Indo-European thematic vowel *-o-. Proto-Italic {{m|itc-pro|*-os}} became Classical Latin {{m|la|-us}} by vowel reduction; this ending is often interpreted as a nominative suffix of its own (an example of [[Appendix:Glossary#rebracketing|rebracketing]]) rather than a sequence of stem-final vowel + {{m|la||-s}}.

Likewise, in the [[Appendix:Latin fourth declension|fourth declension]] and [[Appendix:Latin fifth declension|fifth declension]], ''-s'' is always preceded by the characteristic vowels of these declensions (''-u-'' and ''-ē-'' respectively), with the result that {{m|la|-us}} and {{m|la||-ēs}} are frequently regarded as nominative singular suffixes for these declensions.

The suffix appears most clearly in the [[Appendix:Latin third declension|third declension]], as in {{m|la|urbs}}. Stem-final consonants are often modified or deleted before the suffix: for example, {{suffixusex|la||t1=law|-s|lēx|t3=law|alt1=lēg-|pos3=nominative singular}} and  {{suffixusex|la||t1=tooth|-s|dēns|t3=tooth|alt1=dent-|pos3=nominative singular}}. Etymologically, the Latin third declension includes both original consonant-stem nouns and ''i''-stem nouns: however, these two classes are not consistently distinguished in the nominative singular due to frequent syncope of nominative forms that originally ended in ''*-is'' (such as {{m|ine-pro|*ǵénh₁tis}} > {{m|la|gēns}}), as well as some analogical interchange of endings between these two classes (as in {{m|ine-pro|*ḱwṓ}} and {{m|ine-pro|*h₂yéwHō}} being replaced with {{m|la|canis}} and {{m|la|iuvenis}} respectively). Non-neuter ''n''-stem nouns, such as  {{m|la|homo|homō̆, hominis}} or {{m|la|carō|carō̆, carnis}}, normally replace ''-n-'' with ''-ō̆'' in the nominative singular rather than showing the ending ''-s''; a smaller set of non-neuter ''n''-stem nouns end in ''-n'' in the nominative singular, such as {{m|la|tībīcen}}. ''R''-stem and ''l''-stem nouns (such as {{m|la|āctor}}, {{m|la|cōnsul}}) normally end in ''-r'' or ''-l'' respectively (which developed by regular sound change from word-final ''*-rs'' and ''*-ls'').

The use of the suffix ''-s'' is generally restricted to non-neuter nominative forms, but it is found in the neuter nominative and accusative singular of [[:Category:Latin third declension adjectives of one termination|third declension adjectives "of one termination"]]. From an etymological perspective, third-declension neuter nouns such as {{m|la|genus|genus, generis}} that end in ''-s'' in the nominative singular alternating with ''-r-'' in oblique forms do not end in this suffix: rather, they are derived from stems that originally ended in the consonant *s (which was changed to ''-r-'' by rhotacism when followed by a suffix starting with a vowel).

====Derived terms====
{{suffixsee|la}}

