==Portuguese==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|pt|/ʃ/|a=Portugal,Northeast Brazil,Rio de Janeiro}}
* {{IPA|pt|/s/|a=Central-South Brazil}}

===Etymology 1===

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|pt|-es||after certain consonants and nasal vowels}}

====Suffix====
{{head|pt|suffix form}}

# {{n-g|used to form the regular plural of nouns and adjectives, especially those that end in vowels}}
#* {{RQ:mul:Rowling Harry Potter|pt-BR|7|page=308|text=Você notou os cabelo'''s''' dela, são negro'''s''' e brilhante'''s''' e macio'''s'''...|t=Did you notice her hair, it's dark and bright and soft...}}

=====Usage notes=====
''-s'' vs. {{m|pt|-es}} vs. non-use:
* After ''l'' ({{IPAchar|[ɫ]}} {{IPAchar|[l]}}, {{IPAchar|[w]}}), ''-s'' is used and the ''l'' is replaced with ''i'' ({{IPAchar|[j]}}): {{m|pt|pastel}} → {{m|pt|pastéis}}, {{m|pt|capital}} → {{m|pt|capitais}},
** A scattering of exceptions have or allow ''-es'' after ''l'', the most common being {{m|pt|mal}} → {{m|pt|males}}
** After stressed ''il'', ''-s'' is used and the ''l'' is dropped: {{m|pt|hostil}} → {{m|pt|hostis}}, {{m|pt|canil}} → {{m|pt|canis}}
** After unstressed ''il'', the ending becomes ''-eis'': {{m|pt|réptil}} → {{m|pt|répteis}}, {{m|pt|projétil}} → {{m|pt|projéteis}}
** In sporadic and strongly proscribed Brazilian usage, the same rules are applied to words that end in a vowel + ''u'' or ''o'' ({{IPAchar|[w]}}): {{m|pt|degrau}} → * {{m|pt|degrais}}, {{m|pt|troféu}} → * {{m|pt|troféis}}
* After ''s'':
** ''-es'' if the word is stressed in the last syllable: {{m|pt|convés}} → {{m|pt|conveses}}, {{m|pt|anis}} → {{m|pt|anises}}
** not used otherwise: {{m|pt|status}} → {{m|pt|status}}, {{m|pt|íris}} → {{m|pt|íris}}
* after ''m'', ''-s'' is used and the ''m'' becomes ''n''; this is an orthographic process, since in both cases the final consonant marks vowel nasalisation: {{m|pt|dom}} → {{m|pt|dons}}
* after ''n'', either, but ''-es'' is more common in dialects where it is produced as {{IPAchar|/n/}} rather than vowel nasalisation: {{m|pt|hífen}} → {{m|pt|hifens}} or {{m|pt|hífenes}}
* after ''r'' and ''z'', ''-es'' is used
* after vowels and semivowels, ''-s'' is used
** the plural of words ending in ''-ão'' can be ''-ões'' (by far the most common), ''-ãos'' (usually masculines with a feminine in ''-ã'') or ''-ães'' (only a handful of words)
* loanwords usually follow the same rules: {{m|pt|mouse}} → {{m|pt|mouses}}, {{m|pt|ópera}} → {{m|pt|óperas}}
** in recent English loanwords ending in ''r'', and occasionally in loans from other languages, ''-s'' is used: {{m|pt|player}} → {{m|pt|players}}
** unadapted loanwords ending in unusual consonants usually take ''-s'' or no morpheme: {{m|pt|ankh}} → {{m|pt|ankhs}}, {{m|pt|flood}} → {{m|pt|floods}}, {{m|pt|spam}} → {{m|pt|spam}} or {{m|pt|spams}}
** rarely, and often alongside a regular form, the plural from the original language is used: {{m|pt|campus}} → {{m|pt|campi}} (also {{m|pt|campus}}), {{m|pt|mafioso}} → {{m|pt|mafiosi}} (more commonly {{m|pt|mafiosos}}), {{m|pt|gol}} → {{m|pt|gols}} (very rarely {{m|pt|goles}} or {{m|pt|gois}})
** after ''x'', Hellenisms and Latinisms are usually unchanged (following the rule for words ending in ''s''), but sometimes have ''-es'' and the ''x'' becomes ''c'' ({{IPAchar|/s/}}), especially in Brazilian Portuguese: {{m|pt|tórax}} → {{m|pt|tórax}}, {{m|pt|clímax}} → {{m|pt|clímax}} or {{m|pt|clímaces}}
* more often than not, surnames do not take an extra morpheme in the plural; when they do, they are usually Portuguese surnames with recognisable Portuguese morphemes: {{m|pt|o [[Ferreira]]}} → {{m|pt|os [[Ferreira]]}} or {{m|pt|os [[Ferreiras]]}}
* names of peoples that do not contain Portuguese endings usually do not take the plural morpheme: {{m|pt|polaco}} → {{m|pt|polacos}}; {{m|pt|ashanti}} → {{m|pt|ashanti}} or {{m|pt|ashantis}} (less common)

In informal varieties of Brazilian Portuguese, a noun phrase may take a single plural marker, usually in the article. For example, “the big houses” may be {{m|pt|[[as]] [[casa]] [[grande]]}} where standard Portuguese requires {{m|pt|[[as]] [[casas]] [[grandes]]}}. This usage is very widespread, however, it is proscribed and generally regarded as unacceptable in formal contexts and in serious writing.

In some words that end in O and have a stressed {{IPAchar|/o/}} in the penult, the stressed vowel becomes {{IPAchar|/ɔ/}} in the plural. See [[:Category:Portuguese nouns with metaphonic plurals]].

Colloquial Brazilian Portuguese allows {{IPAchar|/j/}} to be added before {{IPAchar|/s/}} in words stressed in their final syllables. This is blocked by morpheme boundaries, such that {{m|pt|nós|t=we}} may be pronounced {{IPAchar|/nɔjs/}}, but {{m|pt|nós|t=knots}} can only be pronounced as {{IPAchar|/nɔs/}}. {{m|pt|vocês}} and colloquial forms {{m|pt|cês}} and {{m|pt|ocês}} are an exception to this rule, and can be pronounced {{IPAchar|/voˈsejs/}}. Note that this process is usually avoided in formal speech.

===Etymology 2===

====Suffix====
{{pt-suffix}}

# {{lb|pt|Brazil|slang}} {{n-g|used to form slangier forms of certain words}}
