==Spanish==

===Etymology===
Derived from the Latin [[accusative]] plural endings ({{m|la|-ās}}, {{m|la|-ōs}}, {{m|la|-ēs}}).

===Suffix===
{{head|es|suffix form}}

# {{n-g|Used to form the regular plural of nouns and adjectives which end in vowels.}}
#: {{sufex|es|amigo|t1=friend|amigos|t2=friends}}
#: {{sufex|es|cocina|t1=kitchen|cocinas|t2=kitchens}}

====Related terms====
{{col-auto|es|-es}}

==Swedish==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|sv|/s/}}

===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|sv|gmq-osw|-s}}, from {{inh|sv|non|-s}}, originally the genitive singular ending of a-stem nouns. Cognate with {{cog|da|-s}}. The frequent use of the suffix in written and modern colloquial Swedish is possibly the result of foreign influence, either Latin or Middle Low German.

====Clitic====
{{head|sv|clitic}}

# {{n-g|Used to form [[genitive]] phrases, attached to the last word in a noun phrase.}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|sv|-s-}}

===Etymology 2===
{{inh+|sv|non|-sk}}, a grammaticalisation of {{inh|sv|gem-pro|*sek|pos=reflexive pronoun}}.

====Suffix====
{{head|sv|suffix|cat2=inflectional suffixes}}

# {{lb|sv|productive}} {{n-g|Used to form the [[passive]] voice of verbs.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Bilen '''kör'''|The car '''drives'''.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Bilen '''körs'''|The car '''is (being) driven'''.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Bilen '''körde'''.|The car '''drove'''.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Bilen '''kördes'''.|The car '''was (being) driven'''.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Grisen '''äter'''.|The pig '''eats'''.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Grisen '''äts'''.|The pig '''is (being) eaten'''.}}
#* {{quote-song|sv|author=Peter Grönvall; Maria Rådsten; Nanne Grönvall|artist=Cecilia Vennersten|year=1995|title=Det vackraste|trans-title=The most beautiful thing ["thing" from being nominalized]|text=Det är en rikedom, att få '''älska''' och att '''älskas'''.|t=It is a richness, to get to '''love''' and to '''be loved'''.}}
# {{lb|sv|mostly|unproductive}} {{n-g|Used to form the [[middle]] ([[reflexive]] and [[reciprocal]]) voice of verbs.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Jag '''mötte''' honom.|I '''met''' him.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Vi '''möttes'''.|We '''met up'''.|lit=We '''met each other'''.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Han '''slog''' mig.|He '''hit''' me.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Vi '''slogs'''.|We '''fought'''.|lit=We '''hit each other'''.}}
# {{lb|sv|by extension}} {{n-g|Used with certain [[transitive]] verbs, but without an object, to indicate a habitual or natural action.}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Var försiktig! Hästen '''sparkas'''.|Be careful! The horse '''kicks''' [has a disposition to kick].}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Hunden '''bits'''.|The dog '''bites''' [habitually].}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Han '''knuffas''' mycket.|He '''pushes''' people a lot.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Until the middle of the 20th century, the variant suffix {{m|sv|-es}} was used to mark the present passive of all {{m|sv|-er}} verbs of the second and fourth conjugations. This usage is mostly considered archaic today, but {{m|sv||-es}} is still used for {{m|sv||-er}} verbs whose stems end in ''s''.
* See also {{l|sv|vara||be}}, which forms a passive voice for states or completed actions.
* Verbs with the suffix {{m|sv||-s}} that are not passive (including those in 2 and 3 above) are called [[Appendix:Glossary#deponent|deponent verbs]]. In such cases, the passive reading is often also possible. For example, {{m|sv||vi slogs}} might also mean “we were hit” and {{m|sv||hunden bits}} might mean “the dog is bitten”. However, such readings would be unintuitive unless suggested by the context.

===Etymology 3===
Mostly from {{der|sv|en|-s}}.

