* {{l|ar|ـَانِ}} {{qualifier|for the nominative case}}

{{cln|ar|inflectional suffixes}}

==South Levantine Arabic==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|ajp|ar|ـَيْنِ}}, the oblique case of {{inh|ajp|ar|ـَانِ}}.

====Suffix====
{{ajp-suffix|head=ـين|g=d|tr=-ēn}}

# {{n-g|Dual suffix}}
#: {{syn|ajp|تنين|tr=tnēn}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The dual form is rarely used together with a suffix pronoun. In that case the plural is used together with {{l|ajp|تنين|tr=tnēn}}.
*: {{uxi|ajp|[[بنت]][[ين]]|tr=bint'''ēn'''|t='''two''' daughters}}
*: {{uxi|ajp|[[بنت|بنات]][[ي]] [[تنتين]]|tr=banāti '''tintēn'''|t=my '''two''' daughters}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|ajp|ar|ـِينَ}}, the oblique case of {{inh|ajp|ar|ـُونَ}}.

====Suffix====
{{ajp-suffix|head=ـين|g=m-p|tr=-īn}}

# {{n-g|Masculine plural suffix for nouns referring to people}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Most masculine nouns not referring to people have an irregular plural (“broken plural”).

=====See also=====
* {{n-g|Feminine plural suffix}}: {{l|ajp|ـات|tr=-āt}}
-------- end text --------
