===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|en|adverbial}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*leyg-|id=like}}
From {{inh|en|enm|-ly}}, {{m|enm|-li}}, {{m|enm|-liche}}, from {{inh|en|ang|-līċe}}.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix|cat2=adverb-forming suffixes|id=adverb}}

# {{senseid|en|adverb}} {{ngd|Used to form [[adverb]]s from [[adjective]]s and [[noun]]s.}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|sudden|suddenly}}

=====Usage notes=====
In prescriptive usage, derived adverbs in ''-ly'' are often preferred to those which are identical in form to the base adjective (e.g., {{m|en|badly}} instead of {{m|en|bad}}), despite the fact that the latter have been in continuous use since the earliest stages of the language and represent the norm in languages closely related to English, such as Dutch and German. This is the cause of hypercorrections such as ''I feel badly'' (where {{m|en|feel}} actually represents a [[copular verb]], which traditionally requires an adjectival complement rather than an adverb).

Various sound changes and spelling changes occur for ''-ly'':
* If an adjective ends with a consonant followed by ''y'', it changes into ''i'' before adding the suffix (e.g. {{m|en|ready}} > {{m|en|readily}}, {{m|en|easy}} > {{m|en|easily}}).
* If an adjective ends with ''ll'', one ''l'' drops out to avoid a triple letter (e.g. {{m|en|full}} > {{m|en|fully}}, {{m|en|shrill}} > {{m|en|shrilly}}).
* If an adjective ends with a syllabic {{IPAchar|/l̩/}} (spelled ''-le'' after a consonant), euphony causes the ''-le'' to drop out. Examples include {{m|en|-ably}} and {{m|en|-ibly}}, but also {{m|en|noble}} > {{m|en|nobly}}, {{m|en|ample}} > {{m|en|amply}}, and {{m|en|idle}} > {{m|en|idly}}, among others.
* Adjectives ending in ''-ic'' generally take {{m|en|-ally}} ({{m|en|public}} > {{m|en|publicly}} being an exception).

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|-ally}}

