==Middle English==

===Etymology 1===
{{root|enm|ine-pro|*leyg-|id=like}}
{{dercat|enm|gmw-pro|gem-pro|inh=2}}
From {{inh|enm|ang|-līċ}}. Related to {{m|enm|lich}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|enm|-lich|-liche|-lyche|-lych|-li|-lie|-lye|-like|-lik|-lyke|-lyk|-le|-lei|-lice|-lic|-liȝ|-lece|-luch}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|enm|/-liː/|/-liːt͡ʃ/|/-lit͡ʃ/}}

====Suffix====
{{head|enm|suffix|comparative|-lyere|superlative|-lyest}}

# {{senseid|enm|adjectival}} Used to form [[adjective]]s from [[noun]]s, the adjectives having the sense of "like or characteristic of what is denoted by the noun".
# Appended to adjectives in order to render meaning of the adjective either more intense or more approximate.
#: E.g. {{l|enm|ded||no longer alive}}, {{l|enm|dedly||causing, resembling or subject to death}}; {{l|enm|schort||not long}}, {{l|enm|shortly||momentary; very brief}}

=====Usage notes=====
* ''-ly'' is generally the most common variant of this suffix, though in some words, other variants may be more common, such as ''-lich(e)''.

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|enm|head=-ly (adjectival)}}
{{rel-top5|Derived terms}}
