{{qual|Name of letter}}:
* {{IPA|en|/əʊ/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|en-uk-o.ogg|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/oʊ/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-O.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊ|oʊ|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===

====Letter====
{{en-letter}}

# {{Latn-def|en|letter|15|o}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1959|year_published=1972|author=w:Anthony Burgess|title=Beds in the East|series=The Malayan Trilogy|page=421|passage="Supposing somebody sees you, with all those flowers too? Supposing somebody writes him a letter? Ooooh!" (a pure round open Tamil '''O'''.)}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|Os|O's}}

# Something shaped like the letter O.
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Age of Consent|page=213|passage=She was lying in the lee of a fowlhouse in a crumpled posture, as if cohesion had been detached from her joints, which lobbed her in an untidy heap, like a lot of old bones, tied together with string. Her skull was hitched under her humped shoulders and her fallen jaw made a lipless '''O''' of her mouth, giving it an expression of imbecile astonishment.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A blood type that lacks [[A]] or [[B]] antigens and may only receive transfusions of similar type O blood, but may donate to all (neglecting [[Rh]] factor). Synonym: [[universal donor]].

=====See also=====
* {{list:Latin script letters/en}}

====Number====
{{en-number|upper=O|lower=o}}

# {{Latn-def|en|ordinal|15|o}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|O}}, {{m|enm|o}}, from {{inh|en|ang|o}}, from {{der|en|la|o}} and {{cog|grc|ὦ|pos=interjection}}. Featured prominently in William Tyndale's 1525 translation of the [[New Testament]].

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|o}}
* {{l|en|ô}} {{q|obsolete}}

====Particle====
{{head|en|particles}}

# {{senseid|en|vocative}} {{n-g|The [[vocative]] [[particle]], used for direct address.}}
#: '''''O''' Death! '''O''' Death! Won't you spare me over till another year?'' - part of the refrain from the American folk song "A Conversation with Death".
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Romans|2|1, 3|cc|verso=1|Therfore arte thou inexcuſable o man whoſoever thou be that iudgeſt. For in that ſame where in thou iudgeſt another&nbsp;/ thou cõdemneſt thy ſilfe. For thou that iudgeſt doest evẽ the ſame ſilfe thynges. {{...}} Thynkeſt thou '''O''' man that iudgeſt them which do ſoche thyngꝭ and yet doſt evẽ the very ſame&nbsp;/ that thou ſhalt eſcape the iudgemẽt of God?}}
#* '''c. 1810-1820?''', {{w|Samuel Taylor Coleridge}}, ''Notes on Macbeth''
#*: '''O'''! the affecting beauty of the death of Cawdor, and the presentimental speech of the king: {{...}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The word ''O'' is typically written in upper case in modern usage.
* ''O'' is often used in translations from languages which have the [[vocative case]].
* Although it is not strictly archaic, the particle is sometimes used [[archaizingly]]. It conveys a formal or reverential tone.

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|O Antiphon}}
* {{l|en|O Canada}}

