==Persian==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|fa|ای|tr=ay, ey||dialectal}}
* {{alt|fa|ار|tr=ar|گر|tr2=gar||poetic}}
* {{alt|fa|اگه|tr=age||colloquial}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|fa|pal|𐭧𐭲|ts=agar|sc=Phli}}. Compare {{cog|xpr|𐭀𐭊|tr=ag}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{fa-IPA|`agar|teh=`aga}}
* {{audio|fa|LL-Q9168 (fas)-Darafsh-اگر.wav|a=Iran}}

===Conjunction===
{{fa-regional|اگر|اگر|агар}}
{{head|fa|conjunction|tr=agar}}

# [[if]] {{gloss|used in a hypothetical or conditional statement}}
#: {{syn|fa|چنانچه|tr1=čenânče|q1=formal|اگر چنانچه|tr2=agar čenânče|q2=formal}}
#: {{ux|fa|اگر فارسی بلد بودم این کتاب را نمی‌خریدم.|tr='''agar''' fârsi balad budam in ketâb-râ ne-mi-xaridam.|q=more literary|t='''Had''' I known Persian [which I did not], I would not have bought this book.}}
#: {{ux|fa|اگه فارسی بلد بودم این کتاب و نمی‌خریدم.|tr='''age''' fârsi balad budam in ketâb-o ne-mi-xaridam.|q=more colloquial|'''If''' I knew Persian [which I did not], I wouldn't have bought this book.}}
#: {{ux|fa|اگر می‌رفتی، محمد را می‌دیدی.|tr='''agar''' mi-rafti, mohammad-râ mi-didi.|q=more literary|t='''If''' you had gone [but you did not], you would have seen Mohammad.}}
#: {{ux|fa|اگر رفته بودی، محمد را دیده بودی|tr='''agar''' rafte budi, mohammad-râ dide budi.|q=more literary|t='''If''' you had gone [but you did not], you would have seen Mohammad.}}
#: {{ux|fa|اگر رفته باشی، محمد را می‌دیدی.|tr='''agar''' rafte bâši, mohammad-râ mi-didi.|q=more literary|t='''If''' you had gone [and perhaps you did], you would have seen Mohammad.}}
#: {{ux|fa|اگر بروی، محمد را می‌بینی.|tr='''agar''' be-ravi, mohammad-râ mi-bini.|q=more literary|t='''If''' you go, you'll see Mohammad.}}
#* {{quote-book|fa|title=Poem attributed to {{w|Shahid Balkhi}}|year=10th century|text=اگر غم را چو آتش دود بودی / جهان تاریک ماندی جاودانه|tr='''agar''' ğam-râ čo âtaš dud budi / jahân târik mânadi jâvedâne|t='''If''' grief gave off smoke, as fire does / The world would remain forever in the dark.}}
#* {{quote-book|fa|chapter={{lang|fa|{{w|lang=fa|اگر آن ترک شیرازی به دست آرد دل ما را}}}} ({{w|Shirazi Turk}})|title={{lang|fa|{{w|lang=fa|دیوان حافظ}}}}|trans-title=[[divan|Divān]] of {{w|Hafez|Ḥāfeẓ}}|author={{w|Hafez|Xāja Shams-ud-Dīn Moḥammad Ḥāfeẓ-e Shīrāzī}}|text=اگر آن ترک شیرازی به دست آرد دل ما را / به خال هندویش بخشم سمرقند و بخارا را|tr='''agar''' ân tork-e širâzi be dast ârad del-e mâ-râ / be xâl-e hendu-yaš baxšam samarqand o boxârâ-râ|t='''If''' that Shirazi Turk accepts my heart in their hand / for their Indian mole I will give Samarkand and Bukhara.|year=late 14th century}}
#* {{quote-book|fa|author={{w|Gani Kashmiri|Muḥammad Ṭāhir Ğanī Kašmīrī}}|title=fa:دیوان|trans-title=Divān|text=یار تا قتل چنین باشد اگر همراهم / سفر ملک عدم را ز خدا می‌خواهم|tr=yâr tâ qatl čonin bâšad '''agar''' hamrâh-am / safar-e molk-e 'adam râ ze xodâ mi-xâham.|t='''If''' my love accompanies me to my execution / A journey to the other world, I shall seek from God.|year=c. 1650|tlr=Mufti Mudasir Farooqi; Nusrat Bazaz}}
# [[since]], [[given that]], [[if]]
#: {{ux|fa|اگر جواب را نمی‌دانی، چرا حرف می‌زنی؟|tr='''agar''' javâb-râ ne-mi-dâni, čerâ harf mi-zani?|q=more literary|t='''Since''' you don't know the answer, why are you talking?}}
#: {{ux|fa|اگه جواب و نمی‌دونی، چرا حرف می‌زنی؟|tr='''age''' javâb-o ne-mi-duni, čerâ harf mi-zani?|q=more colloquial|t='''Since''' you don't know the answer, why are you talking?}}

====Usage notes====
* {{m-self|fa|اگر|tr=agar}} is associated with the following tenses in the verb of the if-clause ([[protasis]]):
** When the hypothetical suggests something which cannot have been true in the past or is impossible to be fulfilled in the future, the past imperfect or pluperfect tenses are used for the verbs of '''both''' the if-clause and result clause.
** When the hypothetical suggests something which may be fulfilled in the future, the present subjunctive (or sometimes simple past) tense is used for the verb of the if-clause.
** When the hypothetical suggests something which may possibly have been true in the past, the past subjunctive tense is used for the verb of the if-clause.
** When introducing a hypothetical which actually pertains to a real present state, with {{m-self|fa|اگر|tr=agar}} having the meaning of "since", the present tense is used for the verb of the if-clause.
** When used with an imperative in the result clause, the imperative is perceived to already imply that the hypothetical holds true, hence the present tense is used for the verb of the if-clause.
* {{m-self|fa|اگر|tr=agar}} can be omitted, especially colloquially.

