====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

''For idiomatic meanings of phrasal verbs, such as [[carry on]], [[hang on]], [[have on]], [[try on]], etc., please see the individual entries.''
# To an [[operate|operating]] [[state]].
#: {{ux|en|turn the [[television]] '''on'''}}
# So as to cover or be fitted.
#: ''The lid wasn't screwed '''on''' properly.''
#: ''Put '''on''' your hat and gloves.''
# Along, forwards (continuing an action).
#: {{ux|en|drive '''on''', rock '''on'''}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=May 5, 2012
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=Chelsea 2-1 Liverpool
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/17878435
|page=
|passage=He met Luis Suarez's cross at the far post, only for Chelsea keeper Petr Cech to show brilliant reflexes to deflect his header on to the bar. Carroll turned away to lead Liverpool's insistent protests that the ball had crossed the line but referee Phil Dowd and assistant referee Andrew Garratt waved play '''on''', with even a succession of replays proving inconclusive.}}
# In continuation, at length.
#: {{ux|en|and so '''on'''}}
#: {{ux|en|He rambled [[on and on|'''on''' and '''on''']].}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete in the US}} [[later|Later]].
#: {{ux|en|Ten years '''on''', nothing had changed in the village.}}
# {{n-g|Of betting odds, denoting a better-than-even chance.}} ''See also '''[[odds-on]]'''''.
#: {{ant|en|against}}
#: ''That horse is twenty-to-one '''on''', so you need to stake twenty pounds just to win one pound.''

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|later}} {{l|en|after}}, {{l|en|afterward}}/{{l|en|afterwards}}, {{l|en|later}}, {{l|en|subsequently}}, {{l|en|thence}}

=====Antonyms=====
* {{antsense|active, functioning, operating}} {{l|en|off}}
* {{antsense|to an operating state}} {{l|en|off}}

