{{also|wiþ|wið|with-|wiþ-|wįð}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|with}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wiþ|t=against, opposite, toward, with}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wiþi}}, a shortened form of {{der|en|gem-pro|*wiþrą|t=against}}. In Middle English, the word shifted to denote association rather than opposition, displacing {{cog|enm|mid|t=with}}, from {{cog|ang|mid|t=with}}, from {{cog|gem-pro|*midi}}; an earlier model of this meaning shift exists in cognate {{der|en|non|við}}; elsewhere, the converse meaning shift is exemplified by {{cog|sem-srb|-}} {{m|inm|𐩨𐩺𐩬|t=between, amid}} spawning {{cog|sem-srb|-}} {{m|inm|𐩨𐩬|t=against}} and even likewise frequent reverse meaning {{m|inm|𐩨𐩬|t=from}}. The adverb sense is probably a [[Appendix:Glossary#semantic_loan|semantic loan]] from various other Germanic languages, such as {{semantic loan|en|no|med|notext=1}}, {{semantic loan|en|sv|med|notext=1}}, and {{semantic loan|en|de|mit|notext=1}}.<ref>Kaplan, Aidan (2015) “Come with” in the ''Yale Grammatical Diversity Project: English in North America''[http://ygdp.yale.edu/phenomena/come-with], accessed on 2024-05-08. Updated by Tom McCoy (2015) and Katie Martin (2018).</ref>

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|wyth|whith}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}
* {{sense|abbreviations}} {{l|en|wth|w<sup>th</sup>}}, {{l|en|wth|w<sup>t</sup>}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}; {{l|en|w/}}, {{l|en|w}}
* {{sense|[[w:eye dialect|eye dialect]]}} {{l|en|wi'}}, {{l|en|wit}}, {{l|en|wit'}}, {{l|en|wid}}, {{l|en|wif}}, {{l|en|wiv}}

====Pronunciation====
; preconsonantal, final
