-------- end text --------
Page 5 breeze: Skipped, no changes
Page 5 breeze: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Breeze}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɹiːz/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-breeze.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-breeze.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːz|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From the earlier (nautical) term {{m|en|brise}}, {{m|en|brize|t=breeze}}, from {{inh|en|enm|brees|t=wind}}. Ultimate origin {{unk|en|title=obscure}}.

Variously supposed to derive from a {{der|en|gem|-}} source like {{cog|stq|Briese||breeze}}, {{cog|fy|brys|t=a cool wind}}, {{cog|nl|bries|t=breeze}}, early {{cog|nl|brysen|t=to blow cool and fresh}}, or from {{cog|es|brisa||northeast wind}}.<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref><ref>{{R:Merriam Webster Online}}</ref>

The earliest attestations are in {{cog|enm|brees}} (1460), {{cog|ca|brisa}}, and {{cog|it|brezza}} (all in 15th century), with Spanish (1504) and {{cog|pt|briza}} (16th century) following closely after. The aforementioned Dutch cognates and {{cog|fr|brise}}, however, are attested later than the term in English. The only internal hypothesis for any of those languages is a modification of {{noncog|pro|bisa|t=strong wind}}, which is not widely accepted.

Compare also {{noncog|sq|breshër||hail}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|en|brize|qq=obsolete}}
* {{alt|en|briess|qq=obsolete}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[light]], [[gentle]] [[wind]].
#: {{ux|en|The '''breeze''' rustled the papers on her desk.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1793|author=w:William Wordsworth|title=An Evening Walk
|passage=Into a gradual calm the '''breezes''' sink.}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|chapter=Afterglow|page=168|passage='''Breezes''' blowing from beds of iris quickened her breath with their perfume; she saw the tufted lilacs sway in the wind, and the streamers of mauve-tinted wistaria swinging, all a-glisten with golden bees; she saw a crimson cardinal winging through the foliage, and amorous tanagers flashing like scarlet flames athwart the pines.}}
# {{senseid|en|easy thing}}{{lb|en|figurative}} Any activity that is [[easy]], not [[testing]] or [[difficult]].
#: {{ux|en|After studying Latin, Spanish was a '''breeze'''.}}
# {{lb|en|cricket}} Wind blowing across a [[cricket]] [[match]], [[whatever]] its [[strength]].
# An excited or ruffled state of feeling; a flurry of excitement; a disturbance; a quarrel.
#: {{ux|en|The discovery produced a '''breeze'''.}}
# A brief [[workout]] for a [[racehorse]].

=====Synonyms=====
* ''see also'' [[Thesaurus:wind]]
* {{l|en|cakewalk}}, {{l|en|cinch}}, {{l|en|doddle}}, {{l|en|piece of cake}}, {{l|en|walk in the park}}, {{l|en|walkover}}; see also [[Thesaurus:easy thing]]

