==Middle English==

===Etymology 1===
From a conflation of {{inh|enm|ang|bēon}} and {{m|ang|wesan}}, from {{inh|enm|gmw-pro|*beun}} and {{m|gmw-pro|*wesan}}, from {{inh|enm|gem-pro|*beuną}} and {{m|gem-pro|*wesaną}}, from {{inh|enm|ine-pro|*bʰewHeti}} and a conflation of {{m|ine-pro|*h₂wéseti}} and {{m|ine-pro|*h₁ésti}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|enm|be|bean|ben|beon|boen}}
* {{q|from {{m|ine-pro||*h₁ésti}}|rare}} {{alter|enm|seen|sen}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|enm|/bøːn/|a=Early ME}}
* {{IPA|enm|/beːn/}}

====Verb====
{{enm-verb|is|beynge|was|been}}

# {{lb|enm|intransitive}} To [[be]]; to [[exist]] or have [[existence]]:
## {{lb|enm|intransitive}} To [[occur]]; to [[come]] to [[be]].
## {{lb|enm|with adjective or adverb}} To [[be]] with a certain [[quality]] or in a certain [[situation]].
## {{lb|enm|transitive}} To be [[to]] or [[for]] [[someone]] or [[something]].
# {{lb|enm|copulative}} To [[be]] [[something]] or [[someone]].
#* {{RQ:Wycliffe NT Lichfield|Revelation|3|15|118|v|column=1|I woot þi werkis · foꝛ nei[þer] þou art coold nei[þer] þou art hoot / I wolde þat þou '''were''' coold ei[þer] hoot|I know your actions: you aren't cold, but neither are you hot. I'd like it if you '''were''' either cold or hot.}}
#* {{quote-book|enm|year=1407|title=w:The Testimony of William Thorpe|pages=40–41|passage={{...}}Filip of Repintoun whilis he '''was''' a chanoun of Leycetre, Nycol Herforde, dane Geffrey of Pikeringe, monke of Biland and a maistir dyuynyte, and Ioon Purueye, and manye other whiche '''weren''' holden rightwise men and prudent{{...}}}}
## {{lb|enm|with genitive}} To [[be]] someone's; to be in someone's possession.
## {{lb|enm|auxiliary}} {{n-g|Forms the continuous present and perfect.}}
##* {{RQ:Judas|lines=16-17|Sone ſo iudaſ of ſlepe waſ awake. / þritti platen of ſelu[er] from hym '''weren''' itake.|As soon as Judas had awakened from his rest, / Thirty pieces of silver '''had been''' taken from him.}}
## {{lb|enm|auxiliary}} {{n-g|Forms the perfect tense with some intransitive verbs.}}
# {{lb|enm|auxiliary}} {{n-g|Forms various passive constructions.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* ''ben'' may be used elliptically if the subject, complement, or predicate is implicit.
* The perfect tense is usually formed with {{m|enm|haven}}; see that entry for more.

