==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɛn/}}
* {{IPA|en|/bɪn/|a=pin-pen}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-ben.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-Ben.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{homophones|en|Ben|;|been<aa:some accents>|;|bin<aa:pin-pen>}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛn|ɪn|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰeh₂-|id=speak}}
From {{der|en|enm|ben}}, {{m|enm|bene}}, from {{der|en|ang|bēn||prayer, request, favor, compulsory service}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*bōniz||supplication}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰeh₂-||to say}}. Related to {{l|en|ban}}. More at {{l|en|boon}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|bene}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[prayer]]; a [[petition]].

===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|enm|ben}}, {{m|enm|bene}}, variation of {{m|enm|bin}}, {{m|enm|binne||within}}, from {{der|en|ang|binnan||within, in, inside of, into}}, equivalent to {{prefix|en|be|in}}.

====Preposition====
{{en-prep}}

# {{lb|en|Scotland|northern England}} [[in|In]], [[into]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=w:James Kelman|title=Kieron Smith, Boy|page=32|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2009
|passage=And he was waving to me to creep in, so I just did and then just to skip '''ben''' the front and then in the lobby.}}

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|Scotland|Northern England}} [[inside|Inside]].

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|benner|sup=benmost}}

# Inner, [[interior]].

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|ben-end}}, {{l|en|ben-room}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Scotland|Northern England}} [[ben-room|Ben-room]]: The inner [[room]] of a two-room [[hut]] or [[shack]] (as opposed to the [[but]]).

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|but and ben}}

=====References=====
* [http://www.dsl.ac.uk/entry/snd/ben_adv_prep_adj_n1 The Dictionary of the Scots Language]

===Etymology 3===
{{root|en|ar|ب و ن}}
From {{inh|en|enm|been}}, from {{der|en|fro|-}} and {{der|en|ML.|-}}, probably from a North African pronunciation of {{der|en|ar|بَان||ben tree}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A tree, {{taxfmt|Moringa oleifera|species}} or [[horseradish tree]] of Arabia and India, which produces [[oil of ben]].
# The winged [[seed]] of the ben tree.
# The [[oil]] of the ben seed.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|tree}} {{l|en|drumstick tree}}, {{l|en|horseradish tree}}, {{l|en|moringa}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|ben-nut}}
* {{l|en|ben oil}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|ben tree}}
* Arabic: {{t|ar|بَان|m}}
* Russian: {{t|ru|мори́нга|f}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{trans-top|seed of the ben tree}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{trans-top|oil of ben}}
* Russian: {{t|ru|мориндо́вое ма́сло|n}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 4===
From {{der|en|ar|بِن}} and {{der|en|he|בן|tr=ben||son}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|Ben}}
* {{l|en|bin}} {{qualifier|Arabic}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|usually|capitalised}} [[son|Son]] of (used with Hebrew and Arabic surnames).

=====Derived terms=====
{{der2|en|Nickie-ben}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|son of}}
* Arabic: {{t+|ar|بِن|m}}, {{t+|ar|اِبْن|m}}
* Hebrew: {{t+|he|בן|tr=ben|m}}
* Ladino: {{t|lad|ben}}
* Persian: {{t+|fa|بن|sc=fa-Arab}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 5===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|sco|[[ben]], [[benn]]}}, from {{der|en|gd|beinn}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[Scottish]] or [[Irish]] mountain or high peak.

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|Ben Lomond}}
* {{l|en|Ben More}}
* {{l|en|Ben Nevis}}
* {{l|en|Ben Venue}}

===Etymology 6===
UK C16. Probably from {{der|en|la|bene}} or {{der|en|it|bene}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|benar|sup=benat}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|UK|thieves}} {{alternative spelling of|en|bene}}; [[good]].
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1611
|author=w:Thomas Middleton
|title={{w|The Roaring Girl|The Roaring Girle}}
|url=https://archive.org/details/cu31924013133586
|passage=A gage of '''ben''' Rom-bouse, / In a bousing-ken of Rom-vile, / Is '''benar''' than a Caster, / Pecke, pennam, lay, or popler, / Which we mill in deuse a vile.
|footer=[paraphrase] A pot of good wine, / In a pub of London, / Is better than a cloak, / Meat, bread, milk, or porridge, / Which we steal in the countryside.
}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{der2|en|ben-cull|ben mort|fidlam ben}}

===Etymology 7===
Shortening.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK|theatre|slang|obsolete}} A [[benefit]] {{gloss|performance to raise funds}}.
#* '''year?''', ''The Catholic Literary Circular'' (page 75)
#*: In the Chronicles of the Stage, some curious particulars are given relating to Sir Henry Herbert and the well-known Sir William Davidson, by which we learn, amongst other things, that a “'''ben'''” or benefit at Drury Lane, two centuries ago, was worth a hundred pounds.

