==English==
{{slim-wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|o'er}} {{q|adverb, preposition}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈəʊ.və(ɹ)/|a=UK}}
* {{enPR|ō'vər|a=US}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈoʊ.vɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-over.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-over.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊvə(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|o|ver}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|over}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ofer}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*obar}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*uber|t=over}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*upér}}, a comparative form of {{m|ine-pro|*upo}}.

Akin to {{cog|nl|over}}, {{cog|de|ober}}, {{m|de|über}}, {{cog|da|over}}, {{cog|no|over}}, {{cog|sv|över}}, {{cog|is|yfir}}, {{cog|fo|yvir}}, {{cog|got|𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂}}, {{cog|la|super}}, {{cog|grc|ὑπέρ}}, {{cog|sq|upri||group of peasants}}, {{cog|sa|उपरि|tr=upári|sc=Deva}}.
{{doublet|en|uber|super|hyper}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|-}}

# [[discontinued|Discontinued]]; [[ended]] or [[concluded]].
#: ''The show is '''over'''.''
# {{lb|en|informal|of an ongoing situation}} [[hopeless|Hopeless]]; [[irrecoverable]].
#: ''It's too early to tell, but I think it's '''over'''.''
# {{lb|en|professional wrestling|_|slang}} wrestler or faction that is popular with the audience.

=====Usage notes=====
Not normally used attributively (before a noun). (Attributive use occurs rarely in informal language, e.g. "an over relationship".)

"Over" as a  first element in British [[place names]] can mean "[[upper]]" or "[[higher]]", apparently from {{cog|ang|ufera}} or {{m|ang|uferra}}.

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|game over}}
* {{l|en|overness}}

