===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|over||riverbank, seashore, brink}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ōfer||riverbank, seashore, brink, edge, margin, border}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*ōferaz}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|oever||riverbank, shore}}, {{cog|de|Ufer||shore, shoreline, riverbank}}, {{cog|nds|Över||shore, riverbank}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|rare|dialectal|or|obsolete}} A [[shore]], [[riverbank]].
#: {{ux|en|The sea's '''over'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1338|author=Robert Mannyng|title=w:Mannyng's Chronicle
|passage=Cassibola was ready at Dover, & renged (encamped) his men by the '''over'''.}}

=====Usage notes=====
Now mostly found in place names, as in ''Westover'' or ''Overton'', Hampshire (a town built on the River Test). Fell out of use in the 16th century.

=====References=====
* [https://books.google.ru/books?id=eLYiXNHnQWsC&pg=PA255&dq=ofer+dictionary&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiq38O8_brNAhWma5oKHX7RArsQ6AEIPzAC#v=onepage&q=ofer&f=false A Concise Anglo-Saxon Dictionary]
* [http://quod.lib.umich.edu/cgi/m/mec/med-idx?type=byte&byte=134562415&egdisplay=open&egs=134567734 The Middle English Dictionary]

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|en|a=eorv|-vore|Vore|rove|vore}}

