==Middle Low German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|gml|osx|ovar}}, from {{inh|gml|gmw-pro|*obar}}, from {{inh|gml|gem-pro|*uber}}, and Old Saxon ''*uvir'' from {{inh|gml|gem-pro|*ubiri}}.

===Pronunciation===
* '''[[Wiktionary:About_Middle_Low_German#Stem_vowels|Stem vowel]]: ō² '''''or''''' ȫ¹ '''''or''''' ȫ²'''
** {{IPA|gml|/ʊɒvər/|/ʏəvər/|/ʏœvər/|a=originally}}

===Preposition===
{{head|gml|preposition|head=ōver|head2=ȫver}}

# {{lb|gml|accusative}} [[across]], moving through or over something
#: ''over dat rode mêr'' - across the Red Sea
# {{lb|gml|accusative}} [[across]], moving to the other side of something
# {{lb|gml|accusative}} [[in]], [[across]], describing the spread of something
#: ''over alle lant'' - all across the lands / in every land
# {{lb|gml|dative}} [[across]], situated on the other side of
#: ''over deme watere'' - across the water
# {{lb|gml|dative}} [[while]], over the duration of
#: ''over deme werke begripen'' - while working on something
# {{lb|gml|dative}} [[over]], [[at]], [[on]], [[on top of]], describing where something is situated; does not mean [[above]]
#: ''over deme dische'' - at the table

====Usage notes====
It is not clear whether the umlaut was connected with semantic differences.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|gml|aver}}

====Antonyms====
* {{antsense|over}} {{l|gml|under}}

===Adverb===
{{head|gml|adverb|head=ōver|head2=ȫver}}

# [[across]], on the other side
# [[while]]
# [[on top of]], [[additionally]]
# [[#English|over]] (finished, ceased)

====Usage notes====
It is not clear whether the umlaut was connected with semantic differences.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|gml|aver}}

====Antonyms====
* {{antsense|over}} {{l|gml|under}}

