{{also|émerge|émergé|emergé}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{etydate|late 16th century}}. Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|emerger}}, from {{der|en|la|ēmergō|ēmergere|to rise up or out}}, from {{m|la|ē-}} (a variant of {{m|la|ex-||out, forth}}) + {{m|la|mergo|mergere|to dip, to sink}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪˈmɝd͡ʒ/|/iˈmɝd͡ʒ/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|En-US emerge.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɪˈmɜːd͡ʒ/|/iˈmɜːd͡ʒ/|a=RP,AU}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-emerge.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)dʒ|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|emerge}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come into view.
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=12|passage=There were many wooden chairs for the bulk of his visitors, and two wicker armchairs with red cloth cushions for superior people. From the packing-cases had '''emerged''' some Indian clubs,{{nb...}}, and all these articles{{nb...}} made a scattered and untidy decoration that Mrs. Clough assiduously dusted and greatly cherished.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=17|passage=The face which '''emerged''' was not reassuring. It was blunt and grey, the nose springing thick and flat from high on the frontal bone of the forehead, whilst his eyes were narrow slits of dark in a tight bandage of tissue. {{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=w:Edwin Black
|chapter=2|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL4103950W Internal Combustion]
|passage=Throughout the 1500s, the populace roiled over a constellation of grievances of which the forest '''emerged''' as a key focal point. The popular late Middle Ages fictional character Robin Hood, dressed in green to symbolize the forest, dodged fines for forest offenses and stole from the rich to give to the poor. But his appeal was painfully real and embodied the struggle over wood.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=November 10, 2011|author=Jeremy Wilson|work=Telegraph
|title=[http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/england/8882713/England-Under-21-5-Iceland-Under-21-0-match-report.html England Under 21 5 Iceland Under 21 0: match report]
|passage=With such focus from within the footballing community this week on Remembrance Sunday, there was something appropriate about Colchester being the venue for last night’s game. Troops from the garrison town formed a guard of honour for both sets of players, who '''emerged''' for the national anthem with poppies proudly stitched into their tracksuit jackets.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative}} To come out of a situation, object or a liquid.
#: {{ux|en|He '''emerged''' unscathed from the accident.}}
#: {{ux|en|The Soviet Union '''emerged''' from the ruins of an empire.}}
#: {{ux|en|The submarine '''emerged''' from the ocean.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2012|month=March-April
|author=Anna Lena Phillips|volume=100|issue=2|page=172|magazine=w:American Scientist
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/sneaky-silk-moths Sneaky Silk Moths]
|passage=Last spring, the periodical cicadas '''emerged''' across eastern North America. Their vast numbers and short above-ground life spans inspired awe and irritation in humans—and made for good meals for birds and small mammals.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become known.
#: {{ux|en|Gradually the truth '''emerged'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2014-06-21|volume=411|issue=8892|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/books-and-arts/21604535-real-sir-isaac-newton-was-not-first-king-reason-last Magician’s brain]
|passage=The [Isaac] Newton that '''emerges''' from the [unpublished] manuscripts is far from the popular image of a rational practitioner of cold and pure reason. The architect of modern science was himself not very modern. He was obsessed with alchemy.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|to come into view}} {{l|en|come forth}}, {{l|en|forthcome}}, {{l|en|heave in sight}}; see also [[Thesaurus:appear]]
* {{sense|to become known}} [[come to light]], [[transpire]]

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|re-emerge}}, {{l|en|reemerge}}

====Related terms====
