Page 2 descent: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{wikipedia|mul=Descent (mathematics)}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|enm|-}} and {{der|en|xno|descente}}, from {{der|en|xno|descendre||to descend}}; see [[descend]]. Compare [[ascent]], [[ascend]]. {{doublet|en|desant}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈsɛnt/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/dəˈsɛnt/|/dɪˈsɛnt/|/diˈsɛnt/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-descent.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛnt|s=2}}
* {{hmp|en|dissent|qq1=for some dialects}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An instance of [[descending]]; act of coming down.
#: {{ux|en|We climbed the mountain with difficulty, but the '''descent''' was easier.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1947|month=January and February|author=O. S. Nock|title="The Aberdonian" in Wartime|journal=Railway Magazine|page=7|text=The '''descent''' continues, still more steeply to Dundee (Tay Bridge), and approaching from the bridge itself this sharp '''descent''' gives the curious appearance that the station is below the level of the firth.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=October|author='Voyageur'|title=The Cockermouth, Keswick & Penrith Railway|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=601|text=To the north the towering scree-strewn slopes of Saddleback begin to draw nearer as we start the abrupt '''descent''' towards [[Keswick]].}}
#* {{quote-text|en|date=July 15 2012|author=Richard Williams|publisher=Guardian Unlimited|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/sport/2012/jul/15/tour-de-france-2012-bradley-wiggins|title=Tour de France 2012: Carpet tacks cannot force Bradley Wiggins off track
|passage=The next one surrendered his bike, only for that, too, to give him a second flat as he started the '''descent'''.}}
# A [[way]] [[down]].
#: ''We had difficulty in finding the correct '''descent'''.''
# A [[sloping]] [[passage]] or [[incline]].
#: ''The '''descent''' into the cavern was wet and slippery.''
# [[lineage|Lineage]] or [[hereditary]] [[derivation]].
#: ''Our guide was of Welsh '''descent'''.''
# A [[drop]] to a [[lower]] [[status]] or [[condition]]; [[decline]]. {{rfex|en}} <!--current example sounds unlikely-->
#: ''After that, the holiday went into a steep '''descent'''.''
# A falling upon or invasion.
# {{lb|en|topology}} A particular [[extension]] of the idea of [[gluing]].

====Usage notes====
* Sometimes confused with {{m|en|decent}}.

====Antonyms====
* {{antsense|going down}} {{l|en|ascent}}

====Derived terms====
{{der2|en|hypodescent|hyperdescent|bilateral descent|descent group|descent vehicle|gradient descent|graduate student descent|identical by descent|infinite descent|minimum descent altitude|recursive descent}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|descend}}

====Translations====
