===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|sweten}}, from {{inh|en|ang|swǣtan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*swaitijaną|t=to sweat}}. Compare {{cog|nl|zweten}}, {{cog|de|schwitzen}}, {{cog|da|svede}}. {{doublet|en|shvitz}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|past2=sweat|past_ptc2=sweaten|past_ptc2_qual=archaic}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To emit sweat.
#: {{syn|en|perspire}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to excrete moisture through skin.
## To cause to perspire.
##: {{ux|en|His physicians attempted to '''sweat''' him by most powerful sudorifics.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|informal}} To work hard.
#: {{syn|en|slave|slog}}
#: {{ux|en|I've been '''sweating''' over my essay all day.}}
## {{lb|en|video games}} To be extremely [[dedicated]] to winning a game; to play [[competitively]].
##: {{syn|en|tryhard}}
##: {{ux|en|There's no way we can win. These guys are '''sweating''' so hard.}}
##* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/video-games/2022/05/27/skill-based-matchmaking/|work=The Washington Post|passage=With skill-based matchmaking, he wrote, "you have to '''sweat''' 100 percent of the time." They contend their audiences want to see them pull off amazing victories, not struggle endlessly against other top players.|title=Video game developers want fair online games. Some players really don’t.|date=2022-05-27|author=Ethan Davison}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To extract money, labour, etc. from, by [[exaction]] or [[oppression]].
#: {{syn|en|bleed}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''sweat''' a spendthrift}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''sweat''' labourers}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 September 7|author=Tom Allett|title=At the cutting edge of NR's track work|journal=RAIL|issue=965|page=40|text="I've predicted it will last 32 years. The last overhaul we will do on it is at 24 years, but we tend to '''sweat''' the asset at Network Rail and try and save a bit of money, so I've estimated 32 years."}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|informal}} To worry.
#: {{syn|en|fret|worry}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To worry about (something). {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|[[don't sweat it|Don't '''sweat''' it]]!}}
#: {{ux|en|to [[sweat the small stuff|'''sweat''' the small stuff]]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Brooks Barnes|title=Studios battle to save Narnia|journal=The New York Times|date=5 Dec 2010
|passage=There are few matters studio executives '''sweat''' more than maintaining their franchises.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To emit, in the manner of sweat.
#: {{ux|en|to '''sweat''' blood}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|The Cock and the Fox
|passage=With exercise she '''sweat'''<!--sic--> ill humors out.}}
#* {{RQ:King Mist|passage=I was sipping a third, but I had no kind of buzz on; apparently I had '''sweat''' the beer out as rapidly as I drank it.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To emit [[moisture]].
#: {{ux|en|The cheese will start '''sweating''' if you don't refrigerate it.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have drops of water form on (something's [[surface]]) due to [[moisture]] [[condensation]].
#: {{ux|en|Coasters are a good way to stop a '''sweating''' glass from damaging your table.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|plumbing}} To solder (a pipe joint) together.
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To [[stress out#Verb|stress out]], to put under [[pressure#Noun|pressure]].
#: {{ux|en|Stop '''sweatin' ''' me!}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1988|title=Fuck tha Police|artist=N.W.A|passage=But I'ma smoke 'em now and not next time / Smoke any motherfucker that '''sweats''' me}}
#* {{RQ:Noire Thug-A-Licious|page=118|passage=Over the next few days the cops half-ass questioned a couple of people, including me and Pimp, but they wasn't able to put nothing on us. They '''sweated''' Vyreen's husband pretty hard for a while, though.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive|cooking}} To [[cook]] [[slowly]] at low heat, in [[shallow]] [[oil]] and without [[brown]]ing, to reduce moisture content.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Zuni Cafe Cookbook|url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=039324234X|author=Judy Rodgers|year=2002|passage='''Sweating''' is a generally a quiet operation; if the food is whispering, or worse, hissing, the moisture is probably evaporating too rapidly}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Simply Vanilla: Recipes for Everyday Use|page=93|year=2007|url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=1601940009|author=Patty Elsberry; Matt Bolus|passage='''Sweat''' the carrots, onion, celery, leeks, and cabbage in the butter until translucent not allowing them to color in any way.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Bill Neal's Southern Cooking|page=11|url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=080788958X|author=Bill Neal|year=2009|passage=Reduce heat to low, cover pan, and gently '''sweat''' the celery for ten minutes, taking care not to brown it}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Bay Area Homegrown Cookbook|url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=1610597702|year=2011|passage='''Sweat''' the onions and garlic in the oil, stirring occasionally, until they are completely soft (no crunch) but not caramelized.}}
# {{senseid|en|to remove a portion of}} {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To remove a portion of (a coin), as by shaking it with others in a bag, so that the friction wears off a small quantity of the metal.
#* '''1879''', Richard Cobden, ''On the Probable Fall in the Value of Gold'' (originally by Michel Chevalier)
#*: {{quote|en|The only use of it [money] which is interdicted is to put it in circulation again after having diminished its weight by '''sweating''', or otherwise, because the quantity of metal contains is no longer consistent with its impression.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To suffer a penalty; to smart for one's misdeeds.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To take a [[racehorse]] for a short exercise run.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1740|author=Henry Bracken|title=Farriery improv'd
|passage=A Horſe that gains Fleſh in hard Exerciſe, should be '''ſweated''' at leaſt twice in ten Days; and he ſhould run near five Miles in Puſhes, that the Sweat may have Time to diſcharge. Those Horſes which are '''ſweat''' without Covering, or with a very thin one, should run a long Sweat, as wel call it, and ſtand a conſiderable while afterwards with a thick Blanket or two over them, from Head to Tail; otherwiſe the Sweat will not come out well.}}

