===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|-re|id=noun plural}}, {{m|enm|-er}}, from {{inh|en|ang|-ru||pos=plural suffix}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*-izō|pos=plural suffix}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|-er||pos=plural ending}}, {{cog|de|-er||pos=plural ending}}. See also {{l|en|-ren}}.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix|cat2=inflectional suffixes}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|no longer productive}} Suffix used to form the plural of a small number of English nouns.
#: {{m|en|childer|child'''er'''}}, {{m|en|calver|calv'''er'''}}, {{m|en|lamber|lamb'''er'''}}, {{m|en|linder|lind'''er'''}} {{gloss|"loins"}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|-ren}}

===Etymology 4===
From {{inh|en|enm|-er|id=agentive}}, representing various noun-suffixes in {{der|en|fro|-}} and {{der|en|xno|-}}, variously {{m|fro|-er}}, {{m|fro|-ier}} and {{m|fro|-ieur}}, from {{der|en|la|-aris}}, {{m|la|-arius}}, {{m|la|-atorium}}. As a productive suffix, now merged with the occupational sense of [[#Etymology 1|Etymology 1]].

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix|cat2=noun-forming suffixes}}

# [[person|Person]] or [[thing]] connected with.
#: {{suffixusex|en|bottle|butler}}

====See also====
* [[-eer]]

===Etymology 5===
From {{inh|en|enm|-ere|id=comparative}}, from {{inh|en|ang|-ra}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*iʀō}}, {{m|gmw-pro|*-ōʀō}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*-izô}} or {{der|en|gem-pro|*-ōzô}} (a derivative of Etymology 6, below); related to superlative {{m|en|-est}}.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}}

# {{senseid|en|comparative}} {{lb|en|added to certain [[adjective]]s and [[adverb]]s, now especially short ones}} [[more|More]]; {{non-gloss definition|used to form the [[comparative]]}}.
#: {{suffixusex|en|hard|harder}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|wet|wetter}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|fast|faster}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|strong|stronger}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{sense|more; used to form the comparative}} Most adjectives whose comparatives are formed using the suffix '''''-er''''' also form their superlatives using the suffix {{m|en|-est}}.
** Final {{m|en||-y}} preceded by a consonant becomes {{m|en||-i-}} when the suffix '''''-er''''' or {{m|en|-est}} is added.
**: ''ea'''sy''''' &rarr; ''ea'''sier''''' &rarr; ''ea'''siest'''''; ''gr'''ay''''' &rarr; ''gr'''ayer''''' &rarr; ''gr'''ayest'''''
** When the stress is on the final (or only) syllable of the adjective, and this syllable ends in a single consonant preceded by a single vowel, the final consonant is doubled when the suffix is added.
**: ''di'''m''''' &rarr; ''di'''mmer''''' &rarr; ''di'''mmest'''''
** The suffixes '''''-er''''' and {{m|en|-est}} may be used to form the comparative and superlative of most adjectives and adverbs that have one syllable and some that have two or more syllables.
**: ''hot'' &rarr; ''hott'''er''''' &rarr; ''hott'''est'''''; ''fast'' &rarr; ''fast'''er''''' &rarr; ''fast'''est'''''; ''funny'' &rarr; ''funni'''er''''' &rarr; ''funni'''est'''''; ''sugary'' &rarr; ''sugari'''er''''' &rarr; ''sugari'''est'''''
** Some adjectives and adverbs form their comparatives and superlatives irregularly:
**: ''good'' &rarr; ''better'' &rarr; ''best''; ''far'' &rarr; ''farther'' &rarr; ''farthest'', or ''far'' &rarr; ''further'' &rarr; ''furthest'', depending on the meaning
** The comparatives and superlatives of other adverbs and adjectives that have two or more syllables, and adjectives that are participles are formed with {{m|en|more}} and {{m|en|most}}.
**: ''rigid'' &rarr; '''''more''' rigid'' &rarr; '''''most''' rigid''; ''enormous'' &rarr; '''''more''' enormous'' &rarr; '''''most''' enormous''; ''burnt'' &rarr; '''''more''' burnt'' &rarr; '''''most''' burnt''; ''freezing'' &rarr; '''''more''' freezing'' &rarr; '''''most''' freezing''
** If in doubt, use {{m|en|more}} to form the comparative and {{m|en|most}} to form the superlative; for example, {{m|en|thirsty}} may become {{m|en|thirstier}} and {{m|en|thirstiest}}, but {{m|en||more thirsty}} and {{m|en||most thirsty}} are also acceptable.
* Words ending with -ng are pronounced {{IPAchar|/ŋ/}} by most dialects instead of {{IPAchar|/ŋɡ/}}. However, when -er or -est is added to an adjective, the {{IPAchar|/ɡ/}} appears (in most dialects).
*: ''long'' ({{IPAchar|/lɒŋ/}}) &rarr; ''long'''er''''' ({{IPAchar|/ˈlɒŋ.ɡə(ɹ)/}}); ''young'' ({{IPAchar|/jʌŋ/}}) &rarr; ''young'''est''''' ({{IPAchar|/ˈjʌŋ.ɡɪst/}})

