===Etymology 6===
From {{inh|en|enm|-er}}, from {{inh|en|ang|-or}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*-ōʀ}}, {{inh|en|gem-pro|*-ōz}}.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}}

# {{lb|en|added to certain [[adverb]]s}} [[more|More]]; {{non-gloss definition|used to form the comparative}}.

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|used to form the comparative of adverbs}}
* Czech: {{t|cs|-eji}}, {{t|cs|-ěji}}
* Dutch: {{t+|nl|-er}}
* Finnish: {{t|fi|-mpi}}
* French: {{t+|fr|plus}} {{qualifier|used before the adverb}}
* Galician: {{t+|gl|máis}}
* German: {{t+|de|-er}}
* Hungarian: {{t|hu|-bb}}
* Italian: [[più]] {{qualifier|used before the adverb}}
* Macedonian: {{t|mk|по-}}
* Persian: {{t+|fa|ـتر|tr=-tar}}
* Polish: {{t+|pl|bardziej}} {{qualifier|used before the adverb}}
* Portuguese: {{t+|pt|mais}}
* Spanish: {{t+|es|más}} {{qualifier|used before the adverb}}
* Swedish: {{t+|sv|mer}} {{qualifier|used before the adverb}}, {{t+|sv|-are}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 7===
From {{inh|en|enm|-eren}}, {{m|enm|-ren}}, {{m|enm|-rien}}, from {{inh|en|ang|-erian}}, {{m|ang|-rian}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*-rōn}}, {{m|gem-pro|*-iʀōn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*-rōną}} or {{m|gem-pro|*-izōną}}. Cognate with {{cog|fy|-erje}}, {{cog|nl|-eren}}, {{cog|de|-eren}}, {{m|de|-ern}}, {{cog|da|-re}}, {{cog|sv|-ra}}.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}}

# {{senseid|en|verbal frequentative}}{{lb|en|added to a verb or imitative sound}} [[frequently|Frequently]]; {{non-gloss definition|used to form [[frequentative]] verbs}}.
#: {{m|en|twitter|twitt'''er'''}}, {{m|en|clamber|clamb'''er'''}}, {{m|en|bicker|bick'''er'''}}, {{m|en|mutter|mutt'''er'''}}, {{m|en|wander|wand'''er'''}}, {{m|en|flutter|flutt'''er'''}}, {{m|en|flicker|flick'''er'''}}, {{m|en|slither|slith'''er'''}}, {{m|en|smother|smoth'''er'''}}, {{m|en|sputter|sputt'''er'''}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|used to form frequentative}} {{l|en|-le}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|used to form frequentatives}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|-lla|alt=-lla, -llä}}
* Hungarian: {{t|hu|-g}}
* Portuguese: {{t+|pt|-ejar}}
{{trans-bottom}}

=====See also=====
* {{pedia|Frequentative}}

===Etymology 8===
From {{inh|en|enm|-er|id=infinitival}}, from {{der|en|xno|-er}}, {{der|en|fro|-er}}, the infinitive verbal ending.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix|cat2=noun-forming suffixes}}

# {{senseid|en|action noun}} {{lb|en|chiefly|law|added to a verb}} [[instance|Instance]] of (the verbal action); {{non-gloss definition|used to form nouns from verbs}}.
#: {{suffixusex|en|disclaim|disclaimer}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|remit|remitter}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|misname|misnomer}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|rebut|rebutter}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|attain|attainder}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|en|id=action noun}}

===Etymology 9===
From {{inh|en|enm|-er}}, {{m|enm|-ere|id=diminutive|pos=diminutive suffix}}. Compare {{m|en|-el}}.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}}

# {{lb|en|added to a verb or noun}} Used to form diminutives.
#: {{suffixusex|en|shive|shiver}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|slive|sliver}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|splint|splinter}}

===Etymology 10===
{{etymon|en|id=Oxford}}
{{wikipedia|Oxford "-er"}}
Attested in the UK since the 19th century. Originally Rugby School slang. Later adopted by Oxford University and then wider British society.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}}

# {{lb|en|originally|_|public school slang}} {{non-gloss definition|Used to form slang or colloquial equivalents of words}}.
#: {{suffixusex|en|association|soccer|gloss2=association football}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|football|footer|gloss2=association football}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|rugby|rugger}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|Radcliffe|Radder|gloss2=a building at Oxford University}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|-zza}}
{{suffixsee|en|id=Oxford}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|-ers}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|used to form slang equivalents}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|-is}}
* Hungarian: {{t|hu|-ci}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 11===
From {{inh|en|enm|-er}}, from {{inh|en|ang|-er}}, {{m|ang|-or}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*-raz}}. Compare {{m|en|-le}}.

====Suffix====
{{head|en|suffix}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|_|dialectal}} A suffix creating adjectives from verbs, indicating [[aptitude]], [[proneness]], or [[tendency]] toward a specified action:
#: {{suffixusex|en|clive|cliver|gloss2=apt to cleave or adhere to, tenacious, expert as seizing}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|slip|slipper|gloss2=tending to make slip, slippery}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|wake|waker|gloss2=tending to wake, watchful}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{l|en|-le}}

===Etymology 12===
From {{bor|en|cmn|兒|-兒|tr=-ér}}.

====Suffix====
{{head|en|suffix}}

# {{lb|en|Chinese|_|literature}} [[junior|Junior]], child, younger person. {{q|Attached to a name, usually one syllable of the given name.}}
#: {{ux|en|Li’'''er''' said hello to his father.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1979|journal=Women of China|url=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=roGyAAAAIAAJ|page=44|text=Yue’'''er''' began to laugh again and her tears shimmered like dew on a lotus leaf disturbed by a breeze. Then we heard a sound. It was Man’'''er'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|origyear=1934|author=w:Xiao Hong|translator=w:Howard Goldblatt|chapter=The Field of Life and Death|title=The Field of Life and Death & Tales of Hulan River|isbn=0887273920|page=32|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=V3KhI1UutJIC&pg=PA32|text=The fish was laid out on the table, but Ping’'''er''' had not come back, nor had his father.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|origyear=1959|author={{w|Chung Li-ho|Zhong Lihe}}|translator=T. M. McClellan|chapter=The Little Ridge|title=From the Old Country: Stories and Sketches of China and Taiwan|isbn=9780231166300|page=202|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=CWqsAgAAQBAJ&pg=PT242|text=Ying’'''er''' was not yet three years old. Li’'''er''' had always been the one to play with her or to carry her places on his back.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Especially in Mandarin Chinese literature that has been translated into English, the suffix is often left untranslated in unaccented pinyin. This practice is similar to the use of {{m|en|-kun}} / {{m|en|-chan}} / {{m|en|-san}} or {{m|en|sensei}} in English-language Japanese fiction.
* Often, an apostrophe (used to mark syllable boundaries in pinyin) is inserted before the hyphen (as in Li'er), though it can also be omitted (''Yinger'').

=====Coordinate terms=====
* {{l|en|-chan}}

