==French==

===Etymology 1===
Mainly from {{der|fr|la|-āre}}; however, the descendants of some {{m+|la|-ēre}} verbs also became ''-er'' verbs in French.

====Pronunciation====
* {{fr-IPA|-é}}

====Suffix====
{{fr-verb}}

# {{n-g|forms infinitives of first-conjugation verbs}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Many of these verbs are directly descended from Latin, rather than from stem + suffix.
* In newly formed verbs, this suffix may be preceded by a euphonic consonant /t/ after a base ending in an oral vowel to avoid [[hiatus]]. In verbs formed from bases ending in nasal vowels, /n/ is inserted and the nasal vowel is denasalized:
*: {{suffixusex|fr|agio|-er|t1=agio|agioter|t3=to speculate}}
*: {{suffixusex|fr|blabla|-er|t1=chit-chat|blablater|t3=to chit-chat}}
*: {{suffixusex|fr|bourdon|-er|t1=bumblebee; drone|bourdonner|t3=to buzz, drone}}

=====Conjugation=====
{{fr-conj-auto}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{der|fr|la|-āre}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{fr-IPA|-é}}

====Suffix====
{{fr-noun|m}}

# {{n-g|forms nouns indicating the person who exercises a particular activity}}
#: {{syn|fr|-ère|q=female equivalent}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|fr}}

==German==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|[ɐ]}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|de|gmh|-ære}}, {{m|gmh|-er}}, from {{inh|de|goh|-āri}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*-ārī}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*-ārijaz}}, further etymology unknown but possibly from {{der|de|la|-ārius}}.<ref>A. van Loey, "Schönfeld's Historische Grammatica van het Nederlands", Zutphen, 8. druk, 1970, {{ISBN|90-03-21170-1}}; § 175</ref>

====Suffix====
{{de-noun|m}}

# {{senseid|de|agent noun}}{{n-g|Forms agent nouns etc. from verbs, suffixed to the verb stem.}}
#: {{suffixusex|de|arbeiten|t1=to work|Arbeiter|t2=worker}}
#: {{suffixusex|de|bohren|t1=to drill|Bohrer|t2=drill}}
# {{ngd|Forms instance nouns from verbs.}}
#: {{suffixusex|de|husten|t1=to cough|Huster|t2=single cough, instance of coughing}}
#: {{suffixusex|de|hüpfen|t1=to hop|Hüpfer|t2=hop, instance of hopping}}
# {{ngd|Indicates something defined by a number; in the plural often all numbers with the same first digits}}
#: {{suffixusex|de|16|16er|t2=the 16, the 16er, e.g. a bus, a football player, etc.}}
#: {{suffixusex|de|200|200er|t2=a 200, the 200s, e.g. a 200-euro note, or the list items 200 to 299, etc.}}
#: {{suffixusex|de|1990|1990er|t2=[[1990s]], the years 1990 to 1999}}

=====Declension=====
{{de-ndecl|m}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|de|id=agent noun}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|de|-ler}}, {{l|de|-ner}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|de|gmh|-er}}, a plural ending for some neuter nouns.

====Suffix====
{{head|de|inflectional suffixes}}

# {{ngd|Used to form the plurals of some nouns.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The plural ending ''-er'' is used in a fairly large number of neuters (including all those in {{m|de|-tum}}) and a small number of masculines.

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|de|gmh|-ære}}, {{m|de|-er}}, from {{inh|de|goh|-āri}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*warjaz}}.

====Suffix====
{{de-noun|m}}

# {{senseid|de|inhabitant}} {{n-g|Forms nouns indicating an inhabitant of a place, or a person originating from a place.}}

=====Declension=====
{{de-ndecl|m}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|de|id=inhabitant}}

===Etymology 4===
Probably originated from the prepositioned [[genitive]] plural of etymology 3 above, e.g.: ''der Berliner Pfannkuchen'' = "the Berliners’ pancake", and then "the Berlin(er) pancake", reanalysed as an adjective instead of a noun and seen as being in the [[nominative]] singular (due to the ambiguity of the definite article {{m|de|der}}, which is both masculine nominative and plural genitive).<ref>Johann Christoph Adelung, ''Grammatisch-kritisches Wörterbuch der Hochdeutschen Mundart'', vol. 1 (Leipzig, 1793), pages 1848-1852, sub verbo ''4. -Er''</ref><ref>Hermann Möller, ''Ahd. frôno (nhd. fron-) als elliptischer Plural'', in the ''Zeitschrift für deutsche Wortforschung'', volume 4 (editor Friedrich Kluge; Straßburg, 1903), page 95</ref>

====Suffix====
{{head|de|suffix}}

# {{senseid|de|genitival}} {{n-g|Forms invariable adjectives from place names, with a [[genitival]] meaning, indicating origin from or association with that place.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In contemporary German, words formed with this suffix ''-er'' are written with a capital letter (§&nbsp;61 of the official reform spelling rules as of 2018), e.g. ''ein Berliner Pfannkuchen''. In the past, they were sometimes written with a lowercase letter like most other adjectives, e.g. ''ein berliner Pfannkuchen''.<ref>The current official spelling rules prescribe the capital letter without further explanation and without indicating the [[part of speech]] of the words formed with the suffix (compare {{m|de|-isch}}/{{m|de|-sch}}, derivatives of which are labelled adjectives in §&nbsp;62).</ref>
* In case of placenames which are written with a space, the derived word can be written with a space or with a hyphen (§&nbsp;49 of the official reform spelling rules as of 2011), e.g. ''Bad Schandau'' → ''Bad Schandauer'' or ''Bad-Schandauer''.
* Since adjectives in ''-er'' are undeclined, they cannot normally support [[genitive]]s by themselves. However, in the feminine and plural the ending ''-er'' happens to be same as that of a declined (strong) adjective and according pseudo-genitives may be encountered, such as {{m|de||Meldungen Berliner Zeitungen|t=reports of Berlin newspapers}} instead of more proper {{m|de||Meldungen von Berliner Zeitungen}}. Such usage has been discouraged, but is no longer considered an error.

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|de|id=genitival}}

===Etymology 5===
From {{inh|de|gmh|-er}}.

====Suffix====
{{head|de|inflectional suffixes}}

# {{senseid|de|comparative}}{{n-g|Forms the comparative form of adjectives.}}
#: {{suffixusex|de|lang|länger}}
#: {{suffixusex|de|schön|schöner}}
#: {{suffixusex|de|exakt|exakter}}

===References===
<references/>

==Hungarian==

===Etymology===
Possibly from {{bor|hu|en|-er}}, by analogy of word pairs like {{m|hu|blog}} and {{m|hu|blogger}} (whose doubled final consonant is consistently pronounced long in Hungarian, as opposed to English) and/or perhaps earlier borrowed word pairs like {{m|hu|stop}} and {{m|hu|stoppol}}. Other existing slang terms ending in {{m|hu||-er}}, like {{m|hu|[[vaker]], [[haver]], [[sóder]]}}, might have played some role. {{rfv-etym|hu}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{hu-IPA}}

===Suffix===
{{head|hu|suffix}}

# {{lb|hu|slang|slightly|derogatory}} Added to a shortened form of a [[noun]], lengthening the first consonant following its first vowel, to derive a noun.
#: {{m|hu|kalauz||ticket inspector}} → {{m|hu|kaller}}
#: {{m|hu|nyugdíjas||pensioner}} → {{m|hu|nyugger}}
#: {{m|hu|mami||mommy; elderly woman}} → {{m|hu|mammer}}
#: {{m|hu|jobboldali||rightist}} → {{m|hu|jobber}}

====Derived terms====
{{suffixsee|hu|pos=noun}}

===See also===
* {{m|hu|-esz|pos=as in {{m|hu|alkesz}}, {{m|hu|pálesz}}; slang terms}}
* [[Appendix:Hungarian suffixes]]

