==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|plommet|t=ball of lead, plumb of a bob-line}}, recorded since 1382, from {{der|en|fro|plommet}} or {{m|fro|plomet}}, the diminutive of {{m|fro|plom}}, {{m|fro|plum|t=lead, sounding lead}}, from {{der|en|la|plumbum|t=lead}}. The verb is first recorded in 1626, originally meaning “to fathom, take soundings", from the noun.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈplʌmɪt/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈplʌmət/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-plummet.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌmɪt|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|plum|met}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|nautical}} A piece of [[lead]] attached to a line, used in [[sound#Etymology 4|sounding]] the depth of water; a [[plumb bob]] or a [[plumb line]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|3|3|page=13|column=2|text=I'le ſeeke him deeper than ere '''plummet''' ſounded, / And with him there lye mudded.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Isaiah|28|17|passage=Iudgement also will I lay to the line, and righteousnesse to the '''plummet''': and the haile shall sweepe away the refuge of lyes, and the waters shall ouerflow the hiding place.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Martin Chuzzlewit|chapter=21|passage=Each long black hair upon his head hung down as straight as any '''plummet''' line; {{...|but rumpled tufts were on the arches of his eyes, as if the crow whose foot was deeply printed in the corners had pecked and torn them in a savage recognition of his kindred nature as a bird of prey.}}}}
#* {{RQ:London White Fang|chapter=VI|passage=And love was the '''plummet''' dropped down into the deeps of him where like had never gone.}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Return of Tarzan|passage=He told her then of his life since he had returned to the jungle—of how he had dropped like a '''plummet''' from a civilized Parisian to a savage Waziri warrior, and from there back to the brute that he had been raised.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Hence, any [[weight]].
#* {{quote-book |en |year=1945 |author=w:Ernie Pyle |title=Here Is Your War: Story of G.I. Joe |publisher=The World Publishing Company |page=93 |text=His parachute was shot half away, and if he'd jumped he would have fallen like a '''plummet'''.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A piece of lead formerly used by schoolchildren to [[rule#Verb|rule]] paper for writing (that is, to mark with [[rule#Noun|rules]], with [[line]]s).
# A violent or dramatic [[fall]].
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A [[decline]]; a [[fall]]; a [[drop]].
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=December 29, 2010
|author=Chris Whyatt
|title=Chelsea 1 - 0 Bolton
|work=BBC
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9309542.stm
|page=
|passage=Yet another seriously under-par performance is unlikely to provide any real answers to their remarkable '''plummet''' in form - but it proves they can at least churn out a much-needed result.}}

