{{hy-noun}}

# [[thread]]
#: {{ux|hy|բարակ թել|t=thin thread|inline=1}}

====Declension====
{{hy-noun-ի-եր}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|bor=1|rmi|թել}}
* {{desc|bor=1|ota|تل|tr=tel}} {{qual|possibly}} {{see desc}}

{{topics|hy|Sewing}}

==Old Armenian==

===Etymology===
The origin is {{unk|xcl|title=disputed}}. The same word is found in several language groups: Iranian – {{cog|fa|تیلا|t=rope; spinning wheel}}, {{cog|kho|tr=ttīla-}} (from older {{m|kho||*tēla-|sc=Latn}}), {{cog|os|тел}}, {{cog|kmr|têl}}, {{cog|ckb|تێل}}, {{m|ckb|تەل}}; Turkic – {{cog|ota|تل|tr=tel}}, {{cog|az|tel}}, {{cog|crh|tel}}, {{cog|gag|tel}}, {{cog|kum|тел}}, {{cog|nog|тел}}, {{cog|tk|til}}, perhaps also {{cog|xqa|تِلیٖ|tr=tili|t=thong}}, {{cog|cv|тал||strand}}; Northeast Caucasian — {{cog|av|тел}}, {{cog|dar|тел}}, {{cog|lez|тел}}.<ref name=HAB>{{R:xcl:HAB|vol=II|pages=169–170}}</ref><ref>{{R:trk:VEWT|page=471a}}</ref><ref name=SDM>{{R:SDM|­*t῾ḗlù|url=http://starling.rinet.ru/cgi-bin/response.cgi?single=1&basename=%2fdata%2falt%2fturcet&text_number=1130&root=config}}</ref><ref>{{R:ku:Cabolov|vol=II|pages=401–402}}</ref><ref>{{R:kmr:Chyet|page=616}}</ref><ref name=Abaev>{{R:os:Abaev|vol=III|page=288}}</ref> The interrelationship of these is not clear; a Turkic origin that has been suggested<ref name=SDM/><ref>{{R:SIGTYA|vol=VI|pages=811–812}}</ref> is unconvincing.

According to some, the Armenian is a native term inherited from a derivative of {{inh|xcl|ine-pro|*ten-||to stretch, draw}} or {{m|ine-pro|*tek-|id=weave|t=to weave}}.<ref>{{R:xcl:Pedersen:1907|page=234}}</ref><ref>{{R:xcl:HLP|page=233}}</ref><ref>{{R:xcl:HSB|page=263a}}</ref><ref>{{R:xcl:Olsen|page=200}}</ref> The rest are then Armenian borrowings, with a high certainty in case of Turkish.<ref>{{R:tr:Kraelitz:1913|pages=131–132}}</ref><ref name=HAB/><ref>{{R:Gayayan:1977|pages=86–87}}</ref><ref>{{R:tr:Bläsing:1992|pages=13–14}}</ref><ref>{{R:tr:Dankoff|page=47}}</ref><ref>{{R:tr:Eren|tel|page=400b}}</ref>

On the other hand, Bailey derives all from {{cog|ira-old|-}} {{m|unk||*tarθrya-}}, from {{cog|ine-pro|*terh₁-||to rub, turn; to drill, pierce}}, whence notably {{cog|en|thread}}, the Armenian and Turkic being Iranian borrowings, in case of Turkic perhaps via Armenian.<ref>{{R:Bailey:1966|page=524}}</ref><ref>{{R:ira:Bailey:1970|pages=30–33}}</ref><ref>{{R:kho:Bailey|page=129b}}</ref> Abaev adduces also {{cog|fa|تار|tr=târ}} as a cognate.<ref name=Abaev/> Note also {{cog|ckb|تاڵ|t=string, cord}}.

Note also {{cog|he|תּוֹר|tr=tōr|t=line}}, {{cog|tmr|תּוֹרָא|tr=tōrā|t=cord, band}}, deriving from {{cog|akk|𒄙|ts=turru, ṭurru|t=yarn, twine, wire, string; a twist of fibers}} and the source of obsolete {{cog|ar|تُرّ|t=plumb line}}, possibly related to the native verb {{m|sem-pro|*tawar-|t=to go about, to circle, to turn about, to encompass, to come around again; to plait, to braid}}, or ultimately from {{cog|sux|𒄙|tr=dur|t=strip}}.

===Noun===
{{xcl-noun}}

# [[thread]], [[filament]]; [[fibre]]; [[chord]] {{defdate|from 5th c.}}
#: {{ux|xcl|թել ոսկի|t=golden thread|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|թել արծաթի|t=silver thread|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|թել երկաթի|t=iron or metal wire|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|հատանել թելս թելս|t=to reduce to filaments; to atomize|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|թել զթելն յօրինել|t=to weave thread by thread; to adorn symmetrically|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|թելք բանից, պատմութեան|t=the thread of a discourse or story|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|հատանել զթել բանին|t=to break the thread of one's argument|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|թել կենաց|t=the thread of life|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|հատանել զթել կենաց|t=to cut the thread of life, to cut off one's days|inline=1}}

