{{also|Appendix:Variations of "can"}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|Can (verb)}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ǵneh₃-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|can}}, first and third person singular of {{m|enm|connen}}, {{m|enm|cunnen||to be able, know how}}, from {{inh|en|ang|cann|can(n)}}, first and third person singular of {{m|ang|cunnan||to know how}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*kunnan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*kunnaną}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*ǵneh₃-}} (whence also {{m|en|know}}). {{doublet|en|con}}. See also: {{m|en|canny}}, {{m|en|cunning}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|canne||obsolete}}
* {{alter|en|kin||pronunciation spelling}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{qualifier|stressed}}
** {{enPR|kăn}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈkæn/|[ˈkʰan]|[ˈkʰæn]|a=RP,Ireland}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈkæn/|[ˈkʰæn]|[ˈkʰɛən ~ ˈkʰeən]|a=GA,Canada|aa=see {{w|/æ/ raising}}}}
*** {{IPA|en|/ˈkɛn/|a=Philadelphia}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈkæn/|[ˈkʰæn]|[ˈkʰɛːn]|a=AU|aa=see {{w|/æ/ raising}}}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈkɛn/|[ˈkʰɛˑn]|a=NZ}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-can.ogg|a=US}}
** {{rhymes|en|æn|s=1}}
* {{qualifier|unstressed}}
** {{IPA|en|/kən/|[kʰən]|[kʰn̩]}}
** {{audio|en|en-US can (unstressed).ogg|a=US,unstressed}}

====Pronunciation notes====
* Some US dialects that glottalize the final /t/ in ''[[can’t]]'' ({{IPAchar|[kæn(ʔ)]}}), in order to differentiate ''can’t'' from ''can'', pronounce ''can'' as {{IPAchar|/kɛn/}} even when stressed.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|can|able|could|couth|pres_ptc_qual=by [[suppletion]]|past_ptc_qual=obsolete except in adjectival use}}

# {{lb|en|auxiliary|_|verb|defective}} To know how to; to be [[able]] to.
#: {{syn|en|be able to}}
#: {{ant|en|cannot|can't}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''can''' speak English, French, and German.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''can''' play football.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Can''' you remember your fifth birthday?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1449|author=w:Reginald Pecock|title=Represser of over-much weeting [blaming] of the Clergie
|passage=prouyng which eny clerk '''can''' or woel or mai make bi eny maner euydence of resoun or of Scripture, and namelich of resoun into the contrarie.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=July–August| author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/lee-s-langston Lee S. Langston]| magazine=w:American Scientist| title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/4/the-adaptable-gas-turbine The Adaptable Gas Turbine]| passage=Turbines have been around for a long time—windmills and water wheels are early examples. The name comes from the Latin ''turbo'', meaning ''vortex'', and thus the defining property of a turbine is that a fluid or gas turns the blades of a rotor, which is attached to a shaft that '''can''' perform useful work.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|II|ii|passage=If thou '''canst''' awake by four o' the clock, / I prithee call me. Sleep hath seized me wholly.}}
# {{lb|en|modal|_|auxiliary|_|verb|defective|informal}} [[May]]; to be permitted or enabled to.
#: {{syn|en|may}}
#: {{ux|en|You '''can''' go outside and play when you're finished with your homework.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Can''' I use your pen?}}
# {{lb|en|modal auxiliary verb, defective}} To have the [[potential]] to; be [[possible]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Can''' it be Friday already?}}
#: {{ux|en|Teenagers '''can''' really try their parents' patience.}}
#: {{ux|en|Animals '''can''' experience emotions.}}
#* {{RQ:Travers Cuckoo in the Nest|chapter=5|passage=The most rapid and most seductive transition in all human nature is that which attends the palliation of a ravenous appetite.{{...}}'''Can''' those harmless but refined fellow-diners be the selfish cads whose gluttony and personal appearance so raised your contemptuous wrath on your arrival?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Annette Sym|title=Simply Too Good to be True|publisher=Greenleaf Book Group|isbn=9780957716179|page=4|passage=Teenagers '''can''' be so cruel, and nicknames cut deep.}}
# {{lb|en|auxiliary|_|verb|defective}} {{n-g|Used with verbs of perception}}.
#: {{ux|en|'''Can''' you hear that?}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''can''' feel the baby moving inside me.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To [[know]].
#: {{syn|en|cognize|grok|ken}}
#* ca.'''1360-1387''', {{w|William Langland}}, ''{{w|Piers Plowman}}''
#*: I '''can''' rimes of Robin Hood.
#* ca.'''1360-1387''', {{w|William Langland}}, ''{{w|Piers Plowman}}''
#*: I '''can''' no Latin, quod she.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Venus and Adonis|passage=Let the priest in surplice white, / That defunctive music '''can'''.}}
# {{lb|en|India|nonstandard|proscribed}} To be {{q|followed by a word like ''able'', ''possible'', ''allowed''}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2011-11-29|author=Tai-hoon Kim; Hojjat Adeli; Carlos Ramos; Byeong-Ho Kang|title=Signal Processing, Image Processing and Pattern Recognition: International Conferences, SIP 2011, Held as Part of the Future Generation Information Technology Conference, FGIT 2011, in Conjunction with GDC 2011, Jeju Island, Korea, December 8-10, 2011. Proceedings|publisher=Springer Science & Business Media|isbn=9783642271823|page=114
|text=Importance of Identifying Leaf: Identify Plants: If we '''can''' able to identify leaf, we '''can''' easily able to identify plants.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2018-02-15|author=Asha Bajpai|title=Child Rights in India: Law, Policy, and Practice|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=9780199091263
|text=Children in need of care and protection '''can''' allowed to be placed in foster care based on the orders of the CWC. The selection of the foster family is based on the family's ability, intent, capacity, and prior experience of taking care{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2020-05-22|author=Pardeep Kumar; Vasaki Ponnusamy; Vishal Jain|title=Industrial Internet of Things and Cyber-Physical Systems: Transforming the Conventional to Digital: Transforming the Conventional to Digital|publisher=IGI Global|isbn=9781799828051|page=226
|text=It '''can''' possible to design the ruleset refreshes that allow them to subsequently run at precise interludes and these keep informed.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* For missing forms, substitute inflected forms of ''[[be able to]]'', as:
** ''I might be able to go.''
** ''I have been able to go, since I was seven.''
** ''I had been able to go before.''
** ''I will be able to go tomorrow.''
* The word [[could]] also suffices in many tenses. “I would be able to go” is equivalent to “I could go”, and “I was able to go” can be rendered “I could go”. (Unless there is a clear indication otherwise, “could ''verb''” means “was able to ''verb''”, but “could not ''verb''” means “was/were unable to ''verb''”.)
* The present tense negative ''can not'' is usually contracted to ''[[cannot]]'' (more formal) or ''[[can’t]]'' (less formal).
* The use of ''can'' in asking permission sometimes is criticized as being impolite or incorrect by those who favour the more formal alternative ''“may I...?”''.
* ''Can'' is sometimes used rhetorically to issue a command, placing the command in the form of a request. For instance, ''“Can you hand me that pen?”'' as a polite substitution for ''“Hand me that pen.”''
* Some US dialects that glottalize the final /t/ in ''[[can’t]]'' ({{IPAchar|/kæn(ʔ)/}}), in order to differentiate ''[[can’t]]'' from ''can'', pronounce ''can'' as {{IPAchar|/kɛn/}} even when stressed.

