==Scottish Gaelic==

===Etymology===
{{root|gd|ine-pro|*keh₂n-}}
From {{inh|gd|sga|canaid||to sing}}, from {{inh|gd|cel-pro|*kaneti||to sing}}, from {{inh|gd|ine-pro|*keh₂n-}}. Compare {{cog|cy|canu}}, {{cog|la|canō}}, {{cog|grc|καναχέω}}, {{cog|fa|خواندن|tr=xândan}}.

===Verb===
{{gd-verb|past=chan|fut=canaidh|vn=cantainn|vn2=canail|vn3=cantail|pp=cante}}

# to [[say]]
#: {{uxi|gd|cha '''chan''' mi càil mus can mi cus|I won't '''say''' anything before I've said too much}}
# to [[sing]] {{q|a song}}
# {{inflection of|gd|can||futr|indc|dependent}}

====Usage notes====
* In most dialects of Scottish Gaelic still spoken, with the notable exception of Islay, the future and conditional tenses and the imperative form are very often used for the verb {{m|gd|abair}} in place of the actual {{m|gd||abair}} forms, particularly in colloquial language; the {{m|gd||abair}} forms are recognised but considered Biblical or excessively formal. Some northern dialects, such as Skye and Lewis, extend this to verbal noun forms derived from {{m|gd||can}}, such as {{m|gd|cantainn}} and {{m|gd|canail}}.

