==English==
{{wp}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{sense|male given name}} {{alt|en|Jon}}
* {{sense|Gospel of St John}} {{alt|en|Joh.||abbreviation|rare}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|John}}, {{m|enm|Johan}}, {{m|enm|Johannes}}, from {{der|en|xno|Jehan}}, {{m|fro|Johan}}, and also {{der|en|ang|Iohannes}}, both from {{der|en|LL.|Iōhannēs}}, variant of {{der|en|la|Iōannēs}}, from {{der|en|grc-koi|Ἰωάννης}}, from {{der|en|hbo|יוֹחָנָן|tr=Yôḥānān}}, perhaps contracted from a former {{m|he|יְהוֹחָנָן||God is gracious|tr=Yəhōḥānān}}.

{{doublet|en|Jack|Jon|Johan|Johann|Johannes|Jean|Sean|Shane|Shaun|Ian|Ivan|Evan|Juan|Giovanni|Yahya}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/d͡ʒɒn/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/d͡ʒɑn/|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-John.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒn|s=1}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-I learned some phrases-John.wav|a=UK}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|s}}
{{book of the Bible|Gospel of John}}

# {{given name|en|male|from=the Bible < Hebrew}}''; very popular since the Middle Ages.''
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1852|author=D. H. Jacques|title=A Chapter on Names|journal=The Knickerbocker, or, New-York Monthly Magazine|volume=XL|month=August|page=114
|passage='''John''' is a most excellent name, and Smith is a surname which is worthy of respect and honor, but wo to the man on whom they are conjoined! For '''John''' Smith to aspire to senatorial dignities or to the laurel of a poet is simply ridiculous. Who ''is'' '''John''' Smith? He is lost in the multitude of '''John''' Smiths, and individual fame is impossible.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1920|author=John Collings Squire|chapter=Initials|title=Life and Letters: Essays|publisher=Hodder & Stoughton|pages=233–235
|passage=The name I refer to is '''John'''. It has been borne by many illustrious men and an innumerable multitude of the obscure. - - - It is as fixed as the English landscape and the procession of seasons. It never becomes wearisome or tarnished. Nothing affects it; nothing can bring it into contempt; it stands like a rock amid the turbulent waves of human history, as fine and noble a thing now as it was when it first took shape on human lips. It is a name to live up to; but if one who bears it sinks into disrepute it falls not with him, but rather stays in the firmament above him, shining down upon him like a reproachful star.}}
## {{lb|en|informal}} {{n-g|Used generically for a man whose actual name may not be known.}}
##: {{ux|en|a '''John''' Doe murder case; the dreaded Dear '''John''' letter; if we were to ask '''John''' Q. Public his opinion}}
## {{lb|en|informal}} {{n-g|Used frequently to form an idea personified, as in [[John Bull]], [[John Barleycorn]] (see derivations below).}}
# {{lb|en|biblical}} Persons of the Christian Bible: [[John the Baptist]]; and names  possibly referring to one, two or three persons, frequently called "Saint": {{w|John the Apostle}}, {{w|John the Evangelist}} and {{w|John of Patmos}} (also called John the Divine or John the Theologian).
#* {{RQ:King James Version|John|1|6|passage=There was a man sent from God, whose name was '''John'''.}}
# {{lb|en|biblical}} The Gospel of St. John, a book of the [[New Testament]] of the [[Bible]]. Traditionally the fourth of the four [[gospel]]s.
# {{lb|en|biblical}} One of the books in the [[New Testament]] of the [[Bible]], the [[epistle]]s of John ([[1 John]], [[2 John]] and [[3 John]]).
# {{surname|en|from=patronymics}}.

