{{also|Jinn}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|genie}}, {{l|en|ginn}}, {{l|en|djinn}}, {{l|en|djinni}}, {{l|en|djinny}}, {{l|en|jinnee}}, {{l|en|jinni}}, {{l|en|jin}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|ar|pos=collective noun|جِنّ}} (singular {{m|ar|جِنِّيّ}}).
{{root|en|ar|ج ن ن}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/d͡ʒɪn/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪn|s=1}}
* {{homophone|en|gin}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|jinn|jawan|jinnan|jinnah}}

# {{lb|en|Arabic culture}} A human-like [[spiritual]] or [[immaterial]] [[being]], as opposed to ''al-ins'' (people), often invisible but able to [[manifest]] in [[form]] and also inhabit people or animals; origin of the [[genie]] of Western literature, film etc.
# Such beings collectively.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1968|author={{w|Joseph Campbell}}|title=The Hero with a Thousand Faces|year_published=1993|location=London|publisher=Fontana Press|edition=2nd|page=8|passage=There not only jewels but also dangerous''' jinn '''abide: the inconvenient or resisted psychological powers that we have not thought or dared to integrate into our lives.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2020|author=Abu-Hamdiyyah, Muhammad|authorlink=Abu-Hamdiyyah, Muhammad|title= The Qur'an: an introduction|location=|publisher=Routledge|page=101|passage=Surah 18:50: 'And We told the Angels "prostrate yourselves before Adam". So they all prostrated themselves, except Iblees who was one of the '''''jinn'''''.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2020|author=Abu-Hamdiyyah, Muhammad|authorlink=Abu-Hamdiyyah, Muhammad|title= The Qur'an: an introduction|location=|publisher=Routledge|page=101|passage=The second context in which jinn occurs in the Qur'an is where we find both ''ins'' and '''''jinn''''' are mentioned together. Ins is a collective noun signifying humans, recognisable familiar human beings.}}

====Synonyms====
* [[jann]], [[jinni]], [[genie]]

