==Persian==

===Pronunciation===
{{fa-regional|خان|خان|хон}}
{{fa-IPA|xān}}

===Etymology 1===
{{wikipedia|lang=fa|خان (عنوان)}}
{{bor+|fa|trk}}; first attested in Islamic lands in {{w|Karakhanid}} coinage from the 900s.<ref>{{cite-web|title=K̲h̲ān|url=https://referenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/encyclopaedia-of-islam-2/khan-SIM_4183?s.num=1&s.f.s2_parent=s.f.book.encyclopaedia-of-islam-2&s.q=kh%C4%81n|site=Encyclopaedia of Islam, Volume II|last=Boyle|first=J. A.|year=2012}}</ref> See {{cog|otk|𐰴𐰍𐰣|ts=qaɣan}} for more.

====Noun====
{{fa-noun|tr=xân|pl=خان‌ها|pl2=خوانین|pl2tr=xavânin}}

# [[Sir]], [[Mister]]; {{n-g|a title attached after the name of a man, marking politeness and/or social distance.}}
#: {{uxi|fa|داریوش '''خان'''|tr=dâryuš '''xân'''|t='''Mister''' Daryoosh}}
# {{lb|fa|historical}} [[khan]] (nomadic ruler); {{n-g|by extension:}}
#: {{uxi|fa|چنگیز '''خان'''|tr=čengiz '''xân'''|t=Genghis '''Khan'''}}
## {{n-g|A [[Safavid]] provincial governor ranking between {{m|fa|بیگلربیگی|tr=beyglarbeygi|t=governor-general}} and {{m|fa|سلطان|tr=soltân|t=deputy governor}}.}}
## {{n-g|In the [[Mughal]] and [[Qajar]] empires, an extremely common title used for any nobleman and courtier, even if not of nomadic background.}}
## {{n-g|The [[Ottoman]] emperor.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* As a suffix of politeness, {{m|fa|آقا|tr=âqâ}} is more formal and {{m|fa||خان|tr=xân}} is more informal. Nowadays, {{m|fa||خان|tr=xân}} is sometimes used to express sarcasm, since {{m|fa||آقا|tr=âqâ}} is more common in actually formal or polite contexts. Otherwise, it can be felt to be dated.

=====Related terms=====
{{col-auto|fa
|خانیت<tr:xâniyat>
}}

