==Transcription==
Notes on Semitic Romanization:
* '''â''', '''ê''', '''ô''' stand for the vowels of ''f'''a'''ther'' {{IPAchar|[ɑ]}}, ''d'''ay''''' {{IPAchar|[e]}} and ''c'''o'''re'' {{IPAchar|[ɒ]}} in British English.
* '''a''', '''e''', '''i''', '''o''' and '''u''' stand for the vowels of ''h'''a'''t'' {{IPAchar|[a]}}, ''g'''e'''t'' {{IPAchar|[ɛ]}}, ''h'''ea'''t'' {{IPAchar|[i]}}, ''h'''o'''t'' {{IPAchar|[ɔ]}} and ''f'''oo'''l'' {{IPAchar|[u]}}.
* '''ā''', '''ē''', '''ī''', '''ō''' and '''ū''' stand for the same vowels as without the macron, but pronounced longer. {{IPAchar|[aː]}}, {{IPAchar|[ɛː]}}, {{IPAchar|[iː]}}, {{IPAchar|[ɔː]}}, {{IPAchar|[uː]}}
* '''b''', '''d''', '''f''', '''h''', '''k''', '''l''', '''m''', '''n''', '''p''', '''r''', '''s''', '''t''', '''v''', '''w''', '''y''' and '''z''' stand for their equivalents in English.
* '''θ''' stands for the ''th'' of English '''''th'''ick''. {{IPAchar|[θ]}}
* '''ð''' stands for the ''th'' of English '''''th'''is''. {{IPAchar|[ð]}}
* '''j''' stands for the ''j'' of English '''''j'''ob''. {{IPAchar|[dʒ]}}
* '''g''' stands for the ''g'' of English '''''g'''ood''. {{IPAchar|[ɡ]}}
* '''x''' stands for the sound of German ''ch'' in ''A'''ch'''!'' or ''j'' in Spanish '''''j'''ota''. {{IPAchar|[x]}}
* '''ğ''' stands for the voiced counterpart of ''x'', much as Spanish intervocalic ''g''. {{IPAchar|[ɣ]}}
* '''š''' stands for the ''sh'' of English '''''sh'''ip''. {{IPAchar|[ʃ]}}
* '''ś''' stands for the sound of Welsh ''ll'' in '''''Ll'''oyd''. {{IPAchar|[ɬ]}}
* '''ħ''' stands for the typical rough ''h'' sound of Semitic languages. {{IPAchar|[ħ]}}
* '''ʿ''' stands for the voiced counterpart of ''ħ''. {{IPAchar|[ʕ]}}
* '''ʾ''' stands for the glottal stop of cockney English ''bo'''ʼ'''le'' ('bottle'). {{IPAchar|[ʔ]}}
* '''q''' stands for a ''k'' sound produced from the back of the mouth. {{IPAchar|[q]}}
* '''ṭ''', '''ṣ''', '''ḍ''' and '''ẓ''' stand for the sounds derived from ''t'', ''s'', ''d'' and ''z''/''ð''. These are called the pharyngealized, emphatic, or velarized sounds. They affect the sound of the consonants and vowels that follow or precede; they are made by constricting the pharynx during the articulation of the sound. {{IPAchar|[tˤ]|[sˤ]|[dˤ]|[zˤ]}}/{{IPAchar|[ðˤ]}}

Since this page contains mostly ancient languages, the Hebrew romanization has been chosen to correspond to the pronunciation of Tiberian (Biblical) Hebrew, rather than to modern Israeli Hebrew. This means that the following developments of Hebrew are not to be taken into account:
* ''ħ'' and ''x'' merge into ''x'';
* ''w'' and ''v'' merge into ''v'';
* ''q'' and ''k'' merge into ''k'';
* ''ğ'' and ''g'' merge into ''g'';
* ''ś'' and ''s'' merge into ''s'';
* ''ð'' and ''d'' merge into ''d'';
* ''θ'', ''ṭ'', and ''t'' merge into ''t'';
* ''ʿ'' and ''ʾ'' merge into a glottal stop (ʾ);
* emphatic ''ṣ'' becomes a non-emphatic affricate ''ts''.

