==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|stĭk|a=RP,GA}}, {{IPA|en|/stɪk/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-stick.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{homophones|en|stich}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪk|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)teyg-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|stikke||stick, rod, twig}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sticca||rod, twig}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*stikkō}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*stikkô}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)teyg-|t=to pierce, prick, be sharp}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|Stikke||stick}}, {{cog|vls|stik||stick}}. Related to {{m|en|stigma}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}
[[Image:Lichen stick.jpg|right|thumb|Fungi growing on a '''stick''' of wood]]
[[Image:Une canne de marchand Châlus.jpg|right|thumb|A typical walking stick or cane]]

# An elongated piece of wood or similar material, typically put to some use, for example as a [[wand]] or [[baton]].
## A small, thin [[branch]] from a [[tree]] or [[bush]]; a [[twig]]; a [[branch]].
##: {{syn|en|branch|twig|rice|q3=dialectal|kindling|brush|q5=uncountable}}
##: {{ux|en|The beaver's dam was made out of '''sticks'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=w:Henry Petroski
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/4/geothermal-energy Geothermal Energy]
|volume=101|issue=4|magazine=w:American Scientist
|passage=Energy has seldom been found where we need it when we want it. Ancient nomads, wishing to ward off the evening chill and enjoy a meal around a campfire, had to collect wood and then spend time and effort coaxing the heat of friction out from between '''sticks''' to kindle a flame.}}
## A relatively long, thin piece of [[wood]], of any size.
##: {{ux|en|I found several good '''sticks''' in the brush heap.}}
##: {{ux|en|What do you call a boomerang that won't come back? A '''stick'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=1887 August 23|journal=The New York Times|title=[http://query.nytimes.com/mem/archive-free/pdf?res=9F06E1DF1530E633A25750C2A96E9C94669FD7CF At Work on the Thistle]|passage=It is a fine '''stick''', about 70 feet long.}}
## {{lb|en|US}} A [[timber]] [[board]], especially a [[two by four]] (inches).
##: {{syn|en|two by four}}
##: {{ux|en|I found enough '''sticks''' in dumpsters at construction sites to build my shed.}}
## A [[cane]] or [[walking stick]] (usually wooden, metal or plastic)<!--i.e., not necessarily wooden--> to aid in [[walk]]ing.
##: {{syn|en|cane|walking stick}}
##: {{ux|en|I don’t need my '''stick''' to walk, but it’s helpful.}}
##* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=23|passage=The slightest effort made the patient cough. He would stand leaning on a '''stick''' and holding a hand to his side, and when the paroxysm had passed it left him shaking.}}
## A [[cudgel]] or [[truncheon]] (usually of wood, metal or plastic)<!--i.e., not necessarily wooden-->, especially one carried by police or guards.
##: {{ux|en|As soon as the fight started, the guards came in swinging their '''sticks'''.}}
## {{lb|en|carpentry}} The vertical member of a [[cope and stick|cope-and-stick]] joint.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Joseph Beals|chapter=Building Interior Doors|publisher=Taunton Press|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=pnqqL6c3Zp4C Doors]|page=82|isbn=1561582042
|passage=When cutting the door parts, I cut all the copes first, then the '''sticks'''.}}
## {{senseid|en|nautical}}{{lb|en|nautical}} A [[mast]] or part of a mast of a ship; ''also'', a {{l|en|yard|id=nautical}}.
## {{lb|en|figuratively}} A piece (of [[furniture]], especially if wooden).
##: {{syn|en|piece|item}}
##: {{ux|en|We were so poor we didn't have one '''stick''' of furniture.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1862|author=w:W.M. Thackeray|edition=printed in ''Harper's New Monthly Magazine'' volume XXV|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=M3MCAAAAIAAJ The Adventures of Philip]|page=242
|passage=It is more than poor Philip is worth, with all his savings and his little '''sticks''' of furniture.}}
# Any roughly cylindrical (or rectangular) unit of a substance.
#: {{ux|en|Sealing wax is available as a cylindrical or rectangular '''stick'''.}}
## [[Image:NCI butter.jpg|right|thumb|a '''stick''' of butter]]{{lb|en|chiefly|North America}} A small rectangular block, with a length several times its width, which contains by volume one half of a cup of [[shortening]] ([[butter]], [[margarine]] or [[lard]]).
##: {{ux|en|The recipe calls for half a '''stick''' of butter.}}
## [[Image:Chewing gum stick.jpg|right|thumb|a '''stick''' of gum]]A standard rectangular strip of chewing gum.
##: {{ux|en|Don’t hog all that gum, give me a '''stick'''!}}
## {{lb|en|slang}} A cigarette {{qualifier|usually a [[tobacco]] cigarette, less often a [[marijuana]] cigarette}}.
##: {{syn|en|joint|reefer}}
##: {{ux|en|Cigarettes are taxed at one dollar per '''stick'''.}}
# Material or objects attached to a stick or the like.
## A [[bunch]] of something [[wrap]]ped around or attached to a stick.
##: {{ux|en|My parents bought us each a '''stick''' of cotton candy.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} A scroll that is rolled around (mounted on, attached to) a stick.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1611|title=The Bible|edition=[[w:Authorized King James Version|King James Version]]|section={{w|Book of Ezekiel|Ezekiel}} 37:16
|passage=Moreover, thou son of man, take thee one '''stick''', and write upon it{{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|military}} The structure to which a set of [[bomb]]s in a [[bomber]] aircraft are attached and which drops the bombs when it is released. The bombs themselves and, by extension, any load of similar items dropped in quick [[succession]] such as [[paratrooper]]s or containers.
##: {{syn|en|train}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Raymond Mitchell|title=Commando Despatch Rider|page=70|isbn=085052797X|passage=Scores of transport planes streamed in to drop '''stick''' after '''stick''' of containers until the entire sky over the coast was polka-dotted with brightly coloured parachutes.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=w:Farley Mowat|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=of4lqiSlYg8C Aftermath: Travels in a Post-War World]|isbn=0811733386|page=200
|passage=A '''stick''' of bombs fell straight across Wotton; blew up half a dozen houses.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Holly Aho|page=48|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=zRSh5jdFpkUC From Here to There]|isbn=1411675401
|passage=James and I were in the same '''stick''' of five guys going through free fall school last September.}}
# A tool, control, or instrument shaped somewhat like a stick.
## {{lb|en|US|colloquial}} A [[manual transmission]], a vehicle equipped with a manual transmission, {{non-gloss definition|so called because of the stick-like, i.e. twig-like, control (the gear shift) with which the driver of such a vehicle controls its transmission}}.
##: {{syn|en|stickshift|gearstick}}
##: {{ux|en|I grew up driving a '''stick''', but many people my age didn't.}}
### [[File:Shift stick.jpg|right|thumb|the '''stick'''-shift lever in a manual transmission car]]{{lb|en|US|colloquial|uncountable}} Vehicles, collectively, equipped with manual transmissions.
###: {{ux|en|I grew up driving '''stick''', but many people my age didn't.}}
## {{lb|en|aviation}} The control column of an [[aircraft]]; a [[joystick]]. {{qualifier|By convention, a wheel-like control mechanism with a handgrip on opposite sides, similar to the steering wheel of an automobile, can also be called the "stick", although "[[yoke]]" or "[[control wheel]]" is more commonly seen.}}
## {{lb|en|aviation|uncountable}} Use of the stick to control the aircraft.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1941|author=Jay D. Blaufox|title=33 Lessons in Flying|page=47
|passage=For example: in making a turn, should you throw on too much '''stick''' and not enough rudder, you'll sideslip.}}
## {{lb|en|US|_|military slang|WWI}} An [[aircraft]]’s [[propeller]].<!--{{R:en:Lighter:1972|page=107}}--->
## {{lb|en|video games}} A [[joystick]].
## {{lb|en|computing}} A [[memory stick]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2007 May 1|author=Alex Fethiere|journal=Tech front: Alex Fethiere takes eleven notable portables for a high-tech test-drive|title=[http://www.accessmylibrary.com/coms2/summary_0286-33219686_ITM Business Traveler]|passage=For ultimate presentation portability, a Powerpoint can be saved to a '''stick''' as images.}}
## {{lb|en|slang}} A [[handgun]].
##* {{quote-song|en|date=2007-09-18|artist=w:Dropkick Murphys|lyricist=Joseph B. Geoghegan|album=The Meanest of Times|title=Johnny, I Hardly Knew Ya|url= https://genius.com/Dropkick-murphys-johnny-i-hardly-knew-ya-lyrics|passage=A '''stick''' in the hand, a drop in the eye.}}
##* {{quote-song|en|date=2013-04-08|artist=w:Yungen|album=Topic of Discussion|title=Off the Record|url=https://genius.com/Yungen-off-the-record-lyrics|passage=You think you’re real now you’ve got a couple '''sticks'''? My favourite younger, he got stabbed in my bits. And if he died fam, I promise I’d have quit.}}
##* {{quote-song|en|date=2018-07-27<!--ex instagram.com/p/BluI61ED1lD/ contra SC 2019-09-30-->|note=1:52|lyricist=Marty Mula|title=Strip Talk|url=https://soundcloud.com/martymula/strip-talk|passage=Bitch, pig, pull out with the '''stick''' / everything I hit like a lick/ We don’t miss}}
## {{lb|en|dated|metal typesetting}} A [[composing stick]], the tool used by [[compositor]]s to assemble lines of type.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1854|author=Thomas Ford|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=qJIDAAAAQAAJ The Compositor's Handbook]|page=125
|passage={{...}}although the headings may often be in other type, still, as these are composed in the same '''stick''', they cannot fail to justify;{{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|jazz|slang}} The [[clarinet]].
##: {{syn|en|licorice stick|liquorice stick}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1948|author=[[w:Fred Ramsey|Frederic Ramsey, Jr.]]|title=Jazz Parody: Anthology of Jazz Fiction|chapter=Deep Sea Rider|editor=Charles Harvey
|passage=Arsene, boy, ain't you worried about your clarinet? Where'd you leave that '''stick''', man?}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} A stick-like item:
## [[Image:Hockey stick.svg|right|thumb|two hockey '''sticks''', for the goalie at right]][[Image:Womens lacrosse stick head 2.jpg|right|thumb|a lacrosse '''stick''']]{{lb|en|sports|generically}} A long thin implement used to control a ball or [[puck]] in sports like [[hockey]], [[polo]], and [[lacrosse]].
##: {{ux|en|Tripping with the '''stick''' is a violation of the rules.}}
## {{lb|en|horse racing}} The short [[whip]] carried by a [[jockey]].
## {{lb|en|boardsports}} A [[board]] as used in board sports, such as a [[surfboard]], [[snowboard]], or [[skateboard]].<!--
##* '''2005''', surf.co.nz [http://www.surf.co.nz/school/],
##*: Wax your '''stick''' and head down to that spot.-->
## {{lb|en|golf}} The [[pole]] bearing a small flag that marks the hole.
##: {{syn|en|pin|flagstick}}
##: {{ux|en|His wedge shot bounced off the '''stick''' and went in the hole.}}
## {{lb|en|US|slang|uncountable}} The [[cue]] used in [[billiards]], [[pool]], [[snooker]], etc.
##: {{ux|en|His [[stroke]] with that two-piece '''stick''' is a good as anybody's in the club.}}
### The game of [[pool]], or an individual pool game.
###: {{ux|en|He shoots a mean '''stick''' of pool.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Lew Bryson|page=74|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=Slbz8kE-QfoC New York Breweries]|isbn=081172817X|passage=Come in, have a good time, drink some beer, shoot some '''stick''', listen to some music.}}
# {{lb|en|sports|uncountable}} Ability; specifically:
## {{lb|en|golf}} The long-range driving ability of a [[golf club]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1988|author=William Hallberg|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=m7RaAAAAMAAJ The Rub of the Green]|page=219|passage=I doubted that the three iron was enough '''stick'''.}}
## {{lb|en|baseball}} The potential hitting power of a specific [[bat]].
## {{lb|en|baseball}} General hitting ability.
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2002 May 19|author=Mike Lupica|journal=New York Daily News|title=[http://www.nydailynews.com/archives/sports/2002/05/19/2002-05-19_just_need_a_little_mo.html Just Need A Little Mo]|passage=Vaughn has to hit and keep hitting or this will be another year when the Mets don't have enough '''stick''' to win.}}
## {{lb|en|hockey}} The potential accuracy of a hockey stick, implicating also the player using it.
# {{lb|en|slang|dated}} A [[person]] or group of people. {{non-gloss definition|(Perhaps, in some senses, because people are, broadly speaking, tall and thin, like pieces of wood.)}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1967|author=w:Maurice Shadbolt|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=EDU_AAAAIAAJ The Presence of Music: Three Novellas]|page=54
|passage=Your father's a great old '''stick'''. He's really been very good to me.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1923|author={{w|Lucy Maud Montgomery}}|title=Emily of New Moon|chapter=[[s:Emily of New Moon/Chapter 7|Chapter 7]]
|passage=“{{...}}He’s a good doctor but an odd '''stick'''—odder by far than I am, Emily, and yet nobody ever says he’s not all there. Can you account for that? He doesn’t believe in God—and I am not such a fool as that.”}}
## A [[thin]] or [[wiry]] person; particularly a [[flat-chested]] woman.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1967|author=Cecelia Holland|page=39|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=WuZCAAAAIAAJ Rakóssy]
|passage="She's a '''stick''', this one. She lacks your—" he patted her left breast— "equipment."}}
## {{lb|en|magic}} An assistant [[plant#Verb|plant]]ed in the audience.
##: {{syn|en|plant|shill}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Paul Quarrington|page=255|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=bfk-nU-bJkgC The Spirit Cabinet]
|passage=The kid was a '''stick''', a plant, a student from UNLV who picked up a few bucks nightly by saying the words "seven of hearts."}}
## {{lb|en|gambling}} A [[shill]] or house player.
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Howard Fast|title=Helen
|passage=A shill is also called a '''stick''', and the role of the shill or '''stick''' is to make the customer relax and feel at ease.}}
## A stiff, stupidly obstinate person.
## {{lb|en|military|_|aviation|from [[joystick]]}} A [[fighter]] pilot.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=John Darrell Sherwood|page=30|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=agQyH1y_4q4C&isbn=0312979622 Fast Movers: America's Jet Pilots and the Vietnam Experience]
|passage=Bill Kirk, described by Robin as a "hell of a '''stick'''," didn't even attend college until after the Vietnam War.}}
## {{lb|en|military|South Africa}} A small group of (infantry) [[soldier]]s.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Bart Wolffe|page=245|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=5EanTB6yyI8C Persona Non Grata]|isbn=1430304774
|passage=I remember when we dreaded the rain, as our '''stick''' of soldiers walked through the damp, tick-infested long grass of the Zambezi valley,{{...}}}}
# {{senseid|en|punishment}} Encouragement or punishment, or (resulting) vigour or other improved behavior.
## {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[negative]] stimulus or a [[punishment]]. {{non-gloss definition|(This sense derives from the metaphor of using a stick, a long piece of wood, to poke or beat a beast of burden to compel it to move forward.)}}
##: {{cot|en|carrot<id:incentive>}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=1998 January 23|journal=Indian Express|title=[http://www.indianexpress.com/res/web/pIe/ie/daily/19980123/02350944.html Judicial activism has ushered in hope]|passage=What about contempt? Isn't it used by the judiciary as a '''stick''' to dissuade people from writing or talking about them?}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul Krugman|title=Climate Is Now a Culture War Issue|date=2023-08-07|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/08/07/opinion/climate-is-now-a-culture-war-issue.html|work=The New York Times|passage=Back in 2009, when Democrats tried but failed to take significant climate action, their policy proposals consisted mainly of '''sticks'''—limits on emissions in the form of permits that businesses could buy and sell.}}
## {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} [[corporal punishment|Corporal punishment]]; [[beating]]s.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Eve McDougall|page=69|isbn=190155709X|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=8c26i6lLqsQC A Wicked Fist]
|passage=The child killers got some '''stick'''. I saw a woman throw a basin of scalding water over a baby killer.}}
## {{lb|en|slang}} [[vigor|Vigor]]; [[spirit]]; effort, energy, intensity.
##: {{ux|en|He really gave that digging some '''stick'''.|He threw himself into the task of digging.}}
##: {{ux|en|She really gave that bully some '''stick'''.|She berated him.|footer=This sense melts into the previous sense, "punishment".}}
##: {{ux|en|Give it some '''stick'''!}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1979|author=Don Bannister|page=185|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=jKw9AAAAIAAJ Sam Chard]|isbn=071000219X
|passage='Choir gave it some '''stick''' on "Unto Us a Son is Born."' ¶ Cynthia nodded. ¶ 'It was always one of Russell's favourites. He makes them try hard on that.'}}
## {{lb|en|slang}} Vigorous [[drive|driving]] of a car; [[gas]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Martyn J. Pass; Dani Pass|page=163|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=CaGJb0A_alsC Waiting for Red]|isbn=1905237553
|passage=Skunk really gave it some '''stick''' all the way to Caliban's place, we passed a good few Coppers but they all seemed to turn the blind eye.}}
# A measure.
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} An English Imperial unit of length equal to 2 [[inch]]es.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1921|author=w:Elmer Davis|page=61|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=bws3AAAAIAAJ History of the New York Times, 1851-1921]
|passage=There was another speech in that day's news — a speech which The Times printed on the front page because it was part of a front-page story, and in full — it was only two '''sticks''' long; printed in full just after the much longer invocation by the officiating clergyman {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|archaic|rare}} A quantity of [[eel]]s, usually 25.
##: {{syn|en|stich|broach}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1866|author=James Edwin Thorold Rogers|section=Volume 1|page=171|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=ZmNaZfbq5tIC A History of Agriculture and Prices in England]|isbn=140217120X
|passage=The '''stick''' is employed for eels, and contained twenty-five.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Claire Breay|page=62|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=RRtWubS24-YC The Cartulary of Chatteris Abbey]|isbn=0851157505
|passage=In the same charter, Nigel granted another 10 '''sticks''' of eels yielded by the fishery of Polwere to the abbey{{...}}}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{sense|furniture}} Generally used in the negative, or in contexts expressive of poverty or lack.

