====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|carpentry}} To cut a piece of wood to be the stick member of a [[cope-and-stick]] joint.
# {{lb|en|transitive|printing|slang|dated}} To compose; to set, or arrange, in a [[composing stick]].
#: ''to '''stick''' type''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To furnish or set with sticks.
# To hit with a stick.

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|stiken|t=to stick, pierce, stab, remain embedded, be fastened}}, from {{inh|en|ang|stician|t=to pierce, stab, remain embedded, be fastened}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*stikōną|t=to pierce, prick, be sharp}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)tig-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*(s)teyg-|t=to pierce, prick, be sharp}}.

See also the related {{cog|gem-pro|*stikaną}}, whence {{cog|fy|stekke}}, {{cog|nds|steken}}, {{cog|nl|steken}}, {{cog|de|stechen}}; compare also {{cog|da|stikke}}, {{cog|sv|sticka}}.

Cognate with the first etymology (same PIE root, different paths through Germanic and Old English), to {{m|en|stitch}}, and to {{m|en|etiquette}}, via French {{m|fr|étiquette}} – see there for further discussion.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The tendency to stick (remain stuck), [[stickiness]].
## {{lb|en|motor racing}} The [[traction]] of tires on the road surface.
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} That which sticks (remains attached to another surface).
## {{lb|en|fishing}} The amount of fishing line resting on the water surface before a [[cast]]; [[line stick]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Simon Gawesworth|title=Spey Casting|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=mTasJXNIBsgC|isbn=0811701042|page=47
|passage=Problem: A lot of '''stick''' and a lack of energy on the forward stroke.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[thrust]] with a pointed instrument; a [[stab]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=21 Apr 90|journal=Gay Community News|page=11|author=Michael Bronski|title=Innocent Victims|text=What if Veronica Prego was lying about who drew the blood and it was her own carelessness, not Joyce Fogel's, which caused the needle '''stick'''?}}

=====Usage notes=====
The uncountable senses tend to have limited or niche uses, such as to quantify the amount of ''stick'' on a given tool.

=====Derived terms=====
{{col-auto|en|stick clip|stick-slip}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|sticks|sticking|stuck|past2=sticked|past2_qual=archaic}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become or remain [[attach]]ed; to [[adhere]].
#* {{RQ:Holinshed Chronicles|volume=I|book=Ireland|chapter=The Thirde Booke of the Historie of Ireland, Comprising the Raigne of [[w:Henry VIII of England|Henry the Eyght]]: [...]|pages=77–78|pageref=77|column=2|passage=The Citizens in their rage, imagining that euery poſt in the Churche had bin one of y{{sup|e}} Souldyers, ſhot habbe or nabbe at randon{{sic|random}} uppe to the Roode lofte, and to the Chancell, leauing ſome of theyr arrowes '''ſticking''' in the Images.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Psalms|119|31|passage=I haue stucke vnto thy Testimonies: O Lord put me not to shame.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-14|author=w:Jonathan Freedland|volume=189|issue=1|page=18|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/2013/jun/07/obama-apple-google-facebook-tainted-prism Obama's once hip brand is now tainted]|passage=Where we once sent love letters in a sealed envelope, or '''stuck''' photographs of our children in a family album, now such private material is despatched to servers and clouds operated by people we don't know and will never meet.}}
#: {{ux|en|The tape will not '''stick''' if it melts.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[jam]]; to stop moving.
#: {{ux|en|The lever '''sticks''' if you push it too far up.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[tolerate]], to [[endure]], to [[stick with]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Patrick McEvoy|title=Educating the Future GP: the course organizer's handbook|page=7|passage=Why do most course organizers '''stick''' the job for less than five years?}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[persist]].
#: {{ux|en|His old nickname '''stuck'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 10, 2011|author=David Ornstein quoting {{w|David Moyes}}
|work=BBC Sport
|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/16015017.stm Arsenal 1-0 Everton]
|passage="Our team did brilliantly to be in the game. We '''stuck''' at it and did a good job. This is disappointing but we'll think about the next game tomorrow."}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of [[snow]], to remain [[frozen]] on [[land]]ing.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[remain]] [[loyal]]; to remain [[firm]].
#* {{RQ:Plutarch North Lives|chapter=Agis and Cleomenes|page=852|passage=After this contencion, the common people did '''ſticke''' vnto king ''Agis'', and the riche men followed ''Leonidas'', praying and perſwading him not to forſake them: and further, they did ſo intreate the Senators, in whom conſiſteth the chiefe authority,{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Smotherhood: Wickedly Funny Confessions from the Early Years|author=Amanda Lamb|year=2007|passage=What I get from work makes me a better mother, and what I get from being a mother makes me a better journalist. At least that's my story and I'm '''sticking''' to it.}}
#: {{ux|en|Just '''stick''' to your strategy, and you will win.}}
# {{lb|en|dated|intransitive}} To [[hesitate]], to be reluctant; to [[refuse]] (in negative phrases).
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|10|text=For thou art so possess’d with murderous hate<br>That ’gainst thyself thou '''stick’st''' not to conspire.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1712|author=w:John Arbuthnot|title=Law is a Bottomless Pit|location=London|publisher=John Morphew|chapter=1|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004771083.0001.000
|passage=Some '''stick''' not to say, that the Parson and Attorney forg’d a Will, for which they were well Paid {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Browne Christian Morals}}, 2nd edition edited by {{w|Samuel Johnson}}, London: J. Payne, 1756, Part I, p. 12,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/christianmorals01browgoog]</sup>
#*: Though a cup of cold water from some hand may not be without its reward, yet '''stick''' not thou for wine and oil for the wounds of the distressed {{...}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1740|author=James Blair|title=Our Saviour's divine sermon on the mount [...] explained|volume=3|page=26
|passage=And so careful were they to put off the Honour of great Actions from themselves, and to centre it upon God, that they '''stuck''' not sometimes to depreciate themselves that they might more effectually honour him.}}
#* {{RQ:Richardson Pamela|3|letter=37|page=375|text=For he that '''sticks''' not at ''one'' bad Action, will not scruple ''another'' to vindicate himself: And so, Devil-like, become the Tempter, and the Accuser too!}}
#* '''1743''', Thomas Stackhouse, ''A Compleat Body of Speculative and Practical Divinity'', edition 3 (London), page 524:
#*: The ''First-fruits'' were a common Oblation to their Deities; but the chief Part of their Worship consisted in ''sacrificiing'' Animals : And this they did out of a real Persuasion, that their Gods were pleased with their Blood, and were nourished with the ''Smoke'', and Nidor of them; and therefore the more costly, they thought them the more acceptable, for which Reason, they '''stuck''' not sometimes to regale them with ''human'' Sacrifices.
# {{lb|en|dated|intransitive}} To be [[puzzle]]d (''at'' something), have [[difficulty]] [[understand]]ing.
#* {{RQ:Locke Conduct|29–30|He that has to do with young scholars, especially in mathematics, may perceive how their minds open by degrees, and how it is exercise alone that opens them. Sometimes they will '''stick''' a long time at a part of a demonstration, not for want of perceiving the connection of two ideas; that, to one whose understanding is more exercised, is as visible as any thing can be.|section=6}}
# {{lb|en|dated|intransitive}} To cause difficulties, scruples, or hesitation.
#* '''1708''', {{w|Jonathan Swift}}, ''The Sentiments of a Church-of-England-Man, with respect to Religion and Government'', in ''The Works of Jonathan Swift'', 7th edition, Edinburgh: G. Hamilton et al., 1752, Volume I, Miscellanies in Prose, p. 73,<sup>[https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/001022853]</sup>
#*: {{...}} this is the Difficulty that seemeth chiefly to '''stick''' with the most reasonable of those, who, from a mere Scruple of Conscience, refuse to join with us upon the Revolution Principle {{...}}.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[attach]] with [[glue]] or as if by gluing.
#: {{ux|en|'''Stick''' the label on the jar.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To place, set down (quickly or carelessly).
#: {{ux|en|'''Stick''' your bag over there and come with me.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=8|passage=Afore we got to the shanty Colonel Applegate '''stuck''' his head out of the door. His temper had been getting raggeder all the time, and the sousing he got when he fell overboard had just about ripped what was left of it to ravellings.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To press (something with a sharp point) into something else.
#: {{ux|en|The balloon will pop when I '''stick''' this pin in it.}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''stick''' a needle into one's finger}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|Palamon and Arcite
|passage=The points of spears are '''stuck''' within the shield.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|now only in|_|dialects}} To [[stab]].
##* '''circa 1583''', John Jewel, in a sermon republished in '''1847''' in ''The Works of John Jewel, Bishop of Salisbury'', portion 2, page 969:
##*: In certain of their sacrifices they had a lamb, they '''sticked''' him, they killed him, and made sacrifice of him: this lamb was Christ the Son of God, he was killed, '''sticked''', and made a sweet-smelling sacrifice for our sins.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|III|1|text=Thou '''stickest''' a dagger in me: I shall never see my gold again: fourscore ducats at a sitting!  fourscore ducats!}}
##* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=I|page=10|passage={{...}} ſome of them attempted with Spears to '''ſtick''' me in the Sides; but, by good luck, I had on me a [[buff jerkin#Noun|Buff Jerkin]], which they could not pierce.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1809|title=Grafton's chronicle, or history of England|volume=2|page=135
|passage={{...}} would haue [=have] '''sticked''' him with a dagger {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Waverley|passage=It was a shame {{...}} to '''stick''' him under the other gentleman's arm while he was redding the fray.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1908|title=The Northeastern Reporter|volume=85|page=693
|passage=The defendant said he didn't shoot; "he '''sticked''' him with a knife."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To fix on a pointed instrument; to impale.
#: {{ux|en|to '''stick''' an apple on a fork}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To adorn or deck with things fastened on as by piercing.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|II|iv|passage=my shroud of white, '''stuck''' all with yew}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|gymnastics}} To perform (a landing) perfectly.
#: {{ux|en|Once again, the world champion '''sticks''' the dismount.}}
# {{lb|en|botany|transitive}} To [[propagate]] plants by [[cutting]]s.
#: {{ux|en|'''Stick''' cuttings from geraniums promptly.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|joinery}} To run or plane (mouldings) in a machine, in contradistinction to working them by hand. Such mouldings are said to be ''stuck''.
# {{lb|en|dated|transitive}} To bring to a halt; to [[stymie]]; to [[puzzle]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''stick''' somebody with a hard problem}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang|dated}} To impose upon; to compel to pay; sometimes, to [[cheat]].
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1996|artist=w:Lil' Kim|feat=Diddy|title=No Time|album=Hard Core|passage=Chillin' in a Benz with my amigos / Tryin' to '''stick''' a nigga for his pesos}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|US|slang}} To have sexual intercourse with.
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1996|author=w:Lil' Kim|title={{w|Not Tonight (song)|Not Tonight}}|passage=You ain't lickin' this, you ain't '''stickin'''' this}}
#* {{quote-song|en|lyricist=Jordan Houston; Darnell Carlton; Paul Beauregard; Premro Smith; Marlon Goodwin; David Brown; Willie Hutchinson|passage=You leave your girl around me; if she's bad she's gonna get '''stuck'''.|url=https://genius.com/Three-6-mafia-stay-fly-lyrics|title=Stay Fly|publisher=Sony BMG|year=2005|album=Most Known Unknown|artist=Three 6 Mafia (featuring Young Buck, 8 Ball, and MJG)}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|blackjack|chiefly|UK}} To [[stand pat]]: to cease taking any more cards and finalize one's hand.

=====Usage notes=====
In [[Early Modern English]], the past participles {{m|en|stucken}} and {{m|en|sticken}} are occasionally found; they are not known in the modern language, even as archaisms.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|to adhere}} {{l|en|cleave}}, {{l|en|cling}}; see also [[Thesaurus:adhere]]
* {{sense|to stop moving}} {{l|en|jam}}, {{l|en|stall}}; see also [[Thesaurus:stop]]
* {{sense|to tolerate}} {{l|en|live with}}, {{l|en|put up with}}; See also [[Thesaurus:tolerate]]
* {{sense|persist}} {{l|en|abide}}, {{l|en|carry on}}; see also [[Thesaurus:persevere]]
* {{sense|to remain loyal}} {{l|en|stand by}}, {{l|en|stick by}}
* {{sense|to hesitate}} {{l|en|falter}}, {{l|en|waver}}; see also [[Thesaurus:hesitate]]
* {{sense|to be puzzled at}} {{l|en|puzzle}}
* {{sense|to attach with glue}} {{l|en|agglutinate}}, {{l|en|conglutinate}}, {{l|en|glue}}, {{l|en|gum}}, {{l|en|paste}}
* {{sense|to place}} {{l|en|pop}}, {{l|en|set down}}
* {{sense|to press into with a sharp point}} {{l|en|pierce}}, {{l|en|prick}}, {{l|en|puncture}}
* {{sense|to fix on a pointed instrument}} {{l|en|fix}}, {{l|en|impale}}, {{l|en|stake}}, {{l|en|run through}}, {{l|en|transfix}}
* {{sense|to bring to a halt}} {{l|en|stump}}, {{l|en|thwart}}
* {{sense|to have sexual intercourse}} {{l|en|have sex}}; see also [[Thesaurus:copulate]]

