==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|tûrn|a=RP}}, {{IPA|en|/tɜːn/}}
* {{enPR|tûrn|a=GA}}, {{IPA|en|/tɜɹn/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-turn.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-turn.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{homophones|en|tern|tarn}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)n|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*terh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|turnen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|turnian}}, {{m|ang|tyrnan|t=to turn, rotate, revolve}} and {{der|en|fro|torner|t=to turn}}, both from {{der|en|la|tornāre|t=to round off, turn in a lathe}}, from {{m|la|tornus|t=lathe}}, from {{der|en|grc|τόρνος|t=turning-lathe: a tool used for making circles}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*terh₁-|t=to rub, rub by turning, turn, twist, bore}}. Cognate with {{cog|ang|þrāwan|t=to turn, twist, wind}}, whence {{cog|en|throw}}. Displaced native {{ncog|enm|wenden}} from {{ncog|ang|wendan}} (see {{m|en|wend}}), and {{ncog|enm|trenden}} from {{ncog|ang|trendan}} (see {{m|en|trend}}), among several other terms.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|past2=turnt|past2_qual=obsolete}}

# {{non-gloss definition|To make a non-linear physical movement.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of a body, person, etc, to move around an axis through itself.
##: {{ux|en|the Earth '''turns''';&emsp; '''turn''' on the spot}}
##* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=I
|passage="A fine man, that Dunwody, yonder," commented the young captain, as they parted, and as he '''turned''' to his prisoner. "We'll see him on in Washington some day. He is strengthening his forces now against Mr. Benton out there.{{nb...}}."}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To change the [[direction]] or [[orientation]] of, especially by rotation.
##: {{ux|en|'''Turn''' the knob clockwise.}}
##* {{RQ:Jefferies Amateur Poacher|chapter=The First Gun|pages=9–10|pageref=9|passage=It was not far from the house; but the ground sank into a depression there, and the ridge of it behind shut out everything except just the roof of the tallest hayrick. As one sat on the sward behind the elm, with the back '''turned''' on the rick and nothing in front but the tall elms and the oaks in the other hedge, it was quite easy to fancy it the verge of the prairie with the backwoods close by.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/lee-s-langston Lee S. Langston]|magazine=w:American Scientist| title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/4/the-adaptable-gas-turbine The Adaptable Gas Turbine]| passage=Turbines have been around for a long time—windmills and water wheels are early examples. The name comes from the Latin ''turbo'', meaning ''vortex'', and thus the defining property of a turbine is that a fluid or gas '''turns''' the blades of a rotor, which is attached to a shaft that can perform useful work.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To change one's direction of [[travel]].
##: {{ux|en|She '''turned''' right at the corner.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|II|12|I had occasion {{...}} to make a somewhat long business trip to Chicago, and on my return […] I found Farrar awaiting me in the railroad station. He smiled his wonted fraction by way of greeting, […], and finally leading me to his buggy, '''turned''' and drove out of town.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=I stumbled along through the young pines and huckleberry bushes. Pretty soon I struck into a sort of path that, I cal'lated, might lead to the road I was hunting for. It twisted and '''turned''', and, the first thing I knew, made a sudden bend around a bunch of bayberry scrub and opened out into a big clear space like a lawn.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To shape (something) symmetrically by rotating it against a [[stationary]] cutting [[tool]], as on a [[lathe]].
##: {{ux|en|She '''turned''' the table legs with care and precision.}}
## {{lb|en|by extension}} To give form to; to shape or mould; to adapt.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|act=V|scene=i|page=159|column=1|passage={{...}}the Poets pen '''turnes''' them to ſhapes,{{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Spectator|author=Addison|II|108|4 July 1711|85|{{...}}he was perfectly well '''turned''' for the occupations of trade and commerce.}}
##* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|book=VIII|page=145|line=148|text=His limbs how '''turn’d'''! how broad his ſhoulders ſpread!}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To position (something) by folding it, or using its folds.
##: {{ux|en|'''turn''' the bed covers;&emsp; '''turn''' the pages}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}} To navigate through a book or other printed material.
##: {{ux|en|'''turn''' to page twenty;&emsp; '''turn''' through the book}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[twist]] or [[sprain]].
##: {{ux|en|I fell off my bike and '''turned''' my ankle severely.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|cricket}} Of a bowler, to make (the [[ball]]) move [[sideways]] off the [[pitch]] when it [[bounce]]s.
## {{lb|en|intransitive|cricket}} Of a ball, to move sideways off the pitch when it bounces.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|or|transitive}} {{non-gloss definition|To change condition or attitude.}}
## {{lb|en|copulative}} To [[become]] {{gloss|begin to be}}.
##: {{synonyms|en|become|get}}
##: {{ux|en|The leaves '''turn''' brown in autumn. When I asked him for the money, he '''turned''' nasty.}}
##* {{RQ:Congreve Mourning Bride|page=39|passage=Heav'n has no Rage, like Love to Hatred '''turn'd''', / Nor Hell a Fury, like a Woman ſcorn'd.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Junius P. Rodriguez|title=Encyclopedia of Emancipation and Abolition in the Transatlantic World|passage=The former-slaves-'''turned'''-abolitionists Quobna Ottobah Cugoano and Olaudah Equiano were the chief organizers of the Sons of Africa.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=April 21, 2012|author=Jonathan Jurejko|work=BBC Sport| title=[http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/17711736 Newcastle 3-0 Stoke]| passage=The midfielder '''turned''' provider moments later, his exquisite reverse pass perfectly weighted for Cisse to race on to and slide past Stoke keeper Asmir Begovic.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To change the [[color]] of the [[leaf|leaves]] in the [[autumn]].
##: {{ux|en|The hillside behind our house isn't generally much to look at, but once all the trees '''turn''' it's gorgeous.}}
## To change fundamentally; to [[metamorphose]].
##: {{ux|en|Midas made everything '''turn''' to gold.&emsp; He '''turned''' into a monster every full moon.}}
##* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|Silverside|300|At her invitation he outlined for her the succeeding chapters with terse military accuracy; and what she liked best and best understood was avoidance of that false modesty which condescends, '''turning''' technicality into pabulum.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=w:Henry Petroski
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/4/geothermal-energy Geothermal Energy]| volume=101|issue=4|magazine=w:American Scientist| passage=Ancient nomads, wishing to ward off the evening chill and enjoy a meal around a campfire, had to collect wood and then spend time and effort coaxing the heat of friction out from between sticks to kindle a flame. With more settled people, animals were harnessed to capstans or caged in treadmills to '''turn''' grist into meal.}}
### {{lb|en|intransitive}} To sour or spoil; to go bad.
###: {{ux|en|This milk has '''turned'''; it smells awful.}}
### {{lb|en|transitive}} To make acid or sour; to ferment; to curdle.
###: {{ux|en|to '''turn''' cider or wine}}
### {{lb|en|transitive|fantasy}} To change (a person) into a [[vampire]], [[werewolf]], [[zombie]], etc.
###: {{ux|en|How long ago was he '''turned'''?}}
###* {{quote-text|en|year=2017|author=Michael J. Totten|title=Into the Wasteland: A Zombie Novel
|passage=His companions had '''turned''' him on purpose. Annie, bless her heart, was immune.}}
### {{lb|en|intransitive|fantasy}} To transform into a [[vampire]], [[werewolf]], [[zombie]], etc.
###: {{ux|en|Bruce Banner '''turns''' when he is angry: he becomes the Hulk, an incredibly powerful green monster.}}
### {{lb|en|transitive|slang|sometimes|offensive}} To change the sexual orientation or gender of another person, or otherwise awaken a sexual preference.
###* {{quote-book|en|date=2009-09-10|author=W. C. Harris|title=Queer Externalities: Hazardous Encounters in American Culture|publisher=State University of New York Press|isbn=9781438427676|page=154
|text=We may not be made gay or lesbian in the sense of being “'''turned'''” by some error in parenting or child rearing, but we are certainly biologically made and raised (most of us) by straights.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|date=2023-05-15|author=Eliot Borenstein|title=Marvel Comics in the 1970s: The World Inside Your Head|publisher=Cornell University Press|isbn=9781501767845|page=244
|text=An old homophobic fantasy has it that a gay man or lesbian can be “'''turned'''” by a fulfilling sexual encounter with someone of the opposite sex}}
## To reach a certain age.
##: {{ux|en|Charlie '''turns''' six on September 29.}}
## To hinge; to depend.
##: {{ux|en|The decision '''turns''' on a single fact.}}
##* {{RQ:Swift Conduct of the Allies|page=72|passage={{...}} Conditions of Peace certainly do '''turn''' upon Events of War.}}
## To rebel; to go against something formerly tolerated.
##: {{ux|en|The prisoners '''turned''' on the warden.}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Little Dorrit|passage=‘You little Fool!’ returned her sister, shaking her with the sharp pull she gave her arm. ‘Have you no spirit at all? But that’s just the way! You have no self-respect, you have no becoming pride, just as you allow yourself to be followed about by a contemptible little Chivery of a thing,’ with the scornfullest emphasis, ‘you would let your family be trodden on, and never '''turn'''.’}}
## To change personal condition.
### {{lb|en|professional wrestling}} To change personalities, such as from being a [[face]] (good guy) to [[heel]] (bad guy) or ''vice versa''.
### To become giddy; said of the head or brain.
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|act=IV|scene=vii|page=302|column=2|passage=Ile looke no more,&nbsp;/ Leaſt my braine '''turne''',{{...}}}}
### To sicken; to nauseate.
###: {{ux|en|The sight '''turned''' my stomach.}}
### To be nauseated; said of the stomach.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|reflexive}} To change one's course of action; to take a new approach.
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Acts|7|41–42|clxiij|verso=yes|And they made a calfe in thoſe dayes&nbsp;/ and offered ſacrifice vnto the ymage&nbsp;/ and reioyſed in the workes of theyr awne hondes.&nbsp;/ Then God '''turned''' hym ſilfe&nbsp;/ and gave them vp&nbsp;/{{nb...}}}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Exodus|32|12|page=172|columns=1–2|passage='''Turne''' from thy fierce wrath,{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|chapter=Of Simple Ideas of Reflection|section=1|page=51|passage=The Mind receiving the ''Ideas'', mentioned in the foregoing Chapter, from without, when it '''turns''' its view inward upon its ſelf,{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Vance Outsider|page=373|column=2|passage=Little disappointed, then, she '''turned''' attention to "Chat of the Social World," gossip which exercised potent fascination upon the girl's intelligence.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|usually with '''over'''}} To complete.
#: {{ux|en|They say they can '''turn''' the parts in two days.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make (money); [[turn a profit]].
#: {{ux|en|We '''turned''' a pretty penny with that little scheme.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|soccer}} Of a player, to go past an opposition player with the ball in one's [[control#Noun|control]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=May 5, 2012|author=Phil McNulty|work=BBC Sport| title=[http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/17878435 Chelsea 2-1 Liverpool]| passage= Liverpool introduced Carroll for Spearing and were rewarded after 64 minutes when he put them back in contention. Stewart Downing blocked Jose Bosingwa's attempted clearance, which fell into the path of Carroll. He '''turned''' John Terry superbly before firing high past Cech.}}
# To undergo the process of turning on a [[lathe]].
#: {{ux|en|Ivory '''turns''' well.}}
# {{lb|en|obstetrics}} To bring down the feet of a child in the womb, in order to facilitate delivery.
# {{lb|en|printing|dated}} To invert a type of the same thickness, as a temporary substitute for any sort which is exhausted.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[translate]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''turn''' the Iliad}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1735|author=w:Alexander Pope|title=The Prologue to the Satires
|passage=who '''turns''' a Persian tale for half a crown}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|role-playing games}} To magically or divinely [[repel]] [[undead]].

