===Etymology 2===
Partly from {{der|en|xno||*torn}}, from {{der|en|la|turnus}}, from {{der|en|grc|τόρνος}}, and partly an [[action noun]] from the verb {{m|en|turn}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}
[[File:Turn-roundturn-tworoundturns.jpg|right|thumb|A: Turn (16)<br />B: [[round turn|Round turn]]<br />C: Two [[round turn]]s]]

# A change of direction or orientation.
#: {{ux|en|Give the handle a '''turn''', then pull it.}}
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=A Lady in Company|text=With just the '''turn''' of a shoulder she indicated the water front, where{{...}}lay the good ship, ''Mount Vernon'', river packet, the black smoke already pouring from her stacks. In turn he smiled and also shrugged a shoulder.}}
# A movement of an object about its own axis in one direction that continues until the object returns to its initial orientation.
## {{senseid|en|geometry}} {{lb|en|geometry}} A unit of [[plane angle]] [[measurement]] based on this movement.
# A [[walk]] [[to and fro]].
#: {{ux|en|Let's take a '''turn''' in the garden.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|promenade}}
# A chance to use (something) shared in sequence with others.
#: {{ux|en|They took '''turns''' playing with the new toy.}}
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=A Lady in Company|text=With just the turn of a shoulder she indicated the water front, where{{...}}lay the good ship, ''Mount Vernon'', river packet, the black smoke already pouring from her stacks. In '''turn''' he smiled and also shrugged a shoulder.}}
# A spell of work, especially the time allotted to a person in a [[rota]] or [[schedule]].
#: {{ux|en|I cooked tonight, so it's your '''turn''' to do the dishes.}}
# One's chance to make a [[move]] in a game having two or more players.
# A [[figure]] in music, often denoted ~, consisting of the note above the one indicated, the note itself, the note below the one indicated, and the note itself again.
# The time required to [[complete]] a project.
#: {{ux|en|They quote a three-day '''turn''' on parts like those.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|turnaround}}
# A fit or a period of giddiness.
#: {{ux|en|I've had a funny '''turn'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Mrs. Brown and the Emperor of the French|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=brEOAAAAQAAJ&pg=RA1-PA17|journal=[[w:Fun (magazine)|Fun]]|location=London|publisher=Published (for the proprietors) by Thomas Baker|date=23 September 1865|page=17|oclc=752198897|passage=I'm sure I never shall forget the '''turn''' young {{smallcaps|Simmons}} gave me when he came in with that paper as he'd been and copied out of a winder thro' being in a west-end house, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Jekyll and Hyde|chapter=The Last Night|pages=78–79|pageref=79|passage='Then you must know as well as the rest of us that there was something queer about that gentleman—something that gave a man a '''turn'''—I don't know rightly how to say it, sir, beyond this: that you felt it in your marrow kind of cold and thin.'}}
# A change in temperament or circumstance.
#: {{ux|en|She took a '''turn''' for the worse.}}
# {{lb|en|cricket}} A sideways movement of the ball when it bounces (caused by rotation in flight).
# {{lb|en|poker}} The fourth [[communal]] card in [[Texas hold 'em]].
# {{lb|en|poker|obsolete}} The [[flop]] (the first three community cards) in [[Texas hold 'em]].<!--Should this be here??-->
# A deed done to another; an act of [[kindness]] or [[malice]].
#: {{ux|en|One good '''turn''' deserves another.}}
#: {{ux|en|I felt that the man was of a vindictive nature, and would do me an evil '''turn''' if he found the opportunity{{nb...}}.}}
# A single loop of a coil.
# {{lb|en|rope}} A pass behind or through an object.
# [[character|Character]]; [[personality]]; [[nature]].
#* {{RQ:Clarke His Natural Life|I|Typhus Fever|100|It was fortunate for his comfort, perhaps, that the man who had been chosen to accompany him was of a talkative '''turn''', for the prisoners insisted upon hearing the story of the explosion a dozen times over, and Rufus Dawes himself had been roused to give the name of the vessel with his own lips.}}
# {{lb|en|soccer}} An instance of going past an opposition player with the ball in one's control.
# {{lb|en|circus|theatre|especially|physical comedy}} A short skit, act, or routine.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|journal=Theatre Notebook|volume=14-16|page=122
|passage=Between the pieces were individual '''turns''', comic songs and dances.}}
# {{lb|en|printing|dated}} A type turned upside down to serve for another character that is not available.
# {{lb|en|UK|finance|historical}} The [[profit]] made by a [[stockjobber]], being the difference between the buying and selling prices.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1977|author=Michael Arthur Firth|title=Valuation of Shares and the Efficient-markets Theory|page=11
|passage=There are usually at least two jobbers who specialise in the leading stocks, and this acts to keep the jobber's '''turn''' to a reasonable amount {{...}}}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{s|change of direction or orientation}}
* {{s|movement about an axis returning to the original orientation}} 360° turn, [[complete]] [[rotation]], complete turn, full rotation, [[full]] turn
* {{s|single loop of a coil}} {{l|en|loop}}
* {{q|chance to use (something) shared in sequence with others}}: [[go]]
* {{q|one's chance to make a move in a game}}: [[go]], [[move]]
* {{s|figure in music}}
* {{s|time required to complete a project}}
* {{s|fit or period of giddiness}} {{l|en|dizziness}}, {{l|en|dizzy}} [[spell]], [[giddiness]]
* {{s|change in temperament or circumstance}} {{l|en|change}}, {{l|en|swing}}
* {{s|sideways movement of a cricket ball}}

