Page 25 داشتن: Skipped, no changes
Page 25 داشتن: -------- begin text --------
==Persian==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|fa|pal|sc=Phlv|tr=dʾštn'|ts=dāštan|t=to hold, to have}}, {{m|pal|sc=Phlv|tr=YHSNN-tn'|ts=dāštan}}, {{m|pal|𐫅𐫀𐫢𐫤𐫗|ts=dāštan}}, from {{inh|fa|peo|𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎫𐎡𐎹|ts=dārayatiy|gloss=to hold, have}}, from {{der|fa|ira-pro|*dāráyati}},<ref>{{R:ira:Cheung|page=58}}</ref> from {{der|fa|iir-pro|*dʰāráyati|t=to hold, carry; to bear, support}}, from {{der|fa|ine-pro|*dʰer-||to hold}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{fa-IPA|dāšˈtan}}
* {{audio|fa|Fa-داشتن.ogg|a=Iran}}
* {{audio|fa|Fa-دانستن.ogg|a=Iran}}

===Verb===
{{fa-regional|داشتن|داشتن|доштан}}
{{fa-verb|head=دَاشْتَن|tr=dâštan|prstem=دار|tr2=dâr}}

# to [[have]]; to [[possess]], to [[own]]
#: {{uxi|fa|چهار تا بچه '''داریم'''.|tr=čahâr tâ bačče '''dârim'''.|t=We have four children.}}
# to [[hold]]
# {{lb|fa|Iran|auxiliary|not in very literary language}} to be (doing something) {{datedef|from 1870s<ref name="1870s">{{cite-book|chapter=The grammaticalization of progressive aspect in Persian|title=The Grammaticalization of Tense, Aspect, Modality and Evidentiality|year=2017|publisher=De Gruyter Mouton|author=Shadi Davari|author2=Mehrdad Naghzguy-Kohan|pages=163-190|doi=10.1515/9783110519389-007}}</ref>}}
#: {{uxi|fa|'''دارم''' می‌روم.|tr='''dâram''' mi-ravam.|t=I am going.}}
#: {{uxi|fa|'''داشتی''' قدم می‌زدی.|tr='''dâšti''' qadam mi-zadi.|t=You were walking.}}

====Usage notes====
* Unlike most other verbs, the simple present imperfective form of {{m|fa||داشتن}} does not use the durative prefix {{m|fa|می|tr=mi-}}.
* {{m|fa||داشتن}} as the progressive auxiliary is normal in both speech and many text genres, but is still avoided in more conservative literary registers as a colloquialism. Its usage in this sense was first recorded in colloquial usage in the 1870s, having been entirely absent from Classical Persian.<ref name="1870s"></ref> Geographically, its utilisation is limited to Iran, and it is substituted for conjunct verb constructions from {{m|fa|ایستاده|tr=istâde|t=standing}} and {{m|fa|رفتن|tr=raftan|t=to go}} in Transoxiana and Afghanistan, respectively.<ref>{{cite-book|en|title=The Iranian Languages|chapter=Persian and Tajik|chapter_number=8|text=The development in the morphosyntax of these three varieties is the history of increasing differentiation, particularly in their verb systems through the development of new constructions. These include the progressive Aktionsart (...). Persian uses dāštan in its basic meaning 'keep, hold', where both auxiliary and dependent verb are finite. Tajik and Afghan use conjunct verb constructions with istoda 'standing' and raftan 'go', respectively: Pers. dārad ketāb mi-xān-ad, Taj. [kitob-ro xond-a] istoda ast, Afgh. [ketåb-ra xånd-a] mē-rav-ad 'he is reading the book'.|author=Gernot Windfuhr; John R. Perry|year=2009|page=534|url=https://theswissbay.ch/pdf/Books/Linguistics/Mega%20linguistics%20pack/Indo-European/Iranian/Persian%20and%20Tajik%20%28Windfuhr%20%26%20Perry%29.pdf|termlang=fa}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|en|title=Tajik Linguistics|chapter=Aspect in Tajik|chapter_number=4|text=Progressive auxiliary: Tajik ''istodan'' 'to stand', Dari ''raftan'' 'to go', Persian ''dāštan'' 'to have'. (...) What is interesting in the overall picture is that Tajik, Dari, and Persian have all employed an auxiliary for the progressive aspect, though from different sources. (...) This could have happened after the 16th century. (...) In the case of Dari, (...) the periphrastic progressive construction is a "recent" development. (...) The [following] examples represent this construction: ''raft-a mē-ravom'' 'I am going', ''mihmān-hā-rā xošāmadguyi kard-a mē-raft'' '(S)he was welcoming the guests'. (...) The periphrastic progressive construction of the Persian of Iran is a rather recent development, being recorded for the first time in the late 19th century from spoken varieties. Zhukovskij reports to have attested it in a popular folk song in the late 1870s.|author=Roohollah Mofidi;Negin Mohammadi Nafchi|editor=Shinji Ido;Behrooz Mahmoodi-Bakhtiari|year=2023|page=183-229|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=l7GiEAAAQBAJ&pg=PT252|termlang=fa}}</ref> For example, the present continuous sentence "He is reading the book" would be translated as follows (with Classical Persian transliterations):
:: Iranian: {{m|fa||او دارد کتاب را می‌خواند|tr=ō dārad kitāb rā mēxwānad}}
:: Dari: {{m|fa||او کتاب را خوانده می‌رود|tr=ō kitāb rā xwānda mērawad}}
:: Tajik: {{m|fa||او کتاب را خوانده ایستاده است|tr=ō kitāb rā xwānda īstāda ast}}
: Meanwhile, the past continuous sentence "He was reading the book" would be translated as follows:
:: Iranian: {{m|fa||او داشت کتاب را می‌خواند|tr=ō dāšt kitāb rā mēxwānd}}
:: Dari: {{m|fa||او کتاب را خوانده می‌رفت|tr=ō kitāb rā xwānda mēraft}}
:: Tajik: {{m|fa||او کتاب را خوانده ایستاده بود|tr=ō kitāb rā xwānda īstāda būd}}
* In medieval texts, the nuance of {{m|fa||داشتن}} is often closer to English "to hold; to keep", and possession is usually marked by {{m|fa|را|tr=râ|t=at|pos=the indirect object marker}} + {{m|fa|بودن|tr=budan|t=to be}}.

====Conjugation====
{{prs-conj/dāštan}}{{fa-conj/dâštan}}

====Derived terms====
{{compoundsee|fa|pos=verb}}

===References===
<references/>
-------- end text --------
