{{also|Seed}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|seed (disambiguation)|seed}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|sēd|a=RP,US}}, {{IPA|en|/siːd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-seed.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːd|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|cede|sede}}

===Etymology 1===
[[File:Sunflower seeds.JPG|thumb|right|Sunflower seeds (1).]]
{{root|en|ine-pro|*seh₁-|id=sow}}
From {{inh|en|enm|seed}}, {{m|enm|sede}}, {{m|enm|side}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sēd}}, {{m|ang|sǣd||seed, that which is sown}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*sād}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*sēdą}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*seh₁-||to sow, throw}}.

Cognate with {{cog|fy|sied||seed}}, {{cog|nl|zaad||seed}}, {{cog|nds|Saad||seed}}, {{cog|de|Saat||sowing; seed}}, {{cog|is|sæði||seed}}, {{cog|da|sæd||seed}}, {{cog|sv|säd||seed}}, {{cog|la|satiō||seeding, time of sowing, season}}. More at {{l|en|sow}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|sede||obsolete}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|botany}} A [[fertilize]]d and ripened [[ovule]], containing an [[embryo]]nic plant.
# {{senseid|en|fertilized grain}}{{lb|en|countable}} Any small seed-like [[fruit]].
#* {{RQ:Browne Hydriotaphia|chapter=III|page=136|passage=The exiguity and ſmallneſſe of ſome '''ſeeds''' extending to large productions is one of the magnalities of nature, ſomewhat illuſtrating the work of the Creation, and vaſt production from nothing.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=May-June| author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/david-van-tassel David Van Tassel]; [http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/lee-dehaan Lee DeHaan]| title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/3/wild-plants-to-the-rescue Wild Plants to the Rescue]| volume=101| issue=3| magazine=w:American Scientist| passage=Plant breeding is always a numbers game.{{...}}The wild species we use are rich in genetic variation,&nbsp;{{...}}. In addition, we are looking for rare alleles, so the more plants we try, the better. These rarities may be new mutations, or they can be existing ones that are neutral—or are even selected against—in a wild population. A good example is mutations that disrupt '''seed''' dispersal, leaving the '''seeds''' on the heads long after they are ripe.}}
#: {{ux|en|If you plant a '''seed''' in the spring, you may have a pleasant surprise in the autumn.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|agriculture}} Any propagative portion of a plant which may be [[sow]]n, such as true seeds, seed-like fruits, tubers, or bulbs.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Natural Magick|passage={{...}} for hungry birds have devoured '''ſeeds''', and having moiſtened and warmed them in their bellies, a little after have dunged in the forky twiſtes of Trees, and together with their dung excluded the '''ſeed''' whole which erſt they had ſwallowed: and ſometimes it brings forth there where they dung it, {{...}}|year=1669|author=John Baptiſta Porta|page=68|authorlink=Giambattista della Porta|chapter=V|volume_plain=The Third Book Of Natural Magick: {{...}}|url=https://www.google.com.au/books/edition/Natural_Magick_in_twenty_books_Wherein_a/WddkXA6n51UC?hl=en&gbpv=0}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|collective}} An amount of seeds that cannot be readily counted.
#: {{ux|en|The entire field was covered with geese eating the freshly sown '''seed'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[fragment]] of [[coral]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} {{cap|semen}}.
#: {{ux|en|A man must use his '''seed''' to start and raise a family.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Leviticus|15|16|text=And if any mans '''seede''' of copulation goe out from him, then hee shall wash all his flesh in water, and bee vncleane vntill the Euen.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|figurative}} A [[precursor]].
#: {{synonyms|en|germ}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''seed''' of an idea}}
#: {{ux|en|Which idea was the '''seed''' (idea)?}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} The initial state, condition or position of a changing, growing or developing process; the [[ultimate]] precursor in a defined chain of precursors.
## {{lb|en|sports}} The initial position of a competitor or team in a tournament. (seed position)
##: {{ux|en|The team with the best regular season record receives the top '''seed''' in the conference tournament.}}
## The competitor or team occupying a given seed. (seed position)
##: {{ux|en|The rookie was a surprising top '''seed'''.}}
## {{senseid|en|seed number in cryptography}} {{lb|en|cryptography}} The initialization state of a [[pseudorandom number generator]] or similar system. (seed number)
##: {{ux|en|If you use the same '''seed''' you will get exactly the same pattern of numbers.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|editors=Daniel J. Bernstein; Johannes Buchmann; Erik Dahmen|title=Post-Quantum Cryptography|publisher=Springer|isbn=9783540887027|page=44|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=VB598lO47NAC&pg=PA44&dq=%22seed%22+PRNG&hl=&cd=10&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22seed%22%20PRNG&f=false|text=As suggested in [3], space can be saved by using a deterministic pseudo random number generator (PRNG) and storing only the '''seed''' of that PRNG.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Jesse Stay; Thomas Stay; Jacob Cordeiro|title=Minecraft For Dummies|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|isbn=9781118968239|page=242|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jf_9BQAAQBAJ&pg=PA242&dq=%22seed%22+minecraft&hl=&cd=4&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22seed%22%20minecraft&f=false|text='''Seeds''' are Minecraft's way of generating terrain. Each biome you spawn in is determined by the '''seed'''. With a custom '''seed''', you can have a desert biome right next to a mesa biome.}}
## {{lb|en|online|_|marketing}} A commercial message in a creative format placed on relevant sites on the Internet. (seed idea or seed message)
##: {{ux|en|The latest '''seed''' has attracted a lot of users in our online community.}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} {{cap|offspring}}, [[descendants]], [[progeny]].
#: {{ux|en|the '''seed''' of Abraham}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=X|passage=Next him king Leyr in happie peace long raind, / But had no issue male him to succeed, / But three faire daughters, which were well vptraind, / In all that seemed fit for kingly '''seed''' {{...}}}}
# {{cap|race}}; [[generation]]; [[birth]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=a. 1687|author=w:Edmund Waller|title=To Zelinda
|passage=Of mortal '''seed''' they were not held.}}
# {{lb|en|physics}} A [[small]] [[particle]], [[bubble]], or [[imperfection]] that serves as a [[nucleation]] point for some process.
# A small [[bubble]] formed in imperfectly fused [[glass]].

=====Usage notes=====
{{sense|botany|agriculture}} The common use of seed differs from the botanical use. The “seeds” of sunflowers are botanically [[fruit]]s.

=====Hyponyms=====
* {{l|en|crack seed}}

