==English==
{{wikipedia|dan rank}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|en|title of honour}}
From {{inh|en|enm|dan}}, {{m|enm|daun}}, {{m|enm|dam|t=lord}}, from {{der|en|xno|daun}}, {{m|fro|daunz}} and {{der|en|fro|dan}}, {{m|fro|dam}}, from {{der|en|la|dominus}}. {{doublet|en|don}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/dæn/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-dan.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|æn|s=1}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|?}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} {{non-gloss definition|A title of honour or respect similar to "master" or "Sir", used of historical and legendary figures of the past.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1578|author=w:George Gascoigne|chapter=A Moonshine Banquet|title=A Hundred Sundry Flowers
|passage='''Dan''' Phoebus, he with many a low'ring look / Had her beheld in yore in angry wise.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=VI|canto=VII|passage=Old '''Dan''' Geoffrey, in whose gentle spright / The pure well-head of Poesy did dwell.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost|text=This senior-junior, giant-dwarf, '''dan''' Cupid.}}
#* {{RQ:Thomson Castle of Indolence|text=The patriarchal age, / What time '''Dan''' Abraham left the Chaldee land.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1777|author={{w|James Perry (journalist)|James Perry}}|title=The Electrical Eel; or, Gymnotus Electricus
|passage=He did—and in a moment press'd / The place—in Paradise the best, / As by '''Dan''' Moses said.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1842|author=w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|title=w:A Dream of Fair Women
|passage='''Dan''' Chaucer, the first warbler, whose sweet breath / Preluded those melodious bursts, that fill / The spacious times of great Elizabeth / With sounds that echo still.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1846|author=Terence McMahon Hughes|title=The Biliad
|passage='''Dan''' Neptune says that "ere a twelvemonth pass, / The Senate shall to Ireland go to grass."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1962|author=w:A. D. Hope|title=The Ballad of Dan Homer
|passage=Oh, me' name is '''Dan''' Homer, I'm blind, as the Jews, / And I travels around with my head full av news.}}

