==Arabic==

===Etymology 1===
A historically conflated term derived from multiple layers of phono-semantic matching:
* Initially stemming from {{der|ar|sem-pro|*dVn-|t=obligated conduct; decisions of morality or ethics, judgement, decree, ruling}}, where in Arabia it took specifically the foremost connotation of obligations, duty, what is expected or owed; hence also the sense of a monetary debt along with that which is owed to society ethically.
* Cognate terms in other Semitic languages also came with a more developed legal sense, rulings, a set of obligations, a law code; these senses were bolstered semantically borrowing from {{bor|ar|arc|דִּינָא|tr=dīnā}}, {{cog|syc|ܕܺܝܢܳܐ|tr=dīnā|t=judgement}}, {{cog|he|דִּין|tr=din}}, {{cog|gez|ደይን|t=judgement}}, which likely had their specified development from {{der|ar|akk|dīnum|𒁲|tr=dīnum|t=judgement; legal practice, precedent; legal case; court conduct, procedures, rule of law}}. Another possible layer still is the term {{cog|sux|𒁲|tr=di|ts=did|t=lawsuit, trial; legal decision}} which is a term of uncertain origins; potentially an early {{cog|akk|-}} borrowing, at least semantically, or natively derived from {{m|sux|𒁲|tr=di|t=to speak}}, a form of {{m|sux|𒅗|tr=dug₄|ts=dug|t=to speak, talk, say; to order, to negotiate}}.
* The sense of religious creed or a system of religious rules is borrowed from {{bor|ar|pal||tr=dyn'|ts=dēn|sc=Phlv}}, which developed from {{der|ar|peo|𐎭𐎠𐎡𐎴|ts=*daina-|t=a religious-informed or conscientious way of life}}, which underwent influence from {{cog|akk|-}} when the {{w|Achaemenid Empire}} incorporated its Semitic kingdoms. Developing also from {{der|ar|ae-yng|𐬛𐬉𐬥𐬁|tr=dēnā}}, a term already having a predecessor with a religious sense in {{der|ar|ae|𐬛𐬀𐬉𐬥𐬁}}, which is possibly akin to {{cog|sa|ध्यान}}, whence {{m|en|zen}}. This possibly derives in part under the influence of {{der|ar|elx|-|}}, developing from the {{cog|akk|-}} legal sense; religious obligation to deities, the system or conduct of the priesthood regarding divinities.
* The Qurʾān and later Islamic scholarship adds an additional layer of phono-semantic matching, melding the native significations with the connotations imparted by the languages of their neighbors, both from the Semitic family and those not.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|دِين}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|دِين|m|pl=أَدْيَان}}

# {{ar-verbal noun of|دَانَ|form=I|t=to be religious}}
# {{lb|ar|countable|uncountable}} [[religion]], [[creed]], [[credo]], [[faith]], [[conviction]], [[belief]], [[tenet]], [[rite]]
# {{lb|ar|uncountable|verbal noun}} [[conformism]], [[conformance]], [[conformity]], [[compliance]], [[fealty]], [[obedience]]; [[God-fearing]]ness, [[godliness]], [[religiosity]], [[devoutness]]
# [[law]], [[obligations]], [[duty]]
# [[custom]], [[habit]]
#: {{usex|ar|لَيْسَ هٰذَا مِنْ '''دِينِي''' وَلَا دَيْدَنِي|This is not according to my '''habit''' nor to my wont.}}
# [[judgement]], [[decision]], [[ruling]]
## {{lb|ar|rare}} [[requital]], [[compensation]], [[indemnification]]
## {{lb|ar|rare}} [[credit]], [[obligation]], [[account]], [[fall due|falling due]] of a [[debt]]

