==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fa|میرزا|tr=mirzâ}}, from earlier {{m|fa|میرزاد|tr=mirzâd}}, composed of {{m|fa|میر||tr=mir|commander}} (from {{uder|en|ar|-}} title {{m|ar|أَمِير||commander, prince}}) and the suffix {{m|fa|ـزاده|ـزاد، ـزاده|tr=-zâd, -zâde|child of, son of}}, from {{m|fa|زادن|tr=zâdan}}. Akin to {{m|en|Amir}}, {{m|en|amir}}, {{m|en|emir}} and {{m|en|admiral}}.
{{root|en|ar|ء م ر}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{non-gloss definition|A [[title]] denoting the rank of a [[high]] [[nobleman]] or [[prince]], signifying a male-line [[descent]] and relationship to the Imperial Families of [[Turkey]], [[Persia]] and later [[South Asia]], and was the title borne by members of the highest aristocracies in [[Tatar]] states, such as the [[khanate]]s of [[Kazan]] and [[Astrakhan]].}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-prop}}

# {{surname|en}}.

====Usage notes====
* Equivalent to {{m|en|prince}} when following the surname; a common title of respect, like {{m|en|Mr.}}, when preceding it.

====Translations====
{{trans-top|title denoting the rank of a high nobleman or prince}}
* Marathi: {{t|mr|मिर्झा}}
* Persian: {{t+|fa|میرزا|tr=mirzâ}}
* Turkish: {{t+|tr|mirza}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Further reading===
* {{pedia}}

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|en|a=aimrz|Mizar|ziram}}

[[Category:en:Titles]]
