==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|czar|tzar|csar}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|ru|царь}}, from {{der|en|orv|цьсарь}}, from {{der|en|sla-pro|*cěsařь}}, from a {{der|en|gem}} language, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*kaisaraz}}, from {{der|en|la|Caesar}}.<ref>Funk, W. J., ''Word origins and their romantic stories'', New York, Wilfred Funk, Inc.</ref> {{doublet|en|kaiser|Caesar}}.  The spelling ''tsar'' began to replace the older ''czar'' in the nineteenth century. Compare [[w:Medieval Greek|Byzantine Greek]] {{m|grc|Τζαῖσαρ}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/(t)sɑː/|/zɑː/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/(t)sɑɹ/|/zɑɹ/|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/zɐː/|/tsɐː/|a=Australia}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tsar.ogg|a=US}}
