* {{rhymes|en|ɑː(ɹ)|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|Saar}}

===Noun===
{{head|en|noun|plural|tsars}}
[[File:Mikola II.jpg|thumb|Tsar Nicholas II.]]

# {{lb|en|historical}} An [[emperor]] of Russia (1547 to 1917) and of some [[South Slavic]] states.
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=1832-08-01
|author=W. Barnes
|work=Gentleman's Magazine
|title=On the Origin of Language
|location=London
|url=https://archive.org/details/gentlemansmagaz67unkngoog/page/n147/mode/2up
|page=129
|passage=and why, in the name of common sense, should the English call the Czar ('''tsar''') of Russia ''raze''?
 }}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A person with great power; an [[autocrat]].
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=14 March 1969
|origdate=13 March 1969
|title=Report from Border Areas
|journal=Daily Report: Foreign Radio Broadcasts
|url=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=osu.32435063627855&seq=661
|issue=50
|publisher=w:Foreign Broadcast Information Service
|source=Peking NCNA International Service
|oclc=1541329
|page=A 6
|pageurl=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=osu.32435063627855&seq=670
|url2=https://books.google.com/books?id=n8zyL63oJxsC&pg=RA29-PA6
|text=Chin Chien-shih, an old poor peasant of Korean nationality, said: "The wolfish ambition of the new '''tsars''' is exactly the same as that of the old tsars. The Soviet revisionist renegade clique is struggling desperately in the hope of saving itself from doom, but this will only bring on its destruction more quickly."}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|emperor of Russia}} Officially, emperors after 1721 were styled {{m|en|imperator}} ({{m|ru|импера́тор}}) rather than {{m|en||tsar}} ({{m|ru|царь}}), but the latter term is still commonly applied to them.
* The term sometimes refers to other emperors, besides those of Russia, e.g. the monarch of Bulgaria (1908-1946).
* The spelling {{m|en|czar}} is predominant in figurative and informal senses. Scholarly literature prefers ''tsar''.

