==Russian==
{{wikipedia|lang=ru}}
[[File:Alexis I of Russia (1670-1680s, GIM).jpg|thumb|Царь Алексей Михайлович]]

===Etymology===
{{inh+|ru|orv|цьсарь}}, from {{m|orv|цѣсарь}}, from {{inh|ru|sla-pro|*cěsařь}}, from a {{der|ru|gem}} language, from {{der|ru|gem-pro|*kaisaraz}}, from {{der|ru|la|Caesar}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ru-IPA}}
* {{audio|ru|Ru-царь.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{ru-noun+|b||m|a=an|f=цари́ца|adj=ца́рский|dim=царёк}}

# [[tsar]], Russian [[emperor]]
# [[king]] (figuratively, or referring to ancient or non-European monarchs)
#: {{uxi|ru|'''царь''' [[зверь|звере́й]]|king of beasts}}
#: {{uxi|ru|'''царь''' [[и]] [[бог]]|God Almighty|subst=бог//бох}}
#: {{uxi|ru|'''царь''' [[небе́сный]]|Heavenly Father}}
#: {{uxi|ru|'''царь''' '''царе́й'''|King of kings}}
#: {{uxi|ru|[[при]] '''царе́''' Горо́хе|in the year dot; a very long time ago; since the beginning}}
#: {{uxi|ru|[[он|Он]] [[без]] царя́ [[в]] [[голова|голове́]]!|He’s stupid!}}

====Usage notes====
* From 1721 to 1917, Russia was officially an [[:w:Russian Empire|Empire]], and its monarchs referred to as {{m|ru|импера́тор}} (officially), {{m|ru|госуда́рь}}, or {{m|ru|самоде́ржец}}. For much of that time period, referring to the monarch (in his role as the ruler of Russia) as {{m|ru|царь}} would have been seen as a colloquialism. The Russian monarch's official list of titles over non-Russian territories did include e.g. {{m|ru|[[царь]] [[польский|По́льский]]}}, which in English was rendered as “King of Poland”.

