==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kʷ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|quantite}}, from {{der|en|fro|quantité}}, from {{der|en|la|quantitās||quantity}}, from {{m|la|quantus||how much}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkwɒn.tɪ.ti/|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|en-gb-quantity.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{enPR|kwŏnʹ(t)ĭtē|a=GA}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈkwɑn(t)ɪti/|[ˈkʰwɑn(ɾ)ɨɾi]|[ˈkʰwɑn(tʰ)ɨtʰi]}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-quantity2.ogg|a=US}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-quantity.ogg|a=US}} <small>Note: This is with a relaxed middle T, and is only used in colloquial contexts by many speakers.</small>
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkwɑndədi/|/ˈkwɑnɾəɾi/|a=Canada}}
** {{audio|en|en-ca-quantity.ogg|a=CA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkwæn.tɪ.ti/|/ˈkwæn.tɪ.tɪ/|a=obsolete}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}} {{abbreviated|en|qty}}

# A [[fundamental]], [[generic]] [[term]] used when referring to the [[measurement]] (count, amount) of a [[scalar]], [[vector]], number of items or to some other way of [[denominating]] the value of a collection or [[group]] of [[items]].
#: {{ux|en|You have to choose between '''quantity''' and quality.}}
# An indefinite amount of something.
#: ''Some soap making oils are best as base oils, used in a larger '''quantity''' in the soap, while other oils are best added in a small '''quantity'''.''
#: {{ux|en|Olive oil can be used practically in any '''quantity'''.}}
# A specific measured amount.
#: {{ux|en|This bag would normally cost $497.50 for a '''quantity''' of 250, at a price of $1.99 per piece.}}
#: {{ux|en|Generally it should not be used in a '''quantity''' larger than 15 percent.}}
# A considerable measure or amount.
#: {{ux|en|The Boeing P-26A was the first all-metal monoplane fighter produced in '''quantity''' for the U.S. Army Air Corps.}}
# {{lb|en|metrology}} Property of a phenomenon, body, or substance, where the property has a magnitude that can be expressed as number and a reference.
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} Indicates that the entire preceding expression is henceforth considered a single object.
#: {{ux|en|''x ''plus ''y'' '''quantity''' squared equals ''x'' squared plus ''2xy'' plus ''y'' squared.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Jerome E. Kaufmann; Karen Schwitters|title=Elementary and Intermediate Algebra: A Combined Approach|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?vid=ISBN0534490247&id=7RKsTaF92MgC&pg=PA89&lpg=PA89&dq=%22quantity+squared%22&sig=X7wk68CoTM4uHM4AIhpLKSYByRI#PPA90,M1|page=89
|passage=For problems 58-67, translate each word phrase into an algebraic expression. {{...}} '''65.''' x plus 9, the '''quantity''' squared}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=R. Mark Sirkin|title=Statistics For The Social Sciences|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?vid=ISBN141290546X&id=67vpZ5VOj14C&pg=PA137&lpg=PA137&dq=%22quantity+squared%22&sig=PRz6Zbd10l0FeIbF9Zqw5LL8gYU#PPA137,M1|page=137
|passage=The second, <math>(\sum x)^2</math>, read "summation of x, quantity squared," tells us to first add up all the xs to get <math>\sum x</math> and then square <math>\sum x</math> to get <math>(\sum x)^2</math>.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1985|author=Serge Lang|title=Math!: Encounters with High School Students|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?vid=ISBN0387961291&id=guSHaf5TJ70C&pg=PA54&lpg=PA54&ots=Oew4sy2FUZ&dq=%22quantity+cubed%22&sig=4ivw5_O0TYgnLs37-9-DKJTch0o|page=54
|passage='''ANN.''' <math>ra</math> quantity cubed.<br>'''SERGE LANG.''' That's right, <math>(ra)^3</math>.}}
# {{lb|en|phonology}} [[length|Length]] of [[sound]]s.

====Usage notes====
* In mathematics, used to unambiguously [[orate]] mathematical equations; it is extremely rare in print, since there is no need for it there.

