==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[Image:Coticule-Vielsalm-Opinel.jpg|thumb|right|A whetstone being used to sharpen a knife.]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|whestone}}, {{m|enm|whetston}}, {{m|enm|whetesston}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hwetstān}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hwattjastain|t=whetstone}}. Equivalent to {{compound|en|whet|t1=to sharpen|stone}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈwɛtˌstoʊn/|[ˈwɛʔˌstoʊn]|a=US}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈʍɛtˌstoʊn/|[ˈʍɛʔˌstoʊn]|a=US,non-wine-whine}}
* {{IPA|en|/wɛtstəʊn/|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|En_US_whetstone_merged.wav|a=US,wine-whine}}
** {{audio|en|En_US_whetstone_unmerged.wav|a=US,non-wine-whine}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[sharpen]]ing [[stone]]; a hard stone or piece of synthetically [[bonded]] hard minerals that has been formed with at least one flat surface, used to sharpen or [[hone]] an [[edged]] [[tool]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|I|ii|line=192|page=186|column=2|text={{...}}for alwaies the dulneſſe of the foole, is the '''whetstone''' of the wits.}}
#* {{RQ:Woolf Jacob's Room|passage=It was as if a stone were ground to dust; as if white sparks flew from a livid '''whetstone''', which was his spine; as if the switchback railway, having swooped to the depths, fell, fell, fell.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[stimulant]].
# {{lb|en|computing}} {{alt case form of|en|Whetstone|gloss=benchmark}}

