==Arabic==

===Etymology 1===
{{ar-rootbox|س ن و}}

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|I|a|u}}

# to [[irrigate]], to [[water]]
# to [[bring up]], to [[draw]] {{gloss|water}}
# to [[shine]], to [[luster]], to [[gleam]]

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|I|a|u|passive=yes|vn=سَنْو,سَنَاوَة}} <!-- Lane and Almaany سِنَاوَة while Freytag and Kazimirski سَنَاوَة which is called by Lane in col. 3 explicitly a mistranscription -->

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|I|a|u}}

# to [[shine]], to [[luster]], to [[gleam]]

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|I|a|u|passive=no|vn=سَنَاء}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|سَنًا|m}}

# [[shine]], [[luster]], [[gleam]]
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|24|43|passage=أَلَمْ تَرَ أَنَّ اللَٰهَ يُزْجِي سَحَابًا ثُمَّ يُؤَلِّفُ بَيْنَهُ ثُمَّ يَجْعَلُهُ رُكَامًا فَتَرَى الْوَدْقَ يَخْرُجُ مِنْ خِلَالِهِ وَيُنَزِّلُ مِنَ السَّمَاءِ مِن جِبَالٍ فِيهَا مِن بَرَدٍ فَيُصِيبُ بِهِ مَن يَشَاءُ وَيَصْرِفُهُ عَن مَّن يَشَاءُ{{nbsp}}ۖ يَكَادُ '''سَنَا''' بَرْقِهِ يَذْهَبُ بِالْأَبْصَارِ|subst=يَشَاءُ{{nbsp}}ۖ يَ/يَشَاءُ يَ}}

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|سَنًا}}

===Etymology 2===
* From the root {{ar-root|س|ن|و}} meaning "to be splendid or bright", "to be more intense in color"; as the flower is often mixed with {{m|ar|حِنَّاء|tr=|t=henna}} to strengthen its shade; compare cognates {{cog|he|שָׁנִי|tr=shaní|t=scarlet, crimson}}, {{cog|uga|𐎘𐎐|tr=ṯn|t=dyed scarlet}}, {{cog|akk|𒅆𒉌𒌈|tr=šinītum|t=dyeing a textile}}.
* From {{inh|ar|sem-pro|*šinn-|t=tooth}} originally applied to thorn-bushes, but more generally to any bush or bramble; compare {{cog|syc|ܣܢܝܐ}} and {{cog|he|סנה}}.

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|سَنَا|m}}

# [[senna]]

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|سَنَا}}

===References===
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=2|page=367}}
* {{R:ar:Kazimirski|volume=1|page=1154}}
* {{R:ar:Lane|page=1448}}

