===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|interrogative}} In what way; to what extent.
#: '''''What''' does it matter?''
#: '''''What''' do you care?''
# {{n-g|Used before a prepositional phrase to emphasise that something is taken into consideration as a cause or reason; usually used in combination with 'with' (see [[what with]]), and much less commonly with other prepositions.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1787|author=Henry St. John; Lord Viscount Bolingbroke|title=Letters on the Study and Use of History: A Letter to Sir William Windham|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=ZtpkAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA83&dq=%22what+by%22|page=83
|passage=In short; '''what''' by the indiscretion of people here, '''what''' by the rebound which came often back from London, '''what''' by the private interests and ambitious views of persons in the French court, and '''what''' by other causes unnecessary to be examined now, the most private transactions came to light [...]}}
#* '''1815''', Rev. Mr. Milne, letter reprinted in ''The Evangelical Magazine and Missionary Chronicle, Volume 23'', page 82. [https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=EPE6AQAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA82&dq=%22what+from%22]
#*: The Chinese of all ranks, and in every place, received my books gladly, and listened with patience to what I had to say about the true God.—So that '''what''' from opportunities of attending to the object of my Mission among the Chinese—'''what''' from seasons of religious instruction to Dutch and English—'''what''' from intercourse with gentlemen of education and knowledge of the world—'''what''' from occasions of stating clearly the object of Missions, and of endeavouring to remove prejudices against them—and '''what''' from the view of a highly cultivated country, happy under an enlightened and liberal government, I have much reason to be satisfied with this journey [...]

====Translations====
{{trans-top|in what way}}
* Finnish: {{t|fi|[[mikä|mitä]]}}, {{t+|fi|miten}}
* German: {{t+|de|wie}}, {{t+|de|inwiefern}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{checktrans-top}} <!-- was "why", but this does not correspond to any English sense -->
* Hindi: {{t+|hi|क्यों}}
* Malay: {{t+|ms|kenapa}}
* Portuguese: {{t+|pt|por que}}
* Zazaki: {{t|zza|seni}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{checktrans-top}}
* Estonian: {{t+check|et|kui}}, {{t-check|et|milline}}, {{t-check|et|selline}} <!--[[taoline]]-->
* Indonesian: {{t+check|id|apa}}
* Italian: {{t+check|it|che}}
* Spanish: {{t+check|es|qué}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# {{n-g|An expression of surprise or disbelief.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|text='''What''', have his daughters brought him to this pass?}}
#: '''''What'''! [[that's|That’s]] [[amazing]]!''
# What do you want? {{non-gloss definition|An abrupt, usually unfriendly enquiry as to what a person desires.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''What'''? I'm busy.}}
# {{lb|en|British|colloquial|dated}} {{clipping of|en|what do you say|nodot=a}}? {{n-g|Used as a type of [[tag question]] to emphasise a statement and invite agreement, often rhetorically.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Denis Garstin|title=The Shilling Soldiers|year=1918|url=https://archive.org/stream/shillingsoldiers00gars|location=London|publisher=Hodder and Stoughton|page=83|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/shillingsoldiers00gars/page/83/mode/1up|text=“That’s riled them,” said my compaion. “Good work, '''what'''?”}}
#* {{quote-text|en|date=May 12 1991|chapter=[[w:List of Jeeves and Wooster episodes|Kidnapped!]]|title=w:Jeeves and Wooster|section=Series 2, Episode 5
|passage=''Chuffy:'' WHAT? No, no, no, no, no. My casa is your casa, '''what'''?}}
#: {{ux|en|It’s a nice day, '''what'''?}}
# What did you say? [[I beg your pardon]]? {{non-gloss definition|This usage is often considered impolite, with the more polite "[[pardon|Pardon]]?" or "[[excuse me|Excuse me]]?" preferred.}}
#: {{ux|en|— Could I have some of those aarrrrrr mmmm ...<br>— '''What'''?}}
# {{n-g|Indicating a guess or approximation, or a pause to try to recall information.}}
#: {{ux|en|I must have been, '''what''', about five years old.}}

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|wha}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|colloquial British interjection}} {{l|en|what-what}}, {{l|en|wot}}
* {{sense|what did you say?}} {{l|en|come again}}, {{l|en|pardon}}; see also [[Thesaurus:say again]]

