==Middle English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|enm|ang|hwæt}}, from {{inh|enm|gmw-pro|*hwat}}, from {{inh|enm|gem-pro|*hwat}}, from {{inh|enm|ine-pro|*kʷód}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|enm|whad|whote|watte|wath|wad|qhat}}
* {{alter|enm|whæt|whæht|waht|waet|wæht|weht|hweat||Southern West Mid}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|enm|/ʍat/|/wat/}}

====Pronoun====
{{head|enm|pronoun|cat2=interrogative pronouns}}

# [[#English|what]]

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|en|what}}
* {{desc|sco|what|alts=1}}
* {{desc|yol|faade|alts=1}}

====Adverb====
{{head|enm|adverb}}

# [[why|Why]].
#* {{RQ:Chaucer Canterbury Tales|Summoner's Tale|passage='''What''' shulde I tel the answere of the knyght?|translation='''Why''' should I tell the answer of the knight?}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Of Reformation|passage=But '''what''' do I stand reckoning upon advantages and gains lost by the misrule and turbulency of the prelates?}}
# {{n-g|Used to introduce each of two coordinate phrases or concepts; both...and...}}
#* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|5.1|2|III|primum|passage=And as for on C good knyghtes I haue my self / but I fawte / l / for so many haue ben slayne in my dayes / and so Ladegreans delyuerd his doughter Gweneuer vnto Merlyn / and the table round with the C knyghtes / and so they rode fresshly with grete royalte / '''what''' by water and '''what''' by land / tyl that they came nyghe vnto london}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|yol|fad}}

