==Hebrew==
[[File:Hebrew letter shin.png|thumb|A shin rendered in two different fonts.]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|he|LL-Q9288_(heb)-Uziel302-ש.wav}}
* {{homophones|he|ס}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|he|sem-pro|*šinn-|t=tooth}}, the source of {{m|he|שן}}. The association of "tooth" with this letter was the result of folk etymology and based on the corresponding Phoenician letter, {{m|phn|𐤔}}, having a shape resembling a tooth. The letter originally depicted a [[composite bow]], which usually has the tips curving away from the archer when unstrung.<ref>{{R:American Heritage Dictionary|shin}}</ref><ref>Albright, W. F. (1948). "The Early Alphabetic Inscriptions from Sinai and their Decipherment". Bulletin of the American Schools of Oriental Research. 110 (110): 6–22 [p. 15].</ref>

Related to {{cog|syc|ܫ}}, {{cog|ar|ش}}, {{cog|phn|𐤔}}, {{cog|arc|ܫ}}. More at [[w:Shin (letter)|Shin]].

====Letter====
{{head|he|letter|tr=sh, s}}

# [[shin|Shin]] or [[sin]]: the twenty-first letter of the [[Hebrew]] alphabet, after {{m|he|ר}} and before {{m|he|ת}}.
#: '''{{lang|he|שׁ}}''' is shin (with the [[shin dot]] above the right-hand side of the letter), representing {{IPAchar|/ʃ/}}
#: '''{{lang|he|שׂ}}''' is sin (with the [[sin dot]] above the left-hand side of the letter), representing /s/
# The [[numeral]] 300 in Hebrew numbering.

=====Coordinate terms=====
* {{list:Hebrew script letters/he}}

