==Translingual==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology 1===

====Letter====
{{mul-letter|upper=X|lower=x|script=Latn}}

# The twenty-fourth letter of the [[Appendix:Latin script|basic modern Latin alphabet]].

====See also====
{{Latn-script}}

===Etymology 2===
The variable name comes from usage in Spain, as up to the 16th century ⟨x⟩ had the sound value {{IPAchar|/ʃ/}} in {{bor|mul|es|-}}, so was used to transcribe {{der|mul|ar|ش|tr=š}} used for the purpose of a variable as an abbreviation of {{m|ar|شيء|شَيْء|t=thing|tr=šayʔ}}.<ref>{{R:mul:Lagarde:1882}}</ref> Its sound value in Spanish has since then become {{IPAchar|/x/}}, which explains the decision {{w|History of the International Phonetic Alphabet|made}} in the circle of the {{w|International Phonetic Association}} to assign this character to the voiceless velar fricative. Compare {{ncog|en|hickey|t=an object whose name is not recalled; bruise-like mark made during petting by pressing the mouth to the skin}} for the development of the sense of a communicated kiss.

====Pronunciation====
* {{audio|mul|Voiceless velar fricative.ogg|IPA}}

====Symbol====
{{head|mul|symbols}}
{{wikipedia|Voiceless velar fricative}}

# {{lb|mul|IPA}} a [[voiceless]] [[velar]] [[fricative]].
#: {{lb|mul|superscript {{angbr IPA|ˣ}}}} {{IPAfont|[x]}}-fricated release, {{IPAfont|[x]}}-coloring, or a weak, fleeting or epenthetic {{IPAfont|[x]}} &ndash; see [[ˣ|{{angbr IPA|ˣ}}]].
# {{lb|mul|mathematics}} A name for an unknown [[variable]].
# {{lb|mul|mathematics}} The first coordinate in [[Cartesian coordinates]], representing [[horizontal]] position.
# {{non-gloss definition|Used as a placeholder for a digit or value that may vary.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|termlang=mul|year=2008|author=Leonard Richardson; Sam Ruby|title=RESTful Web Services|page=387|passage=The '''5xx''' [i.e. three digits starting "5"] series of status codes is for representing problems on the server side.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|termlang=mul|year=2009|author=Ravi Rikhye|title=Concise World Armies 2009|passage=BRIGADE NUMBERING - TRADITIONAL • Armor Brigade [5'''xx''' series] • Armored Infantry Brigade [3xx series] • Motorized Infantry Brigade [2xx series] • Infantry Brigade [1xx series] • Special Forces [8xx series]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|termlang=mul|year=2010|author=Tri "Slowhabit" Nguyen; Martin Harris|title=Advanced PLO Play|page=53|passage=Although my opponent will have 4'''xxx''' [i.e. a 4 and three other cards] frequently, I have to stack off occasionally to avoid being exploited in this spot.}}
# A [[kiss]] at the end of a letter, or similar missive.
#* {{quote-newsgroup|es|termlang=mul|title=God bless u!|author=tammy martinez|date=September 8, 2009|newsgroup=cir-b-ksa|id=|url=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/cir-b-ksa/d8nQkQqar4E/0JhXMUI297AJ|accessdate=2014-10-26|passage=Que DIOS te bendiga, '''x'''o'''x'''o|translation=GOD bless you '''x'''o'''x'''o}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|pt|termlang=mul|title=Re: Sortido|author=pap...@yahoo.com|date=September 4, 2010|newsgroup=Medicina UFRJ Turma 1970|id=|url=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/nacionaldemedicina1970/8BoNLDmY_fE/KAjMT3ojSpIJ|accessdate=2014-10-26|passage=Pratinha te amo, te amo...'''X'''O'''X'''O luizbola|translation=Parinha I love you, I love you...'''X'''O'''X'''O luizbola}}
#* {{quote-book|en|termlang=mul|year=2012|author=Amanda Jennings|title=Sworn Secret|passage=''i love you :)! '''xxx'''.'' She pressed send and stared at him. She giggled when he reached into his pocket and pulled out his phone.}}
#* {{quote-book|de|termlang=mul|year=2012|author=Maya Rodale|title=Geheimes Spiel der Liebe|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=7mpZAgAAQBAJ&lpg=PT4&dq=liebe%20xoxo&pg=PT4#v=onepage&q=liebe%20xoxo&f=false%20dedication|passage='''X'''O'''X'''O, dein Schreibmädchen, deine Frau|translation='''X'''O'''X'''O, your writing girl, your wife}}
#* {{quote-book|es|termlang=mul|year=2013|author=Arthur Fleischmann; Carly Fleischmann|title=La voz de Carly|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=wgm1ip9ctrAC&lpg=PT168&dq=%22te%20quiero%22%20xoxo&pg=PT168#v=onepage&q=%22te%20quiero%22%20xoxo&f=false|passage=Nos vemos en unos días. Te quiero. '''X'''o'''x'''o.|translation=We see each other in a few days. I want you. '''X'''o'''x'''o.}}
#* {{quote-book|fr|termlang=mul|year=1999|title=Mon évolution par l'Amour|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=uBGqzxf0v5MC&lpg=PA134&dq=%22je%20t'aime%22%20xxxx&pg=PA134#v=onepage&q=%22je%20t'aime%22%20xxxx&f=false|page=134|passage=Je t'aime ! Kristel '''xxxx'''...|translation=I love you ! Kristel '''xxxx'''...}}

=====Usage notes=====
In the mathematical senses, the term is conventionally rendered in italics, as in, “Solve the equation <math>x^3+x=a</math> for <math>x</math>.”

=====References=====
<references/>

===Etymology 3===
The {{w|multiplication sign}} is an arbitrary notation developed in the early 17th century.

====Symbol====
{{head|mul|symbols}}

# {{alternative spelling of |mul|×}}
#: {{ux|en|Dimensions: 1280'''x'''720}}
#: {{ux|en|3'''x''' as much as usual}}
#* {{quote-book|de|termlang=mul|year=2017|author=Karin Loose|title=Gesunde Lebensführung - Gewusst wie!?: Ernährung + Bewegung + Psyche|publisher=BookRix|isbn=9783743825529
|passage=Nebenzahlreichen sekundären Pflanzenstoffen enthält Moringa: 2'''x''' so viel Proteine wie Soja [...]}}

=====Usage notes=====
Using {{m|en|x}} (in upper- or lower-case, italicized or not, and sometimes rotated 90 degrees) to indicate multiplication was common in 17th-century mathematical texts.<ref>{{cite-journal|last=Cajori|first=Florian|title=The Cross X as a Symbol for Multiplication|url=https://www.nature.com/articles/094363c0|journal=Nature|volume=94|year=1914|month=December|pages=363–364|doi=10.1038/094363c0}}</ref> Today, this usage is considered poor typography, but it is nevertheless common because computer keyboards lack a × key.

=====References=====
<references/>

===Etymology 4===
[[File:DOnaTREN.jpg|thumb|right|A [[CD-R]] claiming '''52X''' speed.|upright=1.35]]
[[File:Maxell Branded DVD-R.JPG|thumb|right|A [[DVD-R]] claiming '''8x''' speed.|upright=1.35]]
Originates in given multiples of the speeds specified in the original CD and DVD standards, then, by reason of the actual unit being without name but just implied by the multiplication sign, reinterpreted as or used as if a unit, considering also that data transfer speed is ''variable'', so that now, bolstered by stylization of the sign in the marketing of manufacturers, either spelling, with the multiplication sign or with the ex borrowed from the iconic mathematical variable sign, seems correct.

====Symbol====
{{head|mul|symbols}}

# {{lb|mul|computing}} A [[unit]] of [[transfer rate]] of [[CD]] drives, equal to 150 [[KiB]] per second.
# {{lb|mul|computing}} A [[unit]] of [[transfer rate]] of [[DVD]] drives, equal 1.32 [[MiB]] per second.

====Further reading====
* {{cite-web|url=https://kb.iu.edu/d/adme|work=Indiana University: University Information Technology Services Knowledge Base|title=What is meant by the speed of a CD or DVD drive?|date=2018-01-18}}
* {{pedia|lang=en|Optical disc drive#Rotational mechanism}}
* {{pedia|lang=en|CD and DVD writing speed}}

