Page 48 sun letter: Skipped, no changes
Page 48 sun letter: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{wikipedia|Sun letters}}

===Etymology===
{{calque|en|ar|حَرْف شَمْسِيّ}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|linguistics}} One of fourteen Arabic letters that cause the "L" of a preceding definite article {{m|ar|اَل}} to be assimilated in pronunciation. The sun letter suppresses the sound of the "L" and then [[geminate]]s: al + ṣīn = aṣ-ṣīn. The effect of the sun letter is limited to pronunciation and does not affect the spelling, except that a [[shadda]] may be written over the sun letter and in the fully vocalised spelling lām lacks any diacritics: {{m|ar|الصِّين}}. The fourteen sun letters are {{l|ar|ت}}, {{l|ar|ث}}, {{l|ar|د}}, {{l|ar|ذ}}, {{l|ar|ر}}, {{l|ar|ز}}, {{l|ar|س}}, {{l|ar|ش}}, {{l|ar|ص}}, {{l|ar|ض}}, {{l|ar|ط}}, {{l|ar|ظ}}, {{l|ar|ل}}, {{l|ar|ن}}. Phonetically, they represent {{ll|en|coronal|id=phonetics}} consonants.

====Usage notes====
* {{l|ar|ج}} is not traditionally among the sun letters, as its corresponding sound was originally a palatal {{IPAchar|/ɟ/}}. However, its pronunciation in Modern Standard Arabic varies per region from {{IPAchar|/d͡ʒ/}}, {{IPAchar|/ʒ/}}, to {{IPAchar|/ɡ/}}, respectively both of which are coronal, and velar. Prescriptively, it should still avoid assimilating the definite article in observance of tradition, but in practice, speakers may apply assimilation regardless.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|solar letter}}

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|moon letter}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|sun letter}}
* Arabic: {{t|ar|حَرْف شَمْسِيّ|m}} (plural: {{t|ar|حُرُوف شَمْسِيَّة|m-p}})
* French: {{t|fr|lettre solaire|f}}
* German: {{t+|de|Sonnenbuchstabe|m}}
* Kazakh: {{t|kk|күн әрпі|sc=Cyrl}}
* Malay: {{t|ms|huruf syamsiah}}
* Maltese: {{t|mt|konsonanti xemxin}}
* Russian: {{t|ru|со́лнечная бу́ква|f}}
* Spanish: {{t|es|letra solar|f}}
* Ukrainian: {{t|uk|сонячна літера|f}}, {{t|uk|сонячна буква}}
{{trans-bottom}}

[[Category:en:Arabic letter names]]
