{{mul-symbol}}

# {{ISO 639|2&3}}

==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/læm/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-lam.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|æm|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|lamen}}, {{m|enm|lemen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|lemian}} and {{der|en|non|lemja}}; both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*lamjaną}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|lamm}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To [[beat]] or [[thrash]].
#* '''1930''', {{w|Langston Hughes}} and {{w|Zora Neale Hurston}}, ''{{w|Mule Bone}}'', Act II, Scene 2, in ''The Collected Works of Langston Hughes'', Volume 5: ''The Plays to 1942:'' Mulatto ''to'' The Sun Do Move, edited by Leslie Catherine Sanders, Columbia: University of Missouri Press, 2002, p. 102,
#*: An' fo' I knowed it, he done picked up that bone an' '''lammed''' me ovah de head wid it.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1953|author=w:C. S. Lewis|title=w:The Silver Chair|publisher=Collins|year_published=1998|section=Chapter
|passage=They '''lammed''' each other on the head with great, clumsy stone hammers; but their skulls were so hard that the hammers bounced off again {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|dated|slang}} To [[flee]] or [[run away]].
#* '''1947''', {{w|Bill Finger}}, ''{{w|World's Finest Comics}}'' #30, "The Penny Plunderers!", p. 4:<!--Reproduced at http://goodcomics.comicbookresources.com/2010/11/01/when-we-first-met-1/ -->
#*: [Gangster running away:] Batman and Robin! ''Let's '''lam'''!''
#* {{RQ:Kerouac On the Road|chapter=1|part=1|passage={{...|Meanwhile Dean had gotten a job in a parking lot, had a fight with Marylou in their Hoboken apartment—God knows why they went there—}}and she was so mad and so down deep vindictive that she reported to the police some false trumped-up hysterical crazy charge, and Dean had to '''lam''' from Hoboken.}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[flight#Etymology_2| flight]] or [[escape]].
#: {{uxi|en|on the '''lam'''}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|lambaste}}
* {{l|en|lam into}}
* {{l|en|lam out}}
* {{l|en|on the lam}}
* {{l|en|take it on the lam}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|To [[beat]] or [[thrash]]}}
* Bulgarian: {{t+|bg|бия}}, {{t+|bg|пердаша}}
* Spanish: {{t+|es|paliza|f}}, {{t+|es|golpiza|f}}
