Page 6 meme: Skipping template because lang code 'en' not among --langcodes-to-do: {{suf|en|mime|eme}}
Page 6 meme: Skipped, no changes
Page 6 meme: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Meme|mémé|mème|même|mëmë|mêmê|me̱me|me'me'}}

==English==

===Etymology 1===
Shortened from {{m|en||mimeme}}, equivalent to {{suf|en|mime|eme}}.

{{coin|en|Richard Dawkins|nat=British|occ=biologist|in=1976}} in his book ''{{w|The Selfish Gene}}''. Shortened (after {{m|en|gene}}) from {{m|en||mimeme}} (compare {{cog|en|phoneme}}), anglicized as if from a noun derived from {{der|en|grc|μῑμέομαι}} with the deverbal suffix {{m|grc|-μα}}, from {{m|grc|μῖμος||imitation, copy}}.<ref>
{{cite-book
|year=1976
|title=The Selfish Gene
|author=Richard Dawkins
|authorlink=Richard Dawkins
|passage=We need a name for the new replicator, a noun that conveys the idea of a unit of cultural transmission, or a unit of imitation. 'Mimeme' comes from a suitable Greek root, but I want a monosyllable that sounds a bit like 'gene'. I hope my classicist friends will forgive me if I abbreviate mimeme to meme. If it is any consolation, it could alternatively be thought of as being related to 'memory', or to the French word même. It should be pronounced to rhyme with 'cream'.
}}</ref> The concept was later applied to the Internet by {{w|Mike Godwin}}.<ref>
{{cite-web
|date=1994-01-10
|work=Wired
|author=Mike Godwin
|authorlink=Mike Godwin
|title=Meme, Counter-meme
|url=https://www.wired.com/1994/10/godwin-if-2/
|passage=Not everyone saw the comparison to Nazis as a "meme" - most people on the Net, as elsewhere, had never heard of "memes" or "memetics." But now that we're living in an increasingly information-aware culture, it's time for that to change.}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|mēm}}, {{IPA|en|/miːm/}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːm|s=1}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-meme.ogg|a=US}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|originally}} Any [[unit]] of (originally [[cultural]]) [[information]], such as a [[practice]] or [[idea]], that is [[transmit]]ted [[verbally]] or by [[repeated]] [[action]] from one [[mind]] to another in a comparable way to the transmission of [[gene]]s.
#: {{syn|en|culturgen}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1976|author=w:Richard Dawkins|title=The Selfish Gene
|passage=Examples of '''memes''' are tunes, ideas, catch-phrases, clothes fashions, ways of making pots or of building arches.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2002|author=Rita Carter|title=Exploring Consciousness|page=242
|passage=Related '''memes''' tend to form mutually supporting meme-complexes such as religions, political ideologies, scientific theories, and New Age dogmas.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2014|author=James Lambert|title=A Much Tortured Expression: A New Look At ‘Hobson-Jobson’|journal=International Journal of Lexicography|volume=27|issue=1|page=67|text=The original ''Hobson'' and ''Jobson'' stock comic characters have died out as a '''meme''', as has the application of their names to the Muharram in India.}}
# {{lb|en|Internet}} [[media|Media]], usually humorous, which is copied and circulated [[online]] with slight adaptations, such as basic [[picture]]s, video templates, etc. {{defdate|from 1993}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|group=soc.sexuality.general|year=2005|author=darklily|title=OT: Livejournal|passage=I do...but my journal is a mess. It's mostly filled with '''memes''' and my bitching about a house I am building.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Greg Jarboe|title=You Tube and Video Marketing|edition=2nd
|passage=The idea was to append Keyboard Cat to the end of a blooper video to "play" that person offstage after a mistake or gaffe, like getting the hook in the days of vaudeville. The '''meme''' became popular, Ashton Kutcher tweeted about it to more than 1 million followers, and more than 4,000 such videos have now been made.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Tim Jonze|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/musicblog/2013/feb/08/harlem-shake-meme-gangnam-style|work=The Guardian|date=Feb 8, 2013|title=Harlem Shake meme: the new Gangnam Style?|passage=Harlem Shake '''meme''': the new Gangnam Style? [headline]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jonah Engel Bromwich|title=Life on the Meme Council: Meet the Internet’s Gatekeepers|work=The New York Times|date=2017-12-15|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/15/style/know-your-meme.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Social networks produce inside jokes at a relentless pace. The best, worst, stupidest and funniest of those jokes become '''memes''', and either you get them or you don’t.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Marie Fazio|quotee=Ben Lashes|title=The World Knows Her as ‘Disaster Girl.’ She Just Made $500,000 Off the Meme.|work=The New York Times|date=2021-04-29|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/29/arts/disaster-girl-meme-nft.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=He said that NFT sales had helped establish '''memes''' as a sophisticated art form and “serious pieces of culture.”}}
# {{lb|en|Internet}} A specific instance of a meme, such as an [[image macro]] or a [[video]], often with [[humorous]] superimposed text.
#: {{ux|en|This '''meme''' generator lets you make your own '''memes''' by adding a caption to existing images, or by uploading your own image.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm always posting '''memes''' on the groupchat.}}
# {{lb|en|Internet slang|derogatory}} Something not to be taken [[seriously]]; a [[joke]].
#: {{ux|en|It's a '''meme''' degree, you know. Good luck getting a job from that.}}
#: {{ux|en|Jogging is a '''meme'''.}}
# {{lb|en|internet}} A work produced and shared in response to a prompt or suggestion within an online group.
#: {{co|en|kink '''meme'''}}
#: {{co|en|anon '''meme'''}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{der4|en|antimeme|dank meme|memeable|memedom|memome|memeplex|meme pool|memescape|meme stock|memetic|memetic algorithm|memetic engineering|memetics|memeworthy|memey|memer|meming|meme magic|anon meme|kink meme|meme coin|memeification|animation meme|forced meme
}}

