==Turkish==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|tr|ota|م|tr=-m, ım, -im|t=first-person singular possessive suffix}}, from {{inh|tr|trk-oat|م|tr=-m, um, -üm|t=first-person singular possessive suffix}}, from {{inh|tr|trk-pro|*-im}}. Cognate to {{cog|otk|𐰢|ts=-m, -ım, -im, -um, -üm|t=first-person singular possessive suffix}}.

====Suffix====
{{tr-suffix|inflectional}}

# First-person singular possessive suffix denoting singular possession in words ending in a consonant.
#: {{m|tr|ev||house}} → {{m|tr|evim||my house}}
#: {{m|tr|ofis||office}} → {{m|tr|ofisim||my office}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Turkish has word-final stress. Usually, when the possessive suffix ''-im'' is the last syllable of a word, it takes the stress. See also the first usage note of Etymology 2. However, when suffixed to a word with anomalous stress, the same syllable is stressed as before; for example, ''İzmir'' /ˈiz.miɾ/ + ''-im'' → ''İzmir’im'' /ˈiz.mi.ɾim/ (“my Izmir”).
* If the noun ends in a vowel, the vowel of the possessive suffix is elided, becoming {{m|tr|-m}}:
*: {{m|tr|kedi||cat}}  + ''-im'' → ''kedim'' (“my cat”).
* The suffix obeys [[vowel harmony]] and is used for words whose last vowel is ''e'' or ''i'', and a small number of [[loan words]]; for example, {{m|tr|saat||watch}}  + ''-im'' → ''saatim'' (“my watch”). It may change into {{m|tr|-ım}}, {{m|tr|-um}} and {{m|tr|-üm}} according to the last vowel of the word it is suffixed to. Examples:
*: {{m|tr|kız||girl}} + ''-im'' → ''kızım'' (“my girl (daughter)”) (used when the last vowel is “a” or “ı”);
*: {{m|tr|yol||road}} + ''-im'' → ''yolum'' (“my road”) (used when the last vowel is “o” or “u”);
*: {{m|tr|yüz||face}} + ''-im'' → ''yüzüm'' (“my face”) (used when the last vowel is “ö” or “ü”).
* When a stem ends in a voiced consonant, it may become devoiced in syllable-final position. If a word ends in a thus devoiced “p”, “ç”, “t” or “k”, suffixing it with ''-im'' changes it back into a voiced “b”, “c”, “d” or “ğ”:
*: {{m|tr|sebep||reason}} + ''-im'' → ''sebebim'' (“my reason”);
*: {{m|tr|çekiç||hammer}} + ''-im'' → ''çekicim'' (“my hammer”);
*: {{m|tr|senet||bill}} + ''-im'' → ''senedim'' (“my bill");
*: {{m|tr|çiçek||flower}} + ''-im'' → ''çiçeğim'' (“my flower”).
* In many words that are originally monosyllables ending in two consonants, an epenthic “i” is inserted between these consonants. When suffixed with ''-im'', the epenthic “i” is dropped:
*: {{m|tr|beyin|bey'''i'''n|brain}} + ''-im'' → '''''beyn'''im'' (“my brain”).

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|tr|-miz}}, {{l|tr|-imiz|t=first-person plural possessive suffix}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|tr|ota|ـم}}, {{m|ota|ـن|tr=-im, -ım, -in, -ın, -en|t=first-person singular suffix}}, from {{inh|tr|trk-oat|tr=-ven|t=first-person singular suffix}}, ultimately from {{inh|tr|trk-pro|*-bẹn}} (see {{m|tr|ben|t=I}}). Cognate with {{cog|otk|𐰢𐰤|ts=-men|t=first person singular suffix}}, {{cog|xqa|مَنْ|tr=men|t=I, first person singular postposition}}, {{cog|oui|tr=-men|t=first person singular suffix}}.

The suffix ultimately merged with the first-person singular possessive suffix (see Etymology 1 above) following the road *-bẹn → *-vẹn → *-ẹn → *-ẹm → -im. 11th-century [[Karakhanid]] scholar of [[Turkic]] languages [[w:Mahmud al-Kashgari|Kashgari]] already hints that the Oghuz use *-en dropping /m/ as opposed to the Karakhanid using "-men" Compare {{cog|tk|-in}}, {{m|tk|-än|t=first-person singular suffix}}, {{cog|az|-əm|t=first-person singular suffix}}. For a similar case of loss of initial /b/ at the suffix level, compare {{m|tr|-iz|t=first-person plural suffix}}, from {{cog|trk-cmn|*biz}} (see {{m|tr|biz|t=we}}).

====Suffix====
{{tr-suffix|inflectional}}

# Conjugation of the verb "to be" for first-person singular, simple present tense.
#: {{m|tr|güzel|t=beautiful}} → {{m|tr|güzelim|t=I am beautiful}}
# Personal suffix for "ben" {{gloss|"I" - first person singular}}
#: {{m|tr|gelmek|gel-|t=come}} + {{m|tr|-ecek|t=future tense marker}} + {{m|tr|-im|t=personal suffix}} → {{m|tr|geleceğim|t=I will come}}
#: {{m|tr|sevmek|sev-|t=love}} + {{m|tr|-r|-(e)r|t=present tense marker}} + {{m|tr|-im|t=personal suffix}} → {{m|tr|severim|t=I like, enjoy}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In Turkish, as a word final stress language, when this suffix is at the end of a word it does not take the stress due to not being originally a suffix; therefore a differentiation is realized where the possessive suffix carries the stress. See also the first usage note on Etymology 1.
*: {{m|tr|bel|t=waist}} + {{m|tr|-im|t=first person suffix for "to be"}} → {{m|tr|belim|bélim|t=I am (the) waist}}, as opposed to {{m|tr|bel|t=waist}} + {{m|tr|-im|t=first-person possessive suffix}} → {{m|tr|belim|belím|t=my waist}}
*: {{m|tr|gelmek|gel-|t=come}} + {{m|tr|-r|-(i)r|t=present tense marker}} + {{m|tr|-im|t=personal suffix}} → {{m|tr|gelirim|gelírim|t=I come}}, as opposed to {{m|tr|gelir|t=income}} + {{m|tr|-im|t=first-person possessive suffix}} → {{m|tr|gelirim|gelirím|t=my income}}
*: {{m|tr|gelmek|gel-|t=come}} + {{m|tr|-ecek|-ece'''k'''|t=future tense marker}} + {{m|tr|-im|t=personal suffix}} → {{m|tr|geleceğim|gelecé'''ğ'''im|t=I will come}}, as opposed to {{m|tr|gelecek|gelece'''k'''|t=future}} + {{m|tr|-im|gloss=first-person possessive suffix}} → {{m|tr|geleceğim|gelece'''ğ'''ím|gloss=my future}} or also alternatively {{m|tr|gelecek|gelece'''k'''|gloss=future}} + {{m|tr|-im|gloss=first person suffix for "to be"}} → {{m|tr|geleceğim|gelecé'''ğ'''im|t=I am (the) future}}
* If the word ends in a vowel, it's used with an auxiliary consonant; "y" {{qualifier|for the verb to be}}.
*: {{m|tr|terli|t=sweaty}} → {{m|tr|terliyim|t=I am sweaty}}
* It must be used with an [[apostrophe]] if it's appended to a proper noun.
*: {{m|tr|Zafer}} → {{m|tr|Zafer'im|t=I am Zafer}}
* It's always {{m|tr|-um}} in present continuous tense. And in other tenses, the personal suffix may also be {{m|tr|-ım}}, {{m|tr|-um}}, {{m|tr|-üm}} according to the last vowel of the word.
*: {{m|tr|vermek|ver-|t=give}} + {{m|tr|-iyor|t=present continuous tense marker}} + {{m|tr|-um|t=personal suffix}} → {{m|tr|veriyorum|t=I am giving}}
*: {{m|tr|üzülmek|üzül-|t=be sad}} + {{m|tr|-r|-(ü)r|t=present tense marker}} + {{m|tr|-üm|t=personal suffix}} → {{m|tr|üzülürüm|t=I become sad}}
*: {{m|tr|kalmak|kal-|t=stay}} + {{m|tr|-r|-(ı)r|t=present tense marker}} + {{m|tr|-ım|t=personal suffix}} → {{m|tr|kalırım|t=I stay}}
*: {{m|tr|bulmak|bul-|t=find}} + {{m|tr|-r|-(u)r|t=present tense marker}} + {{m|tr|-um|t=personal suffix}} → {{m|tr|bulurum|t=I find}}

=====Related terms=====
