[[Image:Similar-geometric-shapes.svg|thumb|Mathematics: Figures shown in the same color are similar]]

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sem-}}
From {{der|en|fr|similaire}}, from {{der|en|ML.|similaris}}, extended from {{der|en|la|similis||like}}; akin to {{m|la|simul||together}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪmələ/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪməlɚ/|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-similar.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Having [[trait]]s or [[characteristic]]s in [[common]]; [[alike]], [[comparable]].
#: {{ux|en|My new car is '''similar''' to my old one, except it has a bit more space in the back.}}
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|IV
|passage=So this was my future home, I thought! Certainly it made a [[brave]] picture. I had seen '''similar''' ones fired-in on many a Heidelberg stein. Backed by towering hills,{{...}}a sky of palest Gobelin flecked with fat, fleecy little clouds, it in truth looked a dear little city; the city of one's dreams.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=May-June|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/charles-t-ambrose Charles T. Ambrose]
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/3/alzheimerrsquos-disease-the-great-morbidity-of-the-21st-century Alzheimer’s Disease]
|volume=101|issue=3|page=200|magazine=w:American Scientist
|passage='''Similar''' studies of rats have employed four different intracranial resorbable, slow sustained release systems&mdash;surgical foam, a thermal gel depot, a microcapsule or biodegradable polymer beads.}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} Having the same [[shape]], in particular, having corresponding [[angle]]s [[equal]] and corresponding [[line segment]]s proportional.
# {{lb|en|mathematics|linear algebra}} Of two [[square matrices]]; being such that a [[conjugation]] sends one [[matrix]] to the other.

