==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|lak}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|līk}}, {{IPA|en|/laɪ̯k/}}
* {{IPA|en|/læ̙ːk/|/lɑːk/|a=MLE,Jamaica}}
* {{IPA|en|/lɔɪ̯k/|a=DE}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-to like.ogg|a=UK|text=to like}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-like.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪk|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*leyg-|id=like}}
Verb from {{inh|en|enm|liken}}, from {{inh|en|ang|līcian||to like, to please}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*līkēn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*līkāną}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*leyg-||image; likeness; similarity}}.

Cognate with {{cog|stq|liekje||to be similar, resemble}}, {{cog|nl|lijken||to seem}}, {{cog|nds-de|lieken||to be like; resemble}}, {{cog|de|gleichen||to resemble}}, {{cog|sv|lika||to like; put up with; align with}}, {{cog|no|like||to like}}, {{cog|is|líka||to like}}.

Noun from {{inh|en|enm|like|t=pleasure, will, like}}, from the verb {{der|en|enm|liken|t=to like}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[enjoy]], be pleased by; [[favor]]; be in favor of.
#: {{ant|en|dislike|hate|mislike}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''like''' hamburgers.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''like''' skiing in winter.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''like''' the Seattle Mariners this season.}}
#* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|2|21|text=He may either go or stay, as he best '''likes'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1865|author=w:Lewis Carroll|title=w:Alice's Adventures in Wonderland|section=[[s:Alice's Adventures in Wonderland/Chapter 10|chapter 10]]|passage=“I can tell you more than that, if you '''like''',” said the Gryphon. “Do you know why it’s called a whiting?”}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|VIII|passage=At her invitation he outlined for her the succeeding chapters with terse military accuracy ; and what she '''liked''' best and best understood was avoidance of that false modesty which condescends, turning technicality into pabulum.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[please]].
#* {{quote-text|en|author=Sir {{w|Philip Sidney}}| passage=I willingly confess that it '''likes''' me much better when I find virtue in a fair lodging than when I am bound to seek it in an ill-favoured creature.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=7ZpMAAAAcAAJ&pg=RA1-PA83&dq=dunghill.&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwinxIyUndDiAhWwmeAKHXD6BmQQ6AEINTAC#v=onepage&q=dunghill.&f=false| title=The Countess of Pembroke's Arcadia|year=16th century}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1608|author=w:William Shakespeare| title=w:King Lear| section=act 2, scene 2| passage=His countenance '''likes''' me not.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To derive [[pleasure]] ''of'', ''by'' or ''with'' someone or something.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1662|author={{w|Sir_Thomas_Salusbury,_2nd_Baronet|Thomas Salusbury}}|title=Galileo's Dialogue Concerning the Two Systems of the World|section=(Dialogue Two)|passage=And therefore it is the best way, if you '''like of''' it, to examine these taken from experiments touching the Earth, and then proceed to those of the other kind.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=I|page=2|passage=He used to drop into my chambers once in a while to smoke, and was first-rate company. When I gave a dinner there was generally a cover laid for him. I '''liked''' the man for his own sake, and even had he promised to turn out a celebrity it would have had no weight with me.}}
# To prefer and [[maintain]] (an action) as a regular [[habit]] or [[activity]].
#: {{ux|en|I '''like''' to go to the dentist every six months.}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''likes''' to keep herself physically fit.}}
#: {{ux|en|we '''like''' to keep one around the office just in case.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=2016|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171014193917/https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/lets-learn-english-lesson-6-where-is-the-gym/3225958.html|title=VOA Learning English (public domain)|passage=People in Washington '''like''' to work out!}}
#*: {{audio|en|People in Washington like to work out!.ogg|a=US}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To have an appearance or expression; to look; to seem to be (in a specified condition).
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|act=II|scene=iv|passage=You '''like''' well, and bear your years very well.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To come near; to avoid with difficulty; to escape narrowly.
#: {{ux|en|He '''liked''' to have been too late.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Letters of Horace Walpole: Fourth Earl of Oxford|author=w:Horace Walpole|section=to George Montagu|year=1760|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8xYQAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA440&dq=troops+expedition+Kensington+Garden.&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwikp4TOn9DiAhXSmeAKHWOyBvQQ6AEINDAC&q=troops%20expedition%20Kensington%20Garden.&f=false|passage=He probably got his death, as he '''liked''' to have done two years ago, by viewing the troops for the expedition from the wall of Kensington Garden.}}
# To [[find]] [[attractive]]; to prefer the company of; to have mild romantic feelings for.
#: {{syn|en|fancy|q1=British|enjoy|love}}
#: {{ant|en|dislike|hate|mislike}}
#: {{ux|en|I really '''like''' Sandra but don't know how to tell her.}}
#* {{quote-video game|en|date=2016-12-19|location=Japan|publisher=NTT Solmare|title=Moe! Ninja Girls|system=iOS, Android|scene=Season 1, Enju Ending|text= ―{{nbsp}}Enju: “Apparently when you '''like''' someone, you start talking like them.”}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[liken]]; to [[compare]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1590s|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=w:Henry VI, Part 1|section=act 4, scene 6|passage=And '''like''' me to the peasant boys of France.}}
# {{senseid|en|show approval of via an online vote}} {{lb|en|Internet|transitive}} To show [[support]] for, or [[approval]] of, something posted on the [[Internet]] by marking it with a [[vote]].
#: {{ant|en|unlike#Verb|dislike#Verb}}
#: {{cot|en|heart<id:show approval of via a heart symbol>|love<id:show approval of via a heart symbol>}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''liked''' my friend's last status on Facebook.}}
#: {{ux|en|I can't stand Bloggs' tomato ketchup, but I '''liked''' it on Facebook so I could enter a competition.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=2021|writer=w:Adam McKay|title=w:Don't Look Up|role=Jason Orlean|actor=Jonah Hill|passage=What' up, y'all? I'm the last man on Earth. Shit's all fucked up. Don't forget to '''like''' and subscribe. We out here.}}
# {{lb|en|with 'would' and in certain other phrases}} To [[want]], [[desire]]. ''See also '''''[[would like]]'''''.''
#: {{ux|en|Would you '''like''' a cigarette?}}
#: {{ux|en|We could go to the museum if you '''like'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I don't '''like''' to disturb him when he's working.}}
# {{n-g|Of inanimate objects:}}
## {{lb|en|informal|personification}} To be prone to.
##: {{usex|en|Too bad this cheese '''likes''' to stick to the pan and ruin the whole meal.}}
## {{lb|en|informal|usually in the negative}} Of a computer or other system: to tolerate as an input; to accept.
##: {{ux|en|This self-service checkout doesn't seem to '''like''' my credit card.}}
##: {{ux|en|We were frustrated that our seeming innocent choice for a team name was rejected by the game. Apparently somewhere in the name is a word that the algorithm doesn’t '''like'''.}}
##: {{usex|en|email delivery failed: remote host does not '''like''' recipient}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In its senses of “enjoy” and “maintain as a regular habit”, {{m|en||like}} is a [[catenative]] verb; in the former, it usually takes a [[gerund]] ({{m|en|-ing}} form), while in the latter, it takes a {{m|en|to}}-[[infinitive]]. ''See also'' [[Appendix:English catenative verbs]].
* ''Like'' is only used to mean “want” in certain expressions, such as “if you like” and “I would like”. The conditional form, {{m|en|would like}}, is used quite freely as a polite synonym for {{m|en|want}}.

