====References====
* {{pedia}}

===Etymology 2===
Adjective from {{inh|en|enm|lik}}, {{m|enm|lyk}}, {{m|enm|lich}}, {{m|enm|like}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ġelīċ}} by shortening, influenced by {{der|en|non|líkr}}, {{m|non|glíkr}}; both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*galīkaz|t=like, similar, same}}. Related to {{m|en|alike}}; more distantly, with {{m|en|lich}} and {{m|en|-ly}}. Cognate with {{cog|fy|like||like; as}}, {{cog|stq|gliek||like}}, {{cog|da|lig||alike}}, {{cog|nl|gelijk||like, alike}}, {{cog|de|gleich||equal, like}}, {{cog|is|líkur||alike, like, similar}}, {{cog|no|lik||like, alike}} {{cog|sv|lik||like, similar}}

Adverb from {{inh|en|enm|like}}, {{m|enm|lyke}}, {{m|enm|liche}}, {{m|enm|lyche}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ġelīċe|t=likewise, also, as, in like manner, similarly}} and {{der|en|non|líka|t=also, likewise}}; both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*galīkê}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*galīkaz|t=same, like, similar}}.

Conjunction from {{inh|en|enm|like}}, {{m|enm|lyke}}, {{m|enm|lik}}, {{m|enm|lyk}}, from the adverb {{der|en|enm|like}}.

Preposition from {{inh|en|enm|like}}, {{m|enm|lyke}}, {{m|enm|liche}}, {{m|enm|lyche}}, {{m|enm|lijc}}, {{m|enm|liih|t=similar to, like, equal to, comparable with}}, from {{der|en|enm|like|pos=adjective}} and {{m|enm|like|pos=adverb}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|more}}

# [[similar|Similar]].
#: {{ux|en|My partner and I have '''like''' minds.}}
#: {{ux|en|The two cats were as '''like''' as though they had come from the same litter.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=John Buchan|title=The Outgoing of the Tide
|passage=It will seem strange that in all this time the Presbytery was idle, and no effort was made to rid the place of so fell an influence. But there was a reason, and the reason, as in most '''like''' cases, was a lassie.}}
#* {{RQ:Carlyle Past and Present|2|ch. 3, Landlord Edmund||{{...}}and this is not a sky, it is a Soul and living Face! Nothing '''liker''' the Temple of the Highest, bright with some real effulgence of the Highest, is seen in this world.}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=I opened the man's linen robe, and there over his heart was a dagger-wound, and beneath the woman's fair breast was a '''like''' cruel stab, through which her life had ebbed away.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|_|or|_|Scotland|Southern US}} [[likely|Likely]]; [[probable]].
#* '''1668''', {{w|Robert South}}, ''The Messiah's Sufferings for the Sins of the People'' (sermon, March 20, 1668)
#*: But it is '''like''' the jolly world about us will scoff at the paradox of these practices.
#* {{RQ:Clarendon History|passage=Many were {{...}} not easy to be govern'd, nor '''like''' to conform themselves to such strict rules.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=II|page=151|passage=Finding it was '''like''' to overblow, we took in our Sprit-ſail, and ſtood by to hand the Fore-ſail; but making foul Weather, we look'd the Guns were all faſt, and handed the Miſſen.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=John Buchan|title=The Outgoing of the Tide
|passage=She had waited overlong, and now it was '''like''' that Ailie would escape her toils.}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland|Southern US|otherwise|archaic|usually with '''to'''}} [[inclined]] (''to''), [[prone]] (''to'').
#: {{usex|en|He seems '''like''' to run from any semblance of hard work.}}
#* {{quote-book|en
|author=w:Charles Dickens |chapter=[[s:A Christmas Carol (Dickens, 1843 facsimile)/Stave 3|Stave three: The second of the three spirits]]
|title=A Christmas Carol
|location=Boston |publisher=Atlantic Monthly Press |year=1920 |origyear=1843 |page=96
|text=<p>"If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race," returned the Ghost, "will find him here. What then? If he be '''like''' to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population."</p><p>Scrooge hung his head to hear his own words quoted by the Spirit, and was overcome with penitence and grief.</p>
}}

