==English==
{{number box|en|4}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈk(w)ɔːtə/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈk(w)ɔɹ.tɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈk(w)oː.tɘ/|a=NZ}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-quarter.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-I learned some phrases-quarter.wav|a=UK}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔː(ɹ)tə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|quarter}}, from {{der|en|xno|quarter}}, from {{der|en|la|quartarius}}, from {{m|la|quartus}}. Compare {{cog|es|cuarto||room, quarters; quarter}}. {{doublet|en|quartier}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}
[[File:1999 Obverse Proof.png|thumb|A US quarter, 25 cent coin.]]

# ''A [[fourth]] part of something.''
## {{lb|en|in general sense}} Each of [[four]] equal parts into which something can be divided; a fourth part. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I ate a '''quarter''' of the pizza.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 April 5|author=Philip Haigh|title=Comment: Pay deal a positive result|journal=RAIL|issue=980|page=3|text=For companies such as Trans Pennine Express, it will be even harder. It has the unwelcome claim to being Britain's worst train operator, with recent statistics from the Office of Rail and Road showing it cancelled nearly a '''quarter''' of its services in February, with lack of available train crew a real problem.}}
## {{lb|en|now|chiefly|historical}} A measure of capacity used chiefly for [[grain]] or [[coal]], varying greatly in quantity by time and location. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1882|author=James Edwin Thorold Rogers|title=A History of Agriculture and Prices in England|volume=4|page=204|passage=One of these is 1 Hen. V, cap. 10, defining the '''quarter''' of corn to be eight struck bushels, and putting fines on purveyors who take more.}}
## A fourth part of a [[pound]]; approximately 113 [[gram]]s. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} A measure of [[length]]; originally a fourth part of an [[ell]], now chiefly a fourth part of a [[yard]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
## {{lb|en|now|historical}} A fourth part of the [[night]]; one of the [[watch]]es or divisions of the night. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Mark|6|48|text=And aboute the fourth '''quartre''' of the nyght, he cam unto them, walkinge apon the see [...].}}
## {{lb|en|now|chiefly|financial}} A fourth part of the [[year]]; 3 [[month]]s; a term or season. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
## {{lb|en|time}} A fourth part of an [[hour]]; a period of fifteen [[minute]]s, especially with reference to the quarter before or after the hour. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
## {{lb|en|now|chiefly|historical}} A fourth part of a [[hundredweight]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
## {{lb|en|heraldiccharge}} A [[fourth]] part of a [[coat of arms]], or the [[charge]] on it, [[larger]] than a [[canton]] and [[normally]] on the [[upper]] [[dexter]] [[side]], [[formed]] by a [[perpendicular]] [[line]] from the [[top]] [[meet]]ing a [[horizontal]] [[line]] from the [[side]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
## {{lb|en|North America}} A quarter-[[dollar]], [[divide]]d into 25 [[cent]]s; the [[coin]] of that [[value]] [[mint]]ed in the [[United States]] or [[Canada]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
## {{lb|en|sport}} One of four equal [[period]]s into which a game is divided. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## {{lb|en|Chester|historical}} A quarter of an acre or 40 [[rood]]s.<ref name="Holland"/>
# ''Place or position.''
## A [[region]] or [[place]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|text=I am to haste, / And all who under me thir Banners wave, / Homeward with flying march where we possess / The '''Quarters''' of the North {{...}}.|year=1873}}
## Each of four parts into which the [[earth]] or [[sky]] is divided, corresponding to the four cardinal points of the [[compass]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
## A [[division]] or [[section]] of a [[town]] or [[city]], especially having a particular character of its own, or associated with a particular group etc. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
## One's [[residence]] or dwelling-place; {{i|in plural}} [[room]]s, [[lodgings]], especially as allocated to soldiers or domestic staff. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative|archaic}} A topic or area of endeavour.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1892|author=Robert Louis Stevenson|title=The Wrecker|chapter=10
|passage=“I'll tell you something, too,” retorted the captain, duskily flushing. “I wouldn't sail this ship for the man you are, if you went upon your knees. I've dealt with gentlemen up to now.”<br>“I can tell you the names of a number of gentlemen you'll never deal with any more, and that's the whole of Longhurst's gang,” said Jim. “I'll [[put someone's pipe out|put your pipe out]] in that '''quarter''', my friend. Here, rout out your traps as quick as look at it, and take your vermin along with you. I'll have a captain in, this very night, that's a sailor, and some sailors to work for him.”}}
## {{lb|en|nautical}} The [[aftmost]] [[part]] of a [[vessel|vessel's]] [[side]], [[roughly]] from the [[last]] [[mast]] to the [[stern]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* '''1808–10''', {{w|William Hickey (memoirist)|William Hickey}}, ''Memoirs of a Georgian Rake'', Folio Society 1995, p. 80:
##*: I was one morning walking the deck, when Rogers, whose watch it was, sitting upon the '''quarter''', called to me in his usual style, ‘Come here, Bill.’
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1863|author=w:Charles Reade|title={{w|Hard Cash (novel)|Hard Cash}}|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/3067/3067-h/3067-h.htm|passage=“My men, the schooner coming up on our weather '''quarter''' is a Portuguese pirate.”}}
## {{lb|en|farriery}} The [[part]] on [[either]] [[side]] of a [[horse]]'s [[hoof]] between the [[toe]] and [[heel]], the [[side]] of its [[coffin]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Sewell Black Beauty|page=106|passage=[A]t last she kicked right over the carriage pole and fell down, after giving me a severe blow on my near '''quarter'''.}}
# {{lb|en|often plural}} A section (of a population), especially one having a particular set of values or interests.
#: ''opposition to the policy came from an unexpected '''quarter''', as well as from certain '''quarters''' which had historically opposed it''
#: ''all '''quarters''' of the socialist movement''; ''praise from Conservative '''quarters'''''
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1897|title=National and English Review|page=499
|passage=It is something to have that sacerdotal position so frankly recognized; but, I repeat, the ground of objection is an extraordinary one, coming as it does from a Liberal '''quarter''' in politics.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2003|title=The Advocate|page=44
|passage=V. Gene Robinson&#39;s installation as an Episcopal bishop was greeted largely by silence from gay '''quarters'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Michael Eric Dyson|title=The Black Presidency: Barack Obama and the Politics of Race in America|publisher=Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|isbn=9780544386426
|passage={{...}} and principled criticism of Obama from black '''quarters'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[relations|Relations]] between people. {{defdate|17th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|2|3|passage=In '''quarter''', and in terms like bride and groom.}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Cunning|passage=I knew two that were competitors for the secretary's place, {{...}} and yet kept good '''quarter''' between themselves.}}
# [[accommodation|Accommodation]] [[give]]n to a [[defeated]] [[opponent]]; [[mercy]]; exemption from being killed. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1955|author=J.R.R. Tolkien|title=The Return of the King|page=1110|passage=Hard fighting and long labour they had still; for the Southrons were bold men and grim, and fierce in despair, and the Easterlings were strong and war-hardened and asked for '''no quarter'''.}}
# ''Short forms.''
## {{lb|en|now|rare|rugby|American football}} A [[quarterback]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## {{lb|en|military slang|now|_|rare}} A [[quartermaster]]; a [[quartermaster sergeant]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1925|author=w:Ford Madox Ford|title=No More Parades|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2012|chapter=Parade's End|page=360|passage=Tietjens said: ‘Send the Canadian sergeant-major to me at the double….’ to the '''quarter'''.}}
## A [[quarterfinal]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}

