=====References=====
<references>
<ref name="Holland">Robert Holland, M.R.A.C., ''A Glossary of Words Used in the County of Chester,'' Part I--A to F., English Dialect Society, London, 1884, 3</ref>
</references>

====Adjective====
<!--Both Merriam-Webster and Harrap record it as an adjective in addition to the attributive form of the noun, compare References below. -->
{{en-adj|-}}

# Pertaining to an aspect of a [[#Noun|quarter]].
# {{lb|en|chiefly}} Consisting of a fourth part, a quarter ({{frac|1|4}}, 25%).
#: {{ux|en|a '''quarter''' hour; a '''quarter''' century; a '''quarter''' note; a '''quarter''' pound}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly}} Related to a three-month term, a quarter of a year.
#: {{ux|en|A '''quarter''' day is one terminating a quarter of the year.}}
#: {{ux|en|A '''quarter''' session is one held quarterly at the end of a quarter.}}

=====Usage notes=====
Often used in a combining form ''quarter-''.

=====Antonyms=====
* [[quadruple]]

=====Derived terms=====
{{der3|en|quarter blanket|quarter bottle|quarter century|quarter crack|quarter day|quarter final|quarter horse|quarter-hour|quarter moon|quarter note|quarter pound|quarter session|quarter waiter|quarter year|quarter sovereign}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[divide]] into quarters; to divide by [[four]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To provide housing for military personnel or other equipment.
#: {{ux|en|'''Quarter''' the horses in the third stable.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[lodge]]; to have a temporary residence.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[quartersaw]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1758|author=Thomas Hale|title=A Compleat Body Of Husbandry|page=333|passage=But there is, as in other woods, a great deal of difference between this and the '''quartered''' timber.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|historical}} To [[execute]] (someone) by tying each [[limb]] to a different [[animal]] (such as a [[horse]]) and [[drive|driving]] them in different [[direction]]s.
# {{lb|en|heraldry}} To display different coats of arms in the quarters of a shield.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=June|author=Michael Robbins|title=Heraldry of London Underground Railways|journal=Railway Magazine|page=382|text=It [the Central London Railway] assumed a modest and entirely heraldic device, '''quartering''' the arms of the City of London, the parishes of St. George's, Holborn (St. George and the dragon) and St. Marylebone (the Virgin and Child between two lilies {{...}}), and the county of Middlesex, surmounted by the dragon's wing from the City's crest.}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|to have a temporary residence}} {{l|en|stay over}}, {{l|en|stop}}; See also [[Thesaurus:sojourn]]

=====Antonyms=====
* [[quadruple]] (multiply by four)

