=====See also=====
{{English personal pronouns}}

====Determiner====
{{head|en|determiner|cat2=possessive determiners}}

# [[my|My]]; belonging to [[me]].
## {{lb|en|archaic}} {{non-gloss definition|Used attributively after the noun it modifies.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|V|1|text={{...}} Flesh and blood, / You, brother '''mine''', that entertain'd ambition, / {{...}}}}
##* {{Q|en|year=1905|{{w|E. M. Forster}}|{{w|Where Angels Fear to Tread}}|, chapter 7|quote="Ah, but how beautiful (my baby boy) is! And he is '''mine''', '''mine''' for ever. Even if he hates me he will be '''mine'''. He cannot help it, he is made out of me; I am his father."}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} {{non-gloss definition|Used attributively before a vowel.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1862|month=February|author=w:Julia Ward Howe|title=w:The Battle Hymn of the Republic|journal=The Atlantic Monthly|volume=IX|issue=LII|page=10
|passage='''Mine''' eyes have seen the glory of the coming of the Lord: / {{...}}}}
##* '''1930''' Winter, Packard Motor Car Company, ''The Packard Magazine'', Volume 9, Number 2, page 6,
##*: '''Mine''' host, it seemed, did favors for everybody...

=====Usage notes=====
* {{m|en|my|My}} and {{m|en||mine}} are essentially two forms of the same word, with {{m|en||my}} being used [[attributive]]ly before the noun, and {{m|en||mine}} being used in all other cases, as may be seen in most of the usage examples and quotations above. In this respect, this word is analogous to most of the other possessive pronouns (e.g. {{m|en|your}} vs. {{m|en|yours}}), as well as a number of other noun modifiers, such as {{m|en|lone}}/{{m|en|alone}}.
* Historically, {{m|en||my}} came to be used only before a consonant sound, and later came to be used regardless of the following sound. Nonetheless, {{m|en||mine}} still sees archaic pre-vocalic use, as may be seen in the 1862 quotation above, and in the most formal of writing even into the 20th century.

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|mine host}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|enm|-}}, from {{der|en|fro|mine}}, from {{der|en|LL.|mina}}, from {{der|en|cel-gau|-}} (compare to {{cog|cy|mwyn}}, {{cog|ga|mianach||ore}}), from {{der|en|cel-pro|*mēnis||ore, metal}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}
[[image:Gold mine.jpg|right|thumb|Entrance to a gold mine in Victoria, Australia]]
[[File:Panzermine im Schnitt.jpg|thumb|[[cutaway|Cutaway]] view of an anti-[[tank]] [[landmine]]]]

# {{senseid|en|excavation for obtaining ores or minerals}} An [[excavation]] from which [[ore]] or [[solid]] [[mineral]]s are taken, especially one consisting of [[underground]] [[tunnel]]s.
#: {{hypo|en|[[coal mine]], [[coalmine]]|drift mine|[[gold mine]], [[goldmine]]|open-pit mine|salt mine|[[strip-mine]], [[strip mine]]|iron mine|powder mine|silver mine|tin mine|urban mine}}
#: {{mer|en||[[mine shaft]], [[mineshaft]]|mine car}}
#: {{uxi|en|This diamond comes from a '''mine''' in South Africa.}}
#: {{uxi|en|He came out of the coal '''mine''' with a face covered in black.}}
#: {{uxi|en|Most coal and ore comes from open-pit '''mines''' nowadays.}}
# {{senseid|en|any source of wealth or resources, figuratively}} {{lb|en|figurative}} Any [[source]] of [[wealth]] or [[resource]]s.
#: {{uxi|en|She's a '''mine''' of information about the history of mathematics.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=December|title=Beyond the Channel: U.S.S.R.: Train speeds still rising|journal=Modern Railways|page=418|text=To those seeking information about train services on the Continent, ''Cook's Continental Guide'' is always a '''mine''' of accurate information.}}
# {{senseid|en|excavation for explosives under or near enemy lines}} {{lb|en|military}} A [[passage]] dug [[toward]] or [[underneath]] [[enemy]] [[line]]s, which is then [[pack]]ed with [[explosive]]s.
#: {{ux|en|The most famous '''mine''' of the American Civil War led to the Battle of the Crater.}}
# {{senseid|en|explosive device as weapon}} {{lb|en|military}} A [[device]] intended to [[explode]] when [[step]]ped upon or [[touch]]ed, or when [[approach]]ed by a [[ship]], [[vehicle]], or [[person]].
#: {{hypo|en|Bangalore mine|bounding mine|butterfly mine|[[land mine]], [[landmine]]|limpet mine|magnetic mine|naval mine|proximity mine|proxy mine}}
#: {{hol|en|minefield}}
#: {{uxi|en|His left leg was blown off after he stepped on a '''mine'''.}}
#: {{uxi|en|The warship was destroyed by floating '''mines'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1940|month=May|title=Overseas Railways: Icebound Denmark|journal=Railway Magazine|page=302|text=Pack ice, at times mounting to a height of 35 ft., snow, fog, and floating '''mines''' all played their part in the disorganisation of railway services, and most of the train ferry services were completely suspended for a month or more; [...].}}
# {{senseid|en|type of firework in pyrotechnics}} {{lb|en|pyrotechnics}} A type of [[firework]] that [[explode]]s on the [[ground]], [[shoot]]ing [[spark]]s [[upward]].
# {{senseid|en|cavity made by caterpillar that is eating}} {{lb|en|entomology}} The [[cavity]] made by a [[caterpillar]] while [[feed]]ing inside a [[leaf]].
# {{senseid|en|crypto mine}} {{lb|en|computing}} A [[machine]] or [[network]] of machines used to [[extract]] [[unit]]s of a [[cryptocurrency]].
#: {{ux|en|A change to the blockchain method was contemplated to allow '''mines''' to hog less electric power.}}

