===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|effort}} Planned, usually long-lasting, effort to achieve something; [[ability]] [[coupled]] with [[ambition]], [[determination]], and [[motivation]].
#: {{syn|en|ambition|grit|push|verve|motivation|get-up-and-go|self-motivation}}
#: {{ant|en|inertia|lack of motivation|laziness|phlegm|sloth}}
#: {{ux|en|Crassus had wealth and wit, but Pompey had '''drive''' and Caesar as much again.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=December|author=Michael Robbins|title=John Francis's "History of the English Railway"|journal=Railway Magazine|page=800|text=As we contemplate the half-finished arterial roads and electrification plans of our own age, and the town-planning schemes that gather dust in the public libraries, we can admire the '''drive''' and action of the railway pioneers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author=Fred Matheny|title=Solo Cycling: How to Train and Race Bicycle Time Trials| page=136| passage=I confess that the sight of my minute man ahead, getting closer and closer, gives me a little more '''drive''' even when I think I am going as fast as I can.}}
# Violent or rapid motion; a rushing onward or away; especially, a forced or hurried dispatch of business.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1881|author=w:Matthew Arnold|title=The Incompatibles
|passage=The Murdstonian '''drive''' in business.}}
# An act of driving (prompting) [[game]] animals forward, to be captured or [[hunt]]ed.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1955|author=w:Robin Jenkins|title=The Cone-Gatherers|publisher=Canongate|year_published=2012|page=79
|passage=Are you all ready?’ he cried, and set off towards the dead ash where the '''drive''' would begin.}}
# An act of driving (prompting) [[livestock]] animals forward, to transport a herd.
#: {{synonyms|en|drove#Noun|drift#Noun}}
# {{lb|en|military}} A [[sustained]] [[advance]] in the face of the enemy to take a [[strategic]] [[objective]].
#: {{syn|en|attack|push}}
#: {{ux|en|Napoleon's '''drive''' on Moscow was as [[determined]] as it was [[disastrous]].}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1941|month=August|author=Charles E. Lee|title=Railways of Italian East Africa&mdash;I|journal=Railway Magazine|page=340|text=On the other hand, in Eritrea (once our Forces had recaptured Kassala on January 19) the '''drive''' was generally eastward towards the capital, Asmara, and the Red Sea port of Massaua.}}
# A mechanism used to power or give motion to a vehicle or other machine or machine part.
#: {{syn|en|gear|engine||motor}}
#: {{ux|en|a typical steam '''drive'''}}
#: {{ux|en|a nuclear '''drive'''}}
#: {{ux|en|chain '''drive'''}}
#: {{ux|en|front-wheel '''drive'''}}
#: {{ux|en|Some old model trains have clockwork '''drives'''.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2001
|author=Michael Hereward Westbrook
|title=The Electric Car
|publisher=IET
|isbn=9780852960134
|page=146
|passage=Heat engine-electric hybrid vehicles : The hybrid vehicle on which most development work has been done to date is the one that couples a heat engine with an electric '''drive''' system. The objective remains the same as it was in 1900:}}
# A [[trip]] made in a [[vehicle]] (now generally in a [[motor vehicle]]).
#: {{syn|en|ride|spin|trip}}
#: {{ux|en|It was a long '''drive'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1859|author=Wilkie Collins|title=The Woman in White|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/583/583-h/583-h.htm
|passage=We merely waited to rouse good Mrs. Vesey from the place which she still occupied at the deserted luncheon-table, before we entered the open carriage for our promised '''drive'''.}}
# A [[driveway]].
#: {{syn|en|approach|driveway}}
#: {{ux|en|The mansion had a long, tree-lined '''drive'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=We expressed our readiness, and in ten minutes were in the station wagon, rolling rapidly down the long '''drive''', for it was then after nine. We passed on the way the van of the guests from Asquith.}}
# A type of [[public]] [[roadway]].
#: {{syn|en|avenue|boulevard|road|street}}
#: {{ux|en|Beverly Hills’ most famous street is Rodeo '''Drive'''.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} A place suitable or agreeable for driving; a road prepared for driving.
# {{lb|en|psychology}} [[desire|Desire]] or [[interest]].
#: {{syn|en|desire|impetus|impulse|urge}}
#* '''1995''' March 2, John Carman, "Believe It, You Saw It in Sweeps", SFGate [http://www.sfgate.com/entertainment/article/Believe-It-You-Saw-It-In-Sweeps-3043091.php]
#*: On the latter show, former ''Playboy'' Playmate Carrie Westcott said she'd never met a man who could match her sexual '''drive'''.
# {{lb|en|computer hardware}} An apparatus for [[read]]ing and [[write|writing]] [[data]] to or from a [[mass storage]] [[device]] such as a [[disk]].
#: {{syn|en|disk drive}}
#: {{hypo|en|floppy drive}}
# {{lb|en|computer hardware}} A [[mass storage]] [[device]] in which the mechanism for reading and writing data is integrated with the mechanism for storing data.
#: {{hypo|en|hard drive|flash drive}}
# {{lb|en|golf}} A [[stroke]] made with a [[driver]].
# {{senseid|en|sports}} {{lb|en|baseball|tennis}} A [[ball]] struck in a flat [[trajectory]].
# {{lb|en|cricket}} A type of [[shot]] played by swinging the [[bat]] in a [[vertical]] [[arc]], through the line of the ball, and hitting it along the ground, normally between [[cover]] and [[midwicket]].
# {{lb|en|soccer}} A [[straight]] level [[shot]] or [[pass]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 29, 2010|author=Mark Vesty|work=BBC| title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sporthi/football/eng_prem/9322845.stm Wigan 2-2 Arsenal]| passage=And after Rodallega missed two early opportunities, the first a header, the second a low '''drive''' easily held by Lukasz Fabianski, it was N'Zogbia who created the opening goal.}}
# {{lb|en|American football}} An offensive possession, generally one consisting of several plays and/ or [[first down]]s, often leading to a scoring opportunity.
# {{lb|en|philanthropy}} A charity event such as a [[fundraiser]], [[bake sale]], or [[toy drive]].
#: {{ux|en|a whist '''drive'''}}
#: {{ux|en|a beetle '''drive'''}}
# {{lb|en|retail}} A [[campaign]] aimed at [[sell]]ing more of a certain [[product]], e.g. by offering a [[discount]].
#: {{ux|en|vaccination '''drive'''}}
# {{lb|en|typography}} An [[impression]] or [[matrix]] formed by a [[punch drift]].
# A collection of objects that are driven; a [[mass]] of [[log]]s to be [[float]]ed down a [[river]].
# {{label|en|UK|especially|Bristol|and|Wales|slang}} {{non-gloss definition|Friendly term of address for a [[bus driver]].}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2017-03-21|author=Leonora Brito|title=Dat's Love and Other Stories|publisher=Parthian Books|isbn=9781910901731
|text=Yeah, thanks, '''drive'''!</br>You boyz all goin' shoppin'?</br>We are, '''drive''', says Chip.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2017-07-01|author=Huw Lewis|title=To Hear the Skylark's Song|publisher=Parthian Books|isbn=9781912109876
|text=The coaches dropped us where we had begun, outside the chapel; each child in turn piping up, 'Thank you, '''drive'''!' as we disembarked.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2018-06-28|author=Wilf Merttens|title=Bristol Urban Legends: The Hotwells Crocodile and Other Stories|publisher=The History Press|isbn=9780750988643
|text=Soon every stop on every route was once again punctuated by rounds of 'Cheers '''drive'''! Cheers '''drive'''! Cheers '''drive'''!' And with this little nicety reinstated, all was relatively well in Bristol town.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2019-05-30|author=Ed Clarke|title=The Secret Dragon|publisher=Penguin UK|isbn=9780241360545|text='Cheers, '''Drive'''!' said Dylan as they climbed off the bus.}}

====Usage notes====
* In connection with a mass-storage device, originally the word "drive" referred solely to the reading and writing mechanism. For the storage device itself, the word "[[disk]]" or "[[disc]]" (depending on the type of device) was used instead. This remains a valid distinction for components such as floppy drives or CD drives, in which the drive and the disk are separate and independent items. For other devices, such as hard disks and flash drives, the reading, writing and storage components are combined into an integrated whole, and cannot be separated without destroying the device. In these cases, the words "disk" and "drive" are used interchangeably.

