{{en-noun|~}}

# A group of sentient beings, particularly people, distinguished by common ancestry, heritage or characteristics (see Wikipedia's article on {{w|Historical race concepts|historical definitions of race}}):
#: {{synonyms|en|breed|strain|kind|lineage|people|variety}}
## A large group of people [[distinguish]]ed from others on the basis of a common [[heritage]].
##: {{synonyms|en|clan|ethnicity|ethnic group|ethnie|nationality|tribe}}
##: {{ux|en|The Canadian '''race''' is one of the most vigorous on the globe.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1838|author=w:Abraham Lincoln|url=https://founding.com/founders-library/american-political-figures/abraham-lincoln/address-to-the-young-mens-lyceum-of-springfield-illinois/|title=Young Men's Lyceum address|passage=We toiled not in the acquirement or establishment of them—they are a legacy bequeathed us, by a once hardy, brave, and patriotic, but now lamented and departed '''race''' of ancestors.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=1895-11-11|author=w:Joseph Chamberlain|journal=Speech given to the Imperial Institute|passage=I believe that the British '''race''' is the greatest of the governing '''races''' that the world has ever seen.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1917|month=February|author=Will Irwin|title=War and the Race|journal=The Advocate of Peace|volume=79|number=2|page=50|text=What is to become of the French '''race''' and the British '''race'''—yes, and the German '''race'''—if this thing keeps up?}}
##* {{seemoreCites|en}}
## A large group of people distinguished from others on the basis of common [[physical]] [[characteristic]]s, such as skin color or hair type.
##: {{hyponyms|en|black|white|caucasian|mongoloid}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Race''' was a significant issue during apartheid in South Africa.}}
##: {{ux|en|The Native Americans colonized the New World in several waves from Asia, and thus they are considered part of the same Mongoloid '''race'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1881|month=July|author=w:Edward Burnett Tylor|journal=Popular Science Monthly|title=The '''Races''' of Mankind|url=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Popular_Science_Monthly/Volume_19/July_1881/The_Races_of_Mankind|volume=19|page=309|text=The '''race''' to which most anthropologists refer the native Americans is the Mongoloid of Eastern Asia, who are capable of accommodating themselves to the extremest climates, and who by the form of skull, the light brown skin, straight black hair, and black eyes, show considerable agreement with the American tribes.}}
##* {{quote-av|en|year=1958|writers={{w|Alan Burgess|Burgess, Alan}}; {{w|Isobel Lennart|Lennart, Isobel}}|title=w:The Inn of the Sixth Happiness|url=https://archive.org/details/the-inn-of-the-sixth-happiness|oclc=732947584|time=1:41:15|publisher=20th Century Fox|roles={{w|Curd Jürgens}} as Colonel Lin Nan and {{w|Ingrid Bergman}} as Gladys Aylward|text=Colonel Lin Nan: Would it offend you to be loved by a man of another '''race'''?<br />Gladys Aylward: It would honor me.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=2012|month=March-April|author=w:Jan Sapp|title=Race Finished| volume=100| issue=2| page=164| magazine=w:American Scientist| url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/race-finished| passage=Few concepts are as emotionally charged as that of '''race'''. The word conjures up a mixture of associations—culture, ethnicity, genetics, subjugation, exclusion and persecution. But is the tragic history of efforts to define groups of people by '''race''' really a matter of the misuse of science, the abuse of a valid biological concept?}}
##* {{seemoreCites|en}}
## A large group of people distinguished from others on the basis of shared characteristics or qualities, for example social qualities.
##: {{ux|en|The advent of the Internet has brought about a new '''race''' of entrepreneur.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=1872-10-05|quotee=Prof. G[eorge] C[linton] Swallow|title=Table-Talk|journal=Appletons' Journal|volume=8|number=184|page=386|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ep5GAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA386|text=His opinion is founded on the alleged fact that there are scarely any drunkards in the wine-producing regions, where people drink wine with their food as freely as we do tea or coffee.  "Give us what good wine we need," says the professor, "and the temperance crusade will be wellnigh ended when the present '''race''' of drunkards have passed away.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1911| author=[[w:Robert W. Service|Robert W[illiam] Service]]| chapter=The Men That Don't Fit In| title=The Spell of the Yukon| passage=There's a '''race''' of men that don't fit in, / A '''race''' that can't stay still; / So they break the hearts of kith and kin, / And they roam the world at will.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Eunjoo M. Kim|title=Feasting on the Word: Preaching the Revised Common Lectionary, Year C|editors=David L. Bartlett; Barbara Brown Taylor|page=249|text=Indeed, all of us are called to join the '''race''' of faith. Our identity as Christians is not a burden or an obstacle for our lives, but is rather a gift, {{...}}}}
##* {{seemoreCites|en}}
## {{lb|en|fantasy|scifi|mythology}} A large group of [[nonhuman]]s [[distinguish]]ed from others on the basis of a common [[heritage]].
##: {{ux|en|A treaty was concluded between the '''race''' of elves and the '''race''' of men.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1898|author=Herman Isidore Stern|title=The gods of our fathers: a study of Saxon mythology|page=15|passage=There are two distinct '''races''' of gods known to Norse mythology[.]}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Clifford A. Pickover|title=The Science of Aliens|page=47|text=Imagine a '''race''' of aliens that develops on a dimly lit world perpetually shrouded in clouds so that vision would be less useful for survival than on Earth.}}
##* {{quote-video game|en|developer=w:BioWare|title={{w|Mass Effect (video game)|Mass Effect}}|genre=[[science fiction|Science Fiction]]|location=Redwood City|publisher=Electronic Arts|year=2008|system=PC|scene=Normandy SR-1|isbn=9780784546642|oclc=246633669|passage=Tali: My father is responsible for the lives of seventeen million people—our entire '''race''' is in his hands. And I'm his only child.|footer=<small>''(Note: Tali is a {{w|Quarian}}, an [[extraterrestrial]] species.)''</small>}}
##* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# A group of [[organism]]s distinguished by common characteristics; often an [[informal]] [[infraspecific]] [[rank]] in [[taxonomy]], below [[species]]:
#: {{synonyms|en|kind|strain|variety}}
## {{lb|en|biology}} A [[population]] [[geographically]] separated from others of its [[species]] that develops significantly different characteristics; a [[mating]] group.
##: {{synonyms|en|ecospecies|ecotype|subspecies}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1968|month=December|author=Dale W. Rice; Victor B. Scheffer|title=A List of the Marine Mammals of the World|others=Special Scientific Report—Fisheries number 579|section=Phoca vitulina|publisher=United States Fish and Wildlife Service|page=6|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=h-PYLeB1h5wC&pg=RA4-PA4|text=Two '''races''' are certainly valid.  The Atlantic '''race''' (P. v. vitulina) is distinguishable from the Pacific '''race''' (P. v. richardi Gray, 1864) by skull characters.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=[[w:E. O. Wilson|Edward O[sborne] Wilson]]|title=Sociobiology: The New Synthesis|edition=25th anniversary|publisher=Harvard University Press|isbn=9780674002357|page=9|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FEsCBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA9|text=A population that differs signicatly from other populations belonging to the same species is referred to as a ''geographic '''race''''' or ''subspecies''.  Subspecies are separated from other subspecies by distance and geographic barriers that prevent the exchange of individuals, as opposed to the genetically based "intrinsic isolating mechanisms" that hold species apart.}}
##* {{seemoreCites|en}}
## {{lb|en|botany}} A strain of [[plant]] with characteristics causing it to differ from other plants of the same [[species]].
##: {{hyponyms|en|cultigen|cultivar|indigen}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1859 |author=w:Charles Darwin |chapter=Variation under Domestication| title=w:On the Origin of Species| passage=Nevertheless, as our varieties certainly do occasionally revert in some of their characters to ancestral forms, it seems to me not improbable, that if we could succeed in naturalising, or were to cultivate, during many generations, the several '''races''', for instance, of the cabbage, in very poor soil (in which case, however, some effect would have to be attributed to the direct action of the poor soil), that they would to a large extent, or even wholly, revert to the wild aboriginal stock.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1948|month=June|chapter=Development of '''Races'''|title=Woody-Plant Seed Manual|others=Miscellaneous Publication no. 654|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=United States Department of Agriculture|page=15|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jfnQ3hVDpmgC&pg=PA15|text=Tree '''races''' develop not only in different latitudes, but also at different altitudes and within mountainous regions.  Since climate changes markedly with altitude as well as latitude, both kinds of development are included in the term climatic '''races'''.  In addition, soil or site '''races''' may develop in areas similar climatically but characterized by different soil or site conditions.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|start_date=11 September 1995|date=14 September 1995|title=A Genetic Evaluation of Three Potential '''Races''' of the Rare Kachina Daisy|author=Loreen Allphin; Michael D[ennis] Windham; Kimball T[aylor] Harper|journal=Southwestern Rare and Endangered Plants: Proceedings of the Second Conference|location=Flagstaff, Arizona|page=75|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OvRzhwAPjnoC&pg=PA75|text=Our genetic evaluation suggests that the morphologically distinct '''race''' (Dolores River) is more closely related to the type materials than the ecologically distinct, high-elevation '''race'''.}}
##* {{seemoreCites|en}}
## {{lb|en|animal husbandry}} A [[breed]] or [[strain]] of [[domesticate]]d animal.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1596–1599|author=w:William Shakespeare| title=w:The Merchant of Venice| section=act 5, scene 1| passage=For do but note a wild and wanton herd, / Or '''race''' of youthful and unhandled colts, / Fetching mad bounds.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1799|author=Joshua Rowlin|title=The Complete Cow-Doctor; Or, Farmer's Companion|location=London|edition=2nd|page=42|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=vHdZAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA42|text=They have another breed, called the Dunlop cows, which are allowed to be the best '''race''' for yielding milk in Great Britain or Ireland, not only for large quantities, but also for richness in quality.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1875|title=The Living World|author=Augustus C. L. Arnold|volume=1|page=88|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jasaAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA88|location=Boston|publisher=Samuel Walker & Co|text=Great St. Bernard Dog—This '''race''' is nearly allied to the Newfoundland Dog in form, stature, hair, and colors; but the head and ears are like that of a Water Spaniel.}}
##* {{seemoreCites|en}}
## {{lb|en|mycology|bacteriology}} A [[strain]] of [[microorganism]], [[fungi]], etc.
##: {{synonyms|en|pathotype|pathovar}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=1977-03-24|title=Why is cereal fungus so resistant?|journal=New Scientist|volume=73|number=1044|page=697|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fqCC-Lcdn3oC&pg=PA697|text=Now Mary MacDonald of the Plant Breeding Institute at Maris Lane, Cambridge, has made an interesting study which has duplicated the conditions under which new '''races''' arise. And she has produced at least one new fungal '''race'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=2018|month=December|author=Anna Kolobaeva; Olga Kotik|title=Technological Approaches to Cider Quality|journal=Advances in Engineering Research|volume=151|publisher=Atlantis Press|doi=10.2991/agrosmart-18.2018.68|url=https://www.atlantis-press.com/article/55908713.pdf|text=The type of microorganisms is a very important factor influencing the quality of cider. Yeast of various producers and '''races''' result in different taste and flavor.}}
##* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[category]] or [[kind]] of thing distinguished by common characteristics.
#: {{synonyms|en|class|type|Thesaurus:class}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1786|author=w:Robert Burns|title=Address to the Haggis|text=Fair fa' your honest, sonsie face, / Great chieftain o the puddin'-'''race'''!}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Peculiar [[flavour]], [[taste]], or [[strength]], as of [[wine]]; that quality, or assemblage of qualities, which indicates origin or kind, as in wine; hence, characteristic flavour.
#: {{synonyms|en|typicity}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1625|author=w:Philip Massinger| title=w:A New Way to Pay Old Debts| section=act 1, scene 3| passage=Is it [the wine] of the right '''race'''?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1827|author=w:Christian Isobel Johnstone|title=Elizabeth de Bruce|chapter=A Country Sunday Evening|location=New York|publisher=W. Blackwood|volume=1|page=130|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=-ycMAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA130|text=On the day following Elizabeth's interview with Gideon, this innocent relish—the olives which gave zest, or the walnuts which gave '''race''' and richness, to Monkshaugh's moderate hebdomadal glass of old claret—was not forgotten.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1875|author=w:Sebastian Evans|chapter=The Eve of Morte Arthur|title=In the Studio|location=London|publisher=Macmillan & Co|pages=164–165|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=x91LAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA164|text=So sang the poet in his pride of place, / And Arthur bade the pages plenish well / The cups of all the kings with wine of '''race''', / Osaye or Algarde, Rhenish or Rochell, / Vernage of Venice, Rhodes or Famagust, / Sweet Malvoisie or Cretan Muscadel,—}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Characteristic [[quality]] or [[disposition]].
#: {{synonyms|en|attribute|idiosyncrasy|quirk|trait|Thesaurus:characteristic}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1603–1604|author=w:William Shakespeare| title=w:Measure for Measure| section=act 2, scene 4| passage=And now I give my sensual '''race''' the rein.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1685|author=[[w:Sir William Temple, 1st Baronet|Sir William Temple]]| title=Upon the Gardens of Epicurus; or, Of Gardening| passage={{...}}some great '''race''' of fancy or judgment in the contrivance{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1807|author=w:Alexander Fraser Tytler, Lord Woodhouselee|title=Memoirs of the Life and Writings of the Honourable Henry Home of Kames|volume=1|others=book 2, chapter 1|location=Edinburgh|publisher=William Creech|pages=181|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=q6HkAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA181|text=His conversation, too, had a '''''race''''' and ''flavour'' peculiarly its own: it was nervous, sententious, and tinctured with genuine wit.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[sexual]] [[activity]] of [[conceiving]] and [[bearing]] biological [[offspring]].
#: {{synonyms|en|breeding|procreation|progenation|propagation|reproduction}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1658|author=w:Edward Topsell|chapter=Of the horse|title=The History of Four-Footed Beasts and Serpents|page=234|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/historyoffourfoo00tops/page/234/|text=It behooveth therefore that the Mares appointed for '''race''', be well compacted, of a decent quality, being fair and beautiful to look upon, the belly and loins being great, in age not under three nor above ten years old.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1667|author=w:John Milton|title=w:Paradise Lost|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20/pg20-images.html|section=book 7|text=Male he created thee, but thy consort / Femal for '''Race'''; then bless’d Mankinde, and said, / Be fruitful, multiplie, and fill the Earth[.]}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|uncountable}} [[ancestry|Ancestry]].
#: {{synonyms|en|extraction|family|house|line|pedigree|stirp}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1609|author=w:Ben Jonson|title=Epicœne, or The Silent Woman|section=act 3, scene 2|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/4011/4011-h/4011-h.htm|text=Yes, madam, believe it, she is a gentlewoman of very absolute behaviour, and of a good '''race'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1785|author=[[w:Nathaniel Wraxall|Nathaniel William Wraxall]]|chapter=Henry the Second|title=The History of France Under the Kings of the '''Race''' of Valois|location=London|publisher=C. Dilly|edition=2nd|volume=2|pages=52–53|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rXU_AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA53|text=Wars of religion, more sanguinary, cruel, and ruinous than even those of Henry the fifth and Edward the third, rise in succession under the three last princes of the '''race''' of Valois.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Barry Lyndon|chapter=I|passage=That very estate which the Lyndons now possess in Ireland was once the property of my '''race'''.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A step in a [[lineage]] or [[succession]]; a [[generation]].
#: {{synonyms|en|age group|cohort}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1738|origyear=1728|author=w:Ephraim Chambers|chapter=Race|title=Cyclopaedia: Or, An Universal Dictionary Of Arts and Sciences|location=London|publisher=D. Midwinter|edition=2nd|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=DHOj9vGhuroC&pg=PP496|text=In ſeveral orders of knighthood, as in that of Malta, &c. the candidates muſt prove a nobility of four '''''races''''' or deſcents.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1870|author=w:Charles Dickens|chapter=The Nun's House|title=The Mystery of Edwin Drood|text=Perhaps this is the reason why it is an article of faith with the servants, handed down from '''race''' to '''race''', that the departed Tisher was a hairdresser.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1929|month=December|author=[[w:Johnny Burke (Newfoundland songwriter)|Johnny Burke]]|chapter=No Short Skirts To Their Knees|title=Burke's Popular Songs|location=St. John's, Newfoundland|publisher=Long Brothers|url=https://collections.mun.ca/digital/collection/cns/id/62880|text=For the old stock is fast dying out, Jennie, / And a young '''race''' is taking their place, / In our grandmothers' day they had sense, Jennie, / No powder or paints on their face.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|uncountable}} [[progeny|Progeny]], [[offspring]], [[descendant]]s.
#: {{synonyms|en|get|issue|seed}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1606–1607|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=w:Antony and Cleopatra|section=act 3, scene 13|text=Have I my pillow left unpressed in Rome, / Forborne the getting of a lawful '''race''', / And by a gem of women, to be abused / By one that looks on feeders?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1737|year_published=1814|author=[[w:Richard Glover (poet)|Richard Glover]]|title=Leonidas|location=Baltimore|publisher=Neal, Wills & Cole|others=book 2|page=35|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fNUhAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA35|text=The good man besought him.  Let the king / Propitious hear a parent.  In thy train / I have five sons.  Ah! leave my eldest born, / Thy future vassal, to sustain my age!' / The tyrant fell reply'd. 'Presumptuous man, / Who art my slave, in this tremendous war, / Is not my person hazarded, my '''race''', / My consort?[']}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1842|author=w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|chapter=w:Locksley Hall|title=Poems|volume=2|location=London|publisher=Edward Moxon|page=109|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/tennysonpoems1842-02/page/108|text=There the passions cramp’d no longer shall have scope and breathing-space; / I will take some savage woman, she shall rear my dusky '''race'''. / Iron-jointed, supple-sinew’d, they shall dive, and they shall run, / Catch the wild goat by the hair, and hurl their lances in the sun[.]}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}

