===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|mul|One|ONE}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|mul|en|one}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|mul|[ˈwan]}}<ref>{{cite-book|url=https://www.icao.int/Meetings/anconf12/Document%20Archive/AN10_V2_cons%5B1%5D.pdf|title=Annex 10 to the Convention on International Civil Aviation: Aeronautical Telecommunications; Volume II Communication Procedures including those with PANS status|page=§5.2.1.4.3.1|publisher=International Civil Aviation Organization|edition=6th|date=October 2001|accessdate=23 January 2019|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190331002913/https://www.icao.int/Meetings/anconf12/Document%20Archive/AN10_V2_cons%5B1%5D.pdf|archivedate=31 March 2019}}</ref>

===Noun===
{{head|mul|noun}}

# {{phonetic alphabet|NATO/ICAO=1|num=1|1}}
#: {{syn|mul|unaone|qq1=ITU/IMO}}

{{ICAO letter codewords}}
{{ITU digit codewords}}

===References===
<references/>

==English==
{{number box|en|1}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|wone}}, {{l|en|o}} {{qualifier|both obsolete}}
* {{sense|Arabic numeral}} {{l|en|1}} {{qualifier|see for numerical forms in other scripts}}
* {{sense|Roman numeral}} {{l|en|I}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|oon}}, {{m|enm|on}}, {{m|enm|oan}}, {{m|enm|an}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ān|t=one}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*ain}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*ainaz|t=one}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₁óynos|t=single, one}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|ae}}, {{m|sco|ane}}, {{m|sco|wan}}, {{m|sco|yin|t=one}}; {{cog|frr|ån|t=one}}; {{cog|stq|aan|t=one}}; {{cog|fy|ien||one}}; {{cog|nl|een}}, {{m|nl|één|t=one}}; {{cog|nds-de|een}}; {{cog|de|ein}}, {{m|de|eins|t=one}}; {{cog|da|en|t=one}}; {{cog|sv|en|t=one}}; {{cog|nn|ein|t=one}}, {{cog|is|einn|t=one}}; {{cog|la|ūnus|t=one}} ({{cog|itc-ola|oinos}}); {{cog|ru|оди́н}}, {{cog|es|uno}}. {{doublet|en|a|an|Uno}}.

Use as indefinite personal pronoun influenced by unrelated {{noncog|fr|on}}.<ref name="Etymonline"/>

Verb form from {{inh|en|enm|onen}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/wʌn/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/wan/|[wän]|a=Australia}}
** {{audio|en|En-au-one.ogg|a=AU}}
** {{homophones|en|won}} {{qualifier|Etymology 1}}
* {{IPA|en|/wɒn/|/wʌn/||a=UK}}
** {{rhymes|en|ɒn|s=1}}
** {{homophones|en|won}} {{qualifier|Etymologies 2 and 3}}
* {{enPR|wŭn|a=US}}, {{IPA|en|/wʌn/|[wən]}}
** {{audio|en|En-US-One.oga|a=US}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-one.ogg|a=US}}
** {{rhymes|en|ʌn|s=1}}
** {{homophones|en|won}} {{qualifier|Etymology 1}}
* {{enPR|ōn|a=obsolete}}, {{IPA|en|/oʊn/}}; {{IPA|en|/ɔːn/|a=Early Modern English}}

Around the 14th century, in southwest and western England, the word began to be pronounced with an initial {{IPAchar|/w/}}<ref name="Etymonline">{{R:Etymonline}}</ref><ref name="Century">{{R:Century 1911|atone}}</ref> (compare e.g. {{m|en|woak}}, {{m+|enm|wocke}}, a dialectal form of {{m|en|oak}}),<ref>Christopher Upward, George Davidson, ''The History of English Spelling'' (2011), section "O"</ref> and the spellings {{m|enm|won}} and {{m|enm|wone}} began to be found alongside {{m|enm|on}}, {{m|enm|one}};<ref>''[//quod.lib.umich.edu/m/middle-english-dictionary/dictionary/MED30547 Middle English Dictionary]'': "ō̆n"</ref> the {{IPAchar|/w/}}, though initially nonstandard, had become the norm by the 18th century.<ref name="Etymonline"/> In {{m|en|alone}}, {{m|en|atone}}, and {{m|en|only}},<ref name="Century"/> as well as in the dialectal form {{m|en|'un|un, 'un}}<ref name="Etymonline"/> (and in {{m|en|none}} and {{m|en|no}}),<ref>Oliver Farrer Emerson, ''the History of the English Language'' (1921), page 314</ref> the older pronunciations without {{IPAchar|/w/}} are preserved,<ref name="Etymonline"/><ref name="Century"/> while {{m|en|once}} shows the same {{IPAchar|/w/}}.

====Numeral====
{{head|en|numeral}}

# {{cln|en|cardinal numbers}} The {{l|en|number}} represented by the {{l|en|Arabic numeral}} {{l|en|1}}; the numerical value equal to that cardinal number.
#: {{ux|en|In some religions, there is only '''one''' god.}}
#: {{ux|en|In many cultures, a baby turns '''one''' year old a year after its birth.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''One''' person, '''one''' vote.}}
#* {{RQ:Grey Riders|8|passage=Venters began to count them—'''one'''—two—three—four—on up to sixteen.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|title=One|author=w:Harry Nilsson|year=1968|album=Aerial Ballet|passage='''One''' is the loneliest number that you'll ever do / Two can be as bad as '''one''' / It's the loneliest number since the number '''one'''}}
# {{lb|en|number theory}} The {{l|en|first}} {{l|en|positive}} {{l|en|number}} in the set of {{l|en|natural numbers}}.
# {{lb|en|set theory}} The {{l|en|cardinality}} of the smallest {{l|en|nonempty}} set.
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} The {{l|en|ordinality}} of an element which has no {{l|en|predecessor}}, usually called ''{{l|en|first}}'' or ''{{l|en|number one}}''.

=====Synonyms=====
