===Pronoun===
{{en-pron|subjective and [[objective case|objective]]|it|reflexive and intensive|itself|[[possessive determiner]] and [[pronoun]]|its|plural subjective|they|plural objective case|them}}

# {{non-gloss definition|The third-person singular neuter personal pronoun used to refer to an inanimate object, abstract entity, or non-human living thing.}}
#: {{ux|en|Take this book and put '''it''' on the shelf.}}
#: {{ux|en|Take each day as '''it''' comes.}}
#: {{ux|en|I found a poor little cat. '''It''' seems to be half starving.}}
#* '''2016''', [https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/lets-learn-english-lesson-4/3168920.html VOA Learning English] (public domain)
#*: '''It''' is not a pen. '''It''' is a book.
#*:: {{audio|en|It is not a pen. It is a book.ogg|a=US}}
#* {{quote-web
|en
|date=2018-08-06
|title=Brief Introduction of Nansi
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220216010128/https://nansi.tainan.gov.tw/en/cp.aspx?n=8337
|archivedate=16 February 2022
|work=Nansi District Office, Tainan City
|text=The Nansi District was formerly known as the "Jiaba Community", and was one of the early territories of the Taivoan, as well as where the Zou resided. Later, due to the invasion of the Siraya tribe, the community members later migrated out to regions such as Gongguan, Paoziliao (Kaohsiung County), and Daciouyuan. During the time of the Japanese occupation, because of '''its''' location at the west of the "Nanzihsian River", '''it''' was therefore renamed Nansi ("si" meaning "west"). A village and village hall were established here, under the governance of Sinhua District of Tainan Province. After the war in 1945, '''it''' was renamed Nansi Township, and was changed to Nansi District after the merging of Tainan City and County on December 25th, 2010.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|A third-person singular personal pronoun used to refer to a baby or child, especially of unknown gender.}}
#: {{ux|en|She took the baby and held '''it''' in her arms.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1847|author=Charlotte Brontë|title=Jane Eyre|chapter=IV
|passage=A child cannot quarrel with '''its''' elders, as I had done; cannot give '''its''' furious feelings uncontrolled play, as I had given mine, without experiencing afterwards the pang of remorse and the chill of reaction.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1859|author=Wilkie Collins|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/583/583-h/583-h.htm|title=The Woman in White|passage=I could only encourage Mrs. Clements to speak next of Anne's early days {{...}} "There was nobody else, sir, to take the little helpless creature in hand," replied Mrs. Clements. "The wicked mother seemed to hate '''it'''—as if the poor baby was in fault!—from the day '''it''' was born. My heart was heavy for the child, and I made the offer to bring '''it''' up as tenderly as if '''it''' was my own."<br/>"Did Anne remain entirely under your care from that time?"<br/>"Not quite entirely, sir. Mrs. Catherick had her whims and fancies about '''it''' at times, and used now and then to lay claim to the child, as if she wanted to spite me for bringing '''it''' up.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=Marcus Zusak|title=The Book Thief|section=part 10
|passage=The sky was dripping. Like a tap that a child has tried '''its''' hardest to turn off but hasn't quite managed.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} {{non-gloss definition|An [[affectionate]] third-person singular personal pronoun.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1890|author=George Manville Fenn|title=Black Blood
|passage="{{...}} It's my belief that you don't know your own mind."<br>"I don't, dear," said Hulda, nestling to him.<br>"Why, what a puss '''it''' is!" cried Sir Philip, kissing her tenderly.}}
#* '''1897''', Olive Pratt Rayner (Grant Allen), ''The Type-Writer Girl''
#*: She caught my eye, and laughed. “What a funny girl '''it''' is!” she cried. “You ''are'' so comical! But it isn't the least use your trying to frighten me. I can see the twinkle in your big black eyes; and I like you in spite of your trying to be horrid. Do you know, I liked you from the first moment I saw you.”
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1905|journal=The Harvard Monthly|volume=39-40|page=183
|passage=WILLIAM: You don't like me better?<br>CLARA: Indeed I do.<br>WILLIAM ''(laughing)'': Well, what a dear girl '''it''' is.<br>CLARA ''(flinging her arms around his neck with suddenly disclosed passion):'' Oh, I do love you!}}
# {{lb|en|sometimes|pejorative or offensive}} {{non-gloss definition|A third-person singular personal pronoun used to refer to an animate referent who is [[transgender]] or is neither [[female]] nor [[male]].}}
#* '''1977-1980''', [[w:Lou Sullivan|Lou Sullivan]], personal diary, quoted in '''2019''', Ellis Martin, Zach Ozma (editors), ''We Both Laughed In Pleasure''
#*: Next morning bought her [a drag queen] breakfast & she asked for a couple dollars to get a drink. Gave her $3, walked her to a bar. {{...}} Some teenage boys watched us walking & began shouting. When I left her at the bar door & kissed her goodbye, they began shouting "Ugh! You kissed '''it'''!!"
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|author=Bruce Coville|title=Aliens Ate My Homework|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hYiEAAAAQBAJ&lpg=PP1&dq=aliens%20ate%20my%20homework&pg=PA73#v=onepage|pages=72–73
|passage="Oh, don't be silly. I am neither male nor female. I'm a farfel." {{...}} "'''''It'''''. Refer to me as an '''it'''."<br/>"That seems pretty rude," I said nervously.<br/>"Not as rude as calling me a he or a she," '''it''' said.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=16 Jan 24|title=Singular Purpose: Calculating the Degree of Ethno-Religious Over-representation in the USNo-Fly List|journal=International Journal for Crime, Justice and Social Democracy|author=Matteo Garofalo|text=The  individual  known  as  Maia  Arson  Crimew  was  born  as  Tillie  Kottmann  on  7 August  1999  in  Lucerne,  Switzerland. Kottmann/Crimew has expressed on its website a desire to be referred to by ‘'''it'''’ pronouns (Crimew 2021), so this article will interchangeably refer to '''it''' by its preferred terms as either ‘Maia Arson Crimew’ or ‘'''it'''’.<br>Crimew is  a  well-known  figure  among  hacking and cybersecurity circles. '''It'''  has either  taken  credit  for  or  been attributed  to hacks from several major multinational corporations, including {{...}}}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Used to refer to someone being identified, often on the phone, but not limited to this situation.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''It''''s me. John.}}
#: {{ux|en|Is '''it''' her?}}
#: {{ux|en|'''It''' is I, your king.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|The impersonal pronoun, used without referent as the subject of an impersonal verb or statement (known as the [[dummy pronoun]], [[dummy it|dummy ''it'']] or [[weather it|weather ''it'']]).}}
#: {{ux|en|'''It''' is nearly 10 o’clock.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''It'''’s 10:45.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''It'''’s very cold today.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''It'''’s lonely without you.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|The impersonal pronoun, used without referent, or with unstated but contextually implied referent, in various short [[idiom]]s or expressions.}}
#: ''[[rough it|rough '''it''']]''
#: ''[[live it up|live '''it''' up]]''
#: ''[[stick it out|stick '''it''' out]]''
## {{n-g|Referring to a desirable quality or ability, or quality of being [[successful]], [[fashionable]] or [[in vogue]].}}
##: ''After all these years, she still [[have it|has '''it''']].''
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Seth Wickersham|title=It's Better to Be Feared: The New England Patriots Dynasty and the Pursuit of Greatness|publisher=Liveright Publishing|isbn=9781631498244
|passage=Later that night, a friend told Brady, “Still got '''it'''.” “Never lost '''it''',” he replied. THAT WAS MOSTLY TRUE. But the 2013 season ended with the Patriots coaches wondering whether Brady&#39;s skills were in a subtle but irrevocable decline {{...}}}}
## {{n-g|Referring to sexual intercourse or other sexual activity.}}
##: ''I caught them [[do it|doing '''it''']].''
##: ''Are you [[get it|getting '''it''']] regularly?''
##* {{quote-book|en|title=Dear Doctor Hip Pocrates; advice your family doctor never gave you|year=1968|passage=Is man really the only animal who does "'''it'''" face to face?|page=5|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/deardoctorhippoc00scho/page/4/}}
##* {{RQ:Fry Liar|page=14|passage=The great advantage of English [[public school]] life lies of course in the quality of tutelage it provides. Adrian had received a decent and broad English education in the area of his loins... He had quickly happened upon the truth which many lonely contemporaries would never discover, the truth that everybody, simply everybody, was panting for '''it''' and could, with patience, be ''shown'' that they were panting for '''it'''. So Adrian grabbed what was to hand and had the time of his life genitally—focusing exclusively on his own gender of course, for this was 1973 and girls had not yet been invented.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[sex appeal|Sex appeal]], especially that which goes beyond physical appearance.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1904|author=Rudyard Kipling|title=Mrs Bathurst|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110530022236/http://www.readbookonline.net/readOnLine/8678
|passage='Tisn't beauty, so to speak, nor good talk necessarily. It's just '''It'''. Some women'll stay in a man's memory if they once walked down a street}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1927|author={{w|Dorothy Parker}}|chapter=Madame Glyn Lectures on 'It,' with Illustrations|title=The New Yorker|date_published=1927-11-26|newversion=republished in|title2=The Portable Dorothy Parker|editor2=Brendan Gill|year2=1976|pages2=464-468|publisher2=Penguin|location2=New York|passage=And she had '''It'''. '''It''', hell; she had Those.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|The impersonal pronoun, used as a placeholder for a delayed subject, or less commonly, object; known as the [[dummy pronoun]] (according to some definitions), [[anticipatory it|anticipatory ''it'']] or, more formally in linguistics, a [[syntactic expletive]]. The delayed subject is commonly a ''to''-infinitive, a gerund, or a noun clause introduced by a [[:Category:English subordinating conjunctions|subordinating conjunction]].}}
#: {{ux|en|'''It''' is easy to see how she would think that.}} (with the infinitive clause headed by ''to see'')
#* {{RQ:Dickens Bleak House|text="I know now!" said I. "I have seen this in your face a long while."<br>"No; have you really, my dear?" said he. "What a Dame Durden '''it''' is to read a face!"}}
#: {{ux|en|I find '''it''' odd that you would say that.}} (with the noun clause introduced by ''that'')
#: {{ux|en|'''It''' is hard seeing you so sick.}} (with the gerund ''seeing'')
#: {{ux|en|He saw to '''it''' that everyone would vote for him.}} (with the noun clause introduced by ''that'')
#: {{ux|en|'''It''' is not clear if the report was true.}} (with the noun clause introduced by ''if'')
# [[all|All]] or the [[end]]; something after which there is no more.
#: {{ux|en|Are there more students in this class, or is this '''it'''?}}
#: {{ux|en|That's '''it'''—I'm not going to any more candy stores with you.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} {{n-g|Followed by an omitted and understood relative pronoun}}: That which; [[what]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1643|author=w:Thomas Browne|title=Religio Medici|section=II.2
|passage=In briefe, I am content, and what should providence add more? Surely this is '''it''' [= it which] wee call Happinesse, and this doe I enjoy [...].}}

