==English==
{{wikipedia|Will|mul=Shall and will}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/wɪl/}}
** {{IPA|en|[wɪɫ]|a=RP,US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-will.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-will.ogg|a=US}}
** {{IPA|en|[wɪo̯]|[wɪʊ̯]|a=[[w:L-vocalization|l-vocalizing]]:,_,UK,AU,NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪl|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*welh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|willen}}, {{m|enm|wullen}}, {{m|enm|wollen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|willan||to want}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*willjan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wiljaną}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*welh₁-||to choose, wish}}.

Cognate with {{cog|nl|willen}}, {{cog|nds|willen}}, {{cog|de|wollen}}, {{cog|sv|-}} and {{cog|nn|vilja}}, {{cog|nb|ville}}, {{cog|la|velle||wish|pos=verb}}, {{cog|la|volo}}, {{cog|fr|vouloir}}, {{cog|it|volere}}, and {{cog|sq|vel||to satisfy, be stuffed}}. The verb is not always distinguishable from Etymology 3, below.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|'ll||contraction}}
* {{alter|en|vill|weel||pronunciation spelling}}
* {{alter|en|wil|wille|woll|wyll||obsolete}}

====Verb====
{{head|en|verb|third-person singular simple present|will|present participle|willing|simple past|would|no past participle}}

# {{lb|en|auxiliary}} {{non-gloss definition|Used to express the future tense, sometimes with an implication of [[volition]] or [[determination]] when used in the first person. Compare {{m|en|shall}}.}} {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|One of our salesmen '''will''' visit you tomorrow.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''''will''''' pass this exam.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|IV|text=Good fool, as ever thou '''wilt''' deserve well at my hand, help me to a candle, and pen, ink and paper : as I am a gentleman, I '''will''' live to be thankful to thee for’t.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Tale of Two Cities|passage=“I '''will''', with your leave, relate to you, miss, the story of one of our customers.”|4}}
# {{lb|en|auxiliary}} To be able to, to have the capacity to. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Unfortunately, only one of these gloves '''will''' actually fit over my hand.}}
# {{lb|en|auxiliary}} {{non-gloss definition|Expressing a present tense or perfect tense with some conditional or subjective weakening: "will turn out to", "must by inference".}} {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''will''' be home by now. He always gets home before 6 o'clock.}}
#: {{ux|en|I can't find my umbrella. I '''will''' have left it at home this morning.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Edward Jesko|title=The Polish|passage=“That '''will''' be five zloty.” I reached into my pocket and came up with some coins.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Penny Freedman|title=All The Daughters
|passage=Unless she diverted on the ten minute walk home, she'''’ll''' have got home at about half past.}}
# {{lb|en|auxiliary}} To [[habitually]] do (a given action). {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|[[boys will be boys|Boys '''will''' be boys]].}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Luke|17|7|8|footer=(reflecting similar usage of future in the Greek)|passage=But which of you hauing a seruant plowing, or feeding cattell, '''will''' say vnto him by & by when he is come from the field, Goe and sit downe to meate?  And '''will''' not rather say vnto him, Make ready wherewith I may suppe, and gird thy selfe, and serue me, till I haue eaten and drunken: and afterward thou shalt eate and drinke.}}
#* {{RQ:Mandela Long Walk to Freedom|year=2010|page=28|passage=As young men '''will''', I did my best to appear suave and sophisticated.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2009|author=Stephen Bayley|journal=The Telegraph|section=24 Sep 09
|passage=How telling is it that many women '''will''' volunteer for temporary disablement by wearing high heeled shoes that hobble them?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|chapter=Connubial bliss in America|title=The Economist
|passage=So far neither side has scored a decisive victory, though each '''will''' occasionally claim one.}}
# {{lb|en|auxiliary}} To [[choose]] or [[agree]] to (do something); {{ngd|used to express intention but without any temporal connotations, often in questions and negation}}. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Will''' you marry me?}}
#: {{ux|en|I’ve told him three times, but he '''won’t''' take his medicine.}}
# {{lb|en|now|uncommon|or|literary|transitive}} To [[wish]], [[desire]] (something). {{defdate|chiefly 9th|19th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Do what you '''will'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|God '''willed''' it.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1450|title=The Macro Plays
|passage=If thou '''wilt''' fare well at meat and meal, come and follow me.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1601|author=William Shakespeare|title=Twelfth Night, or What You Will|passage=''Twelfe Night, Or what you '''will''''' (original spelling)}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Matthew|8|2|text=And behold, there came a leper and worshipped him, saying, Lord if thou '''wilt''', thou canst make me clean.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1944|author=St. Augustine|translator=FJ Sheed|title=Confessions
|passage=Grant what Thou dost command, and command what Thou '''wilt'''.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare|intransitive}} To wish or desire (that something happen); to [[intend]] (that). {{defdate|9th|19th c.}}
#: {{usex|en|Consider, [[if you '''will''']], the possibility that the sherry glasses were misplaced accidentally.}}
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Matthew|XXVI|text=the disciples cam to Jesus sayinge unto hym: where '''wylt''' thou that we prepare for the to eate the ester lambe?}}
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|text=see God's goodwill toward men, hear how generally his grace is proposed, to him, and him, and them, each man in particular, and to all. 1 Tim. ii. 4. "God '''will''' that all men be saved, and come to the knowledge of the truth."}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Implying ''will go''.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|text=I’'''ll''' to England.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Historically, '''will''' was used in the simple future sense only in the second and third person, while ''shall'' was used in the first person. Today, that distinction is almost entirely lost, and the verb takes the same form in all persons and both numbers. Similarly, in the intent sense, '''will''' was historically used with the second and third person, while ''shall'' was reserved for the first person. See also the usage note at '''[[shall]]'''.
* The present tense is '''will''' and the past tense is '''[[would]]'''. Early Modern English had a past participle '''[[would]]''' which is now obsolete.
*: Malory: ‘Many tymes he myghte haue had her and he had wold’. John Done: ‘If hee had would, hee might easily [...] occupied the Monarchy.’
* Formerly, '''will''' could be used elliptically for "will go" &mdash; e.g. "I'll to her lodgings" (Marlowe).
* The present participle does not apply to the uses of '''will''' as an auxiliary verb.
* The form of '''will''' with the enclitic {{m|en|-n't}} (or the present tense negative form of ''will'' in the analysis in which {{m|en|-n't}} is an inflectional suffix) is {{m|en|won't||will not}} (rather than the form that would be expected based on a regular application of ''-n't'', {{m|en|willn't}}), while the corresponding form of the past tense ''would'' is {{m|en|wouldn't}}.

=====Derived terms=====
{{col-auto|en|a drowning man will clutch at a straw|a hit dog will holler|accidents will happen|accidents will happen in the best regulated families|boys will be boys|butter will not melt in someone's mouth|cool heads will prevail|cooler heads will prevail|even a worm will turn|faith will move mountains|five will get you ten|give someone an inch and someone will take a mile|heads will roll|if you lie with dogs you will get fleas|if you will|kids will be kids|longways for as many as will|murder will out|one will know the reason why|only time will tell|will you marry me|take care of the pennies and the pounds will take care of themselves|that and a dollar will get you a cup of coffee||the poor we will always have with us|the revolution will not be televised|the terrorists will have won|there are none so blind as those who will not see|throw enough mud at the wall and some of it will stick|time alone will tell|time will tell|too much pudding will choke a dog|truth will out|what's bred in the bone will come out in the flesh|when the cat's away the mice will play|who keeps company with the wolf will learn to howl|will call|will do|will-they-won't-they|by their fruits you will know them|I will yeah|ten will get you twenty|will the real someone please stand up|the sky will fall on your head}}

