{{trans-see|legal document|testament}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|willen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|willian|t=to will}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*willjōn|t=to will}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*welh₁-|t=to choose, wish}}. Cognate with {{cog|nds-de|willen}}, {{cog|de|willen}}. The verb is not always distinguishable from Etymology 1, above.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|past2=would|past2_qual=rare}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To [[instruct]] (that something be done) in one's will. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bequeath]] (something) to someone in one's will (legal document). {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''willed''' his stamp collection to the local museum.}}
# {{senseid|en|enact intention}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To exert one's force of will (intention) in order to compel, or attempt to compel, something to happen or someone to do something. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|All the fans were '''willing''' their team to win the game.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|III|i|passage=They '''willed''' me say so, madam.}}
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|Love's Cure|1|2|passage=Send for music, / And '''will''' the cooks to use their best of cunning / To please the palate.}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|bequeath}} {{l|en|bequeath}}, {{l|en|leave}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{col-auto|en|will on|will the good of another}}

