==Aramaic==

===Determiner===
{{head|arc|determiner|head=הָ־|tr=hā-}}

# [[this]]
#: {{l|arc|הָ[[שַׁתָּא]]|tr=hāšattā|gloss=this year}}
#: {{l|arc|הָ[[כָא]]|tr=hāḵā|gloss=right here}}

==Hebrew==

===Etymology 1===
Possibly akin to {{cog|ar|هَا}} and {{m|ar|هٰذَا|t=this}}; Rubin posits a connection {{cog|akk|annûm|𒀭𒉡𒌝|tr=annûm|t=this}}; the final {{m|he||־נ|tr=-n}} in {{m|he||הַנ־*|tr=*han-}} would've been assimilated to the first syllable of consonant-initial words; this form would then be generalised to vowel-initial words as well.<ref>{{cite-book|year=2005|publisher={{w|Brill}}|author={{w|Aaron D. Rubin|Aaron Rubin}}|chapter=Definite Articles|title=Studies in Semitic Grammaticalization|page=76|isbn=9789004370029|doi=10.1163/9789004370029_005}}</ref> 

Compare {{cog|ar|اَل}}, the standardized Arabic definite article, which is of largely disputed etymology, and {{m|he|זה}} (definite {{m|he|הזה|}}).
{{etymid|he|determ}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|he|/ha/|a=Modern Israeli}} {{qualifier|usually}}, {{IPA|he|/he/}} {{qualifier|sometimes; see usage notes below}}

====Article====
{{head|he|article|head=הַ־|sc=Hebr|tr=ha-}}

# {{lb|he|definite article}} [[the|The]].
#: {{ux|he|הוא מגיע '''ל'''כתה עוד מעט.|tr=hú magía '''la'''kitá ód m'át.|t=He's arriving '''at the''' classroom shortly.|inline=1}}
# [[this|This]]: the [[current]] or [[adjacent]]; {{non-gloss definition|used especially with nouns denoting periods of time, and especially {{m|he|sc=Hebr|יוֹם|tr=yom||day}}.}}
#: {{m|he|היום||today|tr=hayóm}}
#: {{m|he|הערב||tonight, this evening|tr=ha'érev}}
#: {{m|he|הבוקר||this morning|tr=habóker}}
#: {{m|he|הלילה||tonight; last night|tr=haláila}}
#: {{ux|he|'''ה'''פעם אין אף אחד שם.|tr='''ha'''pá'am éin áf ekád shám.|t='''This''' time there's no one there.|inline=1}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In traditional grammar, Hebrew common nouns have three “states”: ''indefinite'' (corresponding to English “a(n)/some __”), ''definite'' (corresponding to English “the __”), and ''construct'' (corresponding to English “a(n)/some/the __ of”). Therefore, the definite article was traditionally considered to be an actual ''part'' of the definite noun. In modern colloquial use, the definite article is often taken as a clitic, attaching to a noun but not actually part of it. For example, the Hebrew term for ''school'' is {{m|he|בית ספר|בֵּית־סֵפֶר|house-of book|tr=beit séfer}}; so in traditional grammar, “the school” is {{m|he||בֵּית־הַסֵּפֶר|house-of-the-book|tr=beit-haséfer}}, but in modern colloquial speech, it is often {{m|he||הַבֵּית־סֵפֶר|the-house-of-book|tr=habeit-séfer}}.
* {{m|he||ה־}} is used not only with nouns, but also with attributive adjectives; that is, attributive adjectives agree in definiteness with the nouns they modify. This agreement is strictly semantic; an attributive adjective takes {{m|he||ה־}} if its noun is ''semantically'' definite, even if the noun does not itself have {{m|he||ה־}}, for example if it’s a proper noun.
* When {{m|he||ה־}} follows {{m|he|לְ־||to, for|tr=l'-}}, {{m|he|בְּ־||in|tr=b'-}}, or {{m|he|כְּ־||like|tr=k'-}}, the two merge, with the consonant being {{m|he||ל}}, {{m|he||ב}}, or {{m|he||כ}} and the vowel being that from the {{m|he||ה־}}.
* In traditional grammar, the consonant after {{m|he||ה־}} receives a ''dagésh khazák'' (gemination), unless it’s one of the letters that cannot take a dagésh ({{m|he||א}}, {{m|he||ה}}, {{m|he||ח}}, {{m|he||ע}}, {{m|he||ר}}), in which case the vowel in the {{m|he||ה־}} changes:
** If the consonant after the {{m|he||ה־}} is {{m|he||א}} or {{m|he||ר}}, or if it’s {{m|he||ע}} and its syllable is stressed, then a ''kamáts'' is used instead of a ''patákh''; so, {{m|he||הָ־|tr=ha-}}.
** If the consonant after the {{m|he||ה־}} is {{m|he||ע}} and its syllable is unstressed, then a ''segól'' is used instead of a ''patákh''; so, {{m|he||הֶ־|tr=he-}}.
** If the consonant after the {{m|he||ה־}} is {{m|he||ה}} or {{m|he||ח}}, then a ''patákh'' is used as usual, unless the {{m|he||ה}} or {{m|he||ח}} has unstressed ''kamáts'' or ''khatáf kamáts'', in which case a ''segól'' is used instead.

====See also====
* {{pedia|lang=he|ה"א הידיעה}}

===Etymology 2===
Compare {{cog|ar|أَ}} (also its purported dialectal, and now obsoleted, variant, {{m|ar|هَ|id=interrogative}}) and {{cog|ar|هَلْ}}.
{{etymid|he|interrogative}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|he|/ha/|a=Modern Israeli}}

====Particle====
{{he-particle|wv=הֲ־|tr=ha-}}

# {{lb|he|archaic|or|poetic}} {{non-gloss definition|An interrogative particle, introducing a yes-no question.}}
#: {{ux|he|'''הֲ'''שָׁמַעְתָּ?|tr='''ha'''shamá'ta?|'''Have''' you heard?|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|he|'''הֲ'''יָדַעְתָּ?|tr='''ha'''yadá'ta?|'''Did''' you know?|inline=1}}
#* {{RQ:Tanach|01|04|09|tsrc=KJV
|passage='''{{...}} הֲ'''שֹׁמֵר אָחִי אָנֹכִי׃
|translation='''Am''' I my brother's keeper?
|transliteration='''ha'''shomér akhí anokhí}}
#* {{RQ:Tanach|09a|21|19|tsrc=ESV
|passage='''הֲ'''רָצַחְתָּ&#8199;וְגַם־יָרָשְׁתָּ
|translation='''Have''' you killed and also taken possession?
|transliteration='''ha'''ratsakhtá ve'gám yarashtá}}
#* {{Q|he|Rachel the Poetess|זמר נגה|1|quote='''הֲ'''תִשְׁמַע קוֹלִי, רְחוֹקִי שֶׁלִּי,|t=Do you hear my voice, far one of mine,|tr='''ha'''tishmá kolí, rekhokí shelí,}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Before a ''sh'va'' this prefix has a ''patach''.

====See also====
* {{pedia|lang=he|ה"א השאלה}}

===References===
<references/>

