==North Levantine Arabic==

===Etymology 1===
{{cln|apc|terms borrowed from Aramaic|terms derived from Aramaic}} Partly a continuation of {{inh|apc|ar|هُنَّ|t=they|pos=feminine}}, but considerably influenced by a pronoun borrowed from {{bor|apc|sem-arw|-}}<ref>{{cite-journal|author=Peter Behnstedt|title=Noch einmal zum Problem der Personalpronomina ''hənne'' (3. Pl.), -''kon'' (2. Pl.) und -''hon'' (3. Pl.) in den syrisch-libanesischen Dialekten|journal=Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft|year=1991|volume=141|issue=2|pages=235-252}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|editors=Tal Davidovich, Ablahad Lahdo, and Torkel Lindquist|author=Stephan Procházka|title=From Tur Abdin to Hadramawt: Semitic Studies Festschrift in Honour of Bo Isaksson on the occasion of his retirement|chapter=Feminine and Masculine Plural Pronouns in Modern Arabic Dialects|year=2014|pages=129–148|location=Wiesbaden|publisher=Harrassowitz}}</ref> and cognate to {{cog|syc|ܗܢܘܢ|tr=hennon|t=they}}. The final ''e'' vowel may have also arisen in analogy to the development of the other third-person pronouns: {{m|apc|هو|tr=huww'''e'''|t=he}}, {{m|apc|هي|tr=hiyy'''e'''|t=she}}, and {{m|ajp|هم|tr=humm'''e'''|t=them|pos=now obsolete}}.

{{m-self|apc|هن|tr=hinne}} superficially resembles {{der|apc|ar|هُنَّ}} enough that any other origin might appear questionable. However, some problems show up if we discard the Aramaic altogether:

{{rel-top|Confounding data}}
* {{m-self|apc|هن|tr=hinne}} is a gender-neutral pronoun, even though Arabic originally distinguished {{m|ar|هُنَّ|pos=feminine}} from {{m|ar|هُم|pos=masculine}}. North Levantine Arabic varieties aren't alone in having replaced both with one {{glossary|common}} pronoun, but this common pronoun typically developed from {{noncog|ar|هُم|pos=masculine}}: see {{noncog|ajp|هم|tr=humme}} and {{noncog|arz|هم|tr=humma}} for examples from closely related varieties. What would have made North Levantine speakers settle on {{der|apc|ar|هُنَّ}} gender-neutrally instead?<!--This is long enough already, but it could maybe also stand to incorporate the bit about how (1) -n forms *are* found in Yemeni dialects, but (2) they're in all likelihood from analogy to verb endings that apc doesn't have-->
* The initial ''u'' in {{der|apc|ar|هُنَّ}} differs from the ''i'' in {{m-self|apc|هن|tr=h'''i'''nne}}. This could be explained by North Levantine Arabic's general tendency to merge ''u'' into ''i'' in initial closed syllables, but Behnstedt observes that the pronoun has ''i'' even in dialects that keep the two vowels distinct. These dialects would be expected to have preserved the ''u'' of {{der|apc|ar|هُنَّ}}.
* A variant of this pronoun found widely in Syria is {{m|apc|هنن|tr=hinnon, hinnen}}. The two pronunciations are nearly identical to the surviving {{cog|amw||t=they|tr=hinnun|pos=masculine}} and {{m|amw||t=they|tr=hinnin|pos=feminine}}, but conspicuously different from {{der|apc|ar|هُنَّ}}, which lacks a second {{m|ar||ن}}.
* Additionally, there are Syrian varieties that do gender this pronoun, but in a strikingly Aramaic-like manner. Two examples from {{w|Hama}} are {{m|apc||هنن|tr=hinnon|pos=masculine}} & {{m|apc||هنن|tr=hinnen|pos=feminine}} in {{w|Al-Suqaylabiyah|as-Suqaylibiyyah}} and {{m|apc||هنو|tr=hinno|pos=masculine}} & {{m|apc||هني|tr=hinni|pos=feminine}} in {{w|Morek}}.
{{rel-bottom}}

Behnstedt points out that Aramaic offers an elegant solution to these problems, but notes that modern Syrian forms like {{m|apc|هني|tr=h'''u'''nni}} from the vicinity of {{w|Homs}} and {{m|apc|هنن|tr=h'''u'''nnin}} in nearby {{w|Manin}} provide reason to account for influence from the Arabic pronoun as well.

The spelling {{m-self|apc|هن}} is in direct association with Standard {{der|apc|ar|هُنَّ}}, while the alternative spellings follow the word's pronunciation.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|apc|هنه|هني||spellings}}
* {{alter|apc|هنن}}

====Pronoun====
{{head|apc|pronoun|g=p|tr=hinne|sc=Arab}} or {{l|apc|هنن|tr=hinnen}}

# [[they]]
#: <small>Enclitic form:</small> {{l-self|apc|ـهن|tr=-(h/w/y)un}}

=====See also=====
{{apc-personal pronouns}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|apc|ar|ـهُنَّ}} or {{m|ar|ـهِنَّ}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|apc|ـن}}

====Suffix====
{{head|apc|suffix|head=ـهن|g=p|tr=-un, -hun, -wun, -yun}}

# ''Enclitic form of'' {{l-self|apc|هن|tr=hinne}} or {{l|apc|هنن|tr=hinnen}}
# [[they]], [[them]], [[their]]

=====Usage notes=====
* The pronunciation in careful speech is “-hun”, but the “h” is in the process of being lost. Some speakers currently use “-un” after a consonant and “-hun” after a vowel or diphthong<!-- Becker 1964 if we need a citation -->, and some others use “-yun” after vowels and diphthongs instead, but it's most-common for it to assimilate to the preceding sound:
** “-un” after a consonant
** “-hun” or “-un”<!-- Attested in Cowell 1964... in a footnote --> after the vowel “a”
** “-wun” after the vowel “o”/“u”
** “-yun” after the vowel “e”/“i” and the diphthong “ay”
* It might also be spelled as {{m|apc|ـن}} when it's pronounced as “-un”, “-wun” or “-yun”.
* The form with “h” attracts stress to the syllable before it automatically. The form with a dropped “h” still behaves this way for some speakers, but others allow the automatic stress rules to take precedent:
** {{m-self|apc|[[كَتَب]][[هُن]]|t=he wrote them}} can only be pronounced ''katábhun'', but {{m-self|apc|[[كَتَبُ]][[هن|ن]]|t=he wrote them}} can be either ''katábun'' or ''kátabun''.
** {{m-self|apc|[[جَرِّب]][[هُن]]|t=try them!|pos=masculine imperative}} can be pronounced ''jarríbhun'' or ''jarríbun'', and {{m-self|apc|[[جَرّبُ]][[هن|ن]]|t=try them!}} can be either ''jarríbun'' or ''járrbun''.
* In Lebanon, some speakers (typically older, rural speakers) realize this enclitic pronoun as ''hinne'' in some cases.
** {{m-self|apc|[[كِيف]][[هن]]|t=he wrote them}}, can be pronounced as ''kīf(h)un'' or ''kīfhinne''.

=====See also=====
{{apc-enclitic pronouns}}

===References===
<references />

==South Levantine Arabic==

===Etymology 1===
Partially borrowed from an {{bor|ajp|arc|-}} pronoun cognate to {{cog|syc|ܗܢܘܢ|tr=hennon}}. Compare with {{cog|apc|هن|tr=hinne}}.

The lack of a second {{m|ar||ـن}}, unlike the Aramaic, is due to influence from the form of {{der|ajp|ar|هُنَّ|t=they|pos=feminine plural}} and analogy with the final vowels of other third-person pronouns: {{m|ajp|هو|tr=huww'''e'''|t=he}}, {{m|ajp|هي|tr=hiyy'''e'''}}, and possibly the aforementioned {{m|ajp|هم|tr=humm'''e'''}}.

The spelling {{m-self|ajp|هن}} is in direct association with Standard {{der|ajp|ar|هُنَّ}}, while the alternative spellings follow the word's pronunciation.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|ajp|هنه}}

====Pronoun====
{{ajp-pronoun|head=هنّ|g=p|tr=hinne}}

# {{lb|ajp|Galilee}} [[they]]
#: {{syn|ajp|هم|tr=humme}}
#: <small>Enclitic form:</small> {{l-self|ajp|ـهن|tr=-hen}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|ajp|ar|ـهُنَّ}} or {{m|ar|ـهِنَّ}}.

====Suffix====
{{ajp-suffix|head=ـهن|g=p|tr=-hen}}

