Page 91 ħ: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Ħ|ꟸ|ћ|ℏ|Appendix:Variations of "h"}}
{{character info}}
==Translingual==

===Etymology===
Originally devised for use in [[Maltese]]; see [[ħ#Maltese|that entry]] for more details. First adopted as the IPA symbol for the voiceless pharyngeal fricative in 1921.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|mul|Voiceless_pharyngeal_fricative.ogg|IPA}}

===Symbol===
{{character info|𐞕}}
{{wikipedia|Voiceless pharyngeal fricative}}
{{head|mul|symbol}}

# {{lb|mul|IPA}} a [[voiceless]] [[pharyngeal]] [[fricative]].
# {{lb|mul|superscript {{angbr IPA|𐞕}}|IPA}} {{IPAfont|[ħ]}}-fricated [[release]] of a plosive (e.g. {{IPAfont|[ʡ𐞕]}}, sometimes implying an [[affricate]] {{IPAfont|[ʡ͜ħ]}}); {{IPAfont|[ħ]}}-coloring; or a weak, fleeting or epenthetic {{IPAfont|[ħ]}}.
# {{lb|mul|[[w:Americanist phonetic notation|NAPA]]}} as IPA.
#: {{syn|mul|ḥ}}
# {{lb|mul|physics}} {{ng|a typesetting error for the reduced Planck's constant, {{angbr|[[ℏ]]}}.}}

====See also====
{{Latn-script}}

[[Category:IPA symbols|H]]

==Maltese==

===Etymology===
The letter mainly continues {{inh|mt|ar|ح}} and {{inh|mt|ar|خ}}. These two phonemes were merged into {{IPAchar|/ħ/}} around {{w|Valetta}} since at least the 18th century, but continued to be distinguished as {{IPAchar|/ħ/}} and {{IPAchar|/x~χ/}} elsewhere. By the later 19th century, however, this merger had established itself throughout the language (thus significantly earlier than that of {{m|mt|għ}}, which see).

Additionally it may continue Arabic {{l|ar|ه|tr=-}} {{IPAchar|/h/}}. This letter was mostly vowelised (see {{m|mt|h}}), but as first or last [[radical]] of a root it sporadically underwent [[fortition]] instead.

The symbol <ħ> was apparently first used by {{coinage|mt|Giusseppe Martin Cannolo|in=1822|nat=Maltese|occ=canon|nobycat=1|notext=1|w=-}} in the first Maltese written Gospel, though it was not popularised until the latter half of the 19th century and the first half of the 20th century, being officially adopted in 1924.<ref>{{cite-book|year=2013|chapter=A note on the dating of ''ħ'', ''għ'' and ''x'' in Maltese|author=Arnold Cassola|title=Perspectives on Maltese Linguistics|editors=Albert Borg, Sandro Caruana, Alexandra Vella|publisher={{w|Akademie Verlag}}|page=16|doi=10.1524/9783050065144.13}},</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{qualifier|phoneme}} {{IPA|mt|/ħ/}}

===Letter===
{{head|mt|letter|upper case|Ħ}}

# {{Latn-def|mt|letter|11|ħe}}

====Usage notes====
* In contemporary Maltese, the letter ''ħ'' represents {{IPAchar|/ħ/}} in all positions. Chiefly word-finally, the same phoneme may also be represented by {{m|mt|għ}} or {{m|mt|h}}. The actual realisation varies (by position and speaker) between {{IPAchar|[χ]}}, {{IPAchar|[ħ]}}, and {{IPAchar|[h]}}. This variation is purely allophonic.

====References====
<references/>

===See also===
* {{list:Latin script letters/mt}}
-------- end text --------
