Page 116 Bakı: Skipped, no changes
Page 116 Bakı: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Appendix:Variations of "baki"}}
==Azerbaijani==
{{wikipedia|lang=az}}
{{az-variant|Бакы|a-cls=باکو|a-n=باکؽ}}

===Etymology===
Baku is long attested under the Perso-Arabic name [[باکو]] (''Bākū''). Early Arabic sources also refer to the city as ''Bākuh'' and ''Bākuya'',<ref>{{cite-book|last=Dunlop|first=D.M.|last2=Bennigsen|first2=A.|last3=Bosworth|first3=C.E.|title=Historic Cities of the Islamic World|editor=Bosworth, C. Edmund|authorlink3=Clifford Edmund Bosworth|publisher=Brill|location=Leiden & Boston|year=2007|url=http://nessia.org/files/original/7fb5287183e94fb6ea34e8c74d7f9de9.pdf|page=47|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180920151802/http://nessia.org/files/original/7fb5287183e94fb6ea34e8c74d7f9de9.pdf|archivedate=20 September 2018}}</ref> all of which seem to come from a [[Persian]] name. Further etymology is unknown.

A [[folk etymology|popular etymology]]<ref>[http://bakucity.preslib.az/en/page/oj8veb7H6a "The origin and etymology", ''Bakucity.preslib.az'']</ref> in the 19th century considered it to be derived from Persian {{m|fa|بادکوبه|tr=bâd-kube||wind-pounded}}, compound of {{m|fa|باد|tr=bâd}} + {{m|fa|کوب|tr=kub}} + {{m|fa|ـه|tr=-e}}.<ref name="Web.archive.org">{{cite-web|url=http://iranchamber.com/podium/culture/020920_politicizing_linguistics.php |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20071013212555/http://iranchamber.com/podium/culture/020920_politicizing_linguistics.php |archivedate=13 October 2007 |title=Culture & Religion on Podium: Politicizing Linguistics | author=Reza Ordoubadian| work=The Podium| publisher=Iran Chamber Society |accessdate=25 July 2009}}</ref> This etymology was first proposed in the 17th century-chronicle {{w|Tarikh-e Alam-ara-ye Abbasi|''Tārīkh-i ʕālam-ārā-yi ʕabbāsī''}}.

Another and even less probable folk etymology explains the name as deriving from ''Baghkuy'', from {{noncog|pal||tr=*bgkwdk'|ts=*baykōyōē, *bakkōyōē|sc=Phlv}} compound of {{m|pal||tr=bg|ts=bay, bag|t=God; lord|sc=Phlv}} + {{m|pal||tr=kwd|ts=kōy|t=street, lane|sc=Phlv}} + {{m|pal||tr=-wyk'|ts=-ōē|sc=Phlv}}, meaning "God's town". The name Baghkuy may be compared with [[Baghdad|Baghdād]] ("God-given") in which dād is the Old Persian word for "give".

During {{w|Azerbaijan Soviet Socialist Republic|Soviet rule}}, the city was spelled in Cyrillic as {{m|az|Бакы}} in Azerbaijani (while the [[Russian]] spelling was and still is {{m|ru|Баку́}}). The modern Azerbaijani spelling, which has been using the Latin alphabet since 1991, is {{m|az|Bakı}}; the shift from the Perso-Arabic letter [[و]] (''ū'') to Cyrillic [[ы]] and, later, Latin ''[[ı]]'' may be compared to that in other Azerbaijani words (e.g. compare {{m|az|قاپو|tr=qāpū}} in old Perso-Arabic spelling with modern Azerbaijani {{m|az|qapı|t=door}}) or in suffixes, as و was often used to transcribe the [[vowel harmony]] in Azerbaijani (which was also the practice in [[Ottoman Turkish]]).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|az|[bɑˈcɯ]}}
{{rfap|az}}

===Proper noun===
{{head|az|proper noun}}

# {{place|az|capital city|c/Azerbaijan|t1=Baku}}

====Declension====
{{az-latin-noun-decl|v|ı}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|az|bakılı||[[Bakuvian]]}}

===References===
<references/>

===Further reading===
* {{pedia|Baku#Etymology}}
