Page 198 رح: Skipped, no changes
Page 198 رح: -------- begin text --------
{{also|رخ}}
==Arabic==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|ar|/ruħ/}}

===Verb===
{{ar-verb-form|رُحْ|I}}

# {{inflection of|ar|رَاحَ||2|m|s|actv|impr}}

==North Levantine Arabic==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|apc|حـ|tr=ḥa-}}

===Etymology===
{{contraction|apc||راح|tr=rāḥ}}, as preserved in cognates like {{cog|afb|راح|tr=rāḥ}}. This is in turn related to the verb {{m|apc|راح|tr=rāḥ|t=to go}} and ultimately derived from {{der|apc|ar|رَاحَ|t=to go}}, but the precise link is uncertain:
* Possibly a contraction of the verb's active participle {{m|apc|رايح|tr=rāyiḥ|t=going}}, which is widely attested as a fully-inflected future auxiliary. This requires {{m|apc||رايح|tr=rāyiḥ}} to have somehow developed into the {{m|apc||راح|tr=rāḥ}} we see in other dialects, however. Usually, the sequence ''*-āyi-'' instead contracted to a diphthong ''-ay-'' in Levantine (as in {{m|apc|حَيط|tr=ḥayṭ|t=wall}} from {{ncog|ar|حَائِط}} and {{m|apc|عَيلة|tr=ʕayle|t=family}} from {{ncog|ar|عَائِلَة}}), making it off-putting that there are no surviving traces of an intermediate form {{m|apc||رَيح*|tr=*rayḥ}}.
* Perhaps directly from the verb {{m|apc|راح|tr=rāḥ|t=[he] went}}. Many other auxiliary particles are also verbs frozen in the third-person-singular past conjugation, including {{m|apc|ماعاد|ما عاد|tr=mā ʕād|t=no longer|lit=he didn't end up}}, {{m|apc|قام|tr=ʔām|t={{q|loosely}} up and|lit=he got up}}, {{m|apc|قال|tr=ʔāl|t=apparently, allegedly|lit=he said}}.

===Particle===
{{apc-particle|head=رَح|tr=raḥ}}

# [[going to]]; {{ngd|used with a following verb to form a future tense}}
#: {{ux|apc|رح إشرب هالقهوة.|tr=raḥ ʔišrab ha-l-ʔahwe.|t=I'm going to drink this coffee.}}

====Usage notes====
* Closer to English {{m|en|going to}} than {{m|en|will}} in many cases, the latter instead being expressible by the future-marking sense of {{m|apc|بـ|tr=b-}}.
* Like with {{m|apc|عم|tr=ʕam}}, the verb can appear either with or without the {{m|apc|بـ|tr=b-}} prefix, with no bearing on the construction's meaning either way. The usage with the ''b-'' is primarily found in Lebanon.
* Also like {{m|apc|عم|tr=ʕam}}, can be negated with either {{m|apc|ما|tr=mā}} or {{m|apc|مش|tr=miš}}.

==South Levantine Arabic==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ajp|حـ|tr=ḥa-}}

===Etymology===
{{contraction|ajp||راح|tr=rāḥ}}, as preserved in cognates like {{cog|afb|راح|tr=rāḥ}}. This is in turn related to the verb {{m|ajp|راح|tr=rāḥ|t=to go}} and ultimately derived from {{der|ajp|ar|رَاحَ|t=to go}}, but the precise link is uncertain, compare with {{cog|apc|رح|tr=raḥ}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ajp-IPA|raḥ<p:rˤɑħ>}}
* {{audio|ajp|LL-Q55633582 (ajp)-Muhammad (AdrianAbdulBaha)-رح.wav|a=Ramallah}}

===Particle===
{{ajp-particle|tr=raḥ}}

# {{lb|ajp|auxiliary}} [[will]], [[going to]]
#: {{syn|ajp|بدّ|tr=bidd}}
#: {{ux|ajp|[[رح]] [[شرب|أشرب]] [[ه]][[قهوة|القهوة]].|tr='''raḥ''' ʔašrab ha-l-ʔahwe.|t=I'm '''going to''' drink this coffee.}}

====Usage notes====
* Closer to English {{m|en|going to}} than {{m|en|will}} in many cases, the latter instead being expressible by the present tense (prefix {{m|ajp|بـ|tr=b-}}).
* As is usually the case with auxiliaries, {{m|ajp|رح}} is followed by the subjunctive form of the verb. Unlike in North Levantine Arabic, use of the present tense form is not common.
* Like {{m|ajp|عم|tr=ʕam}}, it can be negated with either {{m|ajp|ما|tr=mā}} or {{m|ajp|مش|tr=miš}}.
-------- end text --------
