Page 327 Reconstruction:Proto-Semitic/šawmān-: Skipped, no changes
Page 327 Reconstruction:Proto-Semitic/šawmān-: -------- begin text --------
{{original research}}
==Proto-Semitic==
{{rfd|sem-pro}}

===Alternative reconstructions===
* {{alter|sem-pro|*sₓawmān-}}

===Noun===
{{sem-noun|m}}

# [[dove]]

====Reconstruction notes====
It was suggested in Rescher (1911)<ref>{{R:ar:Rescher:1911|pages=521–522}}</ref> to identify {{cog|ar|حَمَام|t=dove}} with {{cog|akk|summum|𒋢𒌝𒈬𒌝|tr=summum|t=dove}}.

He knew some examples where Arabic varies {{m|ar|ح}} with {{m|ar|ه}}, such as {{m|ar|حَرَّشَ}} and {{m|ar|هَرَّشَ}}. About the relevant sound-changes for the first phoneme there was little consciousness then:

Akkadian ''s'' corresponding to West-Semitic ''š'' is secured, leaving no need to read {{m+|akk||šummum}} here, such as for {{m|sem-pro|*šaʔal-|t=to cough}} and even {{m|sem-pro|*šabʕ-|t=seven}}, and has been taken since Goetze (1958) as grounds to posit another Proto-Semitic phoneme {{m|sem-pro||*sₓ}}.<ref>{{R:sem-pro:Goetze:1958}}</ref><ref>{{R:sem-pro:SED|volume=1|pages=LXXII–LXXIII}}</ref>

Whereas a sound-law effective in West-Semitic, visible in {{m|sem-pro|*šim|t=if}}, was formulated in Al-Jallad (2015):<ref>{{R:sem-wes-pro:Al-Jallad:2015}}</ref>
# {{cog|sem-wes-pro|-}}: <code>*š > *h / #_, _#</code>
# Incompletely in {{cog|arc|-}} depending on regio- and chronolect, and in Arabic and Ethio-Semitic: <code>*h > *ʔ / {#_VC}<sup>[-stress]</sup></code>.
Arabic seems to have evaded the second sound shift by shifting {{IPAchar|/h/}} to {{IPAchar|/ħ/}}, bringing it closer to a lovely [[phonestheme]] (as in {{ar-root|ح ب ب|nocat=1}}, compare dialectal {{m|ar|حَبْحَب|t=watermelon}}, a fruit as plump as the pigeon), while {{cog|sem-nwe|-}} opted for popular {{IPAchar|/j/}} in the first position. The Arabic and Akkadian forms have been left out in Militarev and Kogan (2005, 2011)<ref>{{R:sem-pro:SED|volume=2|pages=321–322 No. 252|entry=*yawn(-at)-, *wānay-|e=646}}</ref>,<ref>{{R:sem-pro:Weninger-Handbook|page=210}}</ref> reconstructing but with the Amharic and Northwest Semitic, without the Amorite form<ref>{{R:sem-amo:Golinets:2016|page=64}}</ref> later added by Golinets (2016), an unlikely variation {{inh|he|sem-pro||*yawn-at|nocat=1}} ~ {{m|sem-pro||*wānay-|t=dove}}, but the usual terms for “dove, pigeon” in Arabic and Akkadian were left isolated thus, listing instead the Akkadian<ref>{{R:sem-pro:SED|volume=2|pages=257–258 Nr. 196|entry=''*sVm(V)m(-at)-'' ~ ''*cVm(V)m(-at)-''|e=1679}}</ref> together with but an obscure {{m+|ar|سَمَامَة}} and {{m|ar|سُمَّان}}, which has a native derivation, under ''*sVm(V)m(-at)-'' ~ ''*cVm(V)m(-at)-''.

Assuming this form ''*šawmān-'', the ultimate derivation may be transparent, {{suffix|sem-pro||*ān-}} from the verb {{m|sem-pro|*šawam-}} whence {{cog|ar|سَامَ|t=to rove, to pasture}}, in reference to how pigeons walk around, compare the etymology of {{cog|ar|حَجَل|t=partridge}}; hardly {{m|sem-pro|*šamāy-|t=sky}} whence {{m|ar|سَمَا|t=to be high}} since flying high is not distinctive for doves, while the formal match is also less, unless one reconstructs {{m|sem-pro|*šamwān-}}.

The morphological transparency as a well-known but rather meaningless suffix then accounts for the loss of {{suffix|sem-pro||*ān-}} in Akkadian, while the full form can be seen in the terminative {{m|akk|𒋢𒌝𒈠𒌋𒌋|tr=su-um-ma-niš|ts=summāniš|t=like a dove}},<ref>SpTU III 99:28</ref> though {{m|akk|-āniš}} be assumed an alternative form of {{m|akk|-iš|t=''terminative or adverbial''}}. From the full Akkadian form {{m+|ar|سُمَّان|t=quail}} could only be borrowed, alternatively ''-ān-'' is a West Semitic addition and we have to reconstruct {{m|sem-pro|*šawm-}} respectively {{m|sem-pro|*sₓawm-}}, however compare {{m|sem-pro|*ʔirbay-}}, {{m|sem-pro|*ʔirbayān-|t=locust}} where Arabic and {{cog|xeb|-}} point to an archaism in {{m|sem-pro||*-ān-}}, and in either case, the trailing consonant following the stress was hard to hear and could not resist the pressure of the middle {{IPAchar|/m/}}, hence Amorite and Arabic have regressively assimilated it for economy as {{m|sem-pro||*ām-}} – that is, the change in place of articulation, though its assumption in addition to other transformations seem unparsimonious in connecting the common Semitic term, took place because its retention required to change the place of articulation at every instance of the word’s pronunciation and was thus unparsimonious and ultimately unlikely. Recognizing alteration being more likely than not, ''-ān'' is the ''[[lectio difficilior]]'', additionally supported by the other languages exposing it.

