==Maltese==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|mt|ar|هَا}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|mt|/a/}} {{q|generally}}
** {{homophones|mt|-a}} {{q|but may trigger different stem alternations}} <!-- e.g. sieħba vs. seħibha from sieħeb -->
* {{IPA|mt|/ħa/}} {{q|after ''-h, -ħ, -għ''}}
* {{IPA|mt|/ja/}} {{q|after ''-i, -ie''}}
* {{IPA|mt|/wa/}} {{q|after ''-u''}}

===Suffix===
{{head|mt|suffix}}

# {{senseid|mt|pronoun}} {{ngd|3rd-person feminine singular pronominal suffix}}: [[her]]
#: {{syn|mt|tagħha|qq=only in possessive use}}
#: {{suffixusex|mt|ras|t1=head||alt2=ras'''ha'''|t2=her head}}
#: {{suffixusex|mt|taħt|t1=under||alt2=taħt'''ha'''|t2=under her}}
#: {{suffixusex|mt|jisma'|t1=he hears||alt2=jismagħ'''ha'''|t2=he hears her}}

====Usage notes====
* When the suffix {{m|mt||-ha}} follows one of the diphthongs ''-aj, -ej, -aw, -ew'', there are two possible pronunciations. The vowelised ''-h-'' may either be represented as a glide {{IPAchar|[j]}}, {{IPAchar|[w]}}, or it may lengthen the onset of the diphthong. Hence {{m|mt||[[għajn]]ejha|t=her eyes}} may be pronounced {{IPAchar|/ajˈnɛj.ja/}} or {{IPAchar|/ajˈnɛː.ja/}}. This latter is much more common, particularly as the former merges with {{m|mt||għajnejja|t=my eyes}}.
* When the suffix is added to a verb in ''-a'' that disallows [[imala]], the sequence {{m|mt||-aha}} creates a stressed final vowel: {{m|mt|attakka}} (“he attacked”, {{IPAchar|/atˈtak.ka/}}) → {{m|mt||attakkaha}} (“he attacked her”, {{IPAchar|/at.takˈkaː/}}). In the monosyllabic verbs {{m|mt|ra|t=to see}} and {{m|mt|ta|t=to give}}, the suffix vanishes entirely: {{m|mt||ra}} and {{m|mt||raha}} are both pronounced {{IPAchar|/raː/}}. There is, however, a fairly common nonstandard pronunciation that treats {{m|mt||-aha}} by analogy with {{m|mt||-agħha}} (as in {{m|mt||jismagħha}} above). This yields pronunciations such as {{IPAchar|/at.takˈkaħ.ħa/}}, {{IPAchar|/ˈraħ.ħa/}}.

{{cln|mt|personal pronouns}}

