{{also|Horror}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|horrour}} {{qualifier|UK, hypercorrect spelling or archaic}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ǵʰers-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|horer}}, {{m|enm|horrour}}, from {{der|en|fro|horror}}, from {{der|en|la|horror||a bristling, a shaking, trembling as with cold or fear, terror}}, from {{m|la|horrere||to bristle, shake, be terrified}}. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|ōga}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhɒɹ.ə/|a=RP,New England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhɔɹ.ɚ/|a=GenAm,Canada}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-horror.ogg|a=US}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈhɑɹ.ɚ/|a=NYC,Philadelphia}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈhɔɚ/|q=some accents}}
* {{homophones|en|whore|hoar<aa:some <<rhotic>> <<US!American>> accents with the <<horse-hoarse>>>}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒɹə(ɹ)|ɔː(ɹ)|s=2|s1=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} An [[intense]] [[distressing]] [[emotion]] of [[fear]] or [[repugnance]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1712|year_published=1750|author=w:Joseph Addison|title=[[w:Cato, a Tragedy|Cato: A tragedy]]
|page=44
|passage=Their swarthy Hosts wou'd darken all our Plains, / Doubling the native '''Horror''' of the War, / And making Death more grim.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} Something horrible; that which excites horror.
#: ''I saw many '''horrors''' during the war.''
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=July 3, 1898
|newspaper=Philadelphia Inquirer
|page=22
|passage=The Home Magazine for July (Binghamton and New York) contains ‘The Patriots' War Chant,’ a poem by Douglas Malloch; ‘The Story of the War,’ by Theodore Waters; ‘A Horseman in the Sky,’ by Ambrose Bierce, with a portrait of Mr. Bierce, whose tales of '''horror''' are horrible of themselves, not as war is horrible; ‘A Yankee Hero,’ by W. L. Calver; ‘The Warfare of the Future,’ by Louis Seemuller; ‘Florence Nightingale,’ by Susan E. Dickenson, with two rare portraits, etc.
}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Devin Watson|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=6qUSjdABza8C&pg=PR9&dq=%22more+horror%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjbiZTP1c7OAhVExmMKHfBJCqgQ6AEIUTAK#v=onepage&q=%22more%20horror%22&f=false|title=Horror Screenwriting|passage=Could there be stories with more '''horror''' than these?}}
# {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} Intense [[dislike]] or [[aversion]]; an [[abhorrence]].
#* {{RQ:Orczy Miss Elliott|chapter=The Tragedy in Dartmoor Terrace|passage=“Mrs. Yule's chagrin and '''horror''' at what she called her son's base ingratitude knew no bounds ; at first it was even thought that she would never get over it. {{...}} ”}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[genre]] of [[fiction]] designed to [[evoke]] a feeling of [[fear]] and [[suspense]]. [[Category:en:Horror]]
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=February 11, 1917
|newspaper=New York Times
|section=Book reviews
|page=52
|passage=Those who enjoy '''horror''', stories overflowing with blood and black mystery, will be grateful to Richard Marsh for writing ‘The Beetle.’
}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An individual work in this genre.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1990|author=Wayne Jancik|title=The Billboard Book of One-Hit Wonders|isbn=0823075303|page=156|text=A well-received Johnny Fuller R & B '''horror''' called "Haunted House."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|title=Pierluigi on Cinema
|passage={{...}} there were hastily produced B movies, such as the peplums, the spaghetti westerns, the detective stories, the '''horrors'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|colloquial}} A nasty or ill-behaved person; a [[rascal]] or [[terror]].
#: ''The neighbour's kids are a pack of little '''horrors'''!''
# {{lb|en|informal}} An intense [[anxiety]] or a [[nervous]] [[depression]]; often '''the horrors'''.
# {{lb|en|in the plural|informal}} {{cap|delirium tremens}}.
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|page=53|passage=`My belief is that he had the '''horrors''' without knowin' it.'}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|nightmare}}

====Hypernyms====
* {{l|en|speculative fiction}}

