Page 2 hair: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Hair|haïr}}

==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[Image:Weightless hair.jpg|thumb|Hair in low gravity.]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|her}}, {{m|enm|heer}}, {{m|enm|hær}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hǣr}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hār}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hērą|t=hair}}.

Cognate with {{cog|stq|Hier|t=hair}}, {{cog|fy|hier|t=hair}}, {{cog|nl|haar|t=hair}}, {{cog|nds-de|Haar|t=hair}}, {{cog|de|Haar|t=hair}}, {{cog|sv|-}}, {{cog|da|-}} and {{cog|no|hår|t=hair}}, {{cog|is|hár|t=hair}}. Eclipsed non-native {{noncog|enm|cheveler}}, {{m|enm|chevelere|t=hair}}, borrowed from {{noncog|fro|chevelëure|t=hair, head-hair, coiffure, wig}}.

The modern spelling with ''ai'' is not a regular representation of the vowel developed from Middle English. Rather, it is from {{inh|en|enm|here}} (haircloth) influenced by {{noncog|fro|haire}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|hâr|a=UK}}, {{IPA|en|/hɛə/}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-hair.ogg|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Back ache-hair.wav}}
* {{IPA|en|/hɛ(ə)ɹ/|[hɛɚ]|a=US,Canada,Ireland}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-hair.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/heː/|a=AU}}
** {{IPA|en|/hɛːə/|a=Victoria}}
* {{IPA|en|[hiə]|a=NZ}}
* {{homophones|en|hare|;|air|heir<aa:h-dropping>|;|here<aa:NZ,cheer-chair>}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛə(ɹ)|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}} {{tlb|en|but usually in singular}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[pigment]]ed [[filament]] of [[keratin]] which grows from a [[follicle]] on the skin of [[human]]s and other [[mammal]]s.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Shepheardes Calender|June|year=1586
|passage=And draweth new delights with hoary '''hairs'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=28|page=135|passage=Threading its way out from among his grey '''hairs''', and continuing right down one side of his tawny scorched face and neck, till it disappeared in his clothing, you saw a slender rod-like mark, lividly whitish.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[collection]] or [[mass]] of such growths growing from the skin of humans and animals, and forming a [[covering]] for a part of the head or for any part or the whole body.
#: {{ux|en|In the western world, women usually have long '''hair''' while men usually have short '''hair'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Chesnutt House Behind the Cedars|I|passage=Her abundant '''hair''', of a dark and glossy brown, was neatly plaited and coiled above an ivory column that rose straight from a pair of gently sloping shoulders, clearly outlined beneath the light muslin frock that covered them.}}
# {{lb|en|zoology|countable}} A slender outgrowth from the chitinous cuticle of insects, spiders, crustaceans, and other invertebrates. Such hairs are totally unlike those of vertebrates in structure, composition, and mode of growth.
# {{lb|en|botany|countable}} A [[cellular]] outgrowth of the epidermis, consisting of one or of several cells, whether pointed, hooked, knobbed, or stellated.
#: {{ux|en|Internal '''hairs''' occur in the flower stalk of the yellow frog lily (''[[Nuphar]]'').}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} Any [[slender]], [[flexible]] [[outgrowth]], [[filament]], or [[fiber]] growing or projecting from the surface of an object or organism.
#: {{lb|en|uncountable|by extension}} The [[collection]] or [[mass]] of such outgrowths, filaments, or fibers growing or projecting from the surface of an object or organism.
# {{lb|en|countable|engineering|firearms}} A locking spring or other safety device in the lock of a rifle, etc., capable of being released by a slight pressure on a [[hair-trigger]].
# {{lb|en|countable}} Any very small distance, or degree; a [[hairbreadth]].
#: {{ux|en|Just a little louder please&mdash;turn that knob a '''hair''' to the right.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} [[complexity|Complexity]]; [[difficulty]]; the quality of being [[hairy]].
#* '''January 2014''', {{w|Barack Obama}}, quoted in "[https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2014/01/27/going-the-distance-david-remnick Going the Distance"] by David Remnick, in ''The New Yorker''
#*: Having said all that, those who argue that legalizing marijuana is a panacea and it solves all these social problems I think are probably overstating the case. There is a lot of '''hair''' on that policy.

====Usage notes====
* The word '''hair''' is usually used without an article in singular number when it refers to all the hairs on one's head in general. But if it refers to more than one hair, a few hairs, then it takes the plural form with an article and needs a plural verb.
*: {{usex|en|George has ('''-''') brown hair, but I found '''a''' hair on the sofa and suspect he's getting some gray hair'''s'''.}}
*: {{usex|en|George's ''hair is'' brown, but ''one'' hair I found ''was'' grey, so I think there ''are'' probably more grey ''hairs'' on his head as well.}}

