{{also|Flax}}
==English==
[[Image:Grand-Reng_JPG01.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A field of flax ({{taxfmt|Linum usitatissimum|species}})]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|flax}}, from {{inh|en|ang|fleax}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*flahsą}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pleḱ-||to plait}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|flax}}, {{cog|nl|vlas}}, {{cog|goh|flahs}} ({{cog|de|Flachs}}); the Northern Germanic (and most likely the Gothic too<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20090311054445/http://germazope.uni-trier.de/Projects/WBB/woerterbuecher/dwb/wbgui?lemmode=lemmasearch&mode=hierarchy&textsize=600&onlist=&word=Flachs&lemid=GF04948&query_start=1&totalhits=0&textword=&locpattern=&textpattern=&lemmapattern=&verspattern=#GF04948L0 Etymology] in the Deutsches Wörterbuch of Jakob und Wilhelm Grimm: "however, Old Norse hör ... The Gothic word has not been transmitted, but one might guess ''harvs''"</ref>) stem is different.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/flæks/}}
* {{IPA|en|/flɛks/|a=dialectal,obsolete}}<ref>{{R:Bingham IP|75}}</ref>
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-flax.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|æks|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A plant of the genus ''[[Linum]]'', especially {{taxfmt|Linum usitatissimum|species}}, which has a single, slender stalk, about a foot and a half high, with blue flowers. Also known as [[linseed]], especially when referring to the seeds.
# The [[fiber]]s of {{taxfmt|Linum usitatissimum|species}}, grown to make [[linen]] and related textiles.
# The {{vern|flax bush}}, a plant of the genus {{taxlink|Phormium|genus}}, native to New Zealand, with strap-like leaves up to 3 metres long that grow in clumps.

====Usage notes====
The plural ''flaxes'' is used to indicate multiple [[species]] or varieties of flax; otherwise, ''flax'' is [[uncountable]].

====Derived terms====
{{der2|en|flaxen|{{l|en|New Zealand flax}} ({{taxlink|Phormium|genus}} spp.)|blue flax|false flax|flax bow|flax milk|flax seed|flax-dresser|flax-leaved daphne|flax-stick|Lewis flax|mountain flax|yellow flax<br>''For numerous others see'' {{pedia|Linum#Selected species}}|holy flax}}

