{{also|Boll|bòll|Bøll|bøll|Böll|böll}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|bolle|t=pod; shell}} and {{bor|en|dum|bolle|t=round object}}; both ultimately from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*bullô|t=round object; bowl}}. More at {{m|en|bowl}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɒl/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-boll.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/boʊl/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒl|əʊl|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|bole|bowl}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The [[rounded#Adjective|rounded]] [[seed#Noun|seed]]-[[bear#Verb|bearing]] [[capsule]] of a [[cotton]] or [[flax]] [[plant#Noun|plant]].
#* {{RQ:Northup Twelve Years|chapter=XII|page=167|passage=Sometimes the slave picks down one side of a row, and back upon the other, but more usually, there is one on either side, gathering all that has blossomed, leaving the unopened '''bolls''' for a succeeding picking.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1969|author=w:Maya Angelou|chapter=1|title=w:I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings|location=New York|publisher=Bantam|year_published=1971|page=6|url=https://archive.org/details/iknowwhycagedbi000maya
|passage=The champion picker of the day before was the hero of the dawn. If he prophesied that the cotton in today’s field was going to be sparse and stick to the '''bolls''' like glue, every listener would grunt a hearty agreement.}}
# A protuberance or excrescence growing on the trunks of some trees, a [[burl]].
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} An old [[dry measure]] [[equal#Adjective|equal]] to six [[bushel]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Virgil|chapter=Appendix to the Georgicks|translator=James Hamilton|title=Virgil’s [[w:Eclogues|Pastorals]] Translated into English Prose; as also His [[w:Georgics|Georgicks]],{{nb...|with Such Notes and Reflexions as Make Him Appear to have Wrote like an Excellent Farmer. To which is Added, an Appendix, Shewing Scotland’s Chief and Principal Worldly Interest.}}|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Printed by W. Cheyne,{{nb...|in Craig’s Closs opposite to the Cross.}}; [s]old by J. Traill and G. Crawford,{{nb...|booksellers in the Parliament-Closs}}; and by J. Barry{{nb...|bookseller in Glasgow.}}|year=1742|page=43|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BktWAAAAcAAJ&pg=RA1-PA43|oclc=228755640|passage=I ſowed on this Ground, without any Dung or Manure, a [[lippy|Lippy]] of Oats, from which I had a '''Boll''' wanting a [[chopin|Chopin]].}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Robert Bowmaker|chapter=Number LI. Parish of Dunse, (County of Berwick.)|editor=[[w:Sir John Sinclair, 1st Baronet|John Sinclair]]|title=[[w:Statistical Accounts of Scotland|The Statistical Account of Scotland. Drawn Up from the Communications of the Ministers of the Different Parishes]]|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Printed and sold by William Creech [''et al.'']|year=1792|volume=IV|page=386|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=e1deAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA386|oclc=499791781|passage=The farmers ſervants who have families, and engage by the year, are called hinds, and receive 10 '''bolls''' oats, 2 '''bolls''' barley, and 1 '''boll''' peas, which two laſt articles are called hummel corn, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=September and October|author=Charles E. Lee|title=An Ancient Underground Railway|journal=Railway Magazine|page=274, text from early 19th century|text=The waggons are deep and square; wider at the top than at the bottom, and are fast at all sides. The bottom has hinges, and can be let down to discharge the coals, of which these waggons contain about three '''bolls''' each.}}

