==Russian==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ru|онъ||ru-PRO}}

===Etymology===
{{inh+|ru|orv|онъ}}, from {{inh|ru|sla-pro|*onъ}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ru-IPA}}
* {{audio|ru|Ru-он.ogg}}
* {{rhymes|ru|on|s=1}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|ru|pronoun|cat2=personal pronouns|feminine counterpart|она́|neuter counterpart|оно́}}

# third-person masculine singular pronoun: [[he]], [[it]].
#* {{quote-book|ru|year=1891|author=w:ru:Владимир Короленко|title=s:ru:[[Тени_(Короленко)|Тени (Фантазия)]]|chapter=I|location=|publisher=|newversion=English translation from|translator2=w:en:[[Thomas Seltzer (translator)|Thomas Seltzer]]|title2=The Shades, a Phantasy|location2=|publisher2=|year2=1907|page2=|pageurl2=
|text='''Он''' плоди́т недоуме́ния, '''он''' разруша́ет мне́ния, твё́рдо устано́вленные века́ми, '''он''' говори́т о но́вых доброде́телях, кото́рые на́до познава́ть и разы́скивать, '''он''' говори́т о божестве́, кото́рое нам ещё́ неве́домо.|t='''He''' has wrought confusion, '''he''' overthrows beliefs that have existed since the beginning, '''he''' speaks of new virtues which must be recognised and sought for, '''he''' speaks of a Divinity hitherto unknown to us.}}

====Usage notes====
* Whenever a [[preposition]] stands immediately before any of the [[oblique case]]s of the [[third person|third-person]] [[pronoun]] (singular or plural) and directly governs it, then an {{m|ru||н-}} must be prefixed to the pronoun: {{m|ru|[[от]] [[него́]]||from him}}; {{m|ru|[[на]] [[нём]]||on him}}; {{m|ru|[[у]] [[него́]]||he has}}; {{m|ru|[[к]] [[нему́]]||to him}}; {{m|ru|[[с]] [[ним]]||with him}}.
* This comes from Proto-Slavic prepositions such as {{m|sla-pro|*sъ(n)||with}} (compare {{m+|grc|σύν}} and {{m+|la|cum}}), that originally ended in {{m|sla-pro||-n}} and governed oblique cases. Since the prepositions and the pronouns occurred together so often, it was easy to lose track of which word the final {{m|sla-pro||-n}} belonged to, and so it was reinterpreted as part of the pronouns; compare {{m+|ang|an}}, which was reinterpreted in the same way. Proto-Slavic {{m|sla-pro|*sъ(n)}} {{m|sla-pro|*jь|*jьmi}} became modern Russian {{m|ru|[[с]] [[ни́ми]]}}, and this rule was extended to all prepositions governing any third-person pronoun.
* Note that if the preposition does not directly govern {{m|ru|его́}} (i.e., when {{m|ru|его́}} is a [[possessive pronoun]]), then {{m|ru||н-}} is not added: {{m|ru|[[у]] [[его́]] [[брат|бра́та]]||at his brother's}}; {{m|ru|[[от]] [[его́]] [[мать|ма́тери]]||from his mother}}; {{m|ru|[[в]] [[его́]] [[комната|ко́мнате]]||in his room}}.
* When there is another word separating a preposition and any oblique case of {{m-self|ru|он}}, then {{m|ru||н-}} is not added: {{m|ru|[[у]] [[сам|самого́]] его́||with him himself}}.

====Declension====
{{ru-personal pronouns}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|ru|его́}}
* {{l|ru|свой}}

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|ru|но}}

