==Finnish==

===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|fi|instrumental}}
From {{inh|fi|urj-fin-pro|*-in|pos=instrumental suffix|id=instrumental}}, probably from {{inh|fi|urj-pro|*-ma}}.

====Suffix====
{{fi-suffix|noun}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Forms inanimate instrumental nouns from verbs.}}
#: {{suffixusex|fi|avata|t1=to open|avain|t2=key}}
#: {{suffixusex|fi|keittää|t1=to cook, boil|keitin|t2=appliance for cooking/boiling}}
#: {{suffixusex|fi|puhella|t1=to chat, chatter|puhelin|t2=telephone}}
#: {{suffixusex|fi|elää|t1=to live|eläin|t2=animal}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Forms some diminutive nouns.}}

=====Declension=====
Back vowel harmony declension (includes vowels a, o, u)
{{fi-decl-kytkin|-|||i|a}}
Front vowel harmony declension (includes vowels ä, ö, y)
{{fi-decl-kytkin|-|||i|ä}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|fi|id=instrumental}}
{{col-auto|fi|title=compounds
|leivinjauhe
}}

====See also====
* {{l|fi|-ja}}
* {{l|fi|-ri}}
* {{l|fi|-tär}}
* {{l|fi|-uri}}

===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|fi|superlative}}
From {{inh|fi|urj-fin-pro|*-in|id=superlative|pos=superlative suffix}} (related to {{cog|krl|-in}}), probably from {{inh|fi|urj-pro|*-ma}}. The oblique stem ''-impA, -immA'' is influenced by the oblique stem ''-mpA, -mmA'' of comparatives.

====Suffix====
{{fi-suffix|adjective}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Forms [[superlative]] adjectives.}}
#: {{suffixusex|fi|heikko|t1=weak|heikoin|t2=weakest}}
#: {{suffixusex|fi|matala|t1=low, shallow|matalin|t2=lowest, shallowest}}
#: {{suffixusex|fi|ruma|t1=ugly|rumin|t2=ugliest}}
#: {{suffixusex|fi|suuri|t1=large, big|suurin|t2=largest, biggest}}

=====Declension=====
Back vowel harmony declension (includes vowels a, o, u)
{{fi-decl-sisin|pos=adj|-|a}}
Front vowel harmony declension (includes vowels ä, ö, y)
{{fi-decl-sisin|pos=adj|-|ä}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|fi|id=superlative}}

=====See also=====
* {{l|fi|-mpi}}

===Etymology 3===
{{etymid|fi|instructive}}
See {{m|fi|-n|id=instructive}}.

====Suffix====
{{fi-suffix|inflectional}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Instructive plural suffix.}}
#: {{ux|fi|kaks'''in''' käs'''in'''|with two hands|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|fi|palja'''in''' silm'''in'''|with bare eyes|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|fi|pitk'''in''' askel'''in'''|with long steps|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|fi|hyvissä ajo'''in'''|in good time|inline=1}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|fi|id=instructive}}

===Etymology 4===
{{etymid|fi|possessive}}
Probably by {{metathesis|fi|nocap=1}}.

====Suffix====
{{fi-suffix|possessive}} {{tlb|fi|poetic}}

# {{non-gloss definition|A variant for the first-person singular possessive suffix {{m|fi|-ni}}.}}
#: {{m|fi|rakkain}} (for {{m|fi|rakkaani}})
#: {{m|fi||syödäksein}} (for {{m|fi|syödä|syödäkseni}})

=====Usage notes=====
The use of this suffix is restricted to cases where it is not preceded by a labial vowel ({{m|fi||o}}, {{m|fi||u}}, {{m|fi||y}}, {{m|fi||ö}}), the vowel {{m|fi||i}}, a long vowel (it is shortened if necessary) or a diphthong (since it itself forms a diphthong). It is not used if the resulting word would be monosyllabic.

===Etymology 5===
{{etymid|fi|adjectival}}
From {{inh|fi|urj-fin-pro|*-in|pos=adjectival suffix|id=adjectival}}, a variant of the diminutive suffix {{cog|urj-fin-pro|*-ma}}.

====Suffix====
{{fi-suffix|adjective}}

# {{lb|fi|non-productive}} {{non-gloss definition|Forms some adjectives.}}

=====Declension=====
Back vowel harmony declension (includes vowels a, o, u)
{{fi-decl-kytkin|-|||i|a}}
Front vowel harmony declension (includes vowels ä, ö, y)
{{fi-decl-kytkin|-|||i|ä}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|fi|id=adjectival}}

===Etymology 6===
From {{inh|fi|urj-fin-pro|*-den|pos=genitive plural suffix for some nouns}}, from the nominative plural ending {{m|urj-fin-pro|*-t}} + genitive ending {{m|urj-fin-pro|*-n}} (with an epenthetic *''-e-'').

====Suffix====
{{fi-suffix|inflectional}} {{tlb|fi|rare}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Forms the [[genitive]] plural.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Suffixed to the nominative singular but the final {{m|fi|-i}} changes to {{m|fi|-e-}}.
* Note, however, that the more common suffix for the [[genitive]] plural is -en added to the plural stem ending with i or j. A link consonant d is also sometimes present.
* Nowadays this suffix is rare and usually gives an archaic tone. Certain proper nouns, however, retain this, e.g. {{m|fi|Yhdysvaltain}}, from {{m|fi|Yhdysvallat}} ("United States", singular stem {{m|fi||Yhdysvalta-}}), where also the more modern form {{m|fi|Yhdysvaltojen}} is possible.

=====See also=====
* {{l|fi|-den}}
* {{l|fi|-en}}
* {{l|fi|-n}}
* {{l|fi|-ten}}
* {{l|fi|-tten}}

===Etymology 7===
See {{m|fi|-Vn|id=illative|pos=illative suffix}}.

====Suffix====
{{fi-suffix|inflectional}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Suffix variant for the illative singular, see {{m|fi|-Vn}}.}}

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|fi|a=in|-ni}}

