{{also|Dixie}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|hi|देगची|t=a kettle, a metallic cooking pot}}, from {{der|en|fa-cls|دیگچه|tr=degča|t=a pot, small cauldron}}, from {{af|fa|دیگ|ـچه|tr1=deg|tr2=-ča|t1=pot|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪk.si/|a=UK}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|military}} A large iron [[pot]], used in the army.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1903|title=Minutes of Evidence Taken Before the Royal Commission on the War in South Africa|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=iNcxAQAAMAAJ|publisher=H.M. Stationery Office|passage=four men generally like to mess together, and one cooking pot among them takes the place of a mess-tin or '''"dixie"'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|author=Arthur Guy Empey|title=Over the Top|passage=Then from the communication trenches came '''dixies''' or iron pots, filled with steaming tea, which had two wooden stakes through their handles, and were carried by two men.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1928|author=w:Siegfried Sassoon|title=Memoirs of a Fox-Hunting Man|publisher=Penguin 2013|page=261|passage=And what those ‘'''dixies'''’ of hot tea signified no one knows who wasn't there to wait for them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1929|author=w:Frederic Manning|title=The Middle Parts of Fortune|publisher=Vintage 2014|page=39|passage=Army rum is potent stuff, especially when the supplies of tea and water have run out, and one drinks it neat out of a '''dixie'''.}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|a large iron pot, used in the army}}
* Bengali: {{t+|bn|ডেকচি}}
* Hindi: {{t+|hi|देगची|f}}, {{t+|hi|देगचा|m}}, {{t+|hi|देग|m}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|котело́к|m}}
* Urdu: {{t|ur|دیگچی|f|tr=degcī}}, {{t|ur|فوجی دیگچی|f|tr=faujī degcī}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===See also===
* [[dixie cup]]

