===Etymology 2===
{{wikipedia|chi (force)}}
From the {{bor|en|cmn|-}} {{zh-l|氣}} from the {{bor|en|cmn-wadegiles|-}} romanization: ''chʻi⁴'', from {{der|en|ltc|-}} {{ltc-l|氣|id=1}}, from {{der|en|och|-}} {{och-l|氣|id=1|[[breath]], [[vapor]]}}. Compare modern {{cog|ja|-}} {{ja-r|気|き}}, {{cog|ko|기(氣)}} and {{cog|vi|khí}} ({{m|vi|氣}}).

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/t͡ʃiː/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-chi2.wav|a=Southern England}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{senseid|en|life force}} {{lb|en|philosophy}} A [[life force]] in [[traditional]] [[Chinese]] [[philosophy]], [[culture]], [[traditional Chinese medicine|medicine]], [[etc]], [[related]] (but not [[limited]]) to [[breath]] and [[circulation]].
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1987
|chapter=ch'i
|title=w:Encyclopedia Britannica
|url=https://archive.org/details/encyclopdiabrita17chic/
|edition=15th
|volume=3
|ISBN=0-85229-443-3
|LCCN=85-82101
|OCLC=852039661
|page=186
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/newencyclopaediav03ency/page/186/
|column=3
|text=Early Taoist philosophers and alchemists regarded '''''ch'i''''' as a vital force inhering in the breath and bodily fluids and developed techniques to alter and control the movement of '''''ch'i''''' within the body; their aim was to achieve physical longevity and spiritual power.
|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=w:Eoin Colfer|title=[[w:Artemis Fowl (novel)|Artemis Fowl]]|publisher=w:Viking Press|page=196|text=He took several deep breaths, finding his '''''chi''''' as Butler had taught him.}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=April 2, 2013
|author=David Tanis
|title=Hurry Up, Spring
|journal=w:The New York Times
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130402202252/https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/03/dining/turning-winter-into-spring-with-two-key-techniques.html
|archivedate=02 April 2013
|issn=0362-4331
|oclc=1645522
|section=Dining & Wine
|url2=https://archive.is/e333V
|text=At the greenmarket, it’s still mostly potatoes and apples. There are no tender greens, fava beans, peas, asparagus, artichokes, sorrel, rhubarb or early strawberries.<br>Those harbingers of the season are said to be full of '''chi''', or qi, the Chinese word for life force. We’re craving them as we’re craving lighter, brighter-tasting meals, food that is greener and fresher.}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=January 8, 2017
|author=Leslie Hsu Oh
|title=I tried the Chinese practice of ‘sitting the month’ after childbirth
|journal=w:The Washington Post
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170108174522/https://www.washingtonpost.com/national/health-science/i-tried-the-chinese-practice-of-sitting-the-month-after-childbirth/2017/01/06/54517ee0-ad0b-11e6-a31b-4b6397e625d0_story.html
|archivedate=08 January 2017
|issn=0190-8286
|oclc=2269358
|section=Health & Science
|url2=https://archive.is/FbUIL
|text=According to traditional Chinese medicine, blood carries '''chi''', your “life force,” which fuels all the functions of the body. When you lose blood, you lose '''chi''', and this causes your body to go into a state of yin (cold). When yin (cold) and yang (hot) are out of balance, your body will suffer physical disorders.}}

