{{reconstructed}}
==Proto-Uralic==

===Etymology===
Probably akin to {{cog|ira-pro|*kátah}} (compare {{cog|ae|𐬐𐬀𐬙𐬀|t=house/home, pit}}, {{cog|fa|کده|tr=kade|gloss=house}}), in which case it is a loan in one direction or the other, but the direction is not entirely clear. Many researchers have supported an early loanword from pre-Indo-Iranian into Uralic, but this is not certain, as the Iranian word has no known cognates in Indo-European, not even Indo-Aryan. The similarity may simply be a coincidence.<ref>{{R:fi:EVE|kota|cited=2022-11-29}}</ref>

Moreover, the root may have been a widespread [[Wanderwort]] across Eurasia; compare {{cog|ab|ақыҭа}}, {{cog|az|qutan||(dialectal) dugout for lambs}}, {{cog|xgn-pro|*kotan}} ({{cog|mn|хот||town}}), {{cog|tr|kodak||(dialectal) home}}, {{cog|ain|コタン|tr=kotan|t=village}}, {{cog|ja|鶏|tr=kutakake, kudakake|t=rooster|lit=house rooster|pos=hybrid Ainu-Japanese word}}, {{cog|ta|குடி||house, abode, home, family, lineage, town, tenants}}. Borrowings from Iranian (specifically Scythian) include {{cog|gem-pro|*kutą}}, {{m|gem-pro|*kutǭ}} (whence {{cog|en|cot}}, {{cog|nl|kot}}, {{cog|de|Kate}}) and {{cog|sla-pro|*xata||house}}.

===Noun===
{{head|urj-pro|noun}}

# {{topics|urj-pro|Buildings}} [[hut]], [[tent]], [[teepee]]

====Descendants====
