==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|stich||obsolete or misspelling}}

===Pronunciation===
[[File:Tied herringbone stitch.gif|thumb|Tied herringbone stitch.]]

* {{enPR|stĭch|a=RP,GA}}, {{IPA|en|/stɪt͡ʃ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-stitch.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪtʃ|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)teyg-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|stiche}}, from {{inh|en|ang|stiċe|t=a prick, puncture, stab, thrust with a pointed implement, pricking sensation, stitch, pain in the side, sting}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*stiki}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*stikiz|t=prick, piercing, stitch}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)teyg-|t=to stab, pierce}}.

Cognate with {{cog|nl|steek|t=prick, stitch}}, {{cog|de|Stich|t=a prick, piercing, stitch}}, {{cog|ang|stician|t=to stick, stab, pierce, prick}}. More at {{m|en|stick}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A single pass of a [[needle]] in [[sewing]]; the loop or turn of the [[thread]] thus made.
# An arrangement of stitches in sewing, or method of stitching in some particular way or style.
#: {{ux|en|cross '''stitch'''}}
#: {{ux|en|herringbone '''stitch'''}}
# {{lb|en|countable|and|uncountable}} An intense stabbing [[pain]] under the lower edge of the [[ribcage]], brought on by exercise or laughing.
#: ''I've got a '''stitch'''. I'm going to have to stop and rest.''
#: ''After about fifteen minutes I got terrible '''stitch'''.''
# A local sharp pain (anywhere); an acute pain, like the piercing of a needle.
#: {{ux|en|a '''stitch''' in the side}}
#* {{RQ:Burnet History|volume=I|page=588|passage=He was the next day taken with an oppreſſion, and as it ſeemed with a cold and with '''ſtitches''', which was indeed a pluriſy.}}
#* '''1848''', Gottlieb Heinrich Georg Jahr, ''New Manual; Or, Symptomen-codex'', page 186 ('''1846''', Samuel Hahnemann, ''Materia Medica Pura'', page 73):
#*: Violent continuous '''stitch''' in the region of the heart, the '''stitches''' multiplied when arresting the breathing. {{...}} Feeling of heaviness in the muscles of the neck; he is obliged to bend his neck backwards. Cramp-like pain in right muscles of the neck, terminating in a '''stitch'''; the pain went off after motion and returned afterwards. {{...}} Dull '''stitches''' in the region of the haunch-bones; pressure on the parts causes a simple pain. {{...}} Drawing '''stitch''' in the right thigh, not perceptible when standing or ascending an elevation.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1878|author=Timothy Field Allen|title=The Encyclopedia of pure materia medica v. 8, 1878|page=291
|passage=A sharp '''stitch''' in the left side of the head, on sitting down {{...}} A sharp '''stitch''' in the upper part of the right side of the head, {{...}}}}
# A single turn of the thread round a needle in [[knitting]]; a link, or loop, of yarn
#: {{ux|en|drop a '''stitch'''}}
#: {{ux|en|take up a '''stitch'''}}
# An arrangement of stitches in knitting, or method of knitting in some particular way or style.
# A space of work taken up, or gone over, in a single pass of the needle.
# A fastening, as of thread or wire, through the back of a [[book]] to connect the [[page]]s.
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Any space passed over; distance.
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|reprint=1875|part=2|page=148|passage=[Y]ou have gone a good '''ſtitch''', you may well be a weary; ſit down.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A contortion, or twist.
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|title=The Captain|act=II|scene=ii|page=54|url=https://archive.org/details/BeaumontFletcher1647/page/n191/mode/1up|column=2|passage=If you talke / Or pull your face into a '''ſtich''' againe, / As I love truth I ſhall be very angry.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} Any least part of a fabric or clothing.
#: {{ux|en|to wet every '''stitch''' of clothes}}
#: {{ux|en|She didn’t have a '''stitch''' on.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1828|volume=II|author=w:Thomas Keightley|title=Fairy Mythology|page=237|passage="Why, it's you that are stripping me," replied the Ogress, "and you have not left a '''stitch''' on me." "Where the devil is the quilt?" says the Ogre[.]}}
#* {{quote-song|1=en|year=1983|lyricist=w:Johnny Marr; w:Morrissey|title=w:This Charming Man|artist=w:The Smiths|text=I would go out tonight / But I haven't got a '''stitch''' to wear}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[furrow]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1750| title=The Modern Husbandman - Volume 4 |chapter=Of Plowings preparatory for Sofing Lent Crops of Grain, &c. in Chilturn Grounds|author=William Ellis | page=42| passage=Now plow down your Weat-'''stitches''', by running the Two-wheel Fallow-Plough three or four times through each '''Stitch''', which will almost level the Ground;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1757| title=A compleat body of husbandry|chapter=Of Tillage|author=Thomas Hale| page=241| passage=About DUNSTABLE they plow much in Stitches : and in Essex some very good Farmers practise this Method with very great Success; making five '''Stitches''' when they come to sow, which five make a Perch, so that between every two '''Stitches''' there is a Thorough a Foot wide.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1875| title=The Works of George Chapman: Poems and Minor Translations|author=George Chapman; Algernon Charles Swinburne | page=224| passage={{...}}for your oxen choose Two males of nine years old, for then their use Is most available, since their strengths are then Not of the weakest, and the youthful mean Sticks in their nerves still; nor will these contend With skittish tricks, when they their '''stitch''' should end, To break their plough, and leave their work undone.}}
# The space between two double furrows.

