====Derived terms====
* {{l|hy|բազկաթոռ}}
* {{l|hy|ճոճաթոռ}}

[[Category:hy:Chairs]]

==Old Armenian==

===Etymology===
The origin is {{unc|xcl|nocap=1}}.<ref name=HAB>{{R:xcl:HAB|vol=I|page=89ab}}</ref> An overview of opinions follows.

{{rel-top|Etymological note}}
Windischmann compared with {{cog|grc|θρόνος|gloss=seat, throne}}.<ref>{{R:xcl:Windischmann|page=12}}</ref> The comparison was revived by Marr, who derived both from a “Japhetic” root in the framework of his discredited {{w|Japhetic theory}}.<ref>{{R:Marr|vol=V|pages=76, 363}}</ref>

Müller derived from {{noncog|arc|אתר|tr=ʾtr}}, {{m|arc|אתרא|tr=ʾtrʾ|gloss=place}}, {{noncog|syc|ܐܬܪ|tr=ʾăṯar}}, {{m|syc|ܐܬܪܐ|tr=ʾaṯrā|gloss=place}}, for the sense development comparing {{noncog|fa|گاه|tr=gâh|gloss=throne; place}}.<ref>{{R:xcl:Müller:1863|page=11}}</ref><ref>{{R:xcl:Müller:1894|page=280}}</ref> This is rejected by de Lagarde and Hübschmann.<ref>{{R:xcl:Lagarde:1877|page=7|section=23}}</ref><ref>{{R:xcl:AG|page=300}}</ref>

{{xlit|hy|Հիւնքեարպէյէնտեան}} derived from {{cog|tr|oturmak|gloss=to take a sit}}, {{m|tr|oturak|gloss=seat, stool}}.<ref>{{R:xcl:Hiwnk|pages=153–154}}</ref> Ačaṙyan rejected the comparison because the older forms of the Turkic word contain an -''l''-: compare {{cog|chg|اولتورمق|tr=olturmaq|gloss=to sit}}.<ref name=HAB/>

Karst connected with {{cog|sux|𒉣𒇬|ts=tur|t=animal stall; [[triclinium]]; a place used to hold something or someone}}.<ref>{{R:xcl:Karst:1911|page=408}}</ref>

J̌ahukyan and Olsen mark the origin as unknown, with J̌ahukyan remarking that {{m|xcl||աթոռ}} is a late acquisition absent from Proto-Armenian.<ref>{{R:xcl:HLP|page=264}}</ref><ref>{{R:xcl:HSB|page=24b}}</ref><ref>{{R:xcl:Olsen|page=950}}</ref>

Perhaps from the name of {{m|en|Assur}}, the capital of the Assyrian Empire: compare {{cog|syc|ܐܬܘܪ|tr=ʾtwr|ts=ʾĀṯūr|t=the city of Assur}},<ref>{{cite-book|author=Marciak, Michał|title=Sophene, Gordyene, and Adiabene: Three Regna Minora of Northern Mesopotamia Between East and West|location=Leiden, Boston|publisher=Brill|year=2017|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=hwEtDwAAQBAJ&lpg=PR1&pg=PA317#v=onepage&q=%CA%BEtwr&f=false}}</ref> sometimes transcribed as Āṯōr,<ref>{{R:xcl:Jensen|page=214}}</ref><ref>{{R:Iranica|author=Widengren G.|date=December 15, 1987|url=http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/asoristan|title=ĀSŌRISTĀN}}</ref> {{cog|peo|𐎠𐎰𐎢𐎼𐎠|tr=a-θ-u-r-a|ts=Aθurā|t=Assyria}}, ultimately from {{cog|akk|𒀸𒋩|tr=Aššur}}. The sense development could have been "capital, throne city, the Seat" → "throne, seat". For such a lexicalisation of city names in Mesopotamia, the most advanced civilisation neighbouring Proto-Armenians, compare {{m|xcl|բաւիղ}}, {{m|xcl|քաղաք}}. In this case, a doublet of {{m|xcl|ասորի}}.

{{rel-bottom}}

===Noun===
{{xcl-noun}}

# [[chair]], [[seat]]; [[bench]]; [[throne]]
#: {{ux|xcl|նստուցանել յաթոռ|t=to make one sit on a throne or chair; to throne|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|նստել յաթոռ|t=to sit on a chair or throne|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|աթոռս արկանել|t=to give seats or chairs|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|xcl|ելանել յաթոռոյ|t=to rise up, to get up|inline=1}}
# a seat used in evacuating the bowels or in urinating, [[stool]]
# {{lb|xcl|biblical}} [[throne]] {{gloss|the third highest order of angel in Christian angelology, ranked above minions and below cherubim}}

====Declension====
{{xcl-noun-ո}}

====Derived terms====
