Page 1 -ism: Skipped, no changes
Page 1 -ism: -------- begin text --------
{{also|ism|ISM|-ism-|ism.}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Ultimately from either {{der|en|grc|-ισμός}}, a suffix that forms abstract nouns of action, state, condition, doctrine, from stem of verbs in {{m|grc|-ίζειν}} (whence {{cog|en|-ize}}); or from the related suffix {{der|en|grc|-ισμα}}, which more specifically expressed a finished act or thing done.

Many English nouns in ''-ism'' are loans of Greek nouns in {{m|grc|-ισμός}}, often via Latin and French, such as {{m|en|Judaism}}, a learned English formation from Latin attested from ca. 1500 and ultimately from {{m+|grc|Ἰουδαϊσμός}}. In {{der|en|LL.|-}}, the {{m|la|-ismus}} suffix became the ordinary ending for names of religions and ecclesiastical or philosophical systems or schools of thought, thus {{m|la|chrīstiānismus}} (whence 16th c. {{m|en|Christianism}}) in Tertullian, a trend continued in {{der|en|ML.|-}}, with e.g. {{m|la|pāgānismus}} attested by the 8th century. From the 16th century, such formations became very common in English, until the early 18th century mostly restricted to either root words of Greek or Latin origin ({{m|en|heroism}}, {{m|en|patriotism}}) or proper names ({{m|en|Calvinism}}, {{m|en|Lutheranism}}).

Productivity from root words with evidently non-Latin and non-Greek origin dates to the late 18th century (e.g. {{m|en|blackguardism}}). Reflecting this productivity, use of {{m|en|ism}} as a standalone noun is attested in Edward Pettit (1680) and becomes common from the mid-18th century. The narrowed sense of forming terms for ideologies based on the belief of superiority is based on coinages such as {{m|en|racism}} (1932) or {{m|en|sexism}} (1936) and productive since the 1970s.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪz.əm/|[ɪz.m̩]}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl--ism.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Suffix===
{{en-suffix|cat2=noun-forming suffixes|cat3=productive suffixes}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Used to form nouns of action, process, or result based on the accompanying verb ending in [[-ise]] or [[-ize]].}}
#: [[baptism]] (1300), [[aphorism]] (1528), [[criticism]] (1607), [[magnetism]] (1616)
# {{non-gloss definition|Used to form the name of a school of thought, system, or theory based on the name of its subject or object or alternatively on the name of its founder}} {{q|When de-capitalized, these overlap with the generic "doctrines" sense below, e.g. [[Liberalism]] vs. [[liberalism]].}}
#: [[Lutheranism]] (1560), [[Calvinism]] (1570), [[Protestantism]] (1606), [[Congregationalism]] (1716), [[Mohammedanism]] (1815),: [[Palamism]] (1949)
# {{non-gloss definition|Used to form names of a tendency of action, behaviour, condition, opinion, or state belonging to a class or group of persons, or the result of a [[doctrine]], [[ideology]], or [[principle]] or lack thereof.}}
#: [[atheism]] (1587), [[ruffianism]] (1589), [[giantism]] (1639), [[fanaticism]] (1652), [[theism]] (1678), [[religionism]] (1706), [[patriotism]] (1716), [[heroism]] (1717), [[despotism]] (1728), [[old-maidism]] (1776), [[capitalism]] (1792), [[nationism]] (1798), [[romanticism]] (1803), [[conservatism]] (1832), [[sexualism]] (1842), [[vegetarianism]] (1848), [[externalism]] (1856), [[young-ladyism]] (1869), [[opportunism]] (1870), [[blackguardism]] (1875), [[jingoism]] (1878), [[feminism]] (1895), [[dwarfism]] (1895)
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1990|author=Stephen King|title={{w|The Moving Finger (short story)|The Moving Finger}}|passage=Howard didn't care much for beer, but that night he helped himself to three cans of Vi's new find nevertheless. Vi commented on it, said that if she had known he was going to like it that much, she would have stopped by the drugstore and gotten him an IV hookup. Another time-honored Vi'''-ism'''.}}
#* {{quote-video game|en|year=2020|title=w:Ministry of Broadcast|publisher=w:Hitcents|system=w:Nintendo Switch|scene=game over|text=WE ARE EXPERIENCING BROADCAST NOOB'''ISM'''}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Used to form [[countable]] nouns indicating a [[peculiarity]] or [[characteristic]] of language}}
#: [[Atticism]] (1612), [[Gallicism]] (1656), [[archaism]] (1709), [[Americanism]] (1781), [[colloquialism]] (1834), [[newspaperism]] (1838), [[Shakespearianism]] (1886)
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=w:Nassim Nicholas Taleb|title={{w|The Black Swan: The Impact of the Highly Improbable}}|location=New York|publisher=Random House|isbn=9780399590450|page=136|passage=Note that these sayings attributed to Yogi Berra might be apocryphal {{...}} These sayings remain, however, quintessential '''Berraisms'''.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Used to form names of ideologies expressing belief in the superiority of a certain class within the concept expressed by the root word, or a pattern of behavior or a social norm that benefits members of the group indicated by the root word.}} {{q|Based on a late 20th-century narrowing of the "terms for a doctrine" sense.}}
#: [[racism]] (1932), [[sexism]] (1936), [[classism]] (1971), [[speciesism]] (1975), [[heterosexism]] (1979), [[ableism]] (1981)
# {{lb|en|medicine}} {{non-gloss definition|Used to form names of conditions (syndromes, diseases, disorders, defects, addictions) and therapeutical methods or doctrines.}}
#: [[aleydigism]], [[daturism]], [[senilism]], [[teratism]], [[cocainism]], [[climatism]], [[humorism]]

