===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|fa|pal||tr=AYMT|ts=kay|sc=Phlv}} {{m|pal|𐫐𐫏𐫏|ts=kay}}, {{m|pal|𐫐𐫏|ts=kay}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{fa-IPA|kay}}

====Adverb====
{{fa-regional|کی|کی|кай}}
{{head|fa|adverb|tr=key}}

# {{lb|fa|interrogative}} [[when]]?
#: {{uxi|fa|'''کَی''' خَو می‌رِی؟|tr='''kay''' xaw mē-rī?|t=when are you going to sleep?|footer={{qual|Dari|informal}}}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|fa|pal|𐫐𐫏|ts=kē}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{fa-IPA|`kī}}

====Pronoun====
{{fa-regional|کی|کی|кӣ}}
{{head|fa|pronoun|tr=ki}}

# {{lb|fa|interrogative}} [[who]]?
#: {{uxi|fa|شما '''کی''' هستین؟|tr=šomâ '''ki''' hastin?|Who are you?|footer={{qual|informal polite}}}}

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alt|fa|که|tr=ke||archaic}}

===Etymology 3===
{{inh+|fa|pal||sc=Phlv|tr=kad|ts=kay|t=legendary ancient king of Iran}}, borrowed from {{der|fa|ae|𐬐𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬌|t=poet, bard; vision-poet, Mazdean sacrificer}}, from {{der|fa|ira-pro|*káwHiš}}, from {{der|fa|iir-pro|*káwHiš}}, from {{der|fa|ine-pro|*(s)kewh₁-}}. The visionary poets of the [[Avesta]] were mistakenly interpreted as ancient kings by the [[Sasanian]] [[Zoroastrian]] [[priest]]s.

====Pronunciation====
{{fa-IPA|kay}}

====Noun====
{{fa-noun|tr=key|pl=کیان|pltr=kayân}}

# {{lb|fa|Iranian mythology}} Key, Kay: {{n-g|the title of a number of legendary kings of Iran, belonging to the mythical {{w|Kayanian dynasty}}.}}

