# {{ISO 639|2&3}}

==English==
{{wikipedia|gay (term)}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|gā}}, {{IPA|en|/ɡeɪ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-gay.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪ|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
[[File:Gay couple.jpg|160px|thumb|Male gay couple]]
[[File:Lesbian kiss.jpg|160px|thumb|Female gay couple]]
From {{inh|en|enm|gay}}, from {{der|en|fro|gai||joyful, laughing, merry}}, usually thought to be a borrowing of {{der|en|pro|gai||impetuous, lively}}, from {{der|en|got|*𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌴𐌹𐍃||impetuous}}, merging with earlier {{der|en|fro|jai}} {{gloss|"merry"; see {{l|en|jay}}}}, from {{der|en|frk|*gāhi}};<ref>Alain Rey, ed., ''Dictionnaire historique de la langue française'', vol. 2, s.v. “gai” (Paris: Le Robert, 2006).</ref> both from {{der|en|gem-pro|*ganhuz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*ganhwaz||sudden}}. This is possibly derived from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ǵʰengʰ-||to stride, step}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*ǵʰēy-||to go}},<ref>Marlies Philippa et al., eds., ''Etymologisch Woordenboek van het Nederlands'', A-Z, s.v. “gauw” (Amsterdam UP, 3 Dec. 2009): [http://www.etymologie.nl].</ref><ref>Louis Guinet, ''Les emprunts gallo-romans au germanique'' (Paris: Klincksieck, 1982).</ref> but Kroonen rejects this derivation and treats the Germanic word as having no known etymology.<ref>{{R:gem:EDPG|*ganhu-|167f.}}</ref>
{{rel-top|cognates and sense derivation}}
Cognate with {{cog|nl|gauw||fast, quickly}}, Westphalian {{cog|nds|gau}}, {{m|nds|gai||fast, quick}}, {{cog|de|jäh||abrupt, sudden}}.

Anatoly Liberman, following Frank Chance and Harri Meier, believes {{der|en|fro|gai}} was instead a native development from {{der|en|la|vagus||wandering, inconstant, flighty}}, with *[w] > [g] as in {{cog|fr|gaine}}.<ref>{{cite-web|date=2012-02-01|title=The deep roots of gaiety|author=Anatoly Liberman|work=OUPblog|titleurl=http://blog.oup.com/2012/02/word-origin-roots-gay/}}</ref>

The sense of homosexual (first recorded no later than 1937 by Cary Grant in the film ''Bringing Up Baby'', and possibly earlier in 1922 in the poem "Miss Furr and Miss Skeene" by {{w|Gertrude Stein}}<ref>Blackmer, Corrine E (1995), "Gertrude Stein", in Claude J. Summers, The Gay and Lesbian Literary Heritage, {{ISBN|0-8050-5009-4}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book| author=Gertrude Stein| authorlink=Gertrude Stein| year=1922|title=Geography and Plays| chapter=Miss Furr and Miss Skeene| chapterurl=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/33403/33403-h/33403-h.htm#MISS_FURR_AND_MISS_SKEENE| passage=They stayed there and were gay there, not very gay there, just gay there. They were both gay there, they were regularly working there both of them cultivating their voices there, they were both gay there. Georgine Skeene was gay there and she was regular, regular in being gay, regular in not being gay, regular in being a gay one who was one not being gay longer than was needed to be one being quite a gay one. They were both gay then there and both working there then.}}</ref>) was shortened from earlier ''gay cat'' ("homosexual boy") in underworld and prison slang, itself first attested about 1935, but used earlier for a young tramp or hobo attached to an older one.<ref>Robert K. Barnhart, ed., ''Chambers Dictionary of Etymology'', s.v. “gay” (Edinburgh: Chambers Harrap, [2008], c1988), 425.</ref>

Pejorative usage is probably due to hostility towards homosexuality.

The sense of ‘upright’, used in reference to a dog’s tail, probably derives from the ‘happy’ sense of the word.
{{rel-bottom}}

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}
