====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|in plural}} A [[homosexual]], ''especially'' a [[male]] homosexual.
#: {{cot|en|lesbian}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1969|title=N.Y. Gays: Will the Spark Die?|journal=The Advocate
|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=P2MEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA31&lpg=PA31&dq=%22NY+Gays:+Will+the+Spark+Die?%22 <!-- not a link to the article itself, but a link which verifies the existence of the article -->
|text=[headline] N.Y. '''Gays''': Will the Spark Die?
}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1974|title=Show Business Laid Bare|author=Earl Wilson
|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=QJ8HAQAAMAAJ&q=%22a+gay+named%22
|text="Same-sex dancing, as we call it, is quite legal," a '''gay''' named Lew Todd, who was one of the spokesmen, spoke up.
}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2003|author=Marilyn J. Davidson; Sandra L. Fielden|title=Individual Diversity and Psychology in Organizations|page=73
|passage=Yet that does not mean that the issues, concerns and attitudes of '''gays''' and lesbians in the workplace are not important.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2004|author=Betty Berzon|title=Permanent Partners: Building Gay & Lesbian Relationships That Last|page=20
|passage=Older '''gays''' and lesbians often relegate themselves to separate and unequal meeting places.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Todd J. Ormsbee|title=The Meaning of Gay|page=313
|passage=On June 28, 1970, young '''gays''' in the city held a “Gay-in” in Golden Gate Park, and Gay Sunshine ran a photo of some of the participants in their inaugural issue {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|dialectal|obsolete}} Something which is bright or colorful, such as a picture or a flower.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1839|author=Charles Clark|title=John Noakes and Mary Styles|section=st. 157
|passage=At a stall soon Mary bote / A hume-book full ov '''gays'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1892|author=P. H. Emerson|title=A Son of the Fens|page=73
|passage=I had no books to read, but plenty of '''gays''' to look at.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1893|author=Cozens-Hardy|title=Broad Nrf.|page=38
|passage=‘Can't you mow the aftermath in the churchyard before Sunday?’ ‘Not time enough, sir, but I'll cut off they '''gays'''.’}}
#* {{ante|1900}}, W. R. Eaton of Norfolk, quoted in '''1900''', Joseph Wright, ''The English Dialect Dictionary'':
#*: There's a good child; look at the '''gays''', and keep quiet.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[ornament]], a [[knick-knack]].
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables|passage=Look upon precepts in emblems, as they do to upon '''gays''' and pictures.}}
#* '''1906''', ''Cornish Notes & Queries: (first Series)'' (Cornish Telegraph, Peter Penn), page 132:
#*: If however the stranger be suspected of “sailing under false colours," when they are all in familiar chat about nothing in particular, “Cousin Jacky” will take occasion to say to the new chum, “My dear; ded 'e ever see a duck clunk a '''gay'''?" {{...}} no more deceived by him than a duck can be made to clunk (swallow) a '''gay''' (fragment of broken crockery).

=====Usage notes=====
* {{senseid|en|gay noun notes}}''Gay'' may be regarded as offensive when used as a noun to refer to particular individuals.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20070125105128/http://www.bartleby.com/64/C006/034.html The American Heritage® Book of English Usage: A Practical and Authoritative Guide to Contemporary English] (1996), "gay"</ref>
* ''Gay'' is sometimes used broadly to refer to any man who is attracted to and/or sexually active with other men, or any woman attracted to or active with other women, even if not exclusively, e.g. if their orientation is in fact [[bisexual]].<!--see e.g. a Google Books search for "bisexual gays"--><ref>For example: David Kaufman, ''Untying the Knot: A Husband and Wife's Story of Coming Out Together'' (2012, {{ISBN|1936374900}}): '''Gays''', and apparently lesbians, are discouraged from being openly bisexual. The cultural standard in the gay community is that you have to pick one sex and stick to it.</ref>

=====Synonyms=====
