{{also|-ī|Appendix:Variations of "i"}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
From the {{der|en|ar|-}} [[نسبة|nisba]] suffix {{m|ar|ـِيّ}} or from {{der|en|he|־י|tr=-i}}, both sharing a common {{der|en|sem|-}} root. In English, productive from the 19th century.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|ē}}, {{IPA|en|/iː/}}

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix|id=demonym|cat2=adjective-forming suffixes|cat3=noun-forming suffixes}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Used to form adjectives and nouns describing people of a particular city, region, or country, and the [[language]] spoken by these people.}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|Iraq|Iraqi}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|Israel|Israeli}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|Pakistan|Pakistani}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|Bengal|Bengali}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|Nepal|Nepali}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|w:Desh|Desi}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|suffix to form a demonym (especially in the Middle East)}}
* Arabic: {{t+|ar|ـِيّ}}
* Galician: {{t|gl|-í|m|f}}
* Hebrew: {{t|he|־ִי|m|tr=-i}}
* Hindi: {{t|hi|-ई}}
* Hungarian: {{t+|hu|-i}}
* Spanish: {{t+|es|-í|m|f}}
{{trans-bottom}}

====See also====
* {{l|en|-er|id=demonym}}
* {{l|en|-stan}}

====References====
* OED, s.v. "-i, ''suffix<sup>2</sup>''".

===Etymology 2===
From {{bor|en|la|-ī}} (nominative plural).

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|ī}}, {{IPA|en|/aɪ/|qq=traditional English}}
* {{enPR|ē}}, {{IPA|en|/iː/|qq=rare; imitation of original Latin}}

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}} {{etymid|en|latin}}

# {{n-g|Forms an alternative [[plural]] ending for various words borrowed from [[Latin]] that end in ⟨us⟩ in the singular.}}
#: {{sufex|en|focus|foci}}
#: {{sufex|en|radius|radii}}

=====Usage notes=====
* "Properly" or prescriptively speaking the ending is only applicable to words that were second-declension masculine nouns in Latin, such as the above examples. In practice it is often extended to cases like {{m|en|octopus}} (where the Latin plural was not {{m|la||*octopi}} but rather {{m|la|octopodes}}) and {{m|en|ignoramus}} (which was a verb in Latin, not a noun).

====References====
* OED, s.v. "-i, ''suffix<sup>1</sup>''".

===Etymology 3===
From {{bor|en|it|-i}} (masculine plural), from {{der|en|la|-ī}} (see etymology 2).

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|ē}}, {{IPA|en|/iː/}}

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}} {{etymid|en|italian}}

# {{n-g|Forms an alternative [[plural]] ending for words borrowed from [[Italian]] that end in ⟨o⟩ or ⟨e⟩ in the singular.}}
#: {{sufex|en|concerto|concerti}}
#: {{sufex|en|calzone|calzoni}}

===Etymology 4===
Variant of {{m|en|-ie}} or {{m|en|-y}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|ē}}, {{IPA|en|/iː/}}

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}}

# {{n-g|Used to form [[diminutive]]s of [[given name]]s.}}
#: {{coi|en|[[Becki|Beck'''i''']], [[Benji|Benj'''i''']], [[Jimmi|Jimm'''i''']], [[Lexi|Lex'''i''']], [[Nicki|Nick'''i''']], [[Sammi|Samm'''i''']], [[Sandi|Sand'''i''']], [[Trini|Trin'''i''']], [[Vicki|Vick'''i''']]}}

==Afar==

===Pronunciation===
* {{q|masculine}} {{IPA|aa|/-i/}}
* {{q|feminine}} {{IPA|aa|/-ˈi/}}

===Suffix===
{{head|aa|suffix|head=-i|head2=-í}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Used to create [[nouns]] from class I [[verbs]], denoting either the [[agent]] or the [[instrument]] of the action.}}

====Usage notes====
* Used together with the prefix {{l|aa|t-}} to create [[feminine]] [[nouns]]. The stress will shift to the last syllable.
* Used together with the prefix {{l|aa|y-}} to create [[masculine]] [[nouns]]. The stress will not be on the last syllable.

===References===
* {{R:aa:Hassan Kamil:2015|118}}

==Albanian==

===Etymology===
From the ending {{der|sq|ine-pro|*-osyo}}, from {{inh|sq|ine-pro|*-os}}. cognate to {{cog|cms|-aihi}}.<ref>Matzinger 2015, pp. 62–66</ref><ref>Ismajli 2015, pp. 65–68.</ref><ref>Matzinger, Joachim (2017). "The Lexicon of Albanian". In Klein, Jared; Joseph, Brian; Fritz, Matthias (eds.). Handbook of Comparative and Historical Indo-European Linguistics. 3. Walter de Gruyter. </ref> Related to {{cog|sq|i||of, the, to}}.

