==Indonesian==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|id|[-i]}}

===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|id|ms|-i}}, from {{inh|id|poz-pro|*-i|pos=local transitive suffix}}

====Suffix====
{{head|id|suffix|non-active/imperative/basic|{{color|black|-i}}|active|meng- -i|passive|di- -i|perfective passive|ter- -i}}

# {{l|en|verb}}al {{l|en|suffix}} for manipulating, completing, or placing the object from a {{l|en|noun}}.
# {{l|en|transitive}} {{l|en|verb}}al {{l|en|suffix}} for {{l|en|causation}} in {{l|en|surface}} of the object from an {{l|en|adjective}}.
# {{l|en|transitive}} {{l|en|verb}}al {{l|en|suffix}} for {{l|en|doing}} activity to the object from a {{l|en|transitive}} {{l|en|verb}}.
# {{ngd|[[transitive]] [[locative]] [[imperative]] [[verbal]] [[suffix]] from a [[noun]]}}:
## to apply or give [base] to the object.
##: {{uxi|id|Hormati orang tua!|t=honour/respect parent!}}
## to remove [base] from the object.
##: {{uxi|id|kuliti|(de)skin, peel}}
# {{ngd|[[transitive]] [[adverbial]] [[imperative]] [[verbal]] [[suffix]] from a [[noun]]}}: to act as/be [base] with reference to object
#: {{uxi|id|Sutradarai film ini!|t=Direct this movie!|lit=Become the producer of this film!}}

=====Usage notes=====
* This suffix is never used after verbs ending in final ''-i'' for euphonic reasons and to avoid near-homophony with bare forms (''-∅''). Thus, {{m|id|beli|bel'''i'''|to buy}} does not derive into ''*belii'', although other colloquial suffixes allow violation of the previous rule such as {{m|id|beliin|beli'''in'''|buy it!}}. Other examples include {{m|id|cari}} (← ''*carii'') and {{m|id|capai}} (← ''*capaii'').
* When this suffix is combined with the prefixes {{m|id|meng-}}, {{m|id|di-}}, {{m|id|ter-}}, and {{m|id|ke-}}, the imperative aspect is lost.

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|id|ms|-i}}, from {{der|id|ar|-}} [[نسبة|nisba]] suffix {{m|ar|ـِيّ}}.

====Suffix====
{{head|id|suffix}}

# {{n-g|adjectives suffix (with the meaning “related to ...”)}}

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alter|id|-iah|-ni|-wi|-wiah}}

===Derived terms===
{{suffixsee|id}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:KBBI Daring}}

==Italian==

===Etymology 1===

====Suffix====
{{it-suffix form|verb}}

# {{n-g|used with a stem to form the [[second person|second-person]] [[singular]] [[present]] of regular {{m|it|-are}}, {{m|it|-ere}} verbs and those {{m|it|-ire}} verbs that do not take "isc"}}
# {{n-g|used with a stem to form the second-person [[imperative]] of -ere verbs}}
# {{n-g|used with a stem to form the first-, second- and third-person singular [[present subjunctive]] of {{m|it|-are}} verbs}}
# {{n-g|used with a stem to form the third-person singular imperative of -are verbs}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|it|la|-ī}}, nominative plural, or from {{inh|it|la|-ōs}}, accusative plural, of masculine nouns in {{m|la|-us}}.

====Suffix====
{{it-suffix form|noun}}

# {{n-g|used to form the plural of nouns ending in {{m|it|-o}}}}

===Etymology 3===
There are two competing theories:
# Formed analogically by borrowing the {{m|it|-i}} plural of Italian nouns in {{m|it|-o}}.
# Formed phonologically from the expected Latin nominative/accusative plural {{m|la|-ēs}} according to the sound change ''-es'' > ''-ei'' > ''-i''.

====Suffix====
{{it-suffix form|noun}}

# {{n-g|used to form the plural of nouns ending in {{m|it|-e}}}}

===Etymology 4===
Perhaps from the Latin genitive singular ending {{m|la|-ī}} of masculine nouns in {{m|la|-us}}.

====Suffix====
{{it-noun|mfbysense|#}}

# {{n-g|forms surnames from proper names, occupations, etc.}}
#: {{afex|it|Giacomo<t:James, Jacob>|Giacomi<pos:surname>}}
#: {{afex|it|Giacometto<t:Jim, Jake>|Giacometti<pos:surname>}}
#: {{afex|it|ferraro<t:smith>|Ferrari<pos:surname>}}

