==Volapük==

===Suffix===
{{head|vo|suffix}}

# A [[morpheme]] used to mark the [[accusative]] [[singular]] of a [[word]] (such as a [[noun]] or [[pronoun]]).
#: {{ux|vo|Dog beiton mani. / Mani beiton dog|The dog bites the man.}}
#: {{ux|vo|Mot löfof omi. / Omi löfof mot. (a.s., hicili, soni, u higaaporn).|The mother loves him. (e.g. the (male) child, son, or (male) lovebird).}}

==Welsh==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|cy|/i/}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|cy|cel-bry-pro|*-i}}, from {{inh|cy|cel-pro||*-esa}}, plural ending of neuter s-stems like {{m|cel-pro|*tegos|t=house}} (whence {{cog|cy|tŷ}}, plural {{m|cy|tai}} < ''*tegos'', plural ''*tegesa'').<ref>{{R:cel:Schrijver|page=393}}</ref> Cognate with {{cog|kw|-i}}.

====Suffix====
{{head|cy|suffix}}

# {{n-g|Pluralisation suffix}}
#: {{syn|cy|-aid|-aint|-au|-ed|-edd|-en|-iadau|-iaid|-iau|-ion|-od|-oedd|-on|-ydd|-yr|-ys}}
#: {{suffixusex|cy|plwyf|t1=parish|plwyfi|t2=parishes}}
#: {{suffixusex|cy|maen|t1=stone|meini|t2=stones}}
#: {{suffixusex|cy|toiled|t1=toilet|toiledi|t2=toilets}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|cy|owl|-im}}, from {{inh|cy|cel-bry-pro|*-iβ̃}}.

====Suffix====
{{head|cy|suffix}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Forms [[w:Verbnoun|verbnouns]] from verb stems.}}
#: {{syn|cy|-o|-u}}

=====Usage notes=====
This suffix is mostly used for verbs where the stem ends in the consonant ''w'' (though for some such verbs, such as {{m|cy|cadw}}, the verbnoun is given by the stem without a suffix) or the vowel in the last syllable is ''o'', ''oe'', or sometimes ''a''.<ref>{{R:cy:Morris Jones|section=202 v}}.</ref>

===Etymology 3===

====Suffix====
{{head|cy|suffix}}

# {{non-gloss definition|forming abstract nouns}}, [[-ness]], [[-ment]]
#: {{suffixusex|cy|diog|t1=lazy|diogi|t2=laziness}}
#: {{suffixusex|cy|truan|t1=poor, wretched|trueni|t2=pity, shame}}
#: {{suffixusex|cy|tlawd|t1=poor|tlodi|t2=poverty}}

=====Usage notes=====
''-i'' causes i-[[affection]] of internal vowels.

===Etymology 4===

====Suffix====
{{head|cy|suffix}}

# {{non-gloss definition|used to form pet names}}
#: {{suffixusex|cy|Wil|Wili}}
#: {{suffixusex|cy|Siân|Siani}}
#: {{suffixusex|cy|mam|t1=mother, mum|mami|t2=mummy}}

===Etymology 5===

====Suffix====
{{head|cy|suffix}}

# {{lb|cy|literary}} {{n-g|verb suffix for the [[second person|second-person]] [[singular]] [[present]] [[indicative]]/[[future]]}}
# {{lb|cy|colloquial}} {{n-g|verb suffix for the [[second person|second-person]] [[singular]] [[future]]}}

=====Usage notes=====
In the literary language, ''-i'' causes i-[[affection]] of internal vowels, for example, {{suffixusex|cy|canu|t1=to sing|ceni|t2=you sing, you will sing}}.

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|cy}}

====References====
<references/>

==Ye'kwana==
{{mch-variant}}

===Etymology 1===
{{unk|mch}}; unlike the other possessive suffixes found in the language, {{m|mch||-i}} has no known cognates in any other Cariban language.

====Pronunciation====
* {{mch-IPA}}

====Suffix====
{{head|mch|suffix}}

# {{non-gloss definition|marks a noun as possessed; used mostly with nouns referring to instruments, technologies, and manufactured products, and also with all loanwords}}

===Etymology 2===

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|mch|alt1=-∅|||allophone after diphthongal {{m|mch||i}}}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{mch-IPA}}

====Suffix====
{{head|mch|suffix}}

# {{non-gloss definition|forms the singular of the recent past perfective tense}}
# {{non-gloss definition|forms the singular of the distant past perfective tense when both the agent and patient (if there is one) of the verb are third-person}}

=====Usage notes=====
This suffix does not cause syllable reduction. When it attaches to a stem that ends in a vowel followed by {{m|mch||i}}, this suffix is unrealized (disappears).

The second sense can be readily distinguished from the first because it requires the distant-past third-person marker {{m|mch|kün-}} instead of ordinary person markers.

=====Derived terms=====
{{col-auto|mch|-icho}}

===Etymology 3===
Possibly of the same origin as the previous suffix.

====Pronunciation====
* {{mch-IPA}}

====Suffix====
{{head|mch|suffix}}

# {{non-gloss definition|forms the prohibitive mood}}

=====Usage notes=====
When the patient/object of a verb with this suffix is third-person, ''or'' when the object/patient is first-person and the subject/agent is second-person, it must also take the prefix {{m|mch|ön-}} (or one of its allomorphs) after the person marker. However, this prefix is not found when the object/patient is first-person and the subject/agent is third-person.

===Etymology 4===

====Pronunciation====
* {{mch-IPA}}

====Suffix====
{{head|mch|suffix}}

# {{non-gloss definition|forms the interrogative of the third-person form {{m|mch|na}} of the copula {{m|mch|ei}}}}

===References===
* {{R:mch:Cáceres|112–113, 204–206, 213–222, 233, 440|head=-i}}

