===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|fr|fro|ore}}, from {{inh|fr|VL.|[[hā]] [[horā]]}}, alteration of {{m|la|[[hāc]] [[horā]]||(in) this hour|pos=ablative}}. Compare {{cog|es|ahora}}, {{cog|pt|agora}}.

====Adverb====
{{fr-adv}}

# {{lb|fr|obsolete}} [[now]], [[presently]]

====Conjunction====
{{head|fr|conjunctions}}

# [[yet]], [[however]], [[now]], [[that said]], [[as it happens]] ({{n-g|introduces the second term in a syllogism}})

====Usage notes====
This is often used to introduce contrasting information (like English {{m|en|however}}). However, the information need not be contrasting, but can simply be supplemental information that leads to a subsequent conclusion (similar to English {{m|en|as it happens}}).

===Further reading===
* {{R:fr:TLFi}}

{{cln|fr|coordinating conjunctions}}
{{C|fr|Chemical elements|Yellows|Metals}}

==Ido==

===Etymology===
Borrowing from {{bor|io|fr|or}}, {{bor|io|it|ora}} and {{bor|io|es|ahora}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|io|/ɔr/}}

===Conjunction===
{{head|io|conjunctions}}

# [[now]], [[but]] {{gloss|in argument}}

====Usage notes====
'''Or''' expresses not only a sequence of two propositions, but induces a new argument, a further premise, explanation, motive. When the premise (motive) follows the conclusion, {{m|io|nam}} is used instead.

==Italian==

