===Etymology 2===
[[File:Oil painting of a man smoking an opium pipe, Europe Wellcome L0058627.jpg|thumb|A painting of an opium-smoker which used to hang in Ah Sing’s opium den on [[w:Victoria, London|Victoria Street]] in London, England.]]

Origin uncertain, but probably from {{der|en|yue|癮||[[craving#Noun|craving]]|tr=jan{{sup|5}}}} originally in reference to opium addiction, {{m|yue|煙癮}} or {{m|yue|菸癮}}: {{m|yue|煙}}, {{m|yue|菸||[[smoke#Noun|smoke]], ''specifically'' [[opium]]|tr=jin{{sup|1}}}}. Compare the later ''[[#Etymology 3|yen]]'' (“opium”) and {{m|en|yen-yen}}.<ref>"yen, ''n.''²", in the ''Oxford English Dictionary''. Oxford: Oxford University Press.</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|desire}} A strong [[desire]], [[urge]], or [[yearning]].
#: {{syn|en|craving|desire|hankering|jones|longing|urge|yearning}}
#: {{ux|en|humankind's '''yen''' for knowledge}}
#* '''1934''', Lew Levenson, chapter XX, in ''Butterfly Man'', New York, N.Y.: Castle Books, <small>{{OCLC|917633074}}</small>; republished New York, N.Y.: Castle Books, [1960?], <small>{{OCLC|6191025}}</small>, [https://archive.org/stream/LevensonButterflyMan/Levenson_-_Butterfly_Man#page/n210/mode/1up/ page 208]:
#*: She repeated the words: "You for me and me for you," then hummed: "Two for tea and tea for two&nbsp;…" Her voice trailed off&nbsp;… "All I got is a '''yen''' for Diana and my sweet little cute little Zigzag."
#* {{quote-journal|en|url=https://classic.esquire.com/article/1936/2/1/the-crack-up|title=The Crack-Up|month=February|year=1936|author=w:F. Scott Fitzgerald|work=Esquire|passage=Like most middle-westerners, I have never had any but the vaguest race prejudices—I always had a secret '''yen''' for the lovely Scandinavian blondes who sat on porches in St. Paul but hadn’t emerged enough economically to be part of what was then society.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:J P Donleavy|title=The Ginger Man|year=1955| year_published=1955 (France)| page=342|passage=And Clocklan with a nurse for sure again. Always with nurses. Always with blonde hair. His maid has black and I guess he thrives on variety. And over there are some elderly ones with diamonds on their chests in lieu of the other things. Sometimes have a '''yen''' to get one of them in bed. Old age no object.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Natalie Angier|chapter=Of Hoggamus and Hogwash: Putting Evolutionary Psychology on the Couch|title=Woman: An Intimate Biography|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|Houghton Mifflin Company]]|year=1999|page=382|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=NIonAf-m9OwC&pg=PT382|isbn=978-0-395-69130-4|passage=If a fellow chooses to tell himself that his '''yen''' for the fetching young intern in his office and his concomitant disgruntlement with his aging wife's housekeeping lacunae make perfect Darwinian sense, who am I to argue with him?}}

